/* ============================================================
   BIO 004 Human Anatomy, Fall 2026
   bio004-tbl-apps.js   (source of record for TBL application questions)

   Team application questions for the TBL Study Group tool
   (bio004-tbl-study.html). 404 questions: 276 lecture, 128 lab.
   Written from the card bank, her notes and the atlas, then checked
   by a separate reviewer for wrong keys, second defensible answers,
   false statements, scope and DOK.

   Fields: id, mode (lecture or lab), topic (card bank topic id, or a
   lab topic id from bio004-tbl-lab-data.js), comp (competency id from
   competenciesfall2026.js), type (Ranking, Sequence, Priority,
   Trigger), dok (3 or 4), q, options (4), correctIndex, why, notes
   (one per option, in option order), fix (what to review if missed).
   The app reshuffles choices for every group, so the stored position
   of the correct choice never reaches students.

   Edit questions here. Check edits with:
     node tools/validate_tbl_apps.js bio004-tbl-apps.js
   ============================================================ */
window.BIO004_TBL_APPS = { version: 1, items: [
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-terminology-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-terminology","comp":"w1-directional-terms","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient comes in with three injuries: a fractured right femur, a bruise on the left shoulder, and a cut on the right hand. Which description of how the other two injuries relate to the fracture is correct?","options":["The cut hand is contralateral to the fracture, and the bruised shoulder is ipsilateral to it","The cut hand is ipsilateral to the fracture, and the bruised shoulder is contralateral to it","The cut hand and the bruised shoulder are both ipsilateral to the fracture","The cut hand and the bruised shoulder are both contralateral to the fracture"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Ipsilateral and contralateral compare sides of the body, not limbs. To use them, first decide which side each injury is on: the femur and the hand are both on the right, and the shoulder is on the left. Same side gives ipsilateral, and opposite side gives contralateral, so the hand is ipsilateral to the fracture and the shoulder is contralateral to it.","notes":["This reverses the two terms. Ipsilateral means the same side and contralateral means the opposite side, so the right hand matches the right femur and the left shoulder does not.","Correct. The hand and the femur are both on the right side, so they are ipsilateral, and the left shoulder is on the opposite side, so it is contralateral.","Only the hand shares the right side with the femur. The shoulder is on the left, the opposite side, so it is contralateral. Both would be ipsilateral if the bruise were on the right shoulder.","Being on a different limb is not the same as being on the opposite side. Contralateral only describes the left versus right side of the body, and the hand is on the same side as the femur."],"fix":"Review the paired directional terms, especially ipsilateral and contralateral, and practice deciding which side of the body each injury is on before comparing them."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-terminology-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-terminology","comp":"w1-levels-organization","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these examples from the stomach from the lowest level of structural organization to the highest: the digestive system, a single epithelial cell, the stomach, and the simple columnar epithelium lining the stomach.","options":["Simple columnar epithelium, epithelial cell, stomach, digestive system","Epithelial cell, stomach, simple columnar epithelium, digestive system","Epithelial cell, simple columnar epithelium, digestive system, stomach","Epithelial cell, simple columnar epithelium, stomach, digestive system"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each level of structural organization is built from the one below it. Start by naming the level of each example: a single cell is the cellular level, the simple columnar epithelium is a tissue because it is a group of similar cells doing one job, the stomach is an organ made of several tissues, and the digestive system is a group of organs working together. Once each example has its level, the order follows the chemical, cellular, tissue, organ, organ system, organismal sequence.","notes":["This puts the tissue below the cell. A tissue is built from many similar cells, so the cell has to come first. Students pick this when they read epithelium as a single structure rather than a group of cells.","This puts the organ below the tissue. The stomach is built from several tissues, including its epithelial lining, so the epithelium must rank lower than the stomach.","This puts the organ system below the organ. The digestive system is a group of organs, and the stomach is only one of them, so the stomach must come before the system.","Correct. A cell is the cellular level, a sheet of similar cells is the tissue level, the stomach is an organ, and the digestive system is an organ system."],"fix":"Review the six levels of structural organization and practice naming the level of a real example, such as a cell, a tissue or an organ."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-terminology-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-terminology","comp":"w1-planes-sections","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A radiologist wants one image that shows the trachea lying in front of the esophagus, and the esophagus lying in front of the vertebral column, along the whole length of the neck. Which section is the best choice?","options":["A midsagittal section through the center of the neck","A transverse section through the middle of the neck","A frontal section through the middle of the neck","A parasagittal section through the side of the neck"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Two facts decide this. First, the structures need to be shown front to back, which rules out a frontal plane, because a frontal cut separates front from back instead of showing both. Second, they need to be shown along the full length of the neck, which rules out a transverse cut, because a cross section shows only one level. A vertical plane that runs front to back is a sagittal plane, and because these structures sit on or very near the midline, the midsagittal plane is the one that passes through all of them.","notes":["Correct. A vertical cut on the midline runs from front to back and from top to bottom, so it shows all three midline structures stacked front to back along the full neck.","A transverse section is a horizontal cross section. It does show the trachea in front of the esophagus, but only at one level, not along the whole length of the neck. It would be the right choice if the radiologist wanted to see everything at a single level.","A frontal section divides the body into front and back parts, so a single frontal cut passes through only one of these front to back layers. It cannot show one structure lying in front of another in the same image.","A parasagittal section is vertical but runs off the midline. The trachea, esophagus and vertebral bodies sit on or very near the midline, so a cut through the side of the neck would miss them."],"fix":"Review the six planes and the two parts each one divides the body into, then picture what each cut would show in the neck."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-terminology-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-terminology","comp":"w1-anatomical-position","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient lies prone on the exam table with the arms at the sides and the palms facing the table. A wound is on the surface of the forearm that faces the ceiling. Which directional term describes that surface of the forearm?","options":["Superior, because that surface is facing upward","Posterior, because the terms are defined from anatomical position","Anterior, because that surface faces away from the table","Lateral, because the forearm lies beside the trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Directional terms always refer back to anatomical position, no matter how the patient is lying. In anatomical position the palms face forward, so the palm side of the forearm is anterior. A prone patient faces the table, and with the palms toward the table the forearm is turned the same way relative to the body as in anatomical position. That means the surface facing the ceiling is the back of the forearm, which is posterior.","notes":["Superior means toward the head, not toward the ceiling. Students choose this when they describe the body by how it is lying instead of by anatomical position.","Correct. Prone with the palms toward the table keeps the palms on the same side as the front of the body, so the ceiling side of the forearm is its back surface.","In this position the front of the body and the palms face the table, so the surface facing away from the table is the back. Anterior would be correct if the patient were supine with the palms facing up.","Lateral means away from the midline. The forearm is lateral to the trunk, but that does not name which surface of the forearm faces the ceiling. Lateral would describe the thumb side of the forearm in anatomical position."],"fix":"Review anatomical position, prone and supine, and practice translating a lying patient back into anatomical position before choosing a term."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-terminology-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-terminology","comp":"w1-regional-terms","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A cyclist slides on the road and gets one long scrape that starts on the calf and runs straight up the back of the body to the lower back. In what order does the scrape cross these regions?","options":["Popliteal, sural, gluteal, lumbar","Sural, gluteal, popliteal, lumbar","Sural, popliteal, lumbar, gluteal","Sural, popliteal, gluteal, lumbar"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"To order these you first translate each term into a place: sural is the calf, popliteal is the back of the knee, gluteal is the buttock, and lumbar is the lower back. Then follow the path from bottom to top along the posterior surface. The first two are appendicular regions on the lower limb, and the scrape crosses onto the axial region at the buttock and lower back.","notes":["This starts at the back of the knee instead of the calf. The sural region is the calf, which lies below the popliteal region, so a scrape moving up from the calf reaches sural first.","This puts the buttock before the back of the knee. The popliteal region is the back of the knee, which is reached before the gluteal region on the way up the limb.","This puts the lower back before the buttock. The gluteal region sits below the lumbar region, so a scrape moving upward crosses the buttock first.","Correct. The calf is sural, the back of the knee is popliteal, the buttock is gluteal, and the lower back is lumbar."],"fix":"Review the posterior regional terms of the lower limb and trunk and practice placing them in order from the heel to the head."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-terminology-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-terminology","comp":"w1-regional-terms","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A clinician standing at the foot of the bed faces a supine patient and charts a burn \"on the lateral side of the left antebrachial region, just distal to the antecubital region.\" Where should a student mark the burn on a body diagram?","options":["On the patient's left forearm, on the thumb side, just below the front of the elbow","On the patient's left forearm, on the little finger side, just below the front of the elbow","On the patient's left arm, on the thumb side, just above the front of the elbow","On the patient's right forearm, on the thumb side, just below the front of the elbow"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"This chart entry packs four ideas into one line. Right and left always mean the patient's own sides, not the viewer's. Antebrachial names the forearm, and antecubital names the front of the elbow, so distal to the antecubital region is the upper forearm just below the elbow crease. Lateral is judged in anatomical position, where the palms face forward and the thumb sits on the side away from the midline. Putting all four together places the burn on the thumb side of the patient's left upper forearm.","notes":["Correct. Left means the patient's left, antebrachial is the forearm, lateral is the thumb side in anatomical position, and just distal to the antecubital region places it just below the front of the elbow.","This puts the burn on the medial side. In anatomical position the palms face forward, so the thumb points away from the midline and is lateral, while the little finger is medial.","This confuses antebrachial with brachial. Brachial is the arm between the shoulder and elbow, while antebrachial is the forearm, and distal to the elbow means below it, not above it.","This uses the clinician's left, not the patient's. Charts always describe the patient's own right and left, so a clinician facing the patient has to flip sides."],"fix":"Review the appendicular regional terms of the upper limb and practice reading chart descriptions from the patient's perspective in anatomical position."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cavities-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cavities","comp":"w1-serous-membranes","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"During a thoracentesis, the needle passes through the chest wall and is accidentally pushed too far, into the lung itself. In what order does the needle tip pass these structures?","options":["Visceral pleura, pleural cavity, parietal pleura, lung tissue","Parietal pleura, pleural cavity, visceral pleura, lung tissue","Parietal pleura, visceral pleura, pleural cavity, lung tissue","Pleural cavity, parietal pleura, visceral pleura, lung tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Every serous membrane has the same plan: a parietal layer lining the cavity wall, a visceral layer covering the organ, and a thin fluid-filled cavity between them. A needle entering from outside reaches the wall side first, so it meets the parietal pleura, then the pleural cavity where the fluid normally sits, then the visceral pleura on the lung surface, and finally the lung. A correctly placed thoracentesis needle stops in the pleural cavity.","notes":["This swaps the two layers. The visceral layer covers the organ, so it is the last layer before the lung, while the parietal layer lines the wall and is met first.","Correct. The parietal pleura lines the chest wall, the pleural cavity is the fluid space next to it, and the visceral pleura clings to the lung surface just before the lung tissue.","This puts the cavity inside both layers. The pleural cavity is the thin fluid space between the parietal and visceral pleura, so it must sit between them.","This puts the cavity outside the parietal layer. The parietal pleura is attached to the chest wall, so the needle crosses it before it can enter the fluid space."],"fix":"Review the two-layer plan of a serous membrane and which layer lines the wall and which covers the organ in the pleura."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cavities-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cavities","comp":"w1-peritoneum-relationships","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"During abdominal surgery, a surgeon lifts a loop of bowel easily and sees a fan-shaped double fold of peritoneum running from the posterior abdominal wall to the loop, carrying its blood vessels. Which segment is the surgeon most likely holding?","options":["The ascending colon","The descending colon","The jejunum","The rectum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fan-shaped double fold carrying vessels is a mesentery, and only intraperitoneal organs hang from one. An organ on a mesentery is almost completely wrapped in visceral peritoneum and moves fairly freely, which matches the loop the surgeon lifts. SADPUCKER lists the retroperitoneal structures, and the ascending colon, the descending colon and the rectum are all on that list. The jejunum is not, so it is the intraperitoneal loop on a mesentery.","notes":["The ascending colon is retroperitoneal, the C in SADPUCKER. It is pressed against the posterior wall with peritoneum only on its front, so it has no mesentery and does not lift freely.","The descending colon is retroperitoneal like the ascending colon. It would be the answer if the question asked which segment stays fixed against the back wall.","Correct. The jejunum is intraperitoneal and hangs from the posterior wall by the mesentery, which is why it lifts easily.","The rectum is the R in SADPUCKER. It sits low in the pelvis against the sacrum with no mesentery, so it cannot be lifted out on a fan-shaped fold like this one."],"fix":"Review SADPUCKER and the definition of a mesentery, and sort the parts of the bowel into intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cavities-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cavities","comp":"w1-abdominopelvic-map","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"After a bike crash, a patient has pain in the abdominal zone that lies lateral to the left midclavicular plane and above the subcostal plane. Which organ is the most likely source of the pain?","options":["The spleen","The gallbladder","The descending colon","The sigmoid colon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nine-region grid is drawn by two vertical midclavicular planes and two horizontal planes, the subcostal plane across the lower rib margins and the transtubercular plane across the iliac crests. Lateral to the left midclavicular plane puts the pain in the left column, and above the subcostal plane puts it in the top row, so the region is the left hypochondriac. The spleen is the key organ there, and it is also the classic organ injured by a blow to the left lower ribs.","notes":["Correct. That zone is the left hypochondriac region, and the spleen is its key organ.","The gallbladder sits in the right hypochondriac region, the mirror image of this zone. Students pick it when they read left and right from the viewer instead of the patient.","The descending colon is the key organ of the left lumbar region, which lies below the subcostal plane. It would be the answer if the pain sat between the subcostal and transtubercular planes.","The sigmoid colon lies in the left iliac and hypogastric regions, below the transtubercular plane. It would fit lower left pain, not pain up under the ribs."],"fix":"Review the four planes that draw the nine-region grid and the key organs listed for each region."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cavities-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cavities","comp":"w1-body-cavities","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these spaces from the largest, which contains the others, to the smallest: the mediastinum, the ventral body cavity, the pericardial cavity, and the thoracic cavity.","options":["Thoracic cavity, ventral body cavity, mediastinum, pericardial cavity","Ventral body cavity, thoracic cavity, pericardial cavity, mediastinum","Ventral body cavity, mediastinum, thoracic cavity, pericardial cavity","Ventral body cavity, thoracic cavity, mediastinum, pericardial cavity"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"These spaces nest inside one another like boxes. The ventral body cavity is divided by the diaphragm into the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities. Within the thoracic cavity, the mediastinum is the central partition between the two pleural cavities. The heart and its pericardial cavity sit within the mediastinum, so the pericardial cavity is the smallest space in the set.","notes":["This puts the thoracic cavity above the ventral cavity. The ventral cavity is made of the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities together, so it is the larger space.","This puts the pericardial cavity outside the mediastinum. The mediastinum is the central partition of the chest, and the pericardial cavity is one space within it.","This puts the mediastinum outside the thoracic cavity. The mediastinum is the middle region of the thoracic cavity, between the two pleural cavities.","Correct. The ventral cavity holds the thoracic cavity, the thoracic cavity holds the mediastinum, and the pericardial cavity sits inside the mediastinum around the heart."],"fix":"Review the subdivisions of the ventral body cavity and draw them as nested spaces, from the ventral cavity down to the pericardial cavity."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cavities-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cavities","comp":"w1-mediastinum","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A CT scan shows a mass in the mediastinum sitting directly behind the manubrium of the sternum and pressing back on the trachea. Which compartment holds the mass?","options":["The anterior mediastinum","The superior mediastinum","The middle mediastinum","The posterior mediastinum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The transverse thoracic plane runs from the sternal angle back to the disc between T4 and T5, and it splits the mediastinum into a superior part above and an inferior part below. The sternal angle is the joint where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, so the whole manubrium lies above the plane. A mass directly behind the manubrium is therefore in the superior mediastinum, even though it sits toward the front of the chest. That also fits the trachea being compressed, since the trachea runs through the superior mediastinum.","notes":["The anterior mediastinum is the part of the inferior mediastinum between the body of the sternum and the pericardium, below the transverse thoracic plane. It would be correct if the mass sat behind the body of the sternum rather than the manubrium. Students pick it because the mass lies toward the front.","Correct. The manubrium lies above the sternal angle, so anything directly behind it is above the transverse thoracic plane, in the superior mediastinum where the trachea runs.","The middle mediastinum is below the transverse thoracic plane and holds the heart within the pericardium. It would fit a mass inside the pericardial sac.","The posterior mediastinum is below the transverse thoracic plane, between the pericardium and the vertebral column. It would fit a mass behind the heart next to the descending aorta."],"fix":"Review the transverse thoracic plane, where the sternal angle sits between the manubrium and the body of the sternum, and the four compartments of the mediastinum with one structure in each."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cavities-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cavities","comp":"w1-serous-membranes","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A stab wound just left of the sternum pierces the pericardium, and blood fills the space around the heart. Neither pleural cavity is entered. Where is the blood collecting, and why do both lungs keep inflating?","options":["Between the parietal and visceral pleura of the left lung, which wraps around the heart in the mediastinum","Between the visceral pericardium and the heart muscle, in the anterior mediastinum next to the sternum","Between the parietal and visceral pericardium in the middle mediastinum, a space separate from each pleural cavity","Between the pericardium and the sternum, in the anterior mediastinum, which opens into both pleural cavities"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Three ideas combine here. The pericardium is a serous membrane, so the space that fills with blood is the pericardial cavity between its parietal and visceral layers. The heart and its pericardium sit in the middle mediastinum, the central compartment of the chest. Each lung sits in its own pleural cavity, separate from the mediastinum, so blood around the heart does not collapse either lung, even though it can squeeze the heart itself.","notes":["The pleura surrounds a lung, not the heart, and blood in the left pleural cavity would press on the left lung. The stem also says no pleural cavity was entered.","The visceral pericardium is the epicardium, the outer surface of the heart itself, so there is no cavity between it and the muscle. The heart and its pericardium also sit in the middle mediastinum, not the anterior one.","Correct. The pericardial cavity is the serous space between the two pericardial layers, it lies in the middle mediastinum, and it is walled off from both pleural cavities.","The space between the sternum and the pericardium is the anterior mediastinum, which holds fat, lymph nodes and the thymus remnant, not the space around the heart. The mediastinum also does not open into either pleural cavity, which are separate spaces around each lung."],"fix":"Review the parietal and visceral layers of the pericardium, the compartments of the mediastinum, and how the pleural cavities are kept separate from it."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cell-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cell","comp":"w1-organelles","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"An intestinal cell makes a new brush-border enzyme, a protein that will be placed in its apical membrane. Starting with the gene, in what order is each of these structures used?","options":["Nuclear pore, chromatin, rough ER, Golgi apparatus","Chromatin, nuclear pore, Golgi apparatus, rough ER","Chromatin, rough ER, nuclear pore, Golgi apparatus","Chromatin, nuclear pore, rough ER, Golgi apparatus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The DNA never leaves the nucleus, so the gene in the chromatin is copied into mRNA, and that mRNA exits through a nuclear pore. Ribosomes bound to the rough ER read the mRNA and build proteins meant for membranes or for export. A transport vesicle then carries the new protein to the Golgi apparatus, which modifies, sorts and packages it for delivery to the apical membrane. Each step depends on the one before it, which is why the order is fixed.","notes":["This sends material through the pore before the gene is read. The mRNA copy has to be made from the DNA in the chromatin first, and only then can it leave through a pore.","This puts the Golgi apparatus before the rough ER. Proteins are built on ribosomes of the rough ER and then carried in transport vesicles to the Golgi apparatus for finishing, not the other way around.","This uses the rough ER before the mRNA has left the nucleus. The mRNA must pass out through a nuclear pore before ribosomes on the rough ER can read it.","Correct. The gene is read from the chromatin, the mRNA leaves through a nuclear pore, the protein is built on the rough ER, and the Golgi apparatus sorts and packages it for the membrane."],"fix":"Review the endomembrane system path from the nucleus through the rough ER and Golgi apparatus to the plasma membrane."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cell-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cell","comp":"w1-nucleus","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A toxin plugs every nuclear pore in a cell, so nothing can pass through the nuclear envelope. What is the most direct effect on the cell's protein production?","options":["New mRNA stays trapped in the nucleus, so ribosomes in the cytoplasm receive no new instructions","DNA leaks into the cytoplasm, where lysosomes break it down before it can be used","The chromatin condenses into chromosomes because the nucleolus can no longer work","The Golgi apparatus can no longer send finished proteins into the nucleus to be stored"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The nucleus works like a reference library that never lends out its books. DNA stays inside, and the cell sends out mRNA copies through the nuclear pores to ribosomes in the cytoplasm, where proteins are built. If every pore is plugged, the mRNA cannot reach the ribosomes, and the ribosomal subunits made in the nucleolus are trapped as well. The most direct result is that the cell soon cannot make new proteins.","notes":["Correct. mRNA must leave through the nuclear pores to reach the ribosomes, so blocking the pores stops new proteins from being built.","Nuclear pores never let DNA out, even when they are open. DNA stays inside the nucleus, and only copies of genes leave as mRNA. Plugged pores would keep material in, not let it leak out.","Chromatin condenses into chromosomes when a cell divides, not because pores are blocked. The nucleolus builds ribosomal subunits, and plugged pores would trap those subunits, but that does not make chromatin condense.","The Golgi apparatus sends its products to the plasma membrane, to lysosomes or out of the cell, not into the nucleus for storage. Students pick this when they remember that traffic moves both ways through the pores but forget that the pores carry mRNA out to the ribosomes."],"fix":"Review the structures of the nucleus, especially what the nuclear pores let in and out and why DNA stays inside."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cell-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cell","comp":"w1-generalized-cell","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A mature red blood cell carries a large load of hemoglobin, but it cannot repair itself and is replaced after a few months. Which change to the three basic regions of a generalized cell explains both facts?","options":["It gives up most of its cytoplasm, leaving a nucleus wrapped closely in membrane","It gives up its nucleus and organelles, keeping a membrane around hemoglobin-filled cytoplasm","It gives up its plasma membrane, so hemoglobin forms a coat around the nucleus","It keeps its nucleus and organelles, and stores its hemoglobin inside the nucleus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A generalized cell has three basic regions: the plasma membrane that encloses it, the cytoplasm of organelles suspended in cytosol, and the nucleus. As a red blood cell matures it pushes out its nucleus and loses its organelles, so the cytoplasm inside its plasma membrane has room to fill with hemoglobin. Without a nucleus, the cell has no DNA to direct new proteins, so it cannot repair itself or divide. One structural change, losing the nucleus, explains both the large hemoglobin load and the short life.","notes":["This is the opposite of what happens. Hemoglobin is stored in the cytoplasm, so losing it would leave no room for hemoglobin, and a cell that kept its nucleus could keep making proteins to repair itself.","Correct. Losing the nucleus and organelles frees the cytoplasm for hemoglobin, and without a nucleus the cell cannot make the proteins it needs for repair.","Every living cell, including a red blood cell, is enclosed by a plasma membrane. The hemoglobin sits inside the cell in the cytoplasm, and the nucleus is what is lost as the cell matures.","Hemoglobin fills the cytoplasm, not the nucleus. A cell that kept its nucleus and organelles could still make proteins, so this would not explain why it cannot repair itself."],"fix":"Review the three regions of a generalized cell and how losing the nucleus changes what a red blood cell can hold and what it can do."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cell-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cell","comp":"w1-plasma-membrane","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A Giardia infection damages the microvilli on the cells lining the small intestine, but the cells themselves stay alive. Which problem follows most directly from losing the microvilli?","options":["The cells have much less surface area for absorbing nutrients","The cells can no longer sweep mucus along the intestinal surface","The cells can no longer seal the spaces between neighboring cells","The cells can no longer move themselves along the intestinal wall"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Microvilli are finger-like extensions of the plasma membrane on the apical surface of absorptive cells, each supported by a core of actin microfilaments. Thousands of them on one cell form the brush border and greatly increase the surface that faces the gut lumen. When Giardia damages the microvilli, the cell is still alive but has far less membrane surface for absorbing nutrients. That is why damage to the brush border leads to poor absorption.","notes":["Correct. Microvilli are folds of the apical plasma membrane that multiply the absorptive surface, so losing them cuts the surface available to absorb nutrients.","Sweeping mucus along a surface is the job of cilia, which are longer and move. Microvilli do not beat. This would be the answer if cilia in the airway were damaged.","Sealing the spaces between cells is the job of tight junctions on the lateral surfaces. Microvilli sit on the apical surface and do not join cells to each other.","Moving a whole cell is the job of a flagellum, and the only human example is the sperm tail. Intestinal lining cells stay in place, and microvilli do not move them."],"fix":"Review the surface specializations of the plasma membrane, microvilli, cilia and flagellum, and what each one does."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cell-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cell","comp":"w1-cell-junctions","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these features of an intestinal lining cell by distance from the gut lumen, from closest to farthest: hemidesmosome, microvilli, adherens junction, tight junction.","options":["Tight junction, microvilli, adherens junction, hemidesmosome","Microvilli, adherens junction, tight junction, hemidesmosome","Microvilli, tight junction, hemidesmosome, adherens junction","Microvilli, tight junction, adherens junction, hemidesmosome"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An epithelial cell has an apical surface facing the lumen, lateral surfaces facing its neighbors, and a basal surface resting on the basement membrane. Microvilli extend from the apical surface into the lumen. Along the lateral surfaces, the tight junction lies closest to the apical edge, where it seals the gap between cells, and the adherens junction lies just deep to it. The hemidesmosome is on the basal surface, where it anchors the cell to the basement membrane, so it is farthest from the lumen.","notes":["This puts the tight junction above the microvilli. Microvilli stick out from the apical surface into the lumen, so they are always closest. The tight junction sits just below them on the lateral surfaces.","This swaps the two belt-like junctions. The tight junction seals the cells closest to the apical surface, and the adherens junction forms its adhesion belt just deep to that seal.","This puts the hemidesmosome above a junction between neighboring cells. A hemidesmosome anchors the basal surface to the basement membrane, so it is the farthest from the lumen.","Correct. Microvilli project into the lumen, the tight junction sits just below the apical surface, the adherens belt sits just deep to it, and the hemidesmosome anchors the base of the cell."],"fix":"Review the five cell junctions, where each sits on an epithelial cell, and what each one links to."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-cell-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-cell","comp":"w1-organelles","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A cell in the pancreas makes large amounts of a digestive enzyme, which is a protein. It packages the enzyme in vesicles and releases it by exocytosis into a duct, and the cell stays intact. Which description of this cell fits all of these findings?","options":["Rich in rough ER and Golgi, merocrine secretion, part of an exocrine gland","Rich in smooth ER and lysosomes, holocrine secretion, part of an exocrine gland","Rich in rough ER and Golgi, apocrine secretion, part of an endocrine gland","Rich in smooth ER and Golgi, merocrine secretion, part of an endocrine gland"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"This item ties together three separate ideas. A cell that exports a large amount of protein needs many ribosomes on rough ER to build it and a well developed Golgi apparatus to package it. Releasing the product by exocytosis while the cell stays whole is merocrine secretion, the most common type. Sending the product into a duct makes the gland exocrine, while endocrine glands release into the blood. The pancreas is a mixed gland, and these enzyme-making cells are its exocrine part.","notes":["Correct. Exported proteins need abundant rough ER and Golgi, exocytosis with the cell left intact is merocrine secretion, and release into a duct makes it exocrine.","Smooth ER makes lipids and detoxifies drugs, and lysosomes break material down, so neither builds an exported protein. Holocrine secretion ruptures the whole cell, as in a sebaceous gland, but this cell stays intact.","The organelles are right, but apocrine secretion pinches off the apical part of the cell, as in mammary glands. Endocrine glands release their products into the blood with no duct, and this cell uses a duct.","Merocrine is right, but smooth ER has no ribosomes and does not build proteins for export. A product released into a duct also marks an exocrine gland, not an endocrine one."],"fix":"Review rough versus smooth ER, the three modes of exocrine secretion, and how endocrine and exocrine glands differ."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-epithelial-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-epithelial","comp":"w1-epithelial-id","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"In a patient with years of acid reflux, part of the lining of the lower esophagus is replaced by simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells. What protective feature of the normal esophageal lining has been lost?","options":["A keratinized surface layer that keeps the esophagus waterproof","A carpet of cilia that sweeps food down toward the stomach","Several replaceable cell layers that resist wear from swallowed food","A single thin layer of flat cells that lets material diffuse quickly"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The normal esophagus is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Its many layers protect against abrasion, because surface cells can be worn away while deeper cells divide to replace them. In this change, called metaplasia, one mature tissue type is replaced by another, and the new simple columnar lining has only one layer of cells. That single layer is suited to secretion and absorption, but it has lost the layered protection the esophagus normally relies on.","notes":["The esophagus is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium because its surface stays moist. A keratinized surface is found on the skin.","The esophagus has no cilia. Ciliated epithelium is the pseudostratified lining of the airways, where cilia sweep mucus up toward the throat.","Correct. The normal esophagus is lined by stratified squamous epithelium, and its many layers let it lose surface cells to abrasion without exposing the tissue below.","A single thin layer of flat cells is simple squamous epithelium, found in the lung air sacs and capillary walls. It would be a poor lining for a tube that food scrapes along."],"fix":"Review how epithelia are named by layers and surface cell shape, and match stratified squamous and simple columnar to their usual jobs and locations."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-epithelial-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-epithelial","comp":"w1-epithelial-id","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A slide of the trachea shows tall cells with cilia, scattered goblet cells, and nuclei at several heights. A student labels it stratified columnar epithelium. Which observation would best prove that it is pseudostratified instead?","options":["The nuclei sit at several different heights in the tissue","The apical surface of the tall cells carries cilia","Goblet cells are scattered among the columnar cells","Every cell rests on the basement membrane, even the short ones"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Epithelia are named first by the number of layers and then by the shape of the surface cells. Pseudostratified epithelium looks layered because its nuclei sit at different heights, but it is truly one layer, since every cell touches the basement membrane. Stratified columnar epithelium has deeper cells that never reach the surface. Cilia and goblet cells are useful clues to location, but only contact with the basement membrane settles the number of layers.","notes":["Nuclei at different heights are the reason this tissue looks layered in the first place. That observation fits both tissues, so it cannot tell them apart.","Cilia are common on pseudostratified epithelium of the airways, but cilia describe the surface, not the number of layers. They do not prove whether the tissue is one layer or several.","Goblet cells appear in pseudostratified epithelium and also in simple columnar epithelium. Their presence does not show how many layers the tissue has.","Correct. Pseudostratified means a single layer, so the deciding feature is that every cell touches the basement membrane."],"fix":"Review how simple, stratified and pseudostratified epithelia are defined by their contact with the basement membrane."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-epithelial-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-epithelial","comp":"w1-epithelial-id","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these linings from the thinnest epithelium to the thickest: the lining of the esophagus, the lining of a lung air sac, the lining of the small intestine, and the lining of a kidney tubule.","options":["Lung air sac, kidney tubule, small intestine, esophagus","Kidney tubule, lung air sac, small intestine, esophagus","Lung air sac, small intestine, kidney tubule, esophagus","Lung air sac, kidney tubule, esophagus, small intestine"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"To rank these, first name the epithelium at each site, then compare them. The lung air sac has simple squamous epithelium, one layer of flat cells that keeps the path for diffusion short. The kidney tubule has simple cuboidal cells, the small intestine has taller simple columnar cells, and the esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium with many layers for protection. One layer is always thinner than many, and within single layers, squamous cells are flattest and columnar cells are tallest.","notes":["Correct. These are simple squamous, simple cuboidal, simple columnar and stratified squamous epithelium, in order of increasing thickness.","This puts cuboidal cells thinner than squamous ones. The air sac is lined by simple squamous epithelium, the flattest single layer, so it comes first.","This puts columnar cells thinner than cuboidal ones. The intestine is lined by tall simple columnar cells, which are taller than the cube-shaped cells of a kidney tubule.","This ranks a stratified lining below a single layer. The esophagus has many layers of cells, so it is thicker than the one layer of columnar cells in the intestine."],"fix":"Review the named epithelia with one location for each, and compare their cell shapes and number of layers."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-epithelial-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-epithelial","comp":"w1-epithelial-id","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A nutrient molecule is absorbed from the lumen of the small intestine and carried away in the blood. In what order does it pass these structures?","options":["Microvilli, basement membrane, basal surface of the cell, capillary in the connective tissue","Basement membrane, microvilli, basal surface of the cell, capillary in the connective tissue","Microvilli, basal surface of the cell, basement membrane, capillary in the connective tissue","Microvilli, capillary in the connective tissue, basal surface of the cell, basement membrane"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An intestinal lining cell has microvilli on its apical surface facing the lumen, and tight junctions seal the gaps between neighboring cells, so most nutrients pass through the cells rather than between them. After crossing the cell, the molecule leaves at the basal surface. Below that is the basement membrane, which anchors the epithelium to the connective tissue. Because epithelium has no blood vessels, the molecule must cross into the vascular connective tissue below to reach a capillary.","notes":["This reaches the basement membrane before the molecule has left the cell. The basement membrane lies below the basal surface, so the molecule must leave the cell before it reaches it.","This puts the basement membrane on the lumen side. The microvilli face the lumen, and the basement membrane is at the opposite, basal side of the epithelium.","Correct. The molecule enters at the microvilli on the apical surface, crosses the cell, leaves at the basal surface, passes the basement membrane, and reaches a capillary in the connective tissue below.","This places a capillary inside the epithelium. Epithelium is avascular, so the nearest capillaries are in the connective tissue beneath the basement membrane."],"fix":"Review the apical, lateral and basal surfaces of an epithelial cell, the basement membrane, and why epithelium depends on the connective tissue beneath it."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-epithelial-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-epithelial","comp":"w1-connective-id","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A pathology report says that an epithelial cancer of the bladder lining has just broken through the basement membrane. Why does this change make it more dangerous?","options":["The cancer cells now sit in the lumen of the bladder, where urine can carry them away","The cancer cells now sit in vascular connective tissue, where they can reach blood vessels","The cancer cells now sit in cartilage, which cannot repair the damage they cause","The cancer cells now sit in a second layer of epithelium, which divides even faster"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Epithelium has no blood vessels and depends on diffusion from the connective tissue under it. The basement membrane is the boundary between the two tissues. While a cancer stays above the basement membrane, it has no direct access to blood vessels. Once it breaks through, the cells sit in vascular connective tissue, where they can enter vessels and spread to other parts of the body.","notes":["The lumen lies on the apical side of the epithelium, the opposite side from the basement membrane. Crossing the basement membrane takes cells deeper into the wall, away from the lumen.","Correct. Epithelium is avascular, so once cancer cells cross the basement membrane they reach the connective tissue below, which carries the blood vessels.","The bladder wall has no cartilage beneath its lining. The tissue just below an epithelium is connective tissue proper, such as areolar tissue, which is vascular.","There is no second epithelium below the basement membrane. The basement membrane is the boundary between an epithelium and the connective tissue it rests on."],"fix":"Review how epithelium and connective tissue differ in blood supply and where the basement membrane sits between them."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-epithelial-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-epithelial","comp":"w1-connective-id","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A sharp chip scrapes away the upper cell layers of the lining of the cheek, but the deepest layer of epithelial cells, the basement membrane and the connective tissue beneath are left intact. The scrape heals within days without a scar. What best explains this?","options":["Fibroblasts in the connective tissue below make new epithelial cells to cover the scrape","Blood vessels inside the epithelium bring nutrients straight to the surface cells","The basement membrane divides to make a new sheet of epithelial cells over the scrape","Deep epithelial cells divide to rebuild it, fed by vessels in the connective tissue below"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Healing this scrape depends on several tissue features working together. The cheek is lined by stratified squamous epithelium, whose deepest cells divide often, so they can replace the cells that were scraped away. The epithelium has no blood vessels of its own, so those dividing cells depend on diffusion from the vascular connective tissue beneath the basement membrane. The basement membrane gives the new cells a surface to spread across. Because the connective tissue was not damaged, fibroblasts do not need to lay down scar collagen.","notes":["Fibroblasts build collagen fibers and ground substance, not epithelial cells. When fibroblasts fill a wound with new collagen, the result is a scar, which is what happens when the deep layers are destroyed.","Epithelium is avascular, so there are no blood vessels inside it. Its cells are fed by diffusion from capillaries in the connective tissue under the basement membrane.","The basement membrane is a thin sheet of extracellular matrix, not a layer of cells, so it cannot divide. It does guide new epithelial cells as they spread across a wound.","Correct. The surviving deep epithelial cells can divide and replace the lost layers, and the vascular connective tissue under the basement membrane feeds them by diffusion."],"fix":"Review how epithelium is fed by the connective tissue beneath it, what the basement membrane does, and what fibroblasts build."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-connective-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-connective","comp":"w1-connective-id","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon must replace a torn knee ligament with a graft taken from elsewhere in the patient's body. The new ligament must resist strong pulling along one line. Which donor tissue is the best match?","options":["A strip of dermis, made of dense irregular connective tissue","A piece of ear cartilage, made of elastic cartilage","A strip of tendon, made of dense regular connective tissue","A sheet of areolar tissue from under the skin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A ligament is dense regular connective tissue, with collagen fibers packed in parallel bundles. Collagen resists tension, and lining the bundles up in one direction makes the tissue strongest when it is pulled along that line. A tendon has the same build, so it is the closest match. Dense irregular tissue resists pulling from many directions but is weaker than dense regular tissue along a single line, and cartilage and areolar tissue are not built to resist strong pulling.","notes":["Dense irregular connective tissue is strong, but its collagen runs in all directions. That suits skin, which is pulled from many directions, but it is weaker than parallel fibers when the pull comes from one direction.","Elastic cartilage bends and springs back to its shape, which suits the external ear. It is flexible rather than strong under tension, so it would not hold a knee joint together.","Correct. Tendons and ligaments are both dense regular connective tissue, with collagen bundles running parallel to resist pulling along one line.","Areolar tissue has loosely arranged fibers in a lot of ground substance. It wraps and cushions structures and is far too weak to replace a ligament."],"fix":"Review the connective tissue proper subtypes and match each fiber arrangement to the kind of force it resists."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-connective-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-connective","comp":"w1-connective-id","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A genetic disorder weakens fibrillin, the glycoprotein that gives elastic fibers their strength and stability. Which structure is most at risk of weakening and ballooning outward over time?","options":["The wall of the aorta","A tendon of the forearm","An intervertebral disc","The shaft of the femur"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Elastic fibers are built mainly of elastin, with fibrillin giving them strength and stability, and they can stretch well beyond their resting length and spring back. The wall of a large artery like the aorta is rich in elastic fibers because it must stretch with every heartbeat and then recoil. If those fibers are weak, the wall stretches without fully recoiling and can bulge outward over time. Tendons, discs and bone depend mostly on collagen or calcified matrix, so they are much less affected.","notes":["Correct. The aortic wall is dominated by elastic fibers that stretch with each heartbeat and recoil, so weak elastic fibers leave it prone to bulging.","Tendons are dense regular connective tissue built mostly of parallel collagen fibers, not elastic fibers. A tendon would be most at risk from a disorder that weakens collagen.","The tough outer ring of an intervertebral disc is fibrocartilage, dominated by thick collagen bundles that resist compression. Its strength does not depend mainly on elastic fibers.","Bone gets its strength from a hard, calcified matrix with collagen fibers. It does not rely on elastic fibers and does not need to stretch and recoil."],"fix":"Review the three connective tissue fiber types, what each one is made of, and where each one dominates."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-connective-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-connective","comp":"w1-connective-id","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Connective tissues differ mostly in their matrix. Rank these tissues from the most fluid matrix to the hardest: compact bone, hyaline cartilage, blood, and areolar connective tissue.","options":["Areolar connective tissue, blood, hyaline cartilage, compact bone","Blood, areolar connective tissue, hyaline cartilage, compact bone","Blood, hyaline cartilage, areolar connective tissue, compact bone","Blood, areolar connective tissue, compact bone, hyaline cartilage"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Every connective tissue is cells plus an extracellular matrix of fibers and ground substance, and the matrix decides how the tissue behaves. Blood has the most fluid matrix, plasma. Areolar connective tissue has a soft, gel-like ground substance with loosely arranged fibers. Hyaline cartilage has a firm matrix that still bends, and compact bone has a calcified matrix that makes it the hardest of the four.","notes":["This ranks areolar tissue as more fluid than blood. The matrix of blood is plasma, a true liquid, while areolar ground substance is a gel that holds tissue fluid.","Correct. Plasma is liquid, areolar ground substance is a soft gel, cartilage matrix is firm but bendable, and bone matrix is calcified and hard.","This ranks cartilage as softer than areolar tissue. Cartilage has a firm matrix that holds its shape, while areolar tissue is loose and soft.","This ranks cartilage as harder than bone. Bone matrix is calcified, which makes it hard and inflexible, while cartilage stays pliable."],"fix":"Review the matrix of each connective tissue type, from the fluid plasma of blood to the calcified matrix of bone."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-connective-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-connective","comp":"w1-germ-layers","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Trace the development of an adult tendon in the leg from the early embryo. In what order do these appear?","options":["Embryonic mesenchyme, mesoderm, fibroblasts, dense regular connective tissue","Mesoderm, fibroblasts, embryonic mesenchyme, dense regular connective tissue","Mesoderm, embryonic mesenchyme, dense regular connective tissue, fibroblasts","Mesoderm, embryonic mesenchyme, fibroblasts, dense regular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Most connective tissues come from the mesoderm, the middle germ layer. Within the mesoderm, embryonic mesenchyme forms, and mesenchyme differentiates into every type of connective tissue. In a tendon, mesenchyme gives rise to fibroblasts, which are blast cells that divide and secrete matrix. Those fibroblasts lay down collagen in parallel bundles, producing dense regular connective tissue.","notes":["This puts mesenchyme before the germ layer it comes from. The three germ layers form first, and mesenchyme develops from the middle layer, the mesoderm.","This puts fibroblasts before mesenchyme. Embryonic mesenchyme is the tissue that differentiates into all types of connective tissue, including the fibroblasts.","This puts the finished tissue before the cells that build it. Fibroblasts secrete the collagen fibers and ground substance, so they must be present before the tendon matrix exists.","Correct. Mesoderm gives rise to mesenchyme, mesenchyme gives rise to fibroblasts, and fibroblasts secrete the parallel collagen of the tendon."],"fix":"Review the three germ layers, the role of embryonic mesenchyme, and what fibroblasts build."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-connective-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-connective","comp":"w1-cell-junctions","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"In a patient with severe intestinal inflammation, the tight junctions between cells of the intestinal lining break down. Into which tissue would material from the gut lumen first leak?","options":["The capillaries that run between the epithelial cells","The smooth muscle layer in the wall of the intestine","The areolar connective tissue just beneath the basement membrane","The serous membrane covering the outside of the intestine"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Tight junctions seal neighboring epithelial cells near their apical surfaces, so substances in the lumen cannot slip between the cells into the tissue below. When those seals fail, gut contents can pass between the cells. Directly beneath an epithelium, past the basement membrane, is loose areolar connective tissue, the layer that supports linings and holds tissue fluid. That is the first connective tissue the leaked material reaches.","notes":["Epithelium is avascular, so there are no capillaries between its cells. The nearest blood vessels are in the connective tissue below the basement membrane.","The smooth muscle of the intestinal wall lies deeper than the connective tissue under the lining. Leaked material would have to cross that connective tissue before it could reach the muscle.","Correct. Without tight junctions, material slips between the cells and crosses into the loose areolar connective tissue under the basement membrane.","The serous membrane, the visceral peritoneum, covers the outer surface of the intestine, on the far side of the wall from the lining. It would be the last layer reached, not the first."],"fix":"Review what tight junctions do and which connective tissue lies directly beneath an epithelial lining."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-connective-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-connective","comp":"w1-cell-junctions","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Two patients have blistering skin diseases. In one, the desmosomes between epidermal cells fail. In the other, the hemidesmosomes at the base of the epidermis fail. Which statement best compares the two?","options":["Desmosome failure lifts the whole epidermis off the basement membrane, while hemidesmosome failure splits cells apart within it","Desmosome failure splits cells apart within the epidermis, while hemidesmosome failure lifts the whole epidermis off the basement membrane","Both diseases split the epidermis away from the dermis at the same depth, because both junctions use cadherins","Desmosome failure lets fluid leak between cells, while hemidesmosome failure stops ions passing between cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Desmosomes are made of cadherins linked to intermediate filaments, and they bind neighboring cells firmly, which is why they are abundant throughout the epidermis. Hemidesmosomes use integrins to anchor the basal surface of the deepest cells to the basement membrane. If desmosomes fail, cells separate from each other inside the epidermis. If hemidesmosomes fail, the entire epidermis lifts away from the basement membrane, which makes a deeper blister.","notes":["This reverses the two junctions. Desmosomes connect one cell to another, and hemidesmosomes connect a cell to the basement membrane, so the deeper split comes from hemidesmosome failure.","Correct. Desmosomes bind cell to cell, so their loss splits the epidermis internally, while hemidesmosomes bind cells to the basement membrane, so their loss gives a deeper split.","Desmosomes use cadherins, but hemidesmosomes use integrins to anchor to the basement membrane. The two junctions hold different things together, so their failures split the skin at different depths.","Stopping leaks between cells is the job of tight junctions, and passing ions between cells is the job of gap junctions. Desmosomes and hemidesmosomes are anchoring junctions that resist tension."],"fix":"Review the five cell junctions, comparing desmosomes and hemidesmosomes by what each one binds and its major protein."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue","comp":"w1-muscle-tissue-id","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A student takes muscle samples from the biceps brachii, the wall of the heart and the wall of the stomach. One slide shows spindle-shaped cells, each with a single central nucleus and no striations. Which site did it come from, and how is that muscle controlled?","options":["The heart wall, under involuntary control","The biceps brachii, under voluntary control","The stomach wall, under involuntary control","The stomach wall, under voluntary control"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Identify the tissue first, then link it to location and control. Spindle-shaped cells with one central nucleus and no striations are smooth muscle. Smooth muscle is found in the walls of hollow organs, so of the three sites it fits the stomach wall. Smooth muscle is involuntary, as is cardiac muscle, while skeletal muscle is the only voluntary type.","notes":["Cardiac muscle is involuntary, but its cells are branched, striated and joined by intercalated discs. The absence of striations rules out the heart.","Skeletal muscle like the biceps is voluntary, but its fibers are long, cylindrical, striated and have many nuclei at the edge. These cells have none of those features.","Correct. Spindle-shaped, unstriated cells with one central nucleus are smooth muscle, which lines hollow organs like the stomach and contracts without conscious control.","The site is right, but smooth muscle is not under conscious control. Of the three muscle types, only skeletal muscle is voluntary."],"fix":"Review the three muscle tissue types by striations, cell shape and number of nuclei, along with where each is found and how it is controlled."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue","comp":"cv-cardiac-muscle","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these from the largest structure to the smallest: a gap junction, the muscle of the heart wall, an intercalated disc, and a single cardiac muscle cell.","options":["Heart wall muscle, cardiac muscle cell, intercalated disc, gap junction","Heart wall muscle, intercalated disc, cardiac muscle cell, gap junction","Heart wall muscle, cardiac muscle cell, gap junction, intercalated disc","Cardiac muscle cell, heart wall muscle, intercalated disc, gap junction"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"These structures fit inside one another. The muscle of the heart wall is cardiac muscle tissue, made of many branching cardiac muscle cells. Each cell is joined end to end to its neighbor at an intercalated disc. Inside each disc are desmosomes, which hold the cells together, and gap junctions, which are tiny channels between the cells, so the gap junction is the smallest item in the set.","notes":["Correct. The heart wall is made of many cells, each cell meets the next at an intercalated disc, and gap junctions are small channels within the disc.","This ranks the disc as larger than the cell. An intercalated disc is only the junction at the end of a cell where it meets its neighbor, so it is smaller than the cell.","This ranks the gap junction as larger than the disc. Gap junctions are one of the two junction types found inside an intercalated disc, along with desmosomes.","This ranks one cell as larger than the whole wall. The muscle of the heart wall is a tissue built from a very large number of cardiac muscle cells."],"fix":"Review cardiac muscle tissue, the intercalated disc, and the two junction types found in it."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue","comp":"w1-muscle-tissue-id","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"An intramuscular injection is given straight into the anterior thigh. In what order does the needle pass these tissues?","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, adipose tissue, dense irregular connective tissue, skeletal muscle","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, dense irregular connective tissue, adipose tissue, skeletal muscle","Dense irregular connective tissue, keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, adipose tissue, skeletal muscle","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, dense irregular connective tissue, skeletal muscle, adipose tissue"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"To order these, match each tissue to its layer. The epidermis is keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, the dermis is mostly dense irregular connective tissue, and the hypodermis is mostly adipose tissue. The thigh muscle beneath them is skeletal muscle, long striated fibers attached to bone. An intramuscular needle must cross all three outer layers before it reaches the muscle.","notes":["This puts the fat above the dense connective tissue. The dense irregular tissue is the dermis, which lies directly under the epidermis, and the adipose tissue is the hypodermis, which lies beneath the dermis.","Correct. These are the tissues of the epidermis, the dermis, the hypodermis and the thigh muscle, in order from the surface down.","This puts connective tissue on the body surface. Connective tissue is never on a free surface, so the epithelium of the epidermis must come first.","This puts the muscle above the fat. The hypodermis, made mostly of adipose tissue, lies between the skin and the muscle, which is why a subcutaneous injection reaches fat and an intramuscular one goes deeper."],"fix":"Review the tissue that makes up each layer of the skin and hypodermis, and the features of skeletal muscle tissue."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue","comp":"cv-cardiac-muscle","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A genetic defect weakens the desmosomes in the intercalated discs of a patient's heart, while the gap junctions work normally. What structural problem would you predict?","options":["Neighboring cells tend to pull apart at their ends as the heart contracts","Ions can no longer pass directly from one cell to the next","The cells lose their striations because the sarcomeres come apart","The cells stop branching and become spindle shaped like smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Each intercalated disc contains two kinds of junctions with different jobs. Desmosomes use cadherins linked to intermediate filaments to bind neighboring cells tightly, so the cells do not pull apart under the strong tension of every heartbeat. Gap junctions are channels that let ions pass from one cell to the next. With weak desmosomes but working gap junctions, the expected problem is mechanical: cells separating at their ends as the heart contracts.","notes":["Correct. Desmosomes hold cardiac cells together against the pull of contraction, so weak desmosomes let cells separate at the intercalated discs.","Passing ions directly between cells is the job of gap junctions, which the stem says still work. This would be the answer if the gap junctions failed.","Striations come from the ordered sarcomeres inside each cell. Desmosomes join cells to each other and do not build the sarcomeres.","Cell shape does not depend on desmosomes in the discs. Spindle-shaped, unbranched cells describe smooth muscle tissue, not a change in damaged cardiac muscle."],"fix":"Review the intercalated disc and the separate jobs of its desmosomes and gap junctions."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue","comp":"w1-germ-layers","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A researcher marks every cell descended from the mesoderm of an embryo and later examines four slides from the adult. Which slide would show no marked cells?","options":["Spindle-shaped smooth muscle from the bladder wall","Branching cardiac muscle with intercalated discs","Multinucleated skeletal muscle from the tongue","A multipolar neuron from the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Answering this takes two steps: name the tissue on each slide, then recall its germ layer. Three slides show muscle tissue, smooth, cardiac and skeletal, and muscle tissue develops from mesoderm. The fourth shows a neuron, and neurons develop from ectoderm, the same outer layer that forms the epidermis. That makes the neuron the only unmarked slide.","notes":["Smooth muscle is a muscle tissue, and muscle tissue develops from mesoderm. This slide would be marked.","Cardiac muscle is a muscle tissue, and muscle tissue develops from mesoderm. This slide would be marked.","Skeletal muscle comes from mesoderm, wherever in the body it is found. This slide would be marked.","Correct. Neurons develop from ectoderm, so the neuron would carry no mesoderm mark."],"fix":"Review the three germ layers and the tissues each one gives rise to, especially muscle and nervous tissue."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-muscle-nervous-tissue-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-muscle-nervous-tissue","comp":"w1-muscle-tissue-id","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Six months after a heart attack, a slide from the damaged patch of ventricle wall shows dense collagen fibers with scattered fibroblasts and no striated cells. Which explanation best accounts for both what the slide shows and why the heart now pumps less strongly?","options":["Cardiac cells divided to refill the patch, but the new cells lack intercalated discs","Cardiac cells divide very little, so fibroblasts filled the gap with collagen that cannot contract","Smooth muscle cells moved in to replace the dead cardiac cells and contract too slowly","The dead cardiac cells stayed in place but lost their striations, so they cannot contract"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Start with the slide: dense collagen with fibroblasts and no striated cells is dense connective tissue, not muscle. Next, ask why muscle did not grow back. Mature cardiac muscle cells divide very little, so the heart cannot replace a patch of lost cells, and fibroblasts lay down collagen to fill the gap as a scar. Finally, link structure to function: the scar holds the wall together, but collagen cannot contract, so that part of the wall no longer helps pump and the heart pumps less strongly.","notes":["Cardiac muscle cells divide very little, so they cannot refill a patch this size. New cardiac cells would also be striated, and the slide shows no striated cells at all.","Correct. Cardiac cells divide very little, so fibroblasts filled the dead patch with collagen, and a collagen scar cannot contract.","Smooth muscle would show spindle-shaped cells with one central nucleus each, and the slide shows collagen fibers and fibroblasts instead. Students pick this when they assume some kind of muscle must fill the gap.","The slide shows collagen and fibroblasts rather than muscle cells, so the patch is no longer cardiac muscle at all. Losing striations alone would not explain the dense collagen."],"fix":"Review why cardiac muscle heals with scar tissue, what fibroblasts build, and how to tell dense connective tissue from each muscle tissue on a slide."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-integumentary-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-integumentary","comp":"w1-skin-layers","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A deep cut on the back of the forearm exposes four tissues. Rank them from the most superficial to the deepest: dense irregular connective tissue with thick collagen bundles, adipose tissue, areolar tissue with capillary loops, and keratinized stratified squamous epithelium.","options":["Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, areolar tissue with capillary loops, dense irregular connective tissue, adipose tissue","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, dense irregular connective tissue, areolar tissue with capillary loops, adipose tissue","Areolar tissue with capillary loops, keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, dense irregular connective tissue, adipose tissue","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, areolar tissue with capillary loops, adipose tissue, dense irregular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Match each tissue to its layer, then stack the layers. Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium is the epidermis, the surface layer. The papillary layer of the dermis is areolar tissue and holds the capillary loops in the dermal papillae. Below that, the reticular layer is dense irregular connective tissue with thick collagen bundles. The hypodermis, mostly adipose and areolar tissue, lies deepest and is not part of the skin itself.","notes":["Correct. These are the epidermis, the papillary layer of the dermis, the reticular layer of the dermis, and the hypodermis.","This swaps the two dermal layers. The areolar papillary layer with its capillary loops sits just under the epidermis, and the dense irregular reticular layer lies below it.","This puts connective tissue on the surface. The epidermis is the superficial layer of the skin, and connective tissue is never on a free surface.","This puts the fat above the reticular layer. Adipose tissue makes up the hypodermis, which lies beneath the entire dermis."],"fix":"Review the epidermis, both layers of the dermis, and the hypodermis, along with the tissue that makes up each one."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-integumentary-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-integumentary","comp":"w1-epidermal-strata","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A wooden splinter is driven straight into the skin of the heel. In what order does its tip pass these four epidermal strata?","options":["Spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum","Corneum, granulosum, lucidum, spinosum","Corneum, lucidum, granulosum, spinosum","Lucidum, corneum, granulosum, spinosum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The heel is thick skin, so it has all five strata, including the stratum lucidum. From deep to superficial the strata are basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum and corneum. A splinter enters from outside, so it travels the opposite way: corneum first, then lucidum, granulosum and spinosum, and the basale would be next if it went deeper.","notes":["This is the right series but in the wrong direction. It is the path a keratinocyte follows as it moves up toward the surface, not the path of something entering from outside.","This swaps the lucidum and the granulosum. The thin, clear stratum lucidum sits just under the stratum corneum, between it and the granulosum.","Correct. Moving inward from the surface of thick skin, the splinter meets the stratum corneum, then the lucidum, the granulosum and the spinosum.","This puts the lucidum on the surface. The stratum corneum, made of dead keratin-filled cells, is always the outermost stratum."],"fix":"Review the five epidermal strata in order from deep to superficial and which one is found only in thick skin."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-integumentary-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-integumentary","comp":"w1-skin-accessory","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A crushed fingertip loses its entire nail body, but the nail root and the nail matrix under the proximal nail fold are not damaged. What do you predict will happen?","options":["The lunula will expand forward to cover the nail bed","A new nail will grow out from the nail matrix","The hyponychium will grow backward to form a new nail","No nail will form again, because its dividing cells were in the nail body"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A nail is a plate of dead, hard keratinized cells, so the visible nail body cannot repair itself. New nail cells are made only in the nail matrix, the epithelium just proximal to the nail root, hidden under the skin fold. Because the matrix survived, its dividing cells will produce new nail that slowly grows forward over the nail bed. Damage to the matrix itself is what can leave a nail missing or permanently misshapen.","notes":["The lunula is the pale crescent at the proximal end of the nail body, and it looks pale because the epithelium beneath it is thickened. It does not slide forward to cover the nail bed, and new nail comes from the nail matrix instead.","Correct. The nail matrix holds the dividing cells that make new nail, so with the matrix intact the nail will regrow.","The hyponychium is thickened stratum corneum under the free edge that secures the nail to the fingertip. It does not make new nail cells.","The nail body is made of dead, hard keratinized cells and has no dividing cells. This would be the outcome if the nail matrix were destroyed."],"fix":"Review the parts of a nail and which part contains the dividing cells that produce new nail."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-integumentary-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-integumentary","comp":"w1-dermis-layers","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A burn on a fingertip destroys the epidermis and the papillary layer of the dermis, but the reticular layer survives. Which description of the damaged and surviving structures is correct?","options":["Meissner corpuscles and lamellated corpuscles are lost, because both sit in the papillary layer","Sweat glands and capillary loops are lost, while Meissner corpuscles survive in the reticular layer","Meissner corpuscles and capillary loops are lost, while the coiled parts of the sweat glands survive","Hair follicles and Meissner corpuscles are lost, while capillary loops survive in the reticular layer"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The papillary layer is the thin, superficial part of the dermis, made of areolar tissue. Its dermal papillae hold capillary loops, Meissner corpuscles for touch, and free nerve endings. The thicker reticular layer below is dense irregular connective tissue that holds the blood vessels, hair follicles, sebaceous glands and the coiled secretory parts of the sweat glands. A burn that stops at the bottom of the papillary layer removes the papillae and spares what lies in the reticular layer.","notes":["Meissner corpuscles are in the dermal papillae, but lamellated corpuscles sit deeper, in the hypodermis. They would survive a burn that spares the reticular layer.","This reverses the two layers. Meissner corpuscles and capillary loops are in the papillary layer, and the coiled secretory parts of sweat glands are in the reticular layer.","Correct. Meissner corpuscles and capillary loops sit in the dermal papillae, while the sweat glands are coiled deep in the dermis, in the reticular layer.","A fingertip is thick skin, which has no hair follicles. Capillary loops are also in the dermal papillae, so they would be lost, not saved."],"fix":"Review the papillary and reticular layers of the dermis and the structures and sensory corpuscles each one contains."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-integumentary-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-integumentary","comp":"w1-epidermal-strata","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Four burns of the same size are on the back of a patient's forearm. Which burn is most likely to need a skin graft?","options":["A sunburn that damages the upper epidermis but spares the stratum basale","A blistering burn that reaches the papillary layer and spares the reticular layer","A burn that reaches deep into the reticular layer but spares the bases of follicles and glands","A burn that destroys the epidermis and the entire dermis, leaving yellow fat visible"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"New epidermis can only come from living epidermal cells. In a superficial burn those cells are in the intact stratum basale. In a partial-thickness burn they come from the epidermal cells lining hair follicles and sweat glands that survive deep in the dermis. When the entire dermis is destroyed and the fat of the hypodermis shows, the stratum basale is gone along with the hair follicles and sweat glands that sit in the dermis, so the wound has no source of new epidermis and a graft is needed.","notes":["This is a superficial burn. The stratum basale holds the dividing cells that rebuild the epidermis, and here it is intact, so the skin heals on its own.","This is a partial-thickness burn. Hair follicles and sweat glands in the reticular layer are lined with epidermal cells, and those cells can grow out to resurface the wound.","This deep partial-thickness burn heals slowly, and some do need grafts, but surviving follicle and gland bases still hold epidermal cells. A burn that removes the full dermis has none left, so it is the more likely graft.","Correct. Losing the whole dermis removes the stratum basale along with the hair follicles and sweat glands in the dermis, so no living epidermal cells remain to regrow the surface."],"fix":"Review the burn depths and, for each one, where the surviving epidermal cells that rebuild the skin are found."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m1-integumentary-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m1-integumentary","comp":"w1-skin-accessory","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A 15-year-old has acne on the face and upper back but never on the palms. Which explanation best accounts for both where the acne appears and why it started in the teen years?","options":["Palms have a stratum lucidum that blocks pores, and eccrine glands become active at puberty","Palms lack apocrine sweat glands, and apocrine glands empty onto the face from birth","Palms lack follicles and sebaceous glands, and sebaceous glands become active at puberty","Palms have many eccrine glands that flush the pores, and sebaceous glands become active in early childhood"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Three facts combine here. Sebaceous glands release oily sebum by holocrine secretion, and most of them empty into a hair follicle, where a blocked follicle can become acne. The palms are thick skin, which has no hair follicles and no sebaceous glands, so acne cannot form there. Sebaceous glands stay relatively inactive through childhood and are activated at puberty, which explains why acne usually begins in the teen years.","notes":["Eccrine sweat glands become active soon after birth, and they are especially numerous on the palms. The stratum lucidum is a layer of the epidermis and does not cause acne.","Apocrine sweat glands sit in the axillae, groin and areolae and become active at puberty, not at birth. Acne begins in sebaceous glands, not apocrine sweat glands.","Correct. Acne starts in sebaceous glands that empty into hair follicles. The palms have neither, and these glands are relatively inactive until puberty.","Eccrine sweat glands are numerous on the palms, but the palms have no sebaceous glands at all, so there is nothing to flush. Sebaceous glands are also relatively inactive through childhood and switch on at puberty."],"fix":"Review the four skin glands, comparing where each is found, where its duct empties, and when it becomes active."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-bone-tissue-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-bone-tissue","comp":"w2-long-bone-gross","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon drills a small hole through the middle of an adult femur's shaft, from the outer surface straight into the center, to place a rod inside the bone. In what order does the drill pass through these structures?","options":["Endosteum, compact bone, periosteum, medullary cavity","Periosteum, endosteum, compact bone, medullary cavity","Periosteum, compact bone, endosteum, medullary cavity","Compact bone, periosteum, medullary cavity, endosteum"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The periosteum is the connective tissue membrane covering the outer surface of the bone, so the drill meets it first. Beneath it is the thick wall of compact bone that makes up the diaphysis. The endosteum is the thin membrane lining the internal bone surfaces, so it sits on the inner face of that compact wall, right where the bone opens into the medullary cavity. In an adult, the cavity at the center of the shaft holds yellow marrow, which is mostly fat. The prefixes are the shortcut here: peri- means around, and endo- means within.","notes":["This puts the endosteum on the outside and the periosteum on the inside, which reverses the two membranes. Endo- means within, so the endosteum lines the internal surfaces, and peri- means around, so the periosteum wraps the outer surface.","This places the endosteum between the periosteum and the compact bone. The endosteum lines the inner surfaces that face the medullary cavity, so the drill meets it only after crossing the compact wall.","Correct. The periosteum covers the outside, compact bone forms the wall, the endosteum lines the inside of that wall, and the medullary cavity is the hollow core.","The periosteum covers the outer surface of the bone, so the drill meets it before it reaches the compact bone. This order also puts the endosteum past the cavity, but the endosteum lines the cavity wall rather than lying beyond it."],"fix":"Review the gross anatomy of a long bone, especially which membrane covers the outside and which lines the medullary cavity."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-bone-tissue-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-bone-tissue","comp":"w2-compact-spongy","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A slide of bone tissue shows osteocytes in lacunae linked by canaliculi, but no central canals anywhere. The tissue forms thin bony struts, and the spaces between the struts are filled with red marrow. From which part of a femur was this sample most likely taken?","options":["The interior of the proximal epiphysis","The wall of the diaphysis at mid-shaft","The medullary cavity of the adult shaft","The layer just beneath the periosteum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"No central canals means no osteons, and a tissue built from thin struts is built from trabeculae, so this is spongy bone. Spongy bone fills the epiphyses of a long bone under a thin shell of compact bone. The red marrow fits too, because red marrow fills the spongy bone of the epiphyses. The shaft wall and the layer just under the periosteum are compact bone, and the adult medullary cavity holds yellow marrow rather than a lattice of bone.","notes":["Correct. Trabeculae with no osteons and red marrow in the spaces describe spongy bone, which fills the epiphyses.","The wall of the shaft is compact bone, built from osteons that each have a central canal. This would be the right place if the slide showed concentric lamellae ringing central canals.","The adult medullary cavity is a hollow space filled with yellow marrow, which is mostly fat, not a lattice of bony struts. It would fit a sample that showed fat with no bone matrix at all.","Just under the periosteum are circumferential lamellae, broad sheets of compact bone that wrap the whole shaft. That layer is solid rather than an open lattice, so it would not show red marrow sitting between struts."],"fix":"Review how compact and spongy bone differ under the microscope and where each one sits in a long bone."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-bone-tissue-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-bone-tissue","comp":"w2-bone-cells","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A child is born with osteoclasts that cannot break down bone matrix, while the osteogenic cells, osteoblasts, and osteocytes all work normally. Which change would you predict in the child's long bones?","options":["The bones become thin and porous because new matrix is never added","The bones stay soft and bend because their matrix never hardens","The growth plates close early because chondrocytes stop dividing","The bones become overly dense and the medullary cavity stays narrow"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Osteoclasts are the only bone cells that take bone away. Osteoblasts keep building matrix, but nothing removes it, so bone keeps piling up and becomes abnormally dense. Osteoclasts are also the cells that hollow out the medullary cavity during development and widen it as the bone grows, so without them the cavity stays narrow. Healthy remodeling depends on building and breaking down working together, and losing the breakdown half tips the balance toward too much bone.","notes":["Thin, porous bone happens when breakdown outpaces building, as in osteoporosis. Here the osteoblasts work normally and the osteoclasts do not, so the balance tips the opposite way.","Soft bones that bend come from poorly mineralized matrix, as in rickets. That problem lies with mineral being laid into the matrix, not with the cell that resorbs bone, so it does not follow from this case.","Lengthening at the growth plate depends on chondrocytes dividing in the zone of proliferation, and nothing in this case changes those cartilage cells. Early closure would point to a problem in the plate's cartilage, not in the osteoclast.","Correct. With building intact and no breakdown, bone piles up, and the cells that hollow out and widen the medullary cavity are missing."],"fix":"Review the four bone cells, what each one does, and how building and breakdown work together in remodeling."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-bone-tissue-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-bone-tissue","comp":"w2-ossification-growth","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A slide of a child's growth plate shows four kinds of cartilage cells: enlarged chondrocytes, small cells that anchor the plate, chondrocytes sitting in hardened matrix, and cells dividing and stacking into columns. Rank them by position, starting closest to the epiphysis and ending closest to the diaphysis.","options":["Dividing columns, anchoring cells, enlarged cells, cells in hardened matrix","Anchoring cells, dividing columns, enlarged cells, cells in hardened matrix","Anchoring cells, enlarged cells, dividing columns, cells in hardened matrix","Cells in hardened matrix, enlarged cells, dividing columns, anchoring cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The growth plate reads like a timeline from the epiphysis toward the shaft. The zone of resting cartilage sits against the epiphysis and anchors the plate. Next, in the zone of proliferation, chondrocytes divide and stack into columns. In the zone of hypertrophy those new cells enlarge, and in the zone of calcification the matrix around them hardens. Bone then replaces the calcified cartilage in the zone of ossification beside the diaphysis, so the cells farthest from the epiphysis are the oldest ones.","notes":["This puts the dividing columns ahead of the anchoring cells. The resting zone is the one that anchors the plate to the epiphysis, so it sits at the epiphysis end, with the dividing cells just beneath it.","Correct. Resting, proliferation, hypertrophy, then calcification is the order of the zones from the epiphysis toward the diaphysis.","Cells cannot enlarge before they are made. The zone of proliferation produces the cells, and they grow larger only afterward in the zone of hypertrophy, so the dividing columns come before the enlarged cells.","This is the correct order read backward, from the diaphysis toward the epiphysis. It would be right if the question asked you to start at the shaft side, where the matrix hardens and bone takes over."],"fix":"Review the five zones of the growth plate in order from the epiphysis to the diaphysis and what happens to the cells in each."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-bone-tissue-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-bone-tissue","comp":"w2-cartilage-types","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Four injuries happen in four different tissues. Using what you know about blood supply and the perichondrium, which injury would be expected to heal the most poorly?","options":["A clean fracture straight through the shaft of the radius","A small cut through the elastic cartilage of the external ear","A crack in one of the costal cartilages beside the sternum","A tear in the articular cartilage capping the femur at the knee"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Bone has a rich blood supply through its central and perforating canals, so a fracture brings in the blood, cells, and nutrients that repair needs, and it usually heals well. All cartilage is avascular, so cartilage injuries heal slowly. Most cartilage, including the elastic cartilage of the ear and the hyaline costal cartilages, is wrapped in a perichondrium that carries a blood supply and the stem cells for new cartilage. Articular cartilage has no perichondrium, so it lacks both nearby vessels and that source of new cells, which makes a tear there the least able to repair itself.","notes":["Bone is well supplied with blood through its central and perforating canals, so a fracture brings in repair cells quickly and usually heals well. This would be the best-healing injury on the list, not the worst.","Elastic cartilage is avascular, so this cut heals slowly, but the cartilage of the external ear is wrapped in perichondrium. That sheath carries vessels and stem cells that can add new cartilage at the surface, which gives it an advantage over articular cartilage.","Costal cartilage is hyaline cartilage, the same type as articular cartilage, and neither has blood vessels of its own. The difference is that costal cartilage is covered by perichondrium, so the missing perichondrium, not the cartilage type, decides this one.","Correct. Articular cartilage is avascular and has no perichondrium, so it has neither a blood supply nor a sheath of stem cells to rebuild it."],"fix":"Review why cartilage heals slowly, what the perichondrium provides, and which cartilage surfaces have no perichondrium."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-bone-tissue-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-bone-tissue","comp":"w2-cartilage-growth","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A researcher labels every dividing cell in a growing piece of hyaline cartilage. All of the labeled cells are in the dense connective tissue sheath wrapped around the cartilage, and none are inside the matrix. Which type of growth is this, and how is the cartilage getting bigger?","options":["Appositional growth, adding new layers at the outer surface","Interstitial growth, expanding outward from within the matrix","Appositional growth, expanding outward from within the matrix","Interstitial growth, adding new layers at the outer surface"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The dense connective tissue sheath around cartilage is the perichondrium. Appositional growth starts in the perichondrium, where cells add new cartilage at the outer surface, so the piece grows by layers laid on the outside. Interstitial growth is the opposite pattern: chondrocytes already inside the matrix divide, and the cartilage expands from within. Because no dividing cells were found inside the matrix, interstitial growth is not what is happening here.","notes":["Correct. Dividing cells in the perichondrium mean appositional growth, which adds cartilage at the surface.","Interstitial growth comes from chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix. That would be correct if the labeled cells were in lacunae deep in the cartilage, but none were found there.","The name is right but the direction is wrong. Appositional growth adds material at the outer surface, while growth from within the matrix is the interstitial pattern.","This pairs the interstitial name with the appositional direction. Interstitial means within the spaces, so interstitial growth expands the cartilage from inside rather than by adding layers at the surface."],"fix":"Review the two modes of cartilage growth, where each begins, and which direction each one expands the cartilage."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-skull-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-skull","comp":"w2-skull-markings","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these four landmarks, which lie on or right beside the midline of the skull, from most anterior to most posterior: the sella turcica, the foramen magnum, the frontal sinus, and the crista galli.","options":["Crista galli, frontal sinus, sella turcica, foramen magnum","Frontal sinus, crista galli, sella turcica, foramen magnum","Frontal sinus, sella turcica, crista galli, foramen magnum","Frontal sinus, crista galli, foramen magnum, sella turcica"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each landmark belongs to a bone, and those bones line up from front to back along the middle of the skull. The frontal sinus lies in the frontal bone above the orbits, at the very front. The crista galli rises from the ethmoid bone between the orbits, just behind the frontal sinus. The sella turcica is on the body of the sphenoid, the central wedge of the cranial base. The foramen magnum is in the occipital bone at the back of the cranial base, where the brainstem continues into the spinal cord.","notes":["The crista galli belongs to the ethmoid, which sits behind the frontal sinus rather than in front of it. The frontal sinus is in the forehead above the orbits, so it is the most anterior of the four.","Correct. Frontal bone, then ethmoid, then sphenoid, then occipital is the front-to-back order of the bones that hold these landmarks.","This puts the sphenoid ahead of the ethmoid. The ethmoid lies between the orbits behind the nose, and the sphenoid is the central wedge farther back, so the crista galli comes before the sella turcica.","The foramen magnum is in the occipital bone at the back of the skull, behind the sphenoid. Placing it ahead of the sella turcica would put the occipital bone in the middle of the cranial base, where the sphenoid actually sits."],"fix":"Review where the frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and occipital bones sit along the midline and which marking belongs to each."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-skull-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-skull","comp":"w2-skull-foramina","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"After a car crash, a patient has clear, watery fluid dripping from the nose and can no longer smell coffee. Damage to which skull structure best explains both findings?","options":["The perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone","The nasal bones at the bridge of the nose","The optic canal of the sphenoid bone","The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The olfactory nerve filaments pass from the nasal lining up through the small holes of the cribriform plate to reach the brain. The plate is thin and perforated, and it is all that separates the nasal cavity below from the brain above. A fracture there can tear the olfactory filaments, which explains the lost sense of smell. It can also open a path for cerebrospinal fluid to leak from around the brain into the nose, which explains the clear fluid.","notes":["The perpendicular plate is the vertical part of the ethmoid that forms the upper nasal septum. A break there would injure the septum, but the smell filaments pass through the horizontal cribriform plate, not this vertical one.","The nasal bones form the bridge of the nose and often break in a crash, which can cause a nosebleed. They do not carry the smell filaments and do not separate the nose from the brain, so they explain neither finding.","The optic canal passes the optic nerve, so damage there would affect vision in one eye. It would be the right structure if the patient had lost sight rather than smell.","Correct. The cribriform plate passes the smell filaments and forms the thin roof between the nasal cavity and the brain."],"fix":"Review the markings of the ethmoid bone and which nerve filaments pass through the cribriform plate."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-skull-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-skull","comp":"w2-skull-bones","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A boxer is struck hard on the side of the head in the temple region. He talks normally at first, then grows drowsy over the next hour, and a scan shows blood collecting inside the skull right under the spot that was hit. Which explanation best ties together the bones, the vessel, and the shape of the skull?","options":["Thin bone where four cranial bones meet broke and tore the artery beneath it, and trapped blood pressed on the brain","The squamous suture split and tore the internal carotid artery, which enters the cranial cavity along that suture line","The zygomatic arch snapped and drove fragments into the brain, and the open fontanelles let the skull bones spread apart","The mastoid process cracked and tore the internal jugular vein, which leaves the skull through an opening in the mastoid"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The temple region is the pterion, the H-shaped junction where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet. It is the thinnest part of the skull, and the middle meningeal artery runs just beneath it on the inner surface. A fracture there can tear that artery, and the blood collects inside the skull. Because the adult skull is a rigid case of locked sutures, the growing pool of blood has nowhere to go, so pressure on the brain rises over time, which is why the boxer seemed fine at first and then grew drowsy.","notes":["Correct. The pterion is the thinnest part of the skull, the middle meningeal artery lies under it, and the rigid adult skull cannot make room for the blood.","The internal carotid artery enters the skull through the carotid canal in the base of the temporal bone, not along the squamous suture on the side of the head. The squamous suture joins the parietal and temporal bones, but no major artery enters there.","The zygomatic arch is part of the face, outside the cranial cavity, so breaking it does not tear a vessel inside the skull. The fontanelles close in infancy, so an adult's skull cannot spread apart to make room, which is exactly why the bleeding is dangerous.","The internal jugular vein leaves through the jugular foramen, which lies between the temporal and occipital bones in the skull base, not through the mastoid process. The mastoid also sits behind the ear, not in the temple where the blow landed."],"fix":"Review the pterion, the four bones that meet there, the artery beneath it, and why bleeding inside the rigid adult skull is an emergency."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-skull-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-skull","comp":"w2-skull-cavities","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"To remove a tumor of the pituitary gland, a surgeon passes an instrument in through a nostril and back through the skull to reach the gland. In what order does the instrument pass these spaces and structures?","options":["Nasal cavity, sella turcica, sphenoidal sinus, pituitary gland","Sphenoidal sinus, nasal cavity, sella turcica, pituitary gland","Nasal cavity, sphenoidal sinus, sella turcica, pituitary gland","Nasal cavity, sphenoidal sinus, pituitary gland, sella turcica"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The instrument enters the nasal cavity first, the space behind the nose. The sphenoidal sinus is the air space inside the body of the sphenoid bone, and it drains into the back of the nasal cavity. The sella turcica sits on top of the sphenoid body, directly above that sinus, and its deep seat cradles the pituitary gland. So the surgeon passes from the nose into the sinus, then through the bony floor of the sella turcica to reach the gland resting in it.","notes":["The sella turcica sits on top of the sphenoid body, and the sphenoidal sinus is inside that body just below it. Coming up from the nose, the instrument reaches the sinus before the sella.","The instrument enters through a nostril, so the nasal cavity has to come first. The sphenoidal sinus is deep in the skull and is reached only by passing back through the nasal cavity.","Correct. The sphenoidal sinus lies behind the nasal cavity and just below the sella turcica, which holds the pituitary gland.","The pituitary gland rests in the seat of the sella turcica, so the bony floor of the sella lies between the sinus and the gland. The instrument has to cross that floor before it can reach the gland."],"fix":"Review the body of the sphenoid bone, the sinus inside it, and the sella turcica that sits on top of it."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-skull-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-skull","comp":"w2-skull-cavities","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A baseball strikes a player squarely in the eye. A CT scan shows the bony rim of the orbit intact, but the thin floor of the orbit has broken, and orbital fat has sagged down through the break. Into which space has the fat most likely moved?","options":["The ethmoidal air cells","The maxillary sinus","The sphenoidal sinus","The frontal sinus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The orbital rim is thick and strong, but the floor of the orbit is a thin plate formed mainly by the maxilla. When a blunt object pushes the eye back into the socket, pressure inside the orbit spikes and breaks the weakest wall, the floor. Directly beneath that floor is the maxillary sinus, the large air space in the body of the maxilla. Contents of the orbit can drop through the broken floor into this sinus.","notes":["The ethmoidal air cells lie between the orbits, in the ethmoid bone that forms part of the medial wall. They would be involved if the medial wall had broken, but this scan shows a broken floor.","Correct. The orbital floor is the roof of the maxillary sinus, so a break in the floor opens straight into it.","The sphenoidal sinus is deep in the body of the sphenoid, behind the orbits. It does not lie under the orbital floor, so fat sagging down through the floor would not reach it.","The frontal sinus is in the frontal bone above the orbits, near the orbital roof. Fat falling through a broken floor moves downward, away from the frontal sinus."],"fix":"Review which bone forms the floor of the orbit and where each of the four paranasal sinuses sits relative to the orbit."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-skull-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-skull","comp":"w2-skull-foramina","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A tumor at the back of the orbit presses only on the structures passing through the superior orbital fissure. Which problem would the patient most likely notice?","options":["Loss of vision in the eye on that side","Loss of the sense of smell on that side","The eye on that side does not move normally","Numbness of the chin and the lower lip"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The superior orbital fissure is an opening in the sphenoid bone at the back of the orbit, and it passes the nerves that move the eye. Pressure on those nerves weakens the eye's movements, so the eye may not turn normally and the person may see double. The optic nerve is close by but passes through its own opening, the optic canal, which is why vision itself can be spared when only the fissure is involved.","notes":["Vision depends on the optic nerve, which passes through the optic canal, a separate opening just medial to the fissure. This would be the right choice if the tumor pressed on the optic canal.","The smell filaments pass through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid, in the roof of the nasal cavity, well away from the back of the orbit. A cribriform fracture, not an orbital tumor, explains lost smell.","Correct. The nerves that move the eye pass through the superior orbital fissure, so pressure there disrupts eye movement.","The nerve to the chin and lower lip exits through the mental foramen of the mandible. It does not pass through the orbit, so pressure at the superior orbital fissure would not numb the lip."],"fix":"Review the openings of the sphenoid bone and what passes through the optic canal compared with the superior orbital fissure."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-spine-thorax-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-spine-thorax","comp":"w2-typical-vertebra","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A patient lifts a heavy box and soon has pain shooting down one leg from a herniated lumbar disc. Put the events in the order they happen, starting with the first damage to the disc.","options":["Anulus fibrosus tears, nucleus pulposus pushes through the tear, bulge reaches an intervertebral foramen, spinal nerve is pressed","Nucleus pulposus pushes through the tear, anulus fibrosus tears, bulge reaches an intervertebral foramen, spinal nerve is pressed","Anulus fibrosus tears, bulge reaches an intervertebral foramen, nucleus pulposus pushes through the tear, spinal nerve is pressed","Spinal nerve is pressed, anulus fibrosus tears, nucleus pulposus pushes through the tear, bulge reaches an intervertebral foramen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The intervertebral disc has a tough outer ring, the anulus fibrosus, holding a soft gel core, the nucleus pulposus. A herniation begins when the ring tears. The gel core then pushes out through that tear, and the bulge can reach an intervertebral foramen, the gap between stacked vertebrae where a spinal nerve leaves the column. Pressure on that nerve is what sends pain down the leg, so nerve compression is the last link in the chain.","notes":["Correct. The ring tears first, the core escapes through it, and the bulge then presses on a nerve at the intervertebral foramen.","The nucleus pulposus cannot push through a tear that is not there yet. The anulus fibrosus holds the core in place until it tears, so the tear has to come first.","The bulge is made of nucleus pulposus, so nothing can reach the intervertebral foramen until the core has pushed out through the tear. This order has the bulge arriving before it exists.","Nerve compression is the result of the herniation, not its cause. The leg pain comes at the end of the chain, after disc material has reached the foramen where the nerve exits."],"fix":"Review the two parts of an intervertebral disc and where spinal nerves leave the vertebral column."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-spine-thorax-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-spine-thorax","comp":"w2-regional-vertebrae","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"In lab, a student picks up a cervical vertebra with transverse foramina and a tooth-like peg rising from its body. If disease fused this peg solidly to the ring of the vertebra above it, which head movement would be most limited?","options":["Nodding the head up and down to say yes","Tilting an ear down toward the shoulder","Tipping the head back to look straight up","Turning the head side to side to say no"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The peg is the dens, so this vertebra is the axis, C2. The dens projects up into the front of the ring of the atlas, C1, and the atlas, carrying the skull, pivots around it at the atlanto-axial joint. That rotation is the motion of shaking the head no. Fusing the dens to the atlas would lock that pivot, and the joints lower in the neck can add only part of that turning, while nodding yes would still happen at the atlanto-occipital joint between the atlas and the skull.","notes":["Nodding yes happens at the atlanto-occipital joint, where the occipital condyles rock on the atlas. That joint lies above the atlas and is untouched by fusing the dens, so nodding would still work.","Tilting an ear toward the shoulder is lateral flexion, spread along the joints of the cervical column. It does not depend on the pivot around the dens, so it would not be the movement most limited.","Tipping the head back is extension, which happens at the atlanto-occipital joint and along the cervical spine. Neither depends on the atlas rotating around the dens, so this motion would remain.","Correct. The atlas and skull rotate around the dens at the atlanto-axial joint, so fusing the dens blocks most of the no motion."],"fix":"Review the atlas, the axis and its dens, and which head movement happens at the atlanto-occipital and atlanto-axial joints."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-spine-thorax-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-spine-thorax","comp":"w2-regional-vertebrae","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Four loose vertebrae from one adult skeleton sit on a lab bench: one with costal facets for the tenth pair of ribs, one with transverse foramina and a forked spinous process, one with a thick kidney-shaped body and no rib facets, and one with costal facets for the second pair of ribs. Rank them from the smallest vertebral body to the largest.","options":["Second-rib facets, transverse foramina, tenth-rib facets, kidney-shaped body","Transverse foramina, second-rib facets, tenth-rib facets, kidney-shaped body","Transverse foramina, tenth-rib facets, second-rib facets, kidney-shaped body","Transverse foramina, second-rib facets, kidney-shaped body, tenth-rib facets"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each feature places the vertebra in a region. Transverse foramina and a forked spinous process mark a cervical vertebra, costal facets mark a thoracic vertebra, and a thick kidney-shaped body with no rib facets marks a lumbar vertebra. Facets for the second ribs belong to a vertebra high in the thoracic region, and facets for the tenth ribs belong to one low in the thoracic region. Vertebral bodies generally grow larger from the neck to the lower back because each lower vertebra carries more of the body's weight, so the ranking follows position from top to bottom.","notes":["A vertebra with transverse foramina is cervical, and cervical vertebrae have the smallest bodies in the column. A thoracic vertebra, even one high in the chest, carries more weight and has a larger body.","Correct. Cervical, upper thoracic, lower thoracic, then lumbar follows the increase in body size down the column.","Both of these are thoracic, but the one with facets for the tenth ribs sits lower in the column than the one for the second ribs. Lower vertebrae carry more weight and have larger bodies, so the tenth-rib vertebra belongs after the second-rib vertebra.","The kidney-shaped body with no rib facets is lumbar, and lumbar vertebrae have the largest bodies of all. Placing it before a thoracic vertebra would mean body size shrinks partway down the column, which it does not."],"fix":"Review the identifying features of cervical, thoracic, and lumbar vertebrae and why vertebral body size increases down the column."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-spine-thorax-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-spine-thorax","comp":"w2-spine-regions","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A 4-month-old baby can hold her head up steadily but has not yet started to stand or walk. Which spinal curvatures does she most likely have at this age?","options":["Thoracic and sacral curvatures only","Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral curvatures","Cervical, thoracic, and sacral curvatures","Cervical and lumbar curvatures only"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The thoracic and sacral curvatures are primary curvatures, present at birth as part of the fetal C shape, so she has had them all along. The cervical curvature is secondary and develops as an infant begins to hold up the head, which she can now do. The lumbar curvature is also secondary, but it develops as a child stands and walks, and she has not reached that milestone. That leaves three curvatures: the two primary ones plus the cervical.","notes":["This would describe a newborn who cannot yet hold up the head. The cervical curvature forms when the infant starts holding the head up, which this baby already does.","This is the adult pattern. The lumbar curvature forms only once a child stands and walks, so a baby who has done neither would not have it yet.","Correct. She has both primary curvatures from birth, and holding up her head has added the cervical curvature.","These are the two secondary curvatures, and this pairing leaves out the primary ones. The thoracic and sacral curvatures are present from birth, so they cannot be missing at four months."],"fix":"Review which spinal curvatures are primary and which are secondary, and the milestone that produces each secondary curvature."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-spine-thorax-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-spine-thorax","comp":"w2-thoracic-cage","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"While examining a patient's chest, a clinician finds the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, follows the rib attached at that level out to the side, and then counts down six more ribs. Which description fits the rib she is now touching?","options":["A false rib whose cartilage joins the costal cartilage of the rib above","A true rib that attaches directly to the sternum by its own cartilage","A floating rib that ends freely with no anterior attachment at all","A true rib whose cartilage joins the costal cartilage of the rib above"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The ridge is the sternal angle, which sits at the level of the second rib. Counting down six ribs from the second rib brings her to the eighth rib. Ribs 1 to 7 are true ribs, each reaching the sternum by its own costal cartilage, so the eighth rib is the first of the false ribs. Ribs 8 to 10 reach the sternum only indirectly, because each one's cartilage joins the costal cartilage of the rib above it. Only ribs 11 and 12, the floating ribs, have no anterior attachment.","notes":["Correct. The sternal angle marks rib 2, six ribs below that is rib 8, and rib 8 is a false rib that joins the cartilage of the rib above.","True ribs are pairs 1 to 7. This would be correct if she had counted down five or fewer ribs from the second rib, but six more ribs takes her past the seventh.","Floating ribs are only pairs 11 and 12. Reaching them from the second rib would take nine or ten more ribs, not six.","A true rib reaches the sternum directly by its own cartilage, so it never depends on the cartilage of the rib above. Joining the rib above is the pattern of false ribs 8 to 10."],"fix":"Review the sternal angle as a landmark and how the twelve rib pairs are classified as true, false, or floating."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-spine-thorax-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-spine-thorax","comp":"w2-thoracic-cage","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"An X-ray shows that the knob a short way along the sixth rib, just past its neck, has pulled away from its joint with the spine, while the posterior end of the rib still sits against the vertebral bodies. Which part of the thoracic vertebra has lost contact with the rib?","options":["The vertebral body","The spinous process","The superior articular process","The transverse process"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A typical rib, like the sixth, makes two joints with the spine. The head, at the posterior end, meets costal facets on the vertebral bodies, and that joint is still intact here. The knob just past the neck is the tubercle, and it meets a costal facet on the transverse process. A tubercle that has pulled away has therefore lost its contact with the transverse process.","notes":["The vertebral bodies meet the head of the rib, its posterior end. The X-ray shows the head still in place, so the body has not lost contact.","The spinous process points straight back in the midline and does not articulate with any rib. It serves as an attachment for muscles and ligaments instead.","The superior articular processes join one vertebra to the vertebra above it, not to a rib. They would matter in a question about how stacked vertebrae fit together.","Correct. The tubercle of a rib articulates with the costal facet on the transverse process."],"fix":"Review the parts of a rib and which part of a thoracic vertebra the head and the tubercle each meet."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-upper-extremity-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-upper-extremity","comp":"w2-pectoral-girdle","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A cyclist falls onto an outstretched hand. The force travels up the limb until it reaches the axial skeleton. In what order does the force pass through these bones?","options":["Radius, humerus, clavicle, scapula","Humerus, radius, scapula, clavicle","Radius, humerus, scapula, clavicle","Radius, scapula, humerus, clavicle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"From the hand, force passes into the radius, the forearm bone that forms most of the wrist joint, and up to the elbow, where the radial head meets the capitulum of the humerus. The head of the humerus sits in the glenoid cavity of the scapula, so the force moves next into the scapula. The acromion of the scapula meets the clavicle at the acromioclavicular joint, and the clavicle carries the force to the manubrium at the sternoclavicular joint. The clavicle is the only bony link between the upper limb and the axial skeleton, which is why it so often breaks in this kind of fall.","notes":["The humerus does not touch the clavicle. The head of the humerus sits in the glenoid cavity of the scapula, and the clavicle connects to the scapula at the acromion, so the scapula comes first.","The radius is in the forearm, between the hand and the humerus, so the force reaches it before the humerus. Starting with the humerus skips the forearm, which is the bone the force enters first after a fall on the hand.","Correct. The force runs radius, humerus, scapula, then clavicle, and only the clavicle reaches the sternum.","The radius meets the humerus at the elbow, not the scapula. The scapula is reached only after the force has climbed the humerus to the shoulder joint."],"fix":"Review the joints of the pectoral girdle and how the clavicle links the upper limb to the sternum."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-upper-extremity-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-upper-extremity","comp":"w2-hand-bones","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A skateboarder falls on an outstretched hand and is tender in the small hollow at the base of the thumb. The first X-ray looks normal, yet the doctor splints the wrist and orders another X-ray in two weeks. Which bone is the doctor worried about, and why the caution?","options":["The scaphoid, whose poor blood supply can stop a missed break from healing","The lunate, because it dislocates easily and can crowd the carpal tunnel","The trapezium, because a break there would freeze the thumb's saddle joint","The capitate, because it is the largest carpal and takes most of the load"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hollow at the base of the thumb is the anatomical snuffbox, and tenderness there points to the scaphoid, the proximal-row carpal on the thumb side. The scaphoid is the carpal fractured most often in a fall on an outstretched hand. Its blood supply is poor, so a break can cut part of the bone off from its vessels, and a missed fracture may fail to heal. That is why the wrist is protected and rechecked even when the first X-ray looks normal.","notes":["Correct. Snuffbox tenderness points to the scaphoid, and its poor blood supply makes a missed break risky.","The lunate is the carpal most often dislocated, and it can crowd the carpal tunnel when pushed toward the palm. It sits in the middle of the proximal row, though, not at the base of the thumb, and the worry here is a fracture that may not heal.","The trapezium is on the thumb side, but in the distal row, where it meets the first metacarpal. Snuffbox tenderness after this kind of fall, along with the worry about healing, points to the scaphoid in the proximal row.","The capitate is the largest carpal, but it sits in the center of the distal row, not at the base of the thumb. Size is not the concern in this case; the concern is a bone with a poor blood supply."],"fix":"Review the two rows of carpals and which carpal is most often fractured, which is most often dislocated, and which is the largest."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-upper-extremity-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-upper-extremity","comp":"w2-arm-forearm","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"An X-ray shows a fracture across the middle of the humeral shaft, running through the shallow oblique groove on its posterior surface. Which structure lying in that groove is most at risk?","options":["The axillary nerve","The radial nerve","The ulnar nerve","The median nerve"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The shallow groove that spirals across the back of the humeral shaft is the radial groove, and the radial nerve runs in it right against the bone. A break through the middle of the shaft can therefore stretch or injure the radial nerve. Each of the other nerves runs close to bone at a different spot: the axillary nerve to the surgical neck, the ulnar nerve to the back of the medial epicondyle, and the median nerve to the carpal tunnel at the wrist.","notes":["The axillary nerve runs beside the surgical neck, the narrowing below the tubercles near the shoulder. It would be the concern for a fracture high on the humerus, not one in the middle of the shaft.","Correct. The radial nerve runs in the radial groove on the posterior humeral shaft, so a mid-shaft fracture puts it at risk.","The ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle at the elbow, the spot called the funny bone. It would be at risk in an injury at the distal humerus, not at mid-shaft.","The median nerve's key bony landmark is the carpal tunnel at the wrist, where it can be compressed under the flexor retinaculum. It does not run in the groove on the back of the humerus."],"fix":"Review the radial groove and the bony landmarks where the axillary, ulnar, and median nerves run close to bone."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-upper-extremity-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-upper-extremity","comp":"w2-bone-markings-vocab","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A long tendon must slide back and forth along the surface of a bone while being kept on track. Which kind of bone marking is best suited to hold it, and which humeral marking is an example?","options":["A tuberosity, like the deltoid tuberosity","A fossa, like the olecranon fossa","A foramen, like the nutrient foramen","A sulcus, like the intertubercular sulcus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A sulcus is a groove, a depression shaped like a channel, so a tendon can lie in it and slide lengthwise without slipping off to the side. The intertubercular sulcus between the greater and lesser tubercles of the humerus is this kind of channel, and a tendon of the biceps brachii slides in it. A tuberosity is a rough projection where a tendon anchors rather than slides, a fossa is a shallow basin that receives part of another bone, and a foramen is a hole for vessels and nerves. Matching the shape of a marking to its job is how you classify it.","notes":["A tuberosity is a large, rough projection where a tendon attaches firmly, as the deltoid does at the deltoid tuberosity. It anchors a tendon in place rather than guiding one that slides.","A fossa is a shallow basin, and the olecranon fossa receives the olecranon of the ulna when the elbow straightens. It holds a bony projection, not a sliding tendon.","A foramen is an opening that lets vessels or nerves pass through bone, such as the nutrient artery through the nutrient foramen. A tendon runs along the surface of the bone, not through it.","Correct. A sulcus is a groove, and the intertubercular sulcus holds a biceps tendon as it slides."],"fix":"Review the bone markings vocabulary and sort each term into projection, depression, or opening with a humeral example."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-upper-extremity-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-upper-extremity","comp":"w2-pectoral-girdle","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A student holds a scapula in anatomical position and runs a finger down its lateral side, from the top of the shoulder toward the armpit. Rank these markings from most superior to most inferior: the glenoid cavity, the acromion, the infraglenoid tubercle, and the supraglenoid tubercle.","options":["Supraglenoid tubercle, acromion, glenoid cavity, infraglenoid tubercle","Acromion, supraglenoid tubercle, glenoid cavity, infraglenoid tubercle","Acromion, infraglenoid tubercle, glenoid cavity, supraglenoid tubercle","Acromion, glenoid cavity, supraglenoid tubercle, infraglenoid tubercle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The acromion is the flat process at the lateral tip of the scapular spine, forming the point of the shoulder, so it sits highest. The glenoid cavity, the socket for the humeral head, lies below the acromion. The prefixes place the two tubercles: supra- means above, so the supraglenoid tubercle sits at the upper edge of the socket, and infra- means below, so the infraglenoid tubercle sits at its lower edge. Reading top to bottom gives the acromion, the supraglenoid tubercle, the glenoid cavity, and then the infraglenoid tubercle.","notes":["The acromion forms the point of the shoulder, above the shoulder joint, so it is higher than any marking on the rim of the glenoid cavity. The supraglenoid tubercle sits just above the socket, below the acromion.","Correct. The acromion caps the shoulder, and the two tubercles sit above and below the glenoid cavity, just as their prefixes say.","This reverses the two tubercles. Supra- means above and infra- means below, so the supraglenoid tubercle belongs above the glenoid cavity and the infraglenoid tubercle below it.","The supraglenoid tubercle is named for sitting above the glenoid cavity, so it cannot come after the cavity in a top-to-bottom list."],"fix":"Review the markings of the scapula and use the prefixes supra- and infra- to place each tubercle around the glenoid cavity."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-upper-extremity-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-upper-extremity","comp":"w2-arm-forearm","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"After a forearm fracture, the radius and ulna heal joined by a bridge of bone across the interosseous membrane. The elbow joint itself is undamaged. Which result would you expect, and why?","options":["The palm can no longer turn up and down, but the elbow still bends and straightens","The elbow can no longer bend or straighten, but the palm still turns up and down","Both palm turning and elbow bending are lost, since the two bones now move as one","Neither motion is lost, because turning the palm happens at the wrist between carpals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Turning the palm down (pronation) and up (supination) happens when the radius rotates around the ulna at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints, with the interosseous membrane binding the shafts. A bridge of bone between the shafts locks the radius to the ulna, so the radius can no longer swing around it, and both palm movements are lost. Bending and straightening the elbow uses a different joint, the hinge formed by the trochlear notch of the ulna wrapped around the trochlea of the humerus. That hinge is intact, so the elbow still flexes and extends.","notes":["Correct. The bony bridge stops the radius from rotating around the ulna, but the hinge between the ulna and the humerus is untouched.","This reverses the effect. Elbow bending depends on the hinge between the trochlea and the trochlear notch, which was not damaged, while palm turning depends on the radius rotating around the ulna, which the bridge now blocks.","The two forearm bones now move as a unit, but that unit can still hinge on the humerus at the elbow. Only the rotation of the radius around the ulna is lost, so elbow bending remains.","Pronation and supination happen at the radioulnar joints, where the radius turns around the ulna, not between the carpals at the wrist. Locking the radius to the ulna removes that rotation."],"fix":"Review the three radioulnar joints, the interosseous membrane, and which markings form the hinge of the elbow."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-lower-extremity-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-lower-extremity","comp":"w2-foot-bones","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"When you stand on one foot, body weight passes from the thigh down into the heel. In what order does the weight pass through these structures?","options":["Femoral condyles, tibia, calcaneus, talus","Femoral condyles, talus, tibia, calcaneus","Tibia, femoral condyles, talus, calcaneus","Femoral condyles, tibia, talus, calcaneus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The condyles of the femur rest on the condyles of the tibia at the knee, so weight passes from the thigh into the tibia, the weight-bearing bone of the leg. At the ankle the tibia sits on the talus, the highest tarsal and the one that receives the body's weight from the leg. The calcaneus lies directly beneath the talus and forms the heel, so it receives the load next before it reaches the ground. The fibula is left out of the path because it does not carry body weight.","notes":["The tibia rests on the talus, not on the calcaneus. The calcaneus sits under the talus, so it receives weight only after the talus does.","The talus is a tarsal bone at the ankle, below the leg. The femur meets the tibia at the knee, so the tibia carries the weight before the talus.","Weight comes down from the thigh, so the femoral condyles hand the load to the tibia, not the other way around. This order would only make sense if force were traveling up from the leg.","Correct. Femur to tibia at the knee, tibia to talus at the ankle, then the talus down to the calcaneus in the heel."],"fix":"Review the tarsal bones, especially which one meets the tibia and which one forms the heel."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-lower-extremity-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-lower-extremity","comp":"w2-pelvic-girdle","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"An X-ray of a healthy 10-year-old's hip shows three bony pieces meeting at the hip socket, separated by dark lines. A student worries that the hip bone is broken. Which interpretation is best?","options":["The femoral head has split into three fragments sitting inside the acetabulum","The ilium, ischium, and pubis have not yet fused, and cartilage still joins them","The hip bone has a comminuted fracture, with three fragments around its socket","The lines are the sacroiliac joint and the pubic symphysis seen at an angle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each hip bone begins as three separate bones, the ilium, ischium, and pubis, which fuse at the acetabulum by early adulthood. In a 10-year-old they are still joined by cartilage. Cartilage is not mineralized, so on an X-ray it lets the beam pass and looks like a dark gap, just as the growth plate does in a long bone. Three pieces meeting at the socket with dark lines between them is the normal picture of a hip bone that has not finished fusing.","notes":["The femoral head is a single rounded ball that sits in the acetabulum. The pieces described here form the socket itself, and they meet at the acetabulum because that is where the three hip bones join.","Correct. The three hip bones fuse at the acetabulum only by early adulthood, and the cartilage between them looks dark on an X-ray.","A comminuted fracture does have three or more fragments, which is why the X-ray can look alarming. In a healthy child, though, three pieces meeting neatly at the socket with dark cartilage between them is the expected pattern before fusion.","The sacroiliac joint is at the back of the pelvis, where the ilium meets the sacrum, and the pubic symphysis is at the front midline. Neither lies at the hip socket, so neither can explain lines meeting at the acetabulum."],"fix":"Review the three bones that fuse to form the hip bone, where they meet, and why cartilage looks dark on an X-ray."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-lower-extremity-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-lower-extremity","comp":"w2-leg-bones","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A surgeon needs a straight piece of bone for a graft and plans to take it from the leg. Which site is the best choice if the patient must still be able to bear weight and keep a stable ankle joint?","options":["The lateral malleolus of the fibula","The middle of the tibial shaft","The middle of the fibular shaft","The medial malleolus of the tibia"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tibia carries the body's weight from the femur to the talus, so taking bone from it would put standing at risk. The fibula does not bear body weight, which is why a length of it can be harvested. Its distal end still matters, though: the lateral malleolus forms the outer wall of the socket that holds the talus at the ankle, and the medial malleolus of the tibia forms the inner wall. Taking bone from the middle of the fibular shaft spares both weight bearing and the ankle socket.","notes":["The fibula is the right bone, but the lateral malleolus forms the outer wall of the ankle socket around the talus. Removing it would leave the ankle unstable.","The tibia is the weight-bearing bone of the leg, carrying the load from the femur to the talus. Taking a length of its shaft would make standing unsafe.","Correct. The fibula bears no body weight, and its shaft lies away from the ankle socket that the malleoli form.","The medial malleolus is part of the weight-bearing tibia and forms the inner wall of the ankle socket. Removing it would weaken both weight bearing and ankle stability."],"fix":"Review which leg bone bears weight and how the two malleoli form the ankle socket around the talus."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-lower-extremity-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-lower-extremity","comp":"w2-thigh-knee","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"An 80-year-old with thinned bones falls and breaks the narrow, angled part of the femur just below the ball that sits in the hip socket. Why does the surgeon worry that the ball itself might die even though it was not broken?","options":["The vessels to the head run close to the neck, so the break can cut off its blood","Articular cartilage covers the head, and cartilage has no blood vessels of its own","The small artery in the ligament at the fovea capitis is the head's only blood supply","The head is spongy bone, which cannot heal without osteons and their central canals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The narrow, angled part below the head is the femoral neck, the usual site of a broken hip in older adults whose bone has thinned. The main blood supply to the femoral head runs close to the neck, so a fracture there can tear those vessels. The head itself was not broken, but its bone cells cannot survive without blood, so the head is at risk even though the break is in the neck.","notes":["Correct. The blood supply to the femoral head runs right along the neck, so a neck fracture can cut it off.","Articular cartilage is avascular, but it only caps the joint surface. The bone of the head beneath it does need blood, and losing that supply is what threatens the head.","A small artery does travel in the ligament from the fovea capitis to the acetabulum, but it is not the head's main supply. The larger vessels run along the neck, which is why a neck fracture is the danger.","Spongy bone has no osteons, but its trabeculae are fed from the marrow spaces around them. The problem here is not the type of bone in the head but the vessels torn at the neck."],"fix":"Review the markings of the proximal femur and why a fracture of the femoral neck can threaten the femoral head."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-lower-extremity-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-lower-extremity","comp":"w2-thigh-knee","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these femoral markings from most proximal to most distal: the rough ridge for thigh muscles on the posterior shaft, the narrow region where most broken hips occur, the notch between the condyles where the cruciate ligaments attach, and the small rounded projection on the medial side of the upper femur.","options":["Lesser trochanter, neck, linea aspera, intercondylar fossa","Neck, lesser trochanter, linea aspera, intercondylar fossa","Neck, linea aspera, lesser trochanter, intercondylar fossa","Neck, lesser trochanter, intercondylar fossa, linea aspera"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The four descriptions name the neck, the lesser trochanter, the linea aspera, and the intercondylar fossa. The neck sits just below the head at the top of the femur, and the lesser trochanter projects from the medial side just below the neck. The linea aspera runs down the posterior shaft through the middle of the bone. The intercondylar fossa is the notch between the condyles at the distal end, at the knee.","notes":["The lesser trochanter sits just below the neck, so the neck is more proximal. The neck connects the head to the rest of the femur, right under the ball.","Correct. Neck, lesser trochanter, the ridge on the shaft, then the notch at the knee runs from the hip end to the knee end.","The linea aspera runs along the shaft, which begins below the trochanters. The lesser trochanter is at the proximal end, so it comes before the ridge on the shaft.","The intercondylar fossa is between the condyles at the distal end, at the knee. The linea aspera is on the shaft above it, so the fossa is the most distal of the four."],"fix":"Review the markings of the femur from the head down to the condyles."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-lower-extremity-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-lower-extremity","comp":"w2-bone-markings-vocab","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"In life, the large opening in the lower hip bone framed by the ischium and the pubis is mostly closed over by a membrane, with a small gap left near its upper edge. Using what the word foramen means, what would you predict passes through that gap?","options":["The sciatic nerve heading to the gluteal region","A tendon that glides back and forth to the knee","Blood vessels and a nerve heading to the thigh","The ligament that anchors the head of the femur"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The opening is the obturator foramen, the largest opening in the skeleton. A foramen is a hole that lets vessels or nerves pass through bone, so the gap left in its covering membrane is a passage for exactly those structures. Vessels and a nerve pass through it from the pelvis to reach the thigh. Classifying a marking as an opening tells you to expect vessels or nerves, while a groove would guide a sliding tendon.","notes":["The sciatic nerve does leave the pelvis, but it passes through the greater sciatic notch on the posterior border of the ilium. It would be the right structure for a question about the deep notch at the back of the hip bone.","Tendons that slide are guided by grooves, the sulcus type of depression, such as the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus. A foramen is an opening for vessels and nerves, not a track for a moving tendon.","Correct. A foramen is an opening for vessels and nerves, and the gap in the obturator foramen lets vessels and a nerve reach the thigh.","The ligament of the femoral head runs from the fovea capitis, a small pit on the head, to the acetabulum. It stays inside the hip socket and does not pass through the obturator foramen."],"fix":"Review the bone markings vocabulary and the openings and notches of the hip bone."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-joints-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-joints","comp":"w2-joint-classification","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these adult joints from least movable to most movable: the glenohumeral joint, the sagittal suture, the elbow joint between the humerus and ulna, and the pubic symphysis.","options":["Sagittal suture, pubic symphysis, elbow, glenohumeral joint","Pubic symphysis, sagittal suture, elbow, glenohumeral joint","Sagittal suture, pubic symphysis, glenohumeral joint, elbow","Sagittal suture, elbow, pubic symphysis, glenohumeral joint"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The functional classes make the first cut. An adult suture is immovable, a synarthrosis, so the sagittal suture comes first. The pubic symphysis is a cartilaginous joint joined by a fibrocartilage disc and is slightly movable, an amphiarthrosis. The elbow and the glenohumeral joint are both synovial joints and both diarthroses, so the tie breaks on joint type: the elbow is a hinge that moves around one axis, while the glenohumeral joint is a ball-and-socket joint that moves around three and is the most freely movable joint in the body.","notes":["Correct. An immovable suture, a slightly movable symphysis, then a one-axis hinge and a three-axis ball-and-socket joint.","An adult suture is immovable, while the pubic symphysis gives slightly. The suture is the less movable of the two, so it belongs first.","Both are freely movable synovial joints, but a hinge moves around only one axis. The ball-and-socket glenohumeral joint moves around three, so it is the more movable and belongs last.","The elbow is a synovial joint and a diarthrosis, so it is freely movable. The pubic symphysis is only slightly movable, so it comes before the elbow."],"fix":"Review the three functional classes of joints and how the number of axes compares across synovial joint types."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-joints-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-joints","comp":"w2-synovial-types","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"Severe arthritis destroys the joint between the trapezium and the first metacarpal, and a surgeon fuses it. Which action would the patient find hardest afterward?","options":["Bending the fingertips at the joints between phalanges","Turning the palm up to hold a bowl of soup in one hand","Bending the wrist forward toward the front of the forearm","Touching the thumb tip to the tip of the little finger"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The trapezium and the first metacarpal meet at the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb, a saddle joint. In a saddle joint each surface is both concave and convex, which allows movement around two axes, and that is what lets the thumb swing across the palm. Opposition, touching the thumb tip to the other fingertips, depends on this joint. Fusing it removes that swing, while the finger hinges, the radioulnar joints, and the wrist are unaffected.","notes":["Bending the fingertips happens at the interphalangeal joints, which are hinge joints between phalanges. They are separate from the thumb's carpometacarpal joint and would still work.","Turning the palm up is supination, which happens as the radius rotates around the ulna at the radioulnar joints. That rotation is in the forearm, not at the base of the thumb.","Bending the wrist forward is flexion at the radiocarpal joint, a condyloid joint between the radius and the proximal carpals. The fused joint lies farther out, between a distal-row carpal and the thumb's metacarpal.","Correct. Opposition happens at the saddle joint between the trapezium and the first metacarpal."],"fix":"Review the six synovial joint types with a body example of each, especially the saddle joint of the thumb."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-joints-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-joints","comp":"w2-shoulder-knee","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A basketball player lands from a jump and twists his knee. It swells within minutes, and when the examiner pulls the tibia forward with the knee bent, it slides much farther than on the other side. Which conclusion best fits both findings?","options":["A torn posterior cruciate ligament, with blood from torn vessels in the joint","A torn anterior cruciate ligament, with blood from torn vessels in the joint","A torn anterior cruciate ligament, with excess synovial fluid in the joint","A torn medial meniscus, with synovial fluid building up in the joint"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The anterior cruciate ligament crosses inside the knee and stops the tibia from sliding forward under the femur, so a tibia that slides forward too far means that ligament has failed. The timing of the swelling is the second clue. Swelling within minutes is blood leaking from torn vessels, while excess synovial fluid builds up more slowly, over hours, as the irritated synovial membrane reacts. Together the two findings point to a torn anterior cruciate ligament with bleeding into the joint.","notes":["The posterior cruciate ligament stops the tibia from sliding backward, not forward. It would fit if the tibia sagged backward under the femur.","Correct. Forward sliding of the tibia means the anterior cruciate ligament is torn, and swelling within minutes means blood.","The ligament is right, but the cause of the swelling is not. Excess synovial fluid builds up over hours, so swelling within minutes points to blood from torn vessels.","A torn meniscus does not let the tibia slide forward, because the cruciate ligaments control that motion. The swelling also came within minutes, which points to blood rather than slowly gathering synovial fluid."],"fix":"Review the cruciate ligaments of the knee, the direction each one controls, and what immediate and delayed swelling each mean."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-joints-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-joints","comp":"w2-synovial-structure","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"To drain fluid from a swollen knee, a clinician passes a needle through the side of the joint toward the end of the femur. After the skin, in what order would the needle meet these layers if it went all the way to the bone?","options":["Synovial membrane, fibrous membrane, synovial cavity, articular cartilage","Fibrous membrane, synovial cavity, synovial membrane, articular cartilage","Fibrous membrane, synovial membrane, synovial cavity, articular cartilage","Fibrous membrane, synovial membrane, articular cartilage, synovial cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The articular capsule has two layers. Its outer layer is the fibrous membrane, dense irregular connective tissue attached to the periosteum, and its inner layer is the synovial membrane, which lines the cavity and secretes synovial fluid. Inside the capsule is the synovial cavity, the fluid-filled space the clinician is trying to drain. Across the cavity, the bone end is capped by hyaline articular cartilage, so that is the last layer before bone.","notes":["This reverses the two layers of the capsule. The fibrous membrane is the tough outer layer, and the synovial membrane is the inner lining that faces the cavity.","The synovial membrane lines the cavity as the inner layer of the capsule. The needle has to pass through that lining before it can enter the cavity.","Correct. The outer fibrous membrane, the inner synovial membrane, the fluid-filled cavity, then the cartilage on the bone end.","Articular cartilage covers the bone ends on the far side of the cavity. It is not part of the capsule wall, so the needle crosses the cavity first and meets the cartilage last."],"fix":"Review the two layers of the articular capsule and where the synovial cavity and articular cartilage sit."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-joints-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-joints","comp":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"Severe gum disease breaks down the ligament that holds a tooth's root in its bony socket, and the tooth becomes loose. Which joint is failing, and how is that joint normally classified?","options":["A syndesmosis, a fibrous joint that is normally slightly movable","A synchondrosis, a cartilaginous joint that is normally immovable","A suture, a fibrous joint that is normally immovable in adults","A gomphosis, a fibrous joint that is normally immovable"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A tooth sits in its socket as a cone-shaped peg held by the periodontal ligament, which makes the joint a gomphosis. Dense connective tissue holds the root in place with no cavity, so it is a fibrous joint, and in a healthy mouth it permits no movement, so it is a synarthrosis. When disease destroys the periodontal ligament, nothing holds the peg firmly, which is why the tooth loosens.","notes":["A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint whose bones are united by a ligament across a wider space, like the distal tibiofibular joint. It is slightly movable and joins two bones, not a tooth to its socket.","A synchondrosis is joined by hyaline cartilage, as at the epiphyseal growth plate. A tooth is held by a ligament, not by cartilage, so this is the wrong structural class.","A suture is also a fibrous joint that is immovable in adults, but sutures are found only between the bones of the skull, joined along interlocking edges. A peg held in a socket by a ligament is the gomphosis pattern.","Correct. A tooth in its socket is a gomphosis, a fibrous joint held by the periodontal ligament and normally immovable."],"fix":"Review the fibrous joint types and the two cartilaginous joint types with an example of each."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m2-joints-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m2-joints","comp":"w2-joint-movements","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A soccer player lands on another player's foot, and her own foot is forced so the sole turns sharply toward her other foot. Which movement was forced, and which side of the ankle is most likely injured?","options":["Eversion, injuring the lateral side of the ankle","Inversion, injuring the medial side of the ankle","Inversion, injuring the lateral side of the ankle","Plantar flexion, injuring the front of the ankle"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Turning the sole toward the other foot, medially, is inversion. When the sole turns inward, the outer edge of the foot and ankle is stretched, so the structures on the lateral side take the strain. That is why rolled ankles usually injure the lateral side, including the lateral malleolus of the fibula and the ligaments around it. Eversion is the opposite movement and would stress the medial side instead.","notes":["Eversion turns the sole outward, away from the other foot, which is the opposite of what happened here. Forced eversion would also stretch the medial side rather than the lateral side.","The movement is named correctly, but the side is reversed. Inversion squeezes the medial side together and stretches the lateral side, so the lateral side is the one injured.","Correct. The sole turning inward is inversion, which stretches and injures the lateral side of the ankle.","Plantar flexion points the toes down, a front-to-back movement at the ankle hinge. The sole turning toward the other foot is a side-to-side movement, so plantar flexion does not describe it."],"fix":"Review the special movements of the foot, especially inversion and eversion, and why a rolled ankle injures the lateral side."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-structure-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-structure","comp":"cv-surfaces","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A knife enters the front of the chest just left of the sternum and passes all the way into a ventricle. Rank these layers from the first one the blade passes through to the last.","options":["Fibrous pericardium, myocardium, pericardial cavity, endocardium","Pericardial cavity, fibrous pericardium, myocardium, endocardium","Fibrous pericardium, pericardial cavity, myocardium, endocardium","Fibrous pericardium, pericardial cavity, endocardium, myocardium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The fibrous pericardium is the tough outer sac around the heart, so the blade meets it first. The parietal layer of the serous pericardium lines the inside of that sac, and just inside it is the pericardial cavity, the thin fluid-filled space that separates the sac from the heart. Across the cavity the blade reaches the heart wall itself: the epicardium on the surface, then the thick myocardium, then the endocardium that lines the chamber. Reaching the blood inside a ventricle means the endocardium is the last layer it crosses.","notes":["This order puts the myocardium outside the pericardial cavity, but the myocardium is part of the heart wall, which sits inside the cavity. Students pick this when they picture the cavity as a space inside the heart wall rather than around it.","The pericardial cavity lies inside the fibrous pericardium, so the blade cannot reach the cavity before passing the sac. This order would only work if the cavity sat outside the sac, which it never does.","Correct. The blade passes the fibrous sac, crosses the pericardial cavity, then goes through the heart wall from the outside in, ending at the endocardium that lines the chamber.","This order has the endocardium outside the myocardium, but the endocardium is the innermost layer that lines the chamber. The prefixes help here: endo means within, and myo means muscle, the thick middle layer."],"fix":"Review the layers around and of the heart from outside in, and where the pericardial cavity sits among them."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-structure-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-structure","comp":"cv-surfaces","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"In pericarditis, the serous pericardium becomes inflamed and rough, and the film of fluid between its layers no longer lets them glide. With each heartbeat, a clinician hears a scratchy rubbing sound through the stethoscope. Which two surfaces are rubbing against each other?","options":["The parietal serous layer and the epicardium","The fibrous pericardium and the parietal serous layer","The epicardium and the myocardium","The myocardium and the endocardium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A rub comes from two surfaces that normally slide past each other across a fluid-filled space. The only such space here is the pericardial cavity, which lies between the parietal layer of the serous pericardium (lining the fibrous sac) and the visceral layer (lying on the heart). The visceral layer is the same membrane as the epicardium, the outer layer of the heart wall. So when the fluid film fails and the layers roughen, it is the parietal serous layer and the epicardium that rub with every beat.","notes":["Correct. These two surfaces face each other across the pericardial cavity, and the visceral layer that lies on the heart is the epicardium.","The parietal serous layer is fused to the inner surface of the fibrous pericardium, so the two move together as one sac and never slide on each other. Students choose this when they forget that the cavity lies inside the parietal layer, not outside it.","The epicardium sits directly on the myocardium as part of the same heart wall, with no fluid-filled space between them. Layers that are attached to each other cannot rub.","The endocardium lines the inside of the myocardium and faces the blood in the chambers, not another membrane. A problem here, such as endocarditis, affects the chamber lining and valves rather than causing a rub on the heart surface."],"fix":"Review the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium, which space lies between them, and which heart wall layer is the visceral layer."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-structure-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-structure","comp":"cv-chambers","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Four patients each have damage to one part of the heart. Which patient is most likely to have a lasting drop in the amount of blood pumped out to the body?","options":["A large area of dead myocardium in the right atrium","A patch of inflamed epicardium over the left ventricle","A small opening left where the foramen ovale was","A large area of dead myocardium in the left ventricle"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Three ideas decide this. Pumping blood out to the body is the job of the left ventricle, which sends blood through the aortic valve into the aorta and the systemic circuit. The layer of the wall that actually contracts is the myocardium, so dead myocardium in the left ventricle directly weakens that pump. Cardiac muscle cells divide very little, so the dead area is replaced by scar tissue rather than new muscle, which is why the loss lasts. The other choices either involve a chamber that only tops off a ventricle or a structure that does not supply the force of contraction.","notes":["The right atrium is a thin-walled receiving chamber that only pushes blood a short distance into the right ventricle. Losing atrial muscle trims filling a little, but it does not remove the main pump that sends blood to the body.","The epicardium is the thin serous membrane on the heart surface, and it does not contract. Inflammation here can cause pain and friction, but it does not take away any pumping muscle.","An opening where the foramen ovale was lets some blood pass between the two atria. It mixes blood that should stay separate, but it does not remove any of the muscle that pumps blood out to the body.","Correct. The left ventricle pumps to the body, the myocardium is the layer that contracts, and dead cardiac muscle heals as scar, so the loss of pumping force lasts."],"fix":"Review which chamber pumps blood to the body, which heart wall layer contracts, and how cardiac muscle heals after it dies."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-structure-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-structure","comp":"cv-blood-pathway","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A drop of blood enters the heart from the superior vena cava and travels until it leaves the heart for the body. In what order does it pass these internal features?","options":["Trabeculae carneae of the right ventricle, sinus venarum, conus arteriosus, aortic vestibule","Sinus venarum, trabeculae carneae of the right ventricle, conus arteriosus, aortic vestibule","Sinus venarum, conus arteriosus, trabeculae carneae of the right ventricle, aortic vestibule","Sinus venarum, trabeculae carneae of the right ventricle, aortic vestibule, conus arteriosus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Place each feature in its chamber first, then follow the blood. The sinus venarum is the smooth posterior part of the right atrium where the venae cavae empty, so the drop meets it first. It crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, whose inner walls are covered in trabeculae carneae, and then rises through the conus arteriosus, the smooth outflow tract that leads to the pulmonary valve. After the lungs, the left atrium and the left ventricle, it passes through the aortic vestibule, the smooth outflow tract of the left ventricle, just before the aortic valve.","notes":["This order starts in the right ventricle, but blood from the superior vena cava empties into the right atrium first. The sinus venarum is the part of the right atrium where the venae cavae open, so it has to come before any ventricular feature.","Correct. The drop goes from the sinus venarum of the right atrium, into the ridged right ventricle, out through its smooth conus arteriosus, and much later through the aortic vestibule of the left ventricle.","The conus arteriosus is the outflow tract of the right ventricle, the smooth funnel blood climbs on its way out to the pulmonary valve. Blood fills the ridged main part of the chamber first, so the trabeculae carneae come before the conus.","The aortic vestibule belongs to the left ventricle, which blood reaches only after the lungs. The conus arteriosus is in the right ventricle, so it comes earlier in the trip, not last."],"fix":"Review the internal features of each chamber, including the sinus venarum, trabeculae carneae, conus arteriosus and aortic vestibule, and where each sits along the path of blood."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-structure-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-structure","comp":"bvn-great-vessels","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"During a heart dissection, a student identifies the chamber that forms most of the base of the heart. Which vessels open into this chamber, and which circuit do they complete?","options":["The superior and inferior venae cavae, completing the systemic circuit","The four pulmonary veins, completing the pulmonary circuit","The four pulmonary veins, completing the systemic circuit","The pulmonary trunk, completing the pulmonary circuit"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The base of the heart is its broad superior surface, and it is formed mostly by the left atrium. The left atrium receives the four pulmonary veins, which bring oxygen-rich blood back from the lungs. Blood that left the right ventricle for the lungs returns to the heart through these veins, so they complete the pulmonary circuit. From the left atrium the blood starts the systemic circuit by passing through the mitral valve into the left ventricle.","notes":["The venae cavae do complete the systemic circuit, but they empty into the right atrium, not the chamber that forms the base. This would be the answer if the question asked about the right atrium.","Correct. The left atrium forms most of the base, and its four pulmonary veins bring blood back from the lungs to finish the pulmonary circuit.","The vessels are right, but the circuit is not. The pulmonary veins return blood from the lungs, so they finish the loop to the lungs and back, which is the pulmonary circuit.","The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle and begins the pulmonary circuit rather than completing it. It also carries blood away from the heart, while the chamber at the base receives blood."],"fix":"Review which chamber forms the base of the heart, which vessels enter it, and which circuit each great vessel serves."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-structure-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-structure","comp":"cv-cardiac-muscle","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A pathologist takes a sample from the layer that makes up about 95 percent of the thickness of the heart wall. Which description fits what she sees under the microscope?","options":["Long, unbranched striated cells with many nuclei pushed to the edges","Spindle-shaped cells without striations, each with one central nucleus","A single layer of flat cells resting on a basement membrane","Short, branched striated cells joined end to end by dark discs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The layer that makes up about 95 percent of the heart wall is the myocardium, and the myocardium is built of cardiac muscle tissue. Cardiac muscle cells are short and branched, striated like skeletal muscle, and usually have one central nucleus. The dark lines crossing the fibers are intercalated discs, where neighboring cells join end to end, and they are the feature that identifies cardiac muscle on a slide. Desmosomes in the discs hold the cells together, and gap junctions pass the signal from cell to cell.","notes":["Long, unbranched, multinucleated cells with nuclei at the edges describe skeletal muscle. This would be the answer for a sample taken from a limb muscle, not the heart wall.","Spindle-shaped cells with no striations describe smooth muscle, the tissue in the tunica media of blood vessels and the walls of hollow organs. Cardiac muscle is striated, so this does not fit the myocardium.","A single layer of flat cells on a basement membrane is an endothelium, the smooth lining that forms the inner surface of the endocardium. This would be the answer if the sample came from the thin innermost layer that lines the chambers.","Correct. The thickest layer is the myocardium, and short, branched, striated cells joined by intercalated discs are the signature of cardiac muscle."],"fix":"Review the three heart wall layers and the features that identify cardiac muscle tissue on a slide."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-valves-flow-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-valves-flow","comp":"cv-blood-pathway","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A clot breaks loose from a deep vein in a patient's calf and travels with the blood until it lodges in a lung. In what order does it pass these structures?","options":["Inferior vena cava, tricuspid valve, pulmonary valve, pulmonary artery","Inferior vena cava, pulmonary valve, tricuspid valve, pulmonary artery","Tricuspid valve, inferior vena cava, pulmonary valve, pulmonary artery","Inferior vena cava, tricuspid valve, pulmonary artery, pulmonary valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A clot in a vein simply follows normal venous return. The veins of the lower limb drain into the inferior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium. From there the clot crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, which pumps it through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk and on into a pulmonary artery. The pulmonary arteries branch into narrower and narrower vessels in the lung, so the clot lodges there as a pulmonary embolism before it can ever reach the left side of the heart.","notes":["Correct. The clot rides the inferior vena cava to the right atrium, crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, then leaves through the pulmonary valve toward a pulmonary artery.","This order swaps the two right-side valves. The tricuspid valve is the entrance to the right ventricle and the pulmonary valve is its exit, so the clot must cross the tricuspid valve first.","The tricuspid valve lies between the right atrium and right ventricle, and the inferior vena cava delivers blood to the right atrium. The clot has to arrive through the vena cava before it can reach any valve.","The pulmonary valve guards the exit from the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk, so it comes before the pulmonary arteries. Students pick this when they forget that the arteries lie beyond the valve, outside the heart."],"fix":"Review the path of blood from the venae cavae through the right side of the heart to the lungs, valve by valve."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-valves-flow-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-valves-flow","comp":"bvn-great-vessels","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient's tricuspid valve cannot close. Each time the right ventricle contracts, the veins in the patient's neck bulge with a visible pulse. Which path does the backward-moving blood take to reach the neck?","options":["Right ventricle, left atrium, pulmonary veins, neck veins","Right ventricle, pulmonary trunk, aorta, neck arteries","Right ventricle, right atrium, superior vena cava, neck veins","Right ventricle, right atrium, inferior vena cava, neck veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tricuspid valve sits between the right atrium and the right ventricle and normally snaps shut when the right ventricle contracts. If it cannot close, part of every contraction pushes blood backward into the right atrium. The right atrium is fed from above by the superior vena cava, which drains the head, neck and upper limbs, so the backward surge travels up that vessel into the neck veins. The inferior vena cava drains the trunk and lower limbs, so a surge up it would head toward the abdomen and legs, not the neck.","notes":["The right ventricle has no opening into the left atrium, because the septa keep the two sides separate. The pulmonary veins would carry a backward surge only if the mitral valve, on the left side, were failing.","This path is forward flow, not backward flow, and the right ventricle does not connect to the aorta. A leak through the tricuspid valve sends blood back into the right atrium instead.","Correct. Blood leaks back through the open tricuspid valve into the right atrium, then up the superior vena cava, which drains the neck.","The first steps are right, but the inferior vena cava drains the trunk and lower limbs. Blood surging back up it would move toward the abdomen and legs, so it cannot explain pulsing veins in the neck."],"fix":"Review which valve guards each chamber and which regions the superior and inferior venae cavae drain into the right atrium."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-valves-flow-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-valves-flow","comp":"cv-chambers","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Four patients each have one valve problem, described by how the valve is built. Which problem would most directly reduce how much blood the left ventricle receives before each contraction?","options":["A narrowed valve with three half-moon cusps at the base of the aorta","A narrowed valve with two cusps tied down by chordae tendineae","A leaking valve with three half-moon cusps at the base of the aorta","A narrowed valve with three cusps tied down by chordae tendineae"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"First identify each valve from its description. Half-moon cusps at the base of the aorta mark the aortic valve, two cusps tied by chordae tendineae mark the mitral valve, and three cusps with chordae mark the tricuspid valve. The left ventricle receives its blood from the left atrium through the mitral valve, so the mitral valve is the doorway into that chamber. A narrowed (stenotic) mitral valve lets less blood through, so the left ventricle does not fill normally and blood backs up in the left atrium. The aortic valve is the exit of the left ventricle, so a problem there changes what leaves the chamber rather than what enters it, and the tricuspid valve is on the right side, so it reaches the left ventricle only indirectly, after the lungs.","notes":["Half-moon cusps at the base of the aorta make this the aortic valve, the exit of the left ventricle. Narrowing it makes the ventricle push harder to empty, but it does not directly limit how much blood flows in.","Correct. Two cusps tied by chordae tendineae identify the mitral valve, the entrance to the left ventricle, so narrowing it directly cuts the blood that can flow in from the left atrium.","This is the aortic valve again, but leaking instead of narrowed. A leaking aortic valve lets blood fall back from the aorta into the left ventricle as it relaxes, which adds blood to the chamber rather than reducing it.","Three cusps tied by chordae tendineae identify the tricuspid valve, which limits filling of the right ventricle. It would reduce left ventricle filling only indirectly, after less blood made it through the lungs."],"fix":"Review how to tell each valve apart by its cusps and supports, and which valve is the entrance and which is the exit of each ventricle."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-valves-flow-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-valves-flow","comp":"cv-valves","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A red blood cell leaves the lungs in a pulmonary vein and travels once around the body until it next reaches the lungs. Rank the four heart valves in the order it crosses them.","options":["Tricuspid, pulmonary, mitral, aortic","Mitral, aortic, pulmonary, tricuspid","Mitral, tricuspid, aortic, pulmonary","Mitral, aortic, tricuspid, pulmonary"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Starting in a pulmonary vein puts the cell on the left side of the heart. It enters the left atrium, crosses the mitral valve into the left ventricle, and leaves through the aortic valve into the systemic circuit. After returning through the venae cavae to the right atrium, it crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle and leaves through the pulmonary valve toward the lungs. On each side the atrioventricular valve comes before the semilunar valve, because blood always enters a ventricle before it leaves it.","notes":["This is the correct order for a cell that starts in the venae cavae, not in a pulmonary vein. A cell coming from the lungs enters the left atrium first, so the left-side valves come first.","The left side is right, but the right side is reversed. The pulmonary valve is the exit of the right ventricle and the tricuspid valve is its entrance, so the tricuspid valve must come first.","This order jumps from the left ventricle to the right side and back again, but the septa keep the two sides apart. Blood that crosses the mitral valve must leave through the aortic valve before it can reach the right heart.","Correct. The cell crosses the left atrioventricular and semilunar valves, travels the body, then crosses the right atrioventricular and semilunar valves on its way back to the lungs."],"fix":"Review the four valves in the order blood meets them, and remember that each side has an entrance valve and then an exit valve."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-valves-flow-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-valves-flow","comp":"cv-valve-support","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Days after a heart attack in the left ventricle, a patient develops a new murmur. An echocardiogram shows blood leaking from the left ventricle back into the left atrium with each contraction, yet the mitral cusps and the chordae tendineae themselves look normal. Which damaged structure best explains the leak?","options":["A papillary muscle in the left ventricle","A papillary muscle in the right ventricle","The cusps of the aortic valve","The endocardium lining the left atrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood moving from the left ventricle into the left atrium during contraction means the mitral valve is not staying shut. The mitral cusps are held closed by the chordae tendineae, and the chordae are anchored to papillary muscles, which are projections of the ventricle's own myocardium. A heart attack kills myocardium whose coronary supply is blocked, so a papillary muscle in the left ventricle can die even when the cusps and cords are intact. With its anchor gone, the cords go slack and the mitral cusps swing back into the left atrium with each contraction.","notes":["Correct. The papillary muscles are ventricular myocardium fed by the coronary arteries, and when one dies the chordae lose their anchor and the mitral valve leaks.","Papillary muscles in the right ventricle anchor the tricuspid valve, so their failure would leak blood into the right atrium. The heart attack and the leak are both on the left side.","A failing aortic valve lets blood fall back from the aorta into the left ventricle as the ventricle relaxes. The leak described runs the other way, from ventricle to atrium during contraction.","The endocardium of the left atrium lines the receiving chamber and has no role in holding the mitral valve shut. The structures that keep an atrioventricular valve closed sit in the ventricle below it."],"fix":"Review how the chordae tendineae and papillary muscles hold each atrioventricular valve shut, and why papillary muscles depend on the coronary arteries."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-heart-valves-flow-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-heart-valves-flow","comp":"cv-surfaces","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"Bacteria in the bloodstream settle on a cusp of the mitral valve and roughen it, so the cusp no longer seals cleanly. Which heart wall layer is infected, and where will blood leak when the left ventricle contracts?","options":["The epicardium; blood leaks into the left atrium","The endocardium; blood leaks back from the aorta","The endocardium; blood leaks into the left atrium","The myocardium; blood leaks into the left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The valve cusps are covered on both surfaces by endocardium, the smooth endothelium that also lines the chambers, so an infection on a cusp is an infection of the endocardium (endocarditis). The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and the left ventricle and should close when the ventricle contracts. A roughened cusp that cannot seal lets part of each contraction push blood back into the left atrium. Backflow from the aorta would point to the aortic valve instead, and it would happen as the ventricle relaxes, not as it contracts.","notes":["The leak is right, but the layer is not. The epicardium is the thin membrane on the outer surface of the heart, and it never touches the valves.","The layer is right, but this leak describes a failing aortic valve, which lets blood fall back as the ventricle relaxes. A mitral cusp that cannot seal leaks into the left atrium during contraction.","Correct. The endocardium covers the valve cusps, and a mitral valve that cannot seal lets blood push back into the left atrium when the ventricle contracts.","The leak is right, but the myocardium is the muscle layer of the wall and does not cover the valves. Students choose it because the myocardium is the layer that dies in a heart attack, but an infection on a valve is on the lining, not the muscle."],"fix":"Review which heart wall layer covers the valves and which chambers the mitral valve separates."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-coronary-great-vessels-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-coronary-great-vessels","comp":"cv-coronary","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A dye is injected into the left coronary artery and followed as it supplies the front of the left ventricle and then drains away. In what order does the dye pass these vessels?","options":["Great cardiac vein, myocardial capillaries, anterior interventricular artery, coronary sinus","Anterior interventricular artery, great cardiac vein, myocardial capillaries, coronary sinus","Anterior interventricular artery, myocardial capillaries, coronary sinus, great cardiac vein","Anterior interventricular artery, myocardial capillaries, great cardiac vein, coronary sinus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The left coronary artery branches into the anterior interventricular artery (the LAD), which runs down the front of the heart and supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle. The heart wall is supplied like any organ, by arteries, then capillaries, then veins, so the dye next enters the capillaries of the myocardium. The great cardiac vein runs beside the LAD in the same groove and collects that blood, then empties into the coronary sinus. The coronary sinus is the collecting vein for the cardiac veins and delivers their blood to the right atrium, so it always comes after them.","notes":["This order starts with a vein, but the dye was injected into an artery. Blood in the heart wall always moves from arteries to capillaries to veins, so the great cardiac vein cannot come first.","This order skips past the capillaries straight into a vein. Blood has to pass through the myocardial capillaries, where it supplies the muscle, before it can drain into the cardiac veins.","The coronary sinus collects blood from the cardiac veins, so the great cardiac vein must empty into it, not the other way around. Students pick this when they think of the sinus as a small vessel rather than the large collecting vein.","Correct. The dye follows the LAD, the myocardial capillaries, the great cardiac vein that runs with the LAD, and then the coronary sinus."],"fix":"Review the coronary circulation in order, from the aorta to the right atrium, and which cardiac vein runs beside each coronary artery."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-coronary-great-vessels-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-coronary-great-vessels","comp":"cv-surfaces","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon needs to bypass a blocked artery that supplies the anterior part of the interventricular septum and the apex of the heart. After she opens the pericardial sac, where will she find this artery?","options":["In the myocardium, deep along the interventricular septum","In the epicardium, in the groove between the two ventricles","In the epicardium, in the groove between atria and ventricles","In the endocardium, lining the inside of the left ventricle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Start by naming the artery from the region it feeds. The anterior septum and the apex are supplied by the anterior interventricular artery (the LAD), a branch of the left coronary artery. The coronary arteries run on the surface of the heart within the epicardium, the thin outer layer where fat and the coronary vessels lie. The LAD is named for its course: it runs down the front of the heart in the groove that marks the interventricular septum, between the right and left ventricles. The groove between the atria and the ventricles holds other vessels, such as the right coronary artery, the circumflex artery and the coronary sinus.","notes":["The LAD runs along the line of the interventricular septum, but on the surface of the heart, not buried inside the muscle. The coronary arteries travel in the epicardium and send branches inward to the myocardium.","Correct. The artery that feeds the anterior septum and apex is the LAD, it lies in the epicardium like the other coronary arteries, and it follows the groove between the two ventricles on the front of the heart.","The layer is right, but this groove separates the atria from the ventricles and holds vessels such as the right coronary artery, the circumflex artery and the coronary sinus. The LAD is in the groove between the ventricles.","The endocardium is the smooth endothelium lining the chambers, and it holds no named coronary arteries. Blood inside the chambers cannot feed the thick wall, which is why the arteries run on the outside."],"fix":"Review the region each coronary artery supplies, which heart wall layer the coronary vessels run in, and which surface groove each artery follows."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-coronary-great-vessels-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-coronary-great-vessels","comp":"cv-chambers","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Which single blockage puts the largest amount of the muscle that pumps blood to the body at risk?","options":["A clot in the anterior interventricular artery (LAD)","A clot in the right marginal artery near the right border","A clot in the posterior interventricular artery (PDA)","A clot in the small cardiac vein near the right border"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The muscle that pumps blood to the body is the myocardium of the left ventricle, the chamber that sends blood into the aorta. The LAD, a branch of the left coronary artery, supplies the anterior part of the interventricular septum, much of the front wall of the left ventricle and the apex, so a clot there starves a large share of that muscle. The posterior interventricular artery supplies the posterior septum and part of the left ventricle, a smaller area. The right marginal artery serves the right ventricle, which pumps to the lungs, and a cardiac vein carries blood away from the wall rather than supplying it.","notes":["Correct. The LAD feeds the anterior septum, much of the left ventricle and the apex, which is a large part of the systemic pump.","The right marginal artery runs along the right border of the heart and supplies part of the right ventricle. The right ventricle pumps to the lungs, not the body, and the area is small.","The posterior interventricular artery does supply part of the left ventricle and the posterior septum, so this is a serious blockage. Its territory is smaller than the LAD's, which also reaches the front wall and the apex.","The small cardiac vein drains blood away from the right border of the heart and does not deliver oxygen to any muscle. A blocked artery, not a vein, is what starves the myocardium."],"fix":"Review the regions each coronary artery supplies and which chamber pumps blood to the body."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-coronary-great-vessels-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-coronary-great-vessels","comp":"bvn-great-vessels","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"During surgery, a surgeon must briefly clamp the great vessel that carries oxygen-poor blood away from the heart. Which vessel is it, and which chamber is it attached to?","options":["The superior vena cava, joined to the right atrium","The aorta, joined to the left ventricle","The pulmonary trunk, joined to the right ventricle","The pulmonary veins, joined to the left atrium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Two clues identify the vessel. A vessel that carries blood away from the heart is an artery by definition, and the only great artery that carries oxygen-poor blood is the pulmonary trunk, which takes blood returned from the body toward the lungs. It leaves the right ventricle through the pulmonary valve and splits into the right and left pulmonary arteries. The venae cavae also carry oxygen-poor blood, but they carry it toward the heart, so they are veins.","notes":["The superior vena cava carries oxygen-poor blood, but it carries that blood toward the heart, into the right atrium. A vessel that carries blood toward the heart is a vein, so it does not match the clue about direction.","The aorta does carry blood away from the heart, but its blood is oxygen-rich, fresh from the lungs by way of the left heart. It would be the answer if the question asked about oxygen-rich blood.","Correct. The pulmonary trunk is the artery that carries oxygen-poor blood away from the right ventricle toward the lungs.","The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood toward the heart, into the left atrium, so they fail both clues. Students pick them when they mix up the pulmonary veins with the pulmonary arteries."],"fix":"Review the great vessels, which direction each carries blood, what kind of blood it holds, and which chamber it joins."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-coronary-great-vessels-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-coronary-great-vessels","comp":"cv-blood-pathway","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A red blood cell has just drained from the heart wall through the coronary sinus. Rank these vessels in the order the cell reaches them on its way back to supplying the heart wall.","options":["Aorta, pulmonary trunk, pulmonary veins, left coronary artery","Pulmonary veins, pulmonary trunk, aorta, left coronary artery","Pulmonary trunk, aorta, pulmonary veins, left coronary artery","Pulmonary trunk, pulmonary veins, aorta, left coronary artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The coronary sinus empties into the right atrium, so the cell starts on the right side of the heart with the rest of the oxygen-poor blood. The right ventricle pumps it into the pulmonary trunk toward the lungs, and after picking up oxygen it returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium. The left ventricle then pumps it into the aorta. The coronary arteries are the first branches off the base of the aorta, just above the aortic valve, so the left coronary artery comes last and puts the cell back in the heart wall.","notes":["This order sends the cell straight into the aorta, but the coronary sinus empties into the right atrium. Blood on the right side must go to the lungs before it can reach the aorta.","The pulmonary veins return blood from the lungs, so the cell cannot reach them before the pulmonary trunk carries it to the lungs. This order runs the pulmonary circuit backward.","This order puts the aorta between the pulmonary trunk and the pulmonary veins, as if blood went from the right ventricle straight to the aorta. That only happens through the ductus arteriosus before birth, not in the adult heart.","Correct. The cell goes from the right heart to the pulmonary trunk, returns in the pulmonary veins, leaves in the aorta, and enters the left coronary artery at the base of the aorta."],"fix":"Review where the coronary sinus empties, the full path of blood through both circuits, and where the coronary arteries branch off the aorta."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-coronary-great-vessels-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-coronary-great-vessels","comp":"bvn-fetal-remnants","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On an adult heart, a student finds a short fibrous cord connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aortic arch. What did this structure do before birth?","options":["Carried blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta, past the lungs","Let blood cross from the right atrium to the left atrium, past the lungs","Carried blood from the aorta into the pulmonary trunk, toward the lungs","Carried blood from the umbilical vein past the liver"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cord is the ligamentum arteriosum, the remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus, and it still joins the same two vessels. Before birth the lungs were collapsed and the placenta handled gas exchange, so there was little reason to send blood from the pulmonary trunk into the lungs. The ductus arteriosus gave that blood a shortcut into the aorta and out to the body. After birth the lungs take over, the duct closes, and only the fibrous cord remains.","notes":["Correct. The cord is the ligamentum arteriosum, left behind by the ductus arteriosus, which carried blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta so it could skip the collapsed fetal lungs.","This describes the foramen ovale, the other lung bypass. It was an opening in the interatrial septum, and it leaves a shallow depression called the fossa ovalis rather than a cord between two vessels.","The vessels are right, but the direction is reversed. The point of the fetal shunt was to send blood away from the collapsed lungs, so it carried blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta.","This describes the ductus venosus, which bypassed the liver and became the ligamentum venosum on the liver. It has nothing to do with the vessels at the base of the heart."],"fix":"Review the three fetal shunts, how each rerouted blood, and the adult remnant each one leaves behind."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-conduction-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-conduction","comp":"cv-conduction-anat","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"The signal for one heartbeat travels through four locations on its way to the working muscle of the ventricles. In what order does it pass them?","options":["Floor of the right atrium, right atrial wall near the superior vena cava, upper interventricular septum, ventricle walls","Right atrial wall near the superior vena cava, floor of the right atrium, upper interventricular septum, ventricle walls","Right atrial wall near the superior vena cava, upper interventricular septum, floor of the right atrium, ventricle walls","Right atrial wall near the superior vena cava, floor of the right atrium, ventricle walls, upper interventricular septum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Match each location to its structure, then follow the pathway. The SA node sits in the right atrial wall near the opening of the superior vena cava, and it fires first. The signal crosses the atria to the AV node in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum, where it pauses, and then enters the AV bundle in the upper interventricular septum. The bundle branches carry it down the septum, and the Purkinje fibers spread it through the walls of both ventricles last.","notes":["This order starts at the AV node, but the AV node only relays a signal the SA node has already started. The SA node, in the right atrial wall near the superior vena cava, always fires first.","Correct. The signal goes from the SA node to the AV node, into the AV bundle in the upper septum, and finally out through the Purkinje fibers in the ventricle walls.","This order puts the septum before the floor of the right atrium, which skips the AV node and comes back to it. The signal must pass the AV node in the atrial floor before it can enter the AV bundle in the septum.","The ventricle walls hold the Purkinje fibers, the last part of the pathway, so they cannot come before the septum. The AV bundle and bundle branches in the septum carry the signal down to the walls."],"fix":"Review where each conduction structure sits and the order the signal passes them from the SA node to the Purkinje fibers."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-conduction-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-conduction","comp":"cv-coronary","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A clot blocks a patient's right coronary artery. The SA node keeps firing normally, but the conduction tissue in the floor of the right atrium is starved of blood and stops working. What is the most likely result?","options":["The atria quiver, while the ventricles keep their normal rhythm","The atria stop contracting, because the AV node is where each beat starts","The ventricles quiver rapidly and stop moving blood","The atria keep their rhythm, but the ventricles beat late or out of step"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In most people the right coronary artery supplies both the SA node and the AV node, and the conduction tissue in the floor of the right atrium near the interatrial septum is the AV node. Because the fibrous skeleton insulates the atria from the ventricles, the AV node and AV bundle are the only route the signal can take to reach the ventricles. The SA node still fires and the atria still contract, but the signal is delayed or stopped on its way down. The ventricles then beat late, skip beats, or fall out of step with the atria, which is heart block.","notes":["Quivering atria describe atrial fibrillation, which comes from disorganized signals within the atria themselves. Here the SA node fires normally and the atria are fine; the problem is below them.","The beat starts at the SA node, not the AV node, and the SA node is still firing. The atria therefore keep contracting even though the signal cannot pass on normally.","Rapid quivering of the ventricles is ventricular fibrillation, caused by chaotic signals within the ventricles. A failing AV node delays or stops signals reaching the ventricles; it does not flood them with chaotic ones.","Correct. The right coronary artery usually feeds the AV node, which is the only gateway to the ventricles, so losing it leaves the atria on time and the ventricles late or out of step."],"fix":"Review which coronary artery usually supplies the SA and AV nodes, where the AV node sits, and why it is the only route to the ventricles."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-conduction-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-conduction","comp":"cv-cardiac-muscle","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A chemical blocks the gap junctions in the intercalated discs between the working muscle cells of the ventricles, but it leaves the desmosomes intact. The SA node, the AV node and the atria are not affected. What happens to the ventricle muscle?","options":["The cells pull apart at their ends with each contraction","The ventricles contract normally, since Purkinje fibers reach every cell","The cells stay attached, but the signal no longer spreads cell to cell","The signal never leaves the SA node, so the ventricles stay still"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An intercalated disc contains two kinds of junction with two separate jobs. Desmosomes rivet neighboring cells together so they do not pull apart as the heart contracts, and gap junctions are channels that pass the signal directly from one cell to the next. With the desmosomes intact, the cells stay attached. The Purkinje fibers carry the signal into the ventricle walls, but from there it has to spread from cell to cell through gap junctions, so with them blocked the wall no longer contracts as one coordinated unit.","notes":["Pulling apart is what happens when the desmosomes fail, because they are the anchoring junctions. The drug leaves the desmosomes working, so the cells stay attached.","The Purkinje fibers carry the signal into the ventricle walls, but they do not reach every working cell. The signal then has to spread from cell to cell through gap junctions, so without them the wall cannot contract as one unit.","Correct. Desmosomes keep holding the cells together, but blocked gap junctions stop the signal from passing between cells, so the wall loses its coordinated contraction.","The SA node is in the right atrium, and the stem says the SA node, AV node and atria are not affected. The signal still starts normally; the problem is how it spreads once it reaches the ventricle muscle."],"fix":"Review the two junctions in the intercalated disc and the separate job each one does."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-conduction-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-conduction","comp":"cv-conduction-anat","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Four patients each have a small patch of scar tissue in one part of the conduction system. Whose ventricles are most likely to lose all normal signals from the atria?","options":["Scar across the AV bundle in the interventricular septum","Scar replacing the SA node in the wall of the right atrium","Scar across the left bundle branch on one side of the septum","Scar in a patch of Purkinje fibers near the apex of the heart"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The atria and ventricles are separated by the fibrous skeleton, dense connective tissue that does not conduct the signal. The AV bundle is the one strand of conducting tissue that crosses it, so scar across the AV bundle cuts the only electrical bridge between the atria and the ventricles. If the SA node is lost instead, the AV node can take over as a slower backup pacemaker, and its signal still reaches the ventricles. Damage to one bundle branch or a patch of Purkinje fibers delays the signal to part of the ventricles, but the rest of the pathway still delivers it.","notes":["Correct. The AV bundle is the only conducting path through the fibrous skeleton, so scar across it cuts the ventricles off from the atria.","Losing the SA node removes the pacemaker, but the AV node can take over as a slower backup, and the signal still travels down to the ventricles. The connection between atria and ventricles is intact.","One bundle branch serves one side of the septum and one ventricle, so the other branch still carries the signal down. The ventricles are slowed or uncoordinated, but they are not cut off from the atria.","Purkinje fibers are the last part of the pathway, so a small patch of damage only affects the nearby wall. The signal has already crossed from the atria by the time it reaches them."],"fix":"Review the fibrous skeleton, why the AV bundle is the only electrical bridge to the ventricles, and what can take over if the SA node fails."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-conduction-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-conduction","comp":"cv-valve-support","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Part of the signal reaching the right ventricle takes a shortcut across the chamber, so that a papillary muscle contracts in time to help hold the tricuspid valve shut. Rank these structures in the order the signal reaches them.","options":["Right bundle branch, AV bundle, moderator band, anterior papillary muscle","AV bundle, moderator band, right bundle branch, anterior papillary muscle","AV bundle, right bundle branch, moderator band, anterior papillary muscle","AV bundle, right bundle branch, anterior papillary muscle, moderator band"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The signal enters the ventricles through the AV bundle at the top of the interventricular septum, which then divides into the right and left bundle branches. Part of the right bundle branch crosses the right ventricle inside the moderator band, a muscular band that runs from the septum to the base of the anterior papillary muscle. That papillary muscle anchors chordae tendineae of the tricuspid valve, so getting the signal to it lets it contract in time to keep the cords taut. The papillary muscle is the destination of the shortcut, so it comes last.","notes":["The AV bundle is the single trunk that divides into the bundle branches, so it must come before the right bundle branch. Students pick this when they picture the branches as feeding into the bundle.","The moderator band carries fibers that come from the right bundle branch, so the branch has to come first. The band is the route across the chamber, not the source of the signal.","Correct. The signal runs from the AV bundle into the right bundle branch, crosses the chamber in the moderator band, and arrives at the anterior papillary muscle.","The moderator band runs to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, so the signal crosses the band before it reaches the muscle. Putting the muscle first would mean the signal arrived before traveling the route that carries it."],"fix":"Review the moderator band, which conduction fibers it carries, and how the papillary muscles hold the atrioventricular valves shut."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-conduction-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-conduction","comp":"cv-cardiac-nerves","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient is given a drug that blocks the signals both vagus nerves carry to the heart. The sympathetic nerves to the heart keep working normally. What is the most likely change?","options":["The heart stops, because the vagus nerves start each beat","The heart keeps beating, but loses the input that slows its rate","The heart keeps beating, but loses the input that speeds its rate","The AV node stops passing the signal on to the ventricles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The heartbeat starts in the SA node, which is autorhythmic, so the heart keeps beating without any nerve input at all. The autonomic nerves only adjust the rate the SA node already sets. Parasympathetic fibers that slow the heart travel in the vagus nerves to the cardiac plexus and on to the SA and AV nodes, while sympathetic nerves speed the rate. Blocking the vagal signals therefore removes the slowing input, while the sympathetic speeding input is still in place.","notes":["The vagus nerves do not start the heartbeat. The SA node generates each beat on its own, which is why even a transplanted heart with its nerves cut keeps beating.","Correct. The SA node keeps the heart beating, and the vagus nerves carry the parasympathetic fibers that slow it, so that slowing input is what is lost.","The input that speeds the heart comes from the sympathetic nerves, which the stem says are unaffected. The vagus carries the opposite input, the one that slows the rate.","The AV node is part of the heart's own conduction system and keeps relaying the signal without vagal input. The nerves change how fast the nodes work; they are not needed for the signal to pass."],"fix":"Review the sympathetic and vagal nerve supply to the heart and the difference between starting the heartbeat and adjusting its rate."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-wall-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-wall","comp":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these vessels from the most elastic tissue in the wall to the least: an arteriole, the brachial artery, a capillary, and the aorta.","options":["Aorta, brachial artery, arteriole, capillary","Brachial artery, aorta, arteriole, capillary","Aorta, arteriole, brachial artery, capillary","Aorta, brachial artery, capillary, arteriole"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Start by classifying each vessel. The aorta is an elastic artery, whose thick tunica media is dominated by elastic lamellae so it can stretch and recoil with every heartbeat. The brachial artery is a muscular artery, whose media is mostly smooth muscle with thinner elastic laminae. An arteriole has only one or two layers of smooth muscle, and its internal elastic lamina disappears at its terminal end. A capillary has no tunica media at all, only endothelium on a basement membrane, so it has the least elastic tissue.","notes":["Correct. The elastic aorta comes first, then the muscular brachial artery, then the thin-walled arteriole, and last the capillary, which has no media at all.","The brachial artery is a muscular artery, whose media is mostly smooth muscle. The aorta is the classic elastic artery, so it has more elastic tissue than the brachial artery, not less.","An arteriole has only one or two layers of smooth muscle and loses its internal elastic lamina at its end. A muscular artery like the brachial still has elastic laminae around a much thicker media, so it ranks above the arteriole.","A capillary wall is only the tunica intima, with no media and no elastic laminae. An arteriole at least has a thin media, so it ranks above the capillary."],"fix":"Review the three sizes of artery, what the tunica media of each contains, and why a capillary has no media."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-wall-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-wall","comp":"bvn-vessel-disorders","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient has a growing atherosclerotic plaque in a coronary artery. In which tunic did the damage begin, and what does the plaque do to the heart muscle downstream?","options":["In the tunica externa; the vessel tears loose from the heart surface","In the tunica media; the wall balloons outward into an aneurysm","In the tunica intima; the wall balloons outward and may rupture","In the tunica intima; the narrowed lumen sends the muscle less blood"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Atherosclerosis begins with injury to the tunica intima, the smooth endothelial lining of the artery. Fatty material, cells and debris then build up within the wall as a plaque, which bulges inward and narrows the lumen. In a coronary artery that means less blood reaches the myocardium the artery supplies, which is coronary artery disease. A roughened plaque can also trigger a clot that suddenly blocks the vessel and causes a heart attack.","notes":["The tunica externa is the collagen layer that anchors the vessel, and atherosclerosis does not start there. A plaque grows from the inner lining and narrows the channel rather than detaching the vessel.","An outward bulge of a weakened wall is an aneurysm, a different arterial disorder. Atherosclerosis starts at the intima and narrows the lumen inward.","The starting tunic is right, but ballooning outward describes an aneurysm. A plaque bulges into the lumen and narrows it, so the problem downstream is reduced blood flow.","Correct. Atherosclerosis starts with injury to the tunica intima, and the plaque narrows the lumen so the myocardium downstream receives less blood."],"fix":"Review how atherosclerosis changes an artery wall and how it differs from an aneurysm."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-wall-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-wall","comp":"cv-valves","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Venous valves in the legs are flaps of the tunica intima that close when blood starts to slide backward, and no cords or muscles hold them shut. Which heart valves are most similar to venous valves in how they close?","options":["The tricuspid and mitral valves","The pulmonary and aortic valves","The mitral and aortic valves","The tricuspid and pulmonary valves"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Venous valves close passively: blood that starts to flow backward fills the flaps and presses them together, and no cords hold them. The semilunar valves, the pulmonary and aortic, work the same way, because their pocket-shaped cusps fill with blood falling back from the artery and seal themselves without chordae tendineae. The atrioventricular valves, the tricuspid and mitral, need chordae tendineae and papillary muscles to stop a contracting ventricle from pushing their cusps into the atria. So the semilunar valves are the closest match to venous valves.","notes":["The tricuspid and mitral valves are atrioventricular valves, and they are anchored by chordae tendineae and papillary muscles. That anchoring is exactly what venous valves lack, so these are the least similar.","Correct. The pulmonary and aortic valves are semilunar valves whose pocket-shaped cusps fill with backflowing blood and close on their own, just as venous valves do.","This pair mixes one atrioventricular valve with one semilunar valve. The mitral valve needs chordae tendineae to stay shut, so the pair does not share a single way of closing.","This pair also mixes the two valve types, since the tricuspid valve is an atrioventricular valve with chordae and the pulmonary valve is semilunar. Only the semilunar pair closes the way venous valves do."],"fix":"Review how the semilunar valves close compared with the atrioventricular valves, and what venous valves are made of."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-wall-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-wall","comp":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A phlebotomist aims for the usual blood-draw vein at the front of the elbow but pushes the needle too deep. Bright red blood spurts into the tube in pulses that match the heartbeat. Which vessel did the needle most likely enter?","options":["The basilic vein, which runs up the medial arm","The cephalic vein, which runs up the lateral arm","The brachial artery, a deep muscular artery","The axillary artery, which runs through the armpit"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The usual blood-draw vein at the front of the elbow is the median cubital vein, which crosses the cubital fossa to connect the cephalic and basilic veins. Bright red blood that pulses with the heartbeat comes from an artery, whose thick muscular wall carries blood under high pressure, not from a thin-walled, low-pressure vein. The artery that lies just deep to the median cubital vein in the cubital fossa is the brachial artery, a muscular artery that divides into the radial and ulnar arteries just below the elbow. The axillary artery is also an artery, but it lies up in the armpit, far from the elbow.","notes":["The basilic vein is a superficial vein that the median cubital vein joins, so it is near the needle's target. It is a thin-walled vein carrying darker blood at low pressure, so it would not spurt bright red blood in pulses.","The cephalic vein is the other superficial vein linked by the median cubital vein. Like the basilic, it is thin-walled and carries blood at low pressure, so it would not spurt bright red blood.","Correct. The brachial artery lies just deep to the median cubital vein in the cubital fossa, and an artery under high pressure explains the bright red, pulsing blood.","The axillary artery has the right kind of wall, but it lies in the armpit and becomes the brachial artery before reaching the elbow. A needle at the front of the elbow cannot reach it."],"fix":"Review the superficial veins and the artery at the cubital fossa, and the wall features that tell an artery from a vein."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-wall-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-wall","comp":"bvn-vessel-types","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A white blood cell rides in the blood from a muscular artery toward an infected patch of skin, where it leaves the bloodstream. In what order does it pass these vessels, ending at the one it exits through?","options":["Arteriole, metarteriole, capillary, postcapillary venule","Metarteriole, arteriole, capillary, postcapillary venule","Arteriole, metarteriole, postcapillary venule, capillary","Arteriole, capillary, metarteriole, postcapillary venule"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood leaving a muscular artery enters an arteriole, the smallest kind of artery. The tapered end of the arteriole is the metarteriole, and the capillaries of the bed branch off it. Blood then drains from the capillaries into the postcapillary venule, whose loosely joined, porous wall is where white blood cells usually slip out into the tissue. So the cell travels the arterial side and the capillary bed, then leaves at the first vessel on the venous side.","notes":["Correct. The cell moves from the arteriole to its tapered metarteriole, through a capillary, and exits at the porous postcapillary venule.","The metarteriole is the tapered end of an arteriole, so the arteriole has to come first. Students pick this when they picture the metarteriole as a separate vessel feeding into the arteriole rather than the arteriole's own far end.","This order ends at the capillary, which fits a student who thinks white blood cells leave through capillaries. They usually leave at the postcapillary venule, which receives blood after the capillaries.","Capillaries branch off the metarteriole, so the metarteriole has to come before the capillary. This order puts part of the arteriole after the capillary bed."],"fix":"Review the vessels of the microcirculation in order and which one white blood cells usually leave through."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-wall-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-wall","comp":"bvn-vessel-tunics","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"An inherited disease weakens the elastic fibers in the tunica media of every artery in the body. Which vessel is most at risk of forming an aneurysm?","options":["The brachial artery in the arm","An arteriole feeding a capillary bed in the skin","The aorta just above the heart","A sinusoid in the liver"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An aneurysm is a balloon-like bulge in a weakened artery wall, and it is common in the aorta. The aorta is an elastic artery whose tunica media is dominated by elastic lamellae, so a disease of elastic fibers takes away most of what holds its wall together. It also sits right where the left ventricle pushes each surge of blood out, so its wall is stretched hardest with every beat. The brachial artery and the arterioles have a media made mostly of smooth muscle, and a sinusoid has no tunica media at all, so they depend far less on elastic fibers.","notes":["The brachial artery is a muscular artery, so its tunica media is mostly smooth muscle with only thin elastic laminae. Losing elastic fibers weakens it less than it weakens an elastic artery.","An arteriole has only one or two layers of smooth muscle and little elastic tissue, and it sits far downstream from the heart. It relies the least on elastic fibers of any artery listed.","Correct. The aorta's media is mostly elastic tissue and it takes the full surge from the left ventricle, so weakened elastic fibers put it at the greatest risk of bulging.","A sinusoid is a capillary, and capillaries have no tunica media at all. A disease of the elastic fibers in the media has nothing to weaken in its wall."],"fix":"Review the tissue in the tunica media of elastic arteries, muscular arteries and arterioles, and what an aneurysm is."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-organization-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-organization","comp":"w4-ct-coverings","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A fine needle is pushed straight through the skin of the arm into the middle of one muscle fiber in a fascicle that lies just under the surface of the biceps brachii. In what order does the needle pass through these connective tissue layers?","options":["Epimysium, superficial fascia, perimysium, endomysium","Superficial fascia, perimysium, epimysium, endomysium","Superficial fascia, epimysium, perimysium, endomysium","Superficial fascia, epimysium, endomysium, perimysium"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The superficial fascia is the subcutaneous layer of areolar and adipose tissue under the skin, so the needle meets it before any part of the muscle. The three muscle sheaths are nested by scale. The epimysium wraps the whole muscle, the perimysium wraps each fascicle, and the endomysium wraps each individual fiber. Moving from the surface toward one fiber, the needle has to pass these sheaths from the largest wrapping to the smallest. Knowing what each sheath wraps lets you put any path through a muscle in order.","notes":["The epimysium cannot come first, because the superficial fascia is the subcutaneous layer that lies between the skin and the muscles. A needle has to cross it before it reaches any muscle sheath.","This puts the perimysium outside the epimysium. The epimysium wraps the entire muscle, so it is reached before the perimysium that wraps each fascicle inside it.","Correct. The needle crosses the subcutaneous layer first, then the sheath around the whole muscle, then the sheath around the fascicle, and last the sheath around the single fiber.","This swaps the two inner sheaths. The perimysium wraps a whole fascicle, and the endomysium wraps each fiber inside that fascicle, so the endomysium is always the last sheath before the fiber itself."],"fix":"Review the superficial fascia and the three muscle sheaths, and what each one wraps."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-organization-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-organization","comp":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"An electron micrograph shows a cross section of one skeletal muscle fiber and the tissue just outside it. Rank these structures from the most superficial (farthest out) to the deepest (closest to the center of a myofibril).","options":["Endomysium, sarcolemma, sarcoplasmic reticulum, myofilament","Sarcolemma, endomysium, sarcoplasmic reticulum, myofilament","Endomysium, sarcoplasmic reticulum, sarcolemma, myofilament","Endomysium, sarcolemma, myofilament, sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Start outside the cell and work inward. The endomysium is a connective tissue sheath that wraps each fiber from the outside, and the sarcolemma is the fiber's own plasma membrane just inside it. The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the fiber's smooth endoplasmic reticulum, a network that wraps each myofibril like a sleeve. The myofilaments are the thick and thin protein strands packed inside each myofibril, so they are the deepest. Ranking these correctly links the muscle sheaths to the parts inside the cell.","notes":["Correct. The endomysium sits outside the cell, the sarcolemma is the cell membrane, the sarcoplasmic reticulum wraps each myofibril, and the myofilaments lie inside the myofibril.","The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the fiber, so it lies inside the endomysium, not outside it. The endomysium is the areolar connective tissue sheath that surrounds the whole cell.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum is an organelle inside the cell, so it must be deeper than the sarcolemma. Students pick this when they picture the reticulum as a coat around the whole fiber rather than a sleeve around each myofibril.","The myofilaments are the protein strands inside each myofibril, and the sarcoplasmic reticulum wraps around the outside of that myofibril. That makes the myofilaments the deepest of the four."],"fix":"Review what the endomysium wraps, and where the sarcolemma, sarcoplasmic reticulum and myofilaments sit inside a muscle fiber."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-organization-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-organization","comp":"w4-muscle-tissue-types","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A rare inherited disorder weakens only the desmosomes and gap junctions found in intercalated discs. All other cell junctions in the body are normal. Which tissue would this disorder harm, and what would go wrong in it?","options":["Skeletal muscle, because its fibers would pull loose from their endomysium and tendon","Cardiac muscle, because its sarcomeres would fall apart and its striations would disappear","Smooth muscle, because its spindle cells would separate in the walls of hollow organs","Cardiac muscle, because its cells could pull apart and stop working as one unit"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Intercalated discs are found only in cardiac muscle, where they join the short, branching cells end to end. Each disc holds desmosomes, which anchor neighboring cells to each other, and gap junctions, which link the cells electrically so they work as one unit. If those junctions weakened, the cells could pull apart under the strain of each beat and would stop acting together. The sarcomeres lie inside each cell and are built from myofilaments anchored at Z discs, so the striations would still be there. Skeletal and smooth muscle have no intercalated discs, so a disorder limited to these junctions would leave them alone.","notes":["Skeletal muscle fibers are long single cells, and they are not joined by intercalated discs. Their pull reaches the tendon through the endomysium and the other sheaths, which this disorder does not touch, so this would fit a disorder of the connective tissue sheaths instead.","Cardiac muscle is the right tissue, but the sarcomeres sit inside each cell and are built from myofilaments anchored at Z discs. Intercalated discs join one cell to the next, so the striations would remain while the cells lose their hold on each other.","Smooth muscle cells are spindle shaped with one central nucleus, and they have no intercalated discs. Students pick this because smooth muscle is involuntary like cardiac muscle.","Correct. Intercalated discs are found only in cardiac muscle, where desmosomes hold the cells together and gap junctions link them so they work as one unit."],"fix":"Review the three muscle tissue types, which one has intercalated discs, and what the desmosomes and gap junctions in those discs do."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-organization-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-organization","comp":"w4-fascicle-patterns","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"Two muscles have the same volume. One is multipennate like the deltoid, with many short fascicles angled onto branching tendons. The other is a parallel strap muscle with long fascicles running its full length. Every fiber can shorten to about two thirds of its resting length. Which prediction is correct?","options":["The multipennate muscle moves its insertion farther and also pulls harder","The parallel muscle moves its insertion farther, and the multipennate muscle pulls harder","The parallel muscle pulls harder, and the multipennate muscle moves its insertion farther","Both pull equally hard because of equal volume, but the parallel muscle moves farther"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each fiber shortens by about the same fraction of its length, so a long fascicle shortens by a greater distance than a short one. The parallel muscle, with fascicles running its full length, therefore moves its insertion farther. The multipennate muscle packs many short fascicles at an angle onto its tendons, so more fibers pull on the tendon at once and it produces more force. This is the basic trade-off of fascicle architecture, range of motion against power.","notes":["One design cannot win on both counts. The short fascicles of a multipennate muscle shorten only a short distance, so it gives up range of motion to gain force.","Correct. Long fascicles shorten by a greater distance, while angled packing fits more fibers onto the tendon for more force.","This reverses the trade-off. The long fascicles of the parallel muscle give range, while the many short, angled fibers of the multipennate muscle give power.","Equal volume does not mean equal force. The angled design packs more fibers onto the tendon, so the multipennate muscle pulls harder even at the same size."],"fix":"Review the fascicle arrangement patterns and why parallel muscles favor range while pennate muscles favor power."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-organization-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-organization","comp":"w4-muscle-roles-naming","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"In the lab, a student finds a tag reading extensor carpi radialis brevis on a forearm muscle she has not studied yet. Using the name and what she knows about the forearm compartments, which prediction is best?","options":["It lies in the anterior forearm on the thumb side and flexes the wrist","It lies in the posterior forearm on the thumb side and extends the wrist","It lies in the posterior forearm on the little finger side and extends the wrist","It lies in the posterior forearm on the thumb side and extends the fingers"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A muscle name can be read piece by piece. Extensor names the action, carpi names the region (the carpus, or wrist), and radialis names the position, on the radial or thumb side of the forearm. Brevis tells you it is a short muscle. Because the forearm is divided into an anterior flexor compartment and a posterior extensor compartment, an extensor of the wrist belongs in the posterior compartment. Putting the name together with the compartments tells you where to look and what the muscle does before you ever see it.","notes":["Flexing the wrist is the job of the anterior flexor compartment. The word extensor tells you this muscle belongs to the posterior extensor compartment instead.","Correct. Extensor gives the action, carpi gives the region (the wrist), and radialis gives the position on the radial side, the side of the thumb.","The little finger side is the ulnar side, which a muscle named ulnaris would occupy. Radialis places this muscle beside the radius, on the thumb side.","The word carpi refers to the wrist, not the fingers. A muscle that extends the fingers would carry a term such as digitorum in its name."],"fix":"Review the muscle naming criteria and the anterior flexor and posterior extensor compartments of the forearm."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-organization-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-organization","comp":"w4-ct-coverings","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"The gastrocnemius and soleus both end on the calcaneus through one shared tendon, the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon. Four patients have different injuries to this calf group. Which injury would remove the pull of both muscles on the heel, leaving the patient unable to rise onto the toes?","options":["A strain that tears fibers in the belly of the gastrocnemius","A tear of the perimysium around several soleus fascicles","A torn epimysium over one head of the gastrocnemius","A complete rupture of the calcaneal tendon above the heel"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In each muscle the endomysium, perimysium and epimysium are continuous with one another and merge into the tendon, which is how a fiber's pull reaches the bone. Here two muscles feed a single tendon, so that tendon is the one point every fiber of the group depends on. Injuries inside a belly or to the sheaths around part of one muscle weaken the pull but leave other fibers still connected to the heel. A complete tendon rupture is different because it breaks the only link between the whole group and the calcaneus. Judging how serious an injury is depends on where it falls along that chain from fiber to bone.","notes":["A strain tears some fibers in one muscle belly. The remaining gastrocnemius fibers and the whole soleus still pull through an intact tendon, so the patient is weak but can still push up.","Damage to the perimysium around a few fascicles affects only those bundles in one muscle. The rest of the soleus and the gastrocnemius still reach the heel through the tendon.","Tearing the outer sheath over part of one muscle does not cut the connection to bone. The sheaths and tendon of the rest of the group stay continuous and still carry the pull.","Correct. The tendon is the merged continuation of the sheaths of both muscles and their only link to the calcaneus, so cutting it disconnects all of their pull at once."],"fix":"Review how the three connective tissue sheaths merge into the tendon, and compare a muscle strain with a tendon rupture."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-microanatomy-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-microanatomy","comp":"w4-muscle-organization","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Each structure in this list lies inside some of the others. Rank them from the one that contains all the rest to the one that is contained by all the rest: muscle fiber, sarcomere, fascicle, myofilament.","options":["Fascicle, muscle fiber, sarcomere, myofilament","Muscle fiber, fascicle, sarcomere, myofilament","Fascicle, sarcomere, muscle fiber, myofilament","Fascicle, muscle fiber, myofilament, sarcomere"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Skeletal muscle is a set of nested levels. A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers, and each fiber is a single cell packed with myofibrils. A myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres lined up end to end, so the sarcomere is a segment inside the fiber. Each sarcomere is built from the thick and thin myofilaments whose overlap gives it its bands. To rank the sarcomere you have to remember that it is a piece of a myofibril, which places it between the fiber and the myofilament.","notes":["Correct. A fascicle is a bundle of fibers, each fiber is filled with myofibrils built of sarcomeres, and each sarcomere is made of myofilaments.","A fascicle is a bundle of many muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium. The fiber is inside the fascicle, so the fascicle has to come first.","A sarcomere is a segment of a myofibril, and myofibrils lie inside the muscle fiber. The sarcomere cannot contain the fiber that holds it.","Myofilaments are the thick and thin protein strands that build each sarcomere. Their ordered overlap forms the sarcomere, so they sit inside it."],"fix":"Review the levels of muscle organization from fascicle to myofilament, and where the sarcomere fits in that chain."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-microanatomy-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-microanatomy","comp":"w4-sarcomere","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A researcher moves a fine probe along one myofibril. The probe starts at the M line and moves outward toward one end of the same sarcomere. In what order does it pass these regions?","options":["Zone of overlap, H zone, I band, Z disc","H zone, I band, zone of overlap, Z disc","H zone, zone of overlap, I band, Z disc","I band, zone of overlap, H zone, Z disc"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The M line sits in the center of the H zone, the region that holds thick filaments only. Moving outward, the thin filaments begin, so the next region is the zone of overlap at the edge of the A band where thick and thin filaments interleave. Past the end of the thick filaments only thin filaments remain, which is the I band. The Z disc runs through the center of the I band and marks the end of the sarcomere. Thinking about which filaments are present at each point lets you trace the sarcomere in either direction.","notes":["The zone of overlap lies at the edge of the A band, not right beside the M line. The region surrounding the M line has thick filaments only, which is the H zone.","The I band holds thin filaments only and lies outside the A band. The probe has to cross the zone of overlap at the edge of the A band before it can reach the I band.","Correct. Moving out from the center, the probe crosses thick filaments only, then thick and thin together, then thin only, and ends at the disc that anchors the thin filaments.","This order starts at the wrong end. The I band holds thin filaments only and sits at the outer part of the sarcomere, far from the M line, so a probe starting at the M line meets the H zone first."],"fix":"Review the regions of the sarcomere and which filaments are found in each one."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-microanatomy-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-microanatomy","comp":"w4-myofilament-proteins","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A research mouse has a defect in titin. Its sarcomeres form, the thin filaments are anchored at the Z discs, and the fibers connect normally to the sarcolemma. What problem would you predict when its muscles are passively stretched?","options":["The thin filaments pull loose from the Z discs and the I band disappears","The fibers tear their own sarcolemma during ordinary daily use","The thin filaments grow to uneven lengths across the sarcomere","The sarcomeres overstretch and spring back poorly to resting length"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Titin is the elastic filament of the sarcomere. It spans from the Z disc to the M line, anchors the thick filament, and acts like a spring that resists overstretching and pulls the sarcomere back to resting length. If titin is defective, a stretched sarcomere has nothing to limit how far it is pulled or to return it afterward. The other structural proteins have different jobs. Alpha-actinin anchors thin filaments at the Z disc, nebulin sets thin filament length, and dystrophin links the sarcomere to the sarcolemma.","notes":["Binding the thin filaments to the Z disc is the job of alpha-actinin. The stem also says the thin filaments are anchored normally, so this is ruled out.","Fibers that damage themselves with ordinary use describe a failure of dystrophin, the link between the thin filaments and the sarcolemma. The stem says that link is normal.","Setting the length of the thin filament is the job of nebulin, which runs along it. A titin defect would not change thin filament length.","Correct. Titin runs from the Z disc to the M line, anchors the thick filament, gives elastic recoil and keeps the sarcomere from overstretching."],"fix":"Review the structural proteins of the sarcomere (titin, nebulin, alpha-actinin, myomesin and dystrophin), where each sits and what each does."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-microanatomy-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-microanatomy","comp":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A muscle fiber from a research animal has normal sarcomeres in every myofibril. When its sarcolemma is stimulated, the myofibrils just under the surface respond at once, but those in the center of the wide fiber respond late. Which structural defect best explains this pattern?","options":["The sarcoplasmic reticulum is missing around the outermost myofibrils only","The T tubules never formed, so the surface signal cannot dive into the fiber","Myoglobin is missing from the sarcoplasm throughout the whole fiber","The myonuclei sit in the center of the fiber instead of under the sarcolemma"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A skeletal muscle fiber is a very wide cell, and the signal to contract arrives at its surface membrane, the sarcolemma. T tubules are tunnels of the sarcolemma that dive inward and run between the myofibrils, carrying that signal to the center. There each T tubule sits between two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, forming a triad beside every myofibril. If the T tubules were missing, myofibrils near the surface would still get the signal, but deep ones would lag, and the fiber would not respond as a single unit.","notes":["If the sarcoplasmic reticulum were missing around the outer myofibrils, the surface myofibrils would be the ones to fail, which is the opposite of what was seen. The deep myofibrils are the ones lagging here.","Correct. T tubules are inward extensions of the sarcolemma that carry the surface signal deep into the fiber, so without them the center lags behind the surface.","Myoglobin stores oxygen for sustained work. Losing it would affect endurance across the whole fiber, not create a delay that depends on depth.","Nuclear position is used to identify a skeletal fiber, but the nuclei do not carry the surface signal inward. Moving them would not explain a delay at the center."],"fix":"Review the T tubules, sarcoplasmic reticulum and triad, and why a wide fiber needs T tubules."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-microanatomy-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-microanatomy","comp":"w4-muscle-tissue-types","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A pathologist has a small piece of striated muscle and must decide whether it is skeletal or cardiac muscle. No intercalated discs are visible in the section. Which single finding would settle it in favor of skeletal muscle?","options":["Cross striations of alternating dark and light bands","Sarcomeres bounded by Z discs in every myofibril","Many flattened nuclei pressed just under the sarcolemma","A red sarcoplasm rich in oxygen-storing myoglobin"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"When two tissues share many features, only a feature that one has and the other lacks can settle the question. Skeletal and cardiac muscle are both striated, and both owe their stripes to sarcomeres bounded by Z discs. They differ in their cells. A skeletal fiber is a long cylinder with many nuclei pressed against the sarcolemma, while a cardiac cell is short and branched with one central nucleus. With intercalated discs out of view, the number and position of the nuclei is the deciding clue.","notes":["Both skeletal and cardiac muscle are striated, so stripes cannot tell them apart. Striations would only settle the question if the choice were between skeletal and smooth muscle.","Striations come from aligned sarcomeres, so both striated tissues have sarcomeres bounded by Z discs. This feature is shared and cannot decide between them.","Correct. A skeletal fiber forms from many fused embryonic cells, so it has many nuclei at its edge, while a cardiac cell has one central nucleus.","Cardiac muscle is also red and rich in myoglobin, so an oxygen-rich sarcoplasm does not point to skeletal muscle. It is a shared feature rather than a deciding one."],"fix":"Review how skeletal, cardiac and smooth muscle compare by striations, nuclei and cell shape."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-microanatomy-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-microanatomy","comp":"w4-myofilament-proteins","type":"Sequence","dok":4,"q":"In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, muscle fibers damage themselves with ordinary use because force from the sarcomeres is not passed out of the cell properly. In a healthy fiber, in what order does the pull of a thin filament pass these structures on its way out to the perimysium?","options":["Dystrophin, sarcolemma, endomysium, perimysium","Sarcolemma, dystrophin, endomysium, perimysium","Dystrophin, endomysium, sarcolemma, perimysium","Dystrophin, sarcolemma, perimysium, endomysium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Force made in the sarcomere has to leave the cell to do any work. Dystrophin is the structural protein that links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma, so it is the first step out of the sarcomere. From the sarcolemma the force passes into the endomysium around the fiber, then into the perimysium around the fascicle, and on through the epimysium and tendon to the bone. In Duchenne muscular dystrophy the dystrophin gene is faulty, so this first link fails and the fiber tears itself with ordinary use. Tracing the chain shows how a protein inside the cell connects to the connective tissue sheaths outside it.","notes":["Correct. Dystrophin links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma, and from the membrane the force passes into the endomysium and then the perimysium around the fascicle.","Dystrophin sits on the inner side of the membrane, linking the thin filaments to the sarcolemma. The pull reaches dystrophin before it reaches the membrane.","The endomysium is a connective tissue sheath outside the cell. The pull cannot reach it until it has crossed the sarcolemma, the cell's own membrane.","This swaps the two sheaths. The endomysium wraps each fiber directly, and the perimysium wraps the whole fascicle outside it, so the endomysium comes first."],"fix":"Review the role of dystrophin and how it links the sarcomere to the sarcolemma and the connective tissue sheaths."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-naming-levers-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-naming-levers","comp":"w4-lever-systems","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A student holds her elbow bent at a right angle with the upper arm at her side. A heavy bag hangs from her forearm, and her biceps brachii holds it still. Rank these bag positions from the one that needs the least effort from the biceps to the one that needs the most.","options":["Fingertips, wrist, mid-forearm, near the elbow","Mid-forearm, near the elbow, wrist, fingertips","Near the elbow, wrist, mid-forearm, fingertips","Near the elbow, mid-forearm, wrist, fingertips"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In elbow flexion the elbow joint is the fulcrum, the forearm bones are the lever, the biceps pull is the effort and the bag is the load. Whether a lever works at an advantage or a disadvantage depends on how far the effort and the load each act from the fulcrum. The biceps insertion does not move, so only the load position changes. As the bag slides away from the elbow, it acts over a longer distance, and the biceps has to pull harder to hold it. Moving the load toward the fulcrum is the only change here, so the effort needed rises in step with that distance.","notes":["This reverses the rule. A load far from the fulcrum, such as one held at the fingertips, needs the most effort, not the least.","The point closest to the elbow joint needs less effort than the middle of the forearm, because it is nearer the fulcrum. This order misplaces those two positions.","The wrist is farther from the elbow than the middle of the forearm, so it needs more effort. Students pick this when they lose track of the order along the forearm.","Correct. The elbow is the fulcrum, and the closer the load sits to the fulcrum, the less effort is needed to hold it."],"fix":"Review the parts of a lever and how the distance of the load from the fulcrum changes the effort needed."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-naming-levers-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-naming-levers","comp":"w4-muscle-tissue-types","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A student lists three muscle tissues: the skeletal muscle of the biceps brachii, the cardiac muscle of the heart wall, and the smooth muscle in the wall of the brachial artery. She asks which of them could act as the effort in a body lever. Which answer and reason is correct?","options":["Skeletal and cardiac muscle, because both are striated and can contract with force","Only skeletal muscle, because it attaches to bone and can pull the bone around a joint","All three tissues, because every muscle tissue can contract and shorten","Only smooth muscle, because its slow, steady contraction can hold a bone in place"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In a body lever the bone is the lever, the joint is the fulcrum, and the effort is the pull of a contracting muscle on the bone. All three muscle tissues can contract, so contraction alone does not settle the question. What matters is where each tissue is. Skeletal muscle is attached to the skeleton through tendons, so its pull moves bones. Cardiac muscle forms the heart wall and smooth muscle forms the walls of hollow organs and vessels, so their contractions squeeze the organs they build rather than moving a bone.","notes":["Striations do not decide this. Cardiac muscle is striated, but it forms the heart wall and is not attached to any bone, so it cannot pull a bone around a joint.","Correct. In a body lever the effort is a muscle pulling on a bone, and only skeletal muscle is attached to the skeleton by tendons.","All three can contract, which is why they are all muscle tissue. Only one of them is attached to bone, though, and a lever needs a pull on the bone.","Smooth muscle lines the walls of hollow organs and vessels. It changes the diameter of the vessel it sits in and has no attachment to a bone."],"fix":"Review where each of the three muscle tissues is found, and which structure supplies the effort in a body lever."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-naming-levers-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-naming-levers","comp":"w4-muscle-roles-naming","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"When the deltoid abducts the arm, muscles that run from the trunk to the scapula normally contract to hold the scapula steady. A patient has weakness of those muscles but a healthy deltoid. What is most likely to happen when she tries to raise her arm out to the side?","options":["The scapula shifts as the deltoid pulls, so the arm rises weakly and with less control","The arm cannot rise at all, because the prime mover for abduction has been lost","The arm rises more strongly, because no antagonist is resisting the deltoid","The arm moves toward the midline instead, because the weak muscles were the agonists"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A fixator is a special kind of synergist that holds the origin bone steady so the prime mover can pull efficiently on the insertion. The deltoid arises from the scapula and clavicle and inserts on the humerus. If those trunk to scapula muscles cannot hold the scapula still, part of the deltoid's pull moves the scapula instead of lifting the humerus. The prime mover still works, so the arm can rise, but the movement is weaker and harder to control. Classifying each muscle by its role tells you what is lost when one of them fails.","notes":["Correct. The muscles from the trunk to the scapula act as fixators, and without them the deltoid wastes part of its pull moving its own origin instead of the humerus.","The deltoid is the prime mover for abduction, and it is healthy here. The arm can still rise, but less efficiently, because the bone the deltoid pulls from is not held steady.","The muscles from the trunk to the scapula in this case were steadying the origin, not opposing the movement. Losing them takes away support rather than resistance, so the movement gets weaker, not stronger.","The muscles from the trunk to the scapula were fixators here, and the deltoid remains the agonist. Weak fixators do not reverse the direction of the movement."],"fix":"Review the four muscle roles (agonist, antagonist, synergist and fixator) and what a fixator does for the prime mover."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-naming-levers-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-naming-levers","comp":"w4-muscle-roles-naming","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A clinician wants to feel both ends of the sternocleidomastoid while a patient turns his head against her hand. Using only the muscle's name and the usual rule for origins and insertions, where are its attachments, and which end is the insertion?","options":["Sternum and clavicle below, mastoid process above; the sternal end is the insertion","Sternum and scapula below, mastoid process above; the mastoid end is the insertion","Sternum and clavicle below, mastoid process above; the mastoid end is the insertion","Sternum and clavicle below, occipital bone above; the occipital end is the insertion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Sternocleidomastoid is named for its origin and insertion. Sterno refers to the sternum, cleido to the clavicle, and mastoid to the mastoid process behind the ear. The origin is the attachment to the more stationary bone and the insertion is the attachment to the more movable bone, the one pulled toward the origin. When the patient turns his head, the sternum and clavicle stay still and the head moves, so the mastoid end is the insertion. Reading a name and applying the origin and insertion rule together tells you where to place your hand.","notes":["The attachments are right, but the roles are reversed. The insertion is on the more movable bone, and here the head moves while the sternum stays put.","The middle part of the name, cleido, refers to the clavicle, not the scapula. Reading each root of the name keeps the attachments straight.","Correct. The name lists the sternum, the clavicle (cleido) and the mastoid process, and the mastoid end moves with the head, so it is the insertion.","The last part of the name is mastoid, which names the mastoid process of the temporal bone behind the ear. The occipital bone is not named here."],"fix":"Review the naming criterion of origin and insertion and the rule for telling an origin from an insertion."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-naming-levers-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-naming-levers","comp":"w4-ct-coverings","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"During a biceps curl, the radius is the lever bar, and the pull made by the biceps fascicles is the effort. In what order does that pull travel through these structures to reach the lever bar?","options":["Perimysium, epimysium, tendon, radius","Epimysium, perimysium, tendon, radius","Perimysium, tendon, epimysium, radius","Tendon, perimysium, epimysium, radius"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A fascicle is a bundle of fibers wrapped in perimysium, and the whole muscle is wrapped in epimysium. These sheaths are continuous with each other, and beyond the end of the muscle they merge into the tendon. The tendon of the biceps attaches to the radius, which acts as the lever bar that the effort moves. So the pull travels from the perimysium to the epimysium, into the tendon and onto the bone. Tracing this chain shows how the effort in a lever system actually gets from the muscle to the bone.","notes":["Correct. The pull leaves each fascicle through its perimysium, spreads into the epimysium around the whole muscle, and passes into the tendon that attaches to the radius.","This order runs from the outside of the muscle inward. The pull starts inside the muscle at the fascicles, so the perimysium comes before the epimysium.","The tendon is formed where the sheaths merge beyond the end of the muscle, so it comes after the epimysium. It cannot sit between two of the sheaths.","The tendon is the last connective tissue before the bone, not the first. It is the merged continuation of the sheaths, which carry the pull to it."],"fix":"Review how the muscle sheaths merge into the tendon, and which part of a body lever the bone, joint and muscle play."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-muscle-naming-levers-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-muscle-naming-levers","comp":"w4-lever-systems","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Standing on tiptoe lifts the whole body weight a short distance, while flexing the elbow swings the hand quickly through a wide arc. Which pairing of fascicle architecture and lever class best fits each job?","options":["Tiptoe: fusiform calf muscles on a third-class lever; elbow: pennate biceps on a second-class lever","Tiptoe: pennate calf muscles on a third-class lever; elbow: fusiform biceps on a second-class lever","Tiptoe: pennate calf muscles on a first-class lever; elbow: fusiform biceps on a third-class lever","Tiptoe: pennate calf muscles on a second-class lever; elbow: fusiform biceps on a third-class lever"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Two design choices work together here. Pennate muscles pack many short fibers at an angle onto a tendon for force, while fusiform muscles have long fascicles that favor range and speed. A second-class lever, with the load between the fulcrum and the effort, gives a mechanical advantage, so the calf can lift the whole body. A third-class lever, with the effort between the fulcrum and the load, works at a disadvantage but swings the far end of the bone fast and far. The calf pairs force with force, and the biceps pairs range with range, so each one matches its job.","notes":["This swaps everything. The calf muscles are largely pennate and work on a second-class lever, while the biceps is fusiform and works on a third-class lever.","The muscle shapes are right, but the lever classes are swapped. In standing on tiptoe the load sits between the fulcrum and the effort, which is second class, and in elbow flexion the effort sits in the middle, which is third class.","In a first-class lever the fulcrum sits between the effort and the load, as in head nodding. On tiptoe the ball of the foot is the fulcrum at one end, so it is second class.","Correct. Both the pennate design and the second-class lever favor force for lifting the body, while the fusiform biceps and third-class lever favor speed and range."],"fix":"Review the three lever classes with a body example of each, and the trade-off between pennate and fusiform fascicle patterns."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-cells-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood-cells","comp":"w4-formed-elements","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A technologist counts 100 white cells on a healthy person's smear and sorts them by appearance. Type 1 has a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules. Type 2 has a large round nucleus that nearly fills the cell. Type 3 has three to five nuclear lobes and fine pale granules. Type 4 is the largest cell and has a kidney-shaped nucleus. Rank the types from most numerous to least numerous.","options":["3, 4, 2, 1","3, 2, 4, 1","2, 3, 4, 1","3, 2, 1, 4"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Two steps are needed. First, identify each cell from its look on the smear: the multi-lobed nucleus with pale granules is a neutrophil, the large round nucleus is a lymphocyte, the largest cell with a kidney-shaped nucleus is a monocyte, and the two-lobed nucleus with red-orange granules is an eosinophil. Second, apply the normal order from most to least numerous, neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, then basophils. The memory phrase Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas gives that order. Combined, the types rank 3, 2, 4, 1.","notes":["This places the monocyte (type 4) ahead of the lymphocyte (type 2). Lymphocytes make up about 20 to 40 percent of white cells, far more than monocytes at about 2 to 8 percent.","Correct. Type 3 is the neutrophil, type 2 the lymphocyte, type 4 the monocyte and type 1 the eosinophil, which matches the order neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils.","This puts the lymphocyte ahead of the neutrophil. Neutrophils are the most numerous white cell, at about 50 to 70 percent of the count.","This puts the eosinophil (type 1) ahead of the monocyte (type 4). Eosinophils make up only about 1 to 4 percent, fewer than monocytes."],"fix":"Review how to recognize each of the five leukocytes on a smear and their order from most to least numerous."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-cells-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood-cells","comp":"w4-formed-elements","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A child with a parasitic worm infection has a rise in the one kind of white blood cell that attacks parasites. On her blood smear, which cell should the technologist expect to see more often than usual?","options":["A granulocyte with a lobed nucleus hidden by large dark granules","A granulocyte with three to five nuclear lobes and fine pale granules","A granulocyte with a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules","An agranulocyte with a large round nucleus that fills most of the cell"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"This question links a cell's job to its appearance. The leukocyte that attacks parasites is the eosinophil. On a stained smear the eosinophil is a granulocyte with a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules. The basophil and neutrophil are also granulocytes, but they have dark granules hiding the nucleus or fine pale granules with three to five lobes. Matching the role to the cell, and then the cell to its look, is how a smear is read.","notes":["This is the basophil, the rarest leukocyte, whose dark granules hold histamine and heparin. It releases histamine in inflammation rather than leading the attack on parasites.","This is the neutrophil, the most numerous leukocyte and the first responder to bacterial infection. It would be the cell to expect in a bacterial infection.","Correct. This is the eosinophil, the leukocyte that attacks parasites and moderates allergic reactions.","This is the lymphocyte, the core of specific immunity, which includes the T cells and B cells. It is an agranulocyte, while the parasite fighter is a granulocyte."],"fix":"Review the five leukocytes, the defense role of each, and how each looks on a stained smear."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-cells-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood-cells","comp":"w4-formed-elements","type":"Sequence","dok":4,"q":"A white blood cell will end its life as a macrophage in an infected cut on the skin. Put the stages of its life in order.","options":["Circulating monocyte, marrow monoblast, exit through a venule wall, tissue macrophage","Marrow monoblast, circulating monocyte, tissue macrophage, exit through a venule wall","Marrow monoblast, exit through a venule wall, circulating monocyte, tissue macrophage","Marrow monoblast, circulating monocyte, exit through a venule wall, tissue macrophage"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"This path ties together three parts of the module. Blood cells form in red bone marrow, and the monoblast is the blast cell that develops into the monocyte. The monocyte, the largest leukocyte, then circulates in the blood. White cells leave the bloodstream through the thin, porous walls of venules, which is how they reach infection in the tissues. Once a monocyte enters a tissue it matures into a macrophage that engulfs debris and pathogens.","notes":["The monoblast is the blast cell in the marrow that develops into a monocyte, so it must come before the circulating cell. Blood cells are made in the marrow before they enter the blood.","A monocyte matures into a macrophage after it leaves the blood and enters a tissue. It does not become a macrophage first and then cross the vessel wall.","Venules are where white cells leave the bloodstream for the tissues. A cell has to be in the blood as a monocyte before it can exit through a venule wall.","Correct. The cell forms in red bone marrow from a monoblast, travels in the blood as a monocyte, leaves through a venule wall and matures into a macrophage in the tissue."],"fix":"Review the monoblast and monocyte in hematopoiesis, where white cells leave the bloodstream, and what a monocyte becomes in the tissues."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-cells-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood-cells","comp":"w4-blood-composition","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A tube of a patient's blood is spun in a centrifuge. The thin pale layer between the plasma and the red cells is several times thicker than normal, while the red layer and the plasma look about normal. Which disorder fits this finding best?","options":["Leukemia","Polycythemia","Thrombocytopenia","Anemia"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"When blood is spun, it separates into plasma on top, a thin pale buffy coat in the middle, and packed erythrocytes at the bottom. The buffy coat is made of leukocytes and platelets. A buffy coat several times thicker than normal means far too many of those cells, and leukemia is a cancer of white cell formation that releases large numbers of abnormal leukocytes. Disorders of red cells change the bottom layer instead, and a low platelet count would shrink the middle layer rather than enlarge it.","notes":["Correct. The pale middle layer is the buffy coat of leukocytes and platelets, and leukemia floods the blood with abnormal white cells.","Polycythemia is an excess of erythrocytes. It would make the red layer at the bottom of the tube larger, not the pale middle layer.","Thrombocytopenia is a low platelet count. Fewer platelets would make the buffy coat thinner if anything, not several times thicker.","Anemia is too few erythrocytes or too little hemoglobin. It would shrink the red layer, and the red layer is normal here."],"fix":"Review the three layers of a spun blood sample and which blood disorder affects each component."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-cells-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood-cells","comp":"w4-blood-disorders","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A lab report flags a very low count of the formed element that breaks off from megakaryocyte cytoplasm. The red and white cell counts are normal. Which disorder is this, and what problem should the patient watch for?","options":["Leukopenia, with frequent infections","Hemophilia, with easy bleeding and bruising","Thrombocytopenia, with easy bleeding and bruising","Anemia, with fatigue and shortness of breath"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The first step is to name the formed element. Platelets are not whole cells but fragments of cytoplasm broken off from megakaryocytes in the red bone marrow. A low platelet count is called thrombocytopenia. Platelets gather at a damaged vessel and form a plug, so too few of them leads to easy bleeding and bruising. Hemophilia also causes bleeding, but it comes from a missing clotting protein, not from too few platelets.","notes":["Leukopenia is a low white cell count, which weakens defense against infection. The white count is normal here, and white cells are whole cells, not fragments.","Hemophilia does cause bleeding, but it is an inherited lack of a clotting protein in the plasma, and the platelet count stays normal. It would fit if the platelets were normal and clotting were slow.","Correct. Fragments of megakaryocyte cytoplasm are platelets, and an abnormally low platelet count is thrombocytopenia, which raises the risk of bleeding.","Anemia is too few red cells or too little hemoglobin, which causes fatigue. The red cell count is normal in this case."],"fix":"Review what a platelet is, and match each blood disorder to the component it affects."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-cells-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood-cells","comp":"w4-blood-disorders","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A teenager with sickle cell disease has painful episodes when small vessels in her hands and feet become blocked, and her blood tests also show anemia. Which explanation accounts for both problems?","options":["Sickled cells keep their nucleus, so they are too large to pass through capillaries","Stiff sickled cells cannot fold through capillaries, and they wear out in 10 to 20 days","Sickled cells hold too much hemoglobin, so they swell and burst inside the capillaries","The abnormal hemoglobin lowers the platelet count, so small vessels clot and then bleed"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A healthy erythrocyte is a flexible biconcave disc that can fold to squeeze single file through capillaries narrower than itself. In sickle cell disease an inherited change in the gene for the beta-globin chain produces an abnormal hemoglobin, hemoglobin S, which makes the cells stiff and sickle shaped. Rigid cells cannot fold, so they jam small vessels and starve the tissue downstream, which causes pain. Sickled cells also survive only about 10 to 20 days instead of about 120. When red cells are lost faster than they are replaced, the blood has too few of them, which is anemia.","notes":["Mature sickled cells have no nucleus, just like healthy red cells, so a kept nucleus is not the problem. Their trouble is a rigid, curved shape, and a nucleus alone would not explain why the cells are lost so quickly.","Correct. Rigid cells jam small vessels, which causes the pain, and they survive only 10 to 20 days instead of about 120, which leaves too few red cells.","The problem in sickle cell disease is an abnormal hemoglobin, hemoglobin S, not too much hemoglobin. The abnormal hemoglobin makes the cell stiff and curved rather than swollen.","Sickle cell disease is a disorder of erythrocytes and hemoglobin, not platelets. A low platelet count would be thrombocytopenia, which causes bleeding rather than blocked vessels."],"fix":"Review erythrocyte shape and lifespan, and how the abnormal hemoglobin in sickle cell disease changes the cell."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-routes-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-routes","comp":"bvn-fetal-remnants","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Trace a drop of oxygen-rich blood from the placenta to the fetal left atrium by its most direct route. In what order does it pass these structures?","options":["Umbilical vein, ductus venosus, inferior vena cava, foramen ovale","Umbilical vein, inferior vena cava, ductus venosus, foramen ovale","Ductus venosus, umbilical vein, inferior vena cava, foramen ovale","Umbilical vein, ductus venosus, foramen ovale, inferior vena cava"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Oxygen-rich blood leaves the placenta in the umbilical vein. Most of it then takes the ductus venosus, the shunt through the fetal liver, which empties toward the inferior vena cava. The inferior vena cava delivers it to the right atrium. From there the foramen ovale, an opening in the interatrial septum, lets it pass directly into the left atrium without going through the lungs. Each shunt makes sense once you know which organ it lets blood skip.","notes":["Correct. The umbilical vein brings the blood in, the ductus venosus carries it past the liver to the inferior vena cava, and from the right atrium it crosses the foramen ovale to the left atrium.","The ductus venosus empties toward the inferior vena cava, so it comes before the vena cava, not after it. It is the shunt that gets the blood past the liver on the way.","The umbilical vein is the vessel that carries blood from the placenta into the fetus, so it has to come first. The ductus venosus receives blood from it.","The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum, so blood reaches it only after the inferior vena cava has emptied into the right atrium."],"fix":"Review the fetal vessels and the three shunts, and which organ each shunt lets blood bypass."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-routes-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-routes","comp":"cv-blood-pathway","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A clot breaks free from a deep vein in a patient's calf. Minutes later she is suddenly short of breath. Where has the clot most likely lodged, and why did it not reach her brain?","options":["In a coronary artery, because the right ventricle pumps returning blood into the aorta","In a pulmonary vein, because venous blood passes the lungs before it reaches the right heart","In a brain artery, because the inferior vena cava empties into the left atrium","In a lung artery, because venous blood returns to the right heart, which pumps only to the lungs"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A clot that breaks free from a leg vein simply follows normal venous return. It travels up the inferior vena cava into the right atrium, through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and out the pulmonary trunk. The pulmonary arteries branch into smaller and smaller vessels, so the clot lodges in one of them and blocks flow to part of the lung. This is a pulmonary embolism, which explains her sudden shortness of breath. The right heart pumps only to the lungs, so the clot is trapped there before it could reach the systemic arteries.","notes":["The right ventricle pumps into the pulmonary trunk, not the aorta. Only the left ventricle sends blood into the aorta and on to the coronary arteries.","Venous blood reaches the right heart first and only then travels to the lungs. A clot is caught in the narrowing lung arteries before it could ever reach a pulmonary vein.","The inferior vena cava empties into the right atrium, not the left. That is why a leg clot is carried to the lungs rather than out to the brain.","Correct. The clot follows normal venous return through the inferior vena cava and the right heart into the pulmonary trunk, where it lodges in a lung artery as a pulmonary embolism."],"fix":"Review the path of blood from the inferior vena cava through the right heart to the lungs, and how a deep vein thrombosis becomes a pulmonary embolism."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-routes-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-routes","comp":"bvn-vessel-tunics","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"Years of high blood pressure injure the smooth lining of a patient's coronary artery. Which tunic of the wall holds that lining, and what can develop at that spot?","options":["The tunica media; its elastic fibers fray and the wall tears away from nearby tissue","The tunica intima; plaque builds in the wall and a clot can form on its rough surface","The tunica externa; its collagen thickens and squeezes the vessel shut from outside","The tunica intima; its smooth muscle contracts and permanently narrows the lumen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The smooth inner lining of an artery is the endothelium of the tunica intima, the layer in direct contact with the blood. Hypertension strains and injures the vessel walls over time, and injury to this lining is the starting point of atherosclerosis. Fatty material, cells and debris collect in the wall as a plaque that bulges inward and narrows the lumen. The plaque also roughens the lining, and a clot can form on that rough surface and suddenly block the vessel. Knowing what each tunic is made of tells you which one is injured and why.","notes":["The tunica media is the middle layer of smooth muscle and elastic fibers, not the lining. The lining that blood touches belongs to the tunica intima.","Correct. The smooth lining is the endothelium of the tunica intima, and injury there is where an atherosclerotic plaque begins and where a clot can form on the roughened surface.","The tunica externa is the outermost layer of collagen that anchors the vessel to surrounding tissue. It is the farthest layer from the blood, not the lining that is injured here.","The tunica intima is the right layer, but it has no smooth muscle. It is a lining of simple squamous endothelium on a basement membrane, and the smooth muscle belongs to the tunica media."],"fix":"Review the three tunics of a vessel wall, the tissue in each, and how atherosclerosis begins in the tunica intima."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-routes-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-routes","comp":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A cashier who stands at work all day develops twisted, bulging superficial veins in her lower legs. Which structural failure best explains this?","options":["The tunica media of the leg arteries has weakened and ballooned outward under pressure","The precapillary sphincters stay open and flood the capillary beds of the lower leg","The venous valves, flaps of the tunica intima, no longer stop blood from flowing backward","Plaque has narrowed the leg arteries, so blood backs up into the superficial veins"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Veins return blood to the heart under low pressure, so they depend on one-way valves, which are flaps of the tunica intima, to keep blood from falling back. In the legs, blood has to climb against gravity, and standing for long periods adds to the load. When the valves of the superficial leg veins fail, blood flows backward and pools. The pooled blood stretches the thin vein walls until they become twisted and swollen, which is varicose veins. Arterial disorders narrow or weaken the wall, while venous disorders like this one involve failed valves and pooled blood.","notes":["A weakened artery wall that balloons outward is an aneurysm. It happens in high-pressure arteries such as the aorta, not in the superficial leg veins seen here.","Precapillary sphincters control how much blood enters a capillary bed. They are not what keeps venous blood from flowing backward in a leg vein.","Correct. When the valves fail, blood falls back and pools in the low-pressure veins, stretching them until they twist and bulge as varicose veins.","Plaque in the leg arteries is peripheral artery disease, which causes calf pain on walking because muscles get too little blood. It does not cause pooling in the superficial veins."],"fix":"Review the structure and role of venous valves and how their failure leads to varicose veins."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-routes-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-routes","comp":"bvn-vessel-disorders","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Four patients each have an artery affected by atherosclerosis. Which patient is most likely to suffer death of the tissue that the artery supplies?","options":["A clot on a plaque fully blocks an artery whose region has many anastomoses","A plaque narrows a leg artery and causes calf pain only during walking","A plaque narrows an artery slightly and has no clot on its surface","A clot on a plaque fully blocks an artery whose region has no anastomoses"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Three ideas decide this. First, a plaque narrows an artery slowly, but a clot that forms on its rough surface can block the vessel completely and suddenly. Second, how much the tissue suffers depends on whether blood has another way in. An anastomosis is a junction between vessels that offers an alternate route, so tissue served by anastomoses can still receive blood around a blockage. Third, a partial narrowing, as in peripheral artery disease, still lets some blood through. A complete block in a region with no anastomoses leaves the tissue with no supply at all, so it is the most likely to die.","notes":["The block is just as complete, but anastomoses give blood a collateral route around it. Neighboring arteries can still deliver some blood, so tissue death is less likely.","This is peripheral artery disease. Some blood still gets through, enough at rest, so the muscle is starved only during the extra demand of walking rather than dying.","A slight narrowing with no clot still lets most of the blood through. It is the earliest and least dangerous of these four situations.","Correct. A complete block cuts off flow through that artery, and with no anastomoses there is no backup route, so the tissue loses its blood supply and may die."],"fix":"Review how a clot on a plaque blocks an artery, and how anastomoses provide a backup route for blood."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-vessel-routes-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-vessel-routes","comp":"bvn-circulatory-routes","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A drop of blood leaves the left ventricle, supplies the wall of the small intestine, and travels on through the hepatic portal system. It then returns through the right heart and the lungs to the left atrium. Rank these vessels by how many capillary beds the drop has passed through when it reaches each one, from fewest to most: hepatic vein, aorta, pulmonary vein, hepatic portal vein.","options":["Aorta, hepatic portal vein, hepatic vein, pulmonary vein","Aorta, hepatic vein, hepatic portal vein, pulmonary vein","Aorta, hepatic portal vein, pulmonary vein, hepatic vein","Hepatic portal vein, aorta, hepatic vein, pulmonary vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A portal system is a route in which blood passes through two capillary beds in a row before returning to the heart. Leaving the left ventricle, the drop is in the aorta and has passed no capillary beds. It then passes the capillaries of the intestinal wall and collects in the hepatic portal vein (one bed), crosses the liver sinusoids and leaves in a hepatic vein (two beds). From the inferior vena cava it goes through the right heart and the pulmonary circuit, and after the lung capillaries it returns in a pulmonary vein (three beds). Counting capillary beds is a quick way to see what makes a portal route different from the usual loop.","notes":["Correct. The aorta comes before any capillary bed, the hepatic portal vein follows the intestinal capillaries, the hepatic vein follows the liver sinusoids, and the pulmonary vein follows the lung capillaries.","This swaps the two liver vessels. The hepatic portal vein carries blood into the liver and the hepatic vein drains it out, so the hepatic vein comes after the sinusoids, the second capillary bed.","The pulmonary vein is reached only after the drop has left the liver, crossed the right heart and passed through the lung capillaries. It has passed more capillary beds than the hepatic vein.","The hepatic portal vein is a vein, and it receives blood that has already passed through the capillaries of the intestine. The aorta carries blood straight from the left ventricle, before any capillary bed."],"fix":"Review what makes a portal system different, the route of the hepatic portal circulation, and the pulmonary circuit."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood","comp":"w4-hematopoiesis","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A red bone marrow smear shows four cells of the red cell line. Cell 1 has no nucleus but still holds ribosome remnants. Cell 2 is the first cell committed to becoming a red cell. Cell 3 is ejecting its nucleus as hemoglobin builds up. Cell 4 is an erythroblast. Put them in order from earliest to latest.","options":["4, 2, 3, 1","2, 4, 3, 1","2, 3, 4, 1","2, 4, 1, 3"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Each cell has to be identified before the line can be ordered. The first cell committed to the red line is the proerythroblast, which gives rise to the erythroblast. The normoblast is the stage that ejects its nucleus as hemoglobin builds up. A cell with no nucleus but leftover ribosomes is a reticulocyte, the last immature stage before a mature erythrocyte. So the order is cell 2, then 4, then 3, then 1.","notes":["Cell 2 is the proerythroblast, the first cell committed to the red line, so nothing in the line can come before it. The erythroblast develops from it.","Correct. The order is proerythroblast (cell 2), erythroblast (cell 4), normoblast (cell 3) and reticulocyte (cell 1), the last stage before a mature erythrocyte.","This places the normoblast before the erythroblast. The erythroblast comes directly after the proerythroblast, and the normoblast follows it.","The reticulocyte has already lost its nucleus, so it must come after the normoblast that ejects it. A cell cannot eject a nucleus it no longer has."],"fix":"Review the stages of erythropoiesis from the proerythroblast to the reticulocyte and what marks each one."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood","comp":"w4-hematopoiesis","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A drug damages the myeloid stem cells in a patient's red bone marrow but spares the lymphoid stem cells. Over the following months, which combination of problems is most likely?","options":["Anemia, easy bleeding and frequent infections, while lymphocyte numbers hold steady","Frequent infections only, because the myeloid line makes only the granulocytes","Anemia and infections, but normal clotting because platelets come from the lymphoid line","Low lymphocyte numbers only, because the myeloid line is the source of lymphocytes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Every formed element comes from the hemocytoblast, which divides into two committed lines. The lymphoid line makes only the lymphocytes, and the myeloid line makes everything else: erythrocytes, platelets by way of megakaryocytes, the granulocytes and the monocytes. If the myeloid line fails, each of those falls as the old cells wear out. Too few red cells is anemia, too few platelets raises the risk of bleeding, and too few neutrophils and other defenders invites infection. The lymphoid line was spared, so lymphocytes keep forming.","notes":["Correct. The myeloid line makes erythrocytes, platelets, granulocytes and monocytes, so losing it causes anemia, bleeding and infections, while the lymphoid line keeps making lymphocytes.","The myeloid line makes far more than the granulocytes. It is also the source of the erythrocytes and platelets, so anemia and bleeding would follow too.","Platelets come from megakaryocytes, which belong to the myeloid line, not the lymphoid line. Losing myeloid stem cells would therefore lower the platelet count as well.","This reverses the two lines. Lymphocytes come from the lymphoid line, which was spared, so their numbers would hold steady."],"fix":"Review the myeloid and lymphoid lines of hematopoiesis and which formed elements each one produces."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood","comp":"w4-blood-composition","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient has severe liver failure, and the liver can no longer make proteins normally. Which change in the plasma is most likely?","options":["Antibody levels fall sharply, but clotting and water balance stay unchanged","Clotting slows, but water balance is unchanged because plasma cells make albumin","Less water is held in the blood and clotting slows, but antibodies are still made","Less water is held in the blood, but clotting is unchanged because platelets make fibrinogen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"All plasma proteins except the gamma globulins are made in the liver. Albumin, the most abundant plasma protein, maintains the osmotic pressure that holds water in the blood, so losing it lets water leave the blood. Fibrinogen is the clotting protein that converts to fibrin threads, so losing it slows clotting. The gamma globulins are the antibodies, and they are made by plasma cells rather than the liver, so antibody production carries on. Knowing where each protein is made lets you predict which jobs fail when the liver fails.","notes":["This reverses which proteins depend on the liver. The antibodies are gamma globulins made by plasma cells, so they are the group least affected.","Albumin is made in the liver, not by plasma cells. Losing it means less of the protein that holds water in the blood, so water balance would change.","Correct. The liver makes albumin and fibrinogen, so both fall, while the antibodies (gamma globulins) are made by plasma cells, which keep making them.","Fibrinogen is a plasma protein made in the liver, not a product of platelets. With the liver failing, fibrinogen falls and clotting slows."],"fix":"Review the three main plasma proteins, where each is made, and what each one does."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood","comp":"w4-blood-disorders","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A man's spun blood sample shows a red layer that takes up a much larger share of the tube than normal, while the pale middle layer looks normal. Which disorder best fits, and how does it change the blood?","options":["Anemia, which thins the blood so it carries less oxygen","Leukemia, which crowds the blood with abnormal white cells","Hemophilia, which leaves too few clotting proteins in plasma","Polycythemia, which thickens the blood and slows its flow"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In a spun sample the red layer at the bottom is made of packed erythrocytes, and its share of the total is the hematocrit. A red layer much larger than normal means a high hematocrit, which means far too many red cells. An excess of erythrocytes is polycythemia. With so many cells packed in, the blood becomes thicker and flows more slowly. Reading which layer changed points you to the formed element involved.","notes":["Anemia means too few red cells or too little hemoglobin, which would make the red layer smaller than normal. Here it is larger.","Leukemia adds abnormal leukocytes, which sit in the pale buffy coat, not the red layer. It would thicken the middle layer of the tube.","Hemophilia is a lack of a clotting factor in the plasma. It does not change the share of the tube taken up by red cells.","Correct. The red layer shows the hematocrit, and a hematocrit far above normal means an excess of erythrocytes, which is polycythemia."],"fix":"Review what the hematocrit measures and which blood disorder affects each formed element."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood","comp":"w4-formed-elements","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these formed elements in a healthy person's blood from the most numerous to the least numerous: lymphocytes, erythrocytes, neutrophils, platelets.","options":["Erythrocytes, neutrophils, lymphocytes, platelets","Erythrocytes, platelets, neutrophils, lymphocytes","Platelets, erythrocytes, neutrophils, lymphocytes","Erythrocytes, platelets, lymphocytes, neutrophils"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"This ranking combines two orders you already know. Among the three formed elements, erythrocytes are by far the most numerous, platelets are intermediate, and leukocytes are the least numerous, at about 4,000 to 11,000 per microliter compared with 150,000 to 400,000 platelets. Within the leukocytes, the order runs neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils. Since neutrophils and lymphocytes are both leukocytes, they come after the platelets, with neutrophils first.","notes":["Platelets are small fragments, but they are far more numerous than any leukocyte, at about 150,000 to 400,000 per microliter. Students choose this when they confuse small size with small numbers.","Correct. Erythrocytes are by far the most numerous, platelets are intermediate, and among the leukocytes, neutrophils outnumber lymphocytes.","Platelets are intermediate in number, not the most numerous. Erythrocytes outnumber every other formed element by far.","Neutrophils are the most numerous leukocyte, at about 50 to 70 percent of white cells, while lymphocytes make up about 20 to 40 percent. This order swaps them."],"fix":"Review the relative numbers of the three formed elements and the order of the five leukocytes from most to least numerous."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m3-blood-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m3-blood","comp":"w4-blood-composition","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A lab needs to measure the amount of fibrinogen in a patient's blood. Which sample should the lab use?","options":["Serum from a tube in which the blood was left to clot","The buffy coat from a spun tube of blood","Plasma from a tube treated so the blood cannot clot","Packed red cells from the bottom of a spun tube"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Fibrinogen is the largest plasma protein and the clotting protein, and it converts to fibrin threads when blood clots. Plasma is the liquid matrix of blood and holds all the plasma proteins, including fibrinogen. Serum is what remains after blood has clotted, so it is plasma with the clotting proteins removed. To measure fibrinogen, the lab needs plasma from blood that was kept from clotting. Knowing the difference between plasma and serum tells you which sample still contains the protein.","notes":["Serum is the fluid left after blood clots, and the clotting proteins have been used up in the clot. Fibrinogen would already be gone from it.","The buffy coat is the thin layer of leukocytes and platelets. Fibrinogen is a dissolved protein in the plasma, not a part of this cell layer.","Correct. Fibrinogen is a plasma protein, and plasma keeps it only if the blood is kept from clotting.","The packed red cells are the erythrocytes. Fibrinogen is dissolved in the plasma above them, so this sample would contain almost none."],"fix":"Review the difference between plasma and serum and the role of fibrinogen."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-upper-respiratory-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-upper-respiratory","comp":"resp-upper-tract","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient has had a stuffy, runny nose for two weeks. Now she feels pressure and aching in both cheeks, just below the eyes, and the ache gets worse when she bends forward. Which explanation best fits her anatomy?","options":["The infection spread from the nasal cavity into the frontal sinuses, which drain into it","The infection spread from the nasopharynx into the auditory tubes, which open beside it","The infection spread from the nasal cavity into the maxillary sinuses, which drain into it","The infection spread from the nasal cavity into the sphenoid sinuses, which drain into it"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The paranasal sinuses are air-filled cavities in the frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, and maxillary bones, and each one is lined with the same mucosa as the nasal cavity and drains into it. Because the spaces connect, an infection in the nasal cavity can spread into a sinus. The location of the pain tells you which sinus is involved. The maxillary bones form the cheeks below the eyes, so aching there points to the maxillary sinuses.","notes":["The frontal sinuses are in the frontal bone, above the eyes in the forehead. This would be the answer if the pressure were across the forehead rather than in the cheeks.","The auditory tubes open into the lateral walls of the nasopharynx and connect the throat to the middle ear. An infection that travels this way causes ear pain and pressure, not aching in the cheeks.","Correct. The maxillary sinuses sit in the maxillary bones of the cheeks, and like all paranasal sinuses they share the nasal mucosa and drain into the nasal cavity.","The sphenoid sinuses are deep in the sphenoid bone, behind the nasal cavity in the middle of the skull. Students pick it because it is one of the four sinuses, but its location does not match pain in the cheeks."],"fix":"Review the four bones that hold the paranasal sinuses, where each bone sits in the face, and where the sinuses drain."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-upper-respiratory-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-upper-respiratory","comp":"resp-upper-tract","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A pathologist examines a small tissue sample taken from the wall of the pharynx. The lining is pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells, not stratified squamous epithelium. Which conclusion about where the sample came from is best supported?","options":["Above the soft palate, in the region that carries air only","Behind the oral cavity, in the region that holds the palatine tonsils","Below the epiglottis, in the region where the air and food paths divide","Behind the tongue, in the region that holds the lingual tonsils"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lining of each pharyngeal region matches what passes through it. The nasopharynx sits behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate, and the soft palate lifts to seal it during swallowing, so it carries air only. It keeps the pseudostratified ciliated columnar lining with goblet cells that sweeps mucus, like the rest of the airway. The oropharynx and laryngopharynx carry food as well as air, so they need the tougher stratified squamous epithelium to stand up to friction. A ciliated lining therefore places the sample in the nasopharynx.","notes":["Correct. Only the nasopharynx, above the soft palate, is spared from food, so only it keeps the delicate ciliated lining.","The palatine tonsils sit in the oropharynx, behind the mouth. Food rubs along this region, so it is lined with stratified squamous epithelium. This would fit a sample with many layers of flat surface cells.","The laryngopharynx runs from the epiglottis down to the esophagus and carries both air and food. Its lining is stratified squamous, so it does not match a ciliated sample.","The lingual tonsils at the base of the tongue face the oropharynx, which carries swallowed food. Students may pick it because tonsils guard the airway, but this region is lined with stratified squamous epithelium."],"fix":"Review the three regions of the pharynx, what each one carries, and the epithelium that lines each."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-upper-respiratory-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-upper-respiratory","comp":"resp-larynx","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A paramedic needs to find the cricothyroid membrane for an emergency airway. She runs a fingertip down the midline of the front of the neck, starting below the chin. Rank these structures in the order her fingertip meets them, from first to last.","options":["Cricoid cartilage, laryngeal prominence, cricothyroid membrane, tracheal rings","Laryngeal prominence, cricoid cartilage, cricothyroid membrane, tracheal rings","Laryngeal prominence, tracheal rings, cricothyroid membrane, cricoid cartilage","Laryngeal prominence, cricothyroid membrane, cricoid cartilage, tracheal rings"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The laryngeal prominence, or Adam's apple, is the forward ridge of the thyroid cartilage, the large shield near the top of the larynx. Directly below the thyroid cartilage is the cricoid cartilage, the only complete ring in the airway, and the cricothyroid membrane fills the gap between the two. Below the cricoid, the trachea begins with its C-shaped rings. So the fingertip slides off the prominence, dips into the soft membrane, meets the firm cricoid ring, and then reaches the tracheal rings.","notes":["The cricoid cartilage sits below the thyroid cartilage, not above it. Students who remember that the cricoid is a ring sometimes picture it at the top of the larynx, but it forms the bottom.","This puts the membrane below the cricoid cartilage. The cricothyroid membrane is named for the two cartilages it connects, so it must lie between the thyroid cartilage above and the cricoid below.","The tracheal rings are the lowest of these structures, since the trachea begins below the larynx. This order would only work if the trachea sat above the cricoid cartilage.","Correct. Thyroid cartilage, then the membrane, then the cricoid ring, then the trachea, from top to bottom."],"fix":"Review the order of the thyroid cartilage, cricoid cartilage, and trachea in the neck and where the cricothyroid membrane lies between them."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-upper-respiratory-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-upper-respiratory","comp":"resp-larynx","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"Suppose a disease stiffened the epiglottis so that it behaved like the hyaline cartilage of the thyroid and cricoid cartilages. Which problem would this patient most likely notice?","options":["A weak, hoarse voice, because the vocal folds could no longer vibrate","Coughing while drinking, because liquid would slip into the larynx","A collapsed airway, because the larynx would lose its firm framework","Food caught in the nasopharynx, because the soft palate could not lift"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The epiglottis is made of elastic cartilage, unlike the hyaline thyroid and cricoid cartilages, and that is what lets it bend down over the laryngeal inlet with every swallow and spring back afterward. Hyaline cartilage is firm and does not bend this way. A stiff epiglottis could not fold over the inlet, so food and especially liquid would slip into the larynx. The coughing fit is the airway trying to clear what got in.","notes":["Sound comes from the true vocal folds, which are moved by the arytenoid cartilages. The epiglottis sits above them and plays no part in vibration, so stiffening it would not change the voice this way.","Correct. An epiglottis that cannot bend cannot seal the laryngeal inlet, so swallowed liquid enters the airway and triggers coughing.","Stiffer cartilage holds an airway open more firmly, not less. The airway would be at risk of collapse if its cartilage softened or were lost, which is the opposite change.","The soft palate, not the epiglottis, closes off the nasopharynx during swallowing. A change in the epiglottis affects the opening into the larynx far below."],"fix":"Review the type of cartilage in the epiglottis and how its flexibility lets it cover the laryngeal inlet during a swallow."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-upper-respiratory-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-upper-respiratory","comp":"resp-zones","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A student claims that the upper respiratory tract and the conducting zone are just two names for the same set of structures. Which structure shows most clearly that the claim is wrong?","options":["The trachea, because it conducts air yet belongs to the lower tract","The pharynx, because it conducts air and belongs to the upper tract","The alveolar duct, because it exchanges gases in the lower tract","The nasal cavity, because it conditions air in the upper tract"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The two groupings sort the same parts in different ways. The tracts are grouped by location: the upper tract includes the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx, and the lower tract includes the trachea, bronchi, and lungs. The zones are grouped by function: the conducting zone runs from the nose all the way to the terminal bronchioles, and the respiratory zone is where gas exchange happens. To disprove the claim you need a structure that fits one group but not the other. The trachea only conducts air, so it is in the conducting zone, but it lies in the lower tract, so the two groups cannot be the same.","notes":["Correct. The trachea is a conducting airway in the lower tract, so the conducting zone reaches well beyond the upper tract.","The pharynx is in the upper tract and in the conducting zone, so it fits the student's claim instead of breaking it. A structure that disproves the claim must belong to one group and not the other.","The alveolar duct is in the lower tract and the respiratory zone, so it sits outside both groups named in the claim. It does not show that the upper tract and the conducting zone differ.","The nasal cavity is both in the upper tract and in the conducting zone. Like the pharynx, it is consistent with the claim, so it cannot prove it wrong."],"fix":"Review which structures belong to the upper and lower tracts and which belong to the conducting and respiratory zones, and notice where the two groupings do not line up."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-upper-respiratory-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-upper-respiratory","comp":"resp-histo","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A very fine dust particle is breathed in through the nose. It slips past each defense of the airway until it lands on the wall of an alveolus, where it is finally engulfed. In which order did it meet these defenses?","options":["Mucus over the conchae, vibrissae, ciliated tracheal lining, alveolar macrophages","Vibrissae, ciliated tracheal lining, mucus over the conchae, alveolar macrophages","Vibrissae, mucus over the conchae, alveolar macrophages, ciliated tracheal lining","Vibrissae, mucus over the conchae, ciliated tracheal lining, alveolar macrophages"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The airway cleans air in layers, in the same order that air travels. The coarse hairs of the nasal vestibule, just inside the nostrils, catch large particles first. Next, the conchae swirl air against the mucus-covered lining of the nasal cavity, where finer particles stick. Farther down, the ciliated lining of the trachea traps particles in mucus and sweeps them up toward the pharynx. The alveoli have no cilia or goblet cells, so anything that reaches them is engulfed by alveolar macrophages, the dust cells.","notes":["The vibrissae sit in the nasal vestibule, just inside the nostrils, so they come before the conchae deeper in the nasal cavity. This order would fit only if air passed the conchae before entering the nose.","The nasal conchae are in the nasal cavity, which air crosses long before it reaches the trachea in the lower tract. This order sends the particle down the neck and then back up into the nose.","The alveolar macrophages work at the very end of the airway, in the respiratory zone. Placing them before the trachea would mean the particle reached the alveoli before passing through the windpipe.","Correct. The defenses follow the path of air: vestibule hairs, nasal mucus, tracheal cilia, and then the macrophages that guard the alveoli."],"fix":"Review where the vibrissae, the conchae, the ciliated airway lining, and the alveolar macrophages sit along the path of inhaled air."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lower-respiratory-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lower-respiratory","comp":"resp-lungs-pleura","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A man is stabbed just above the medial part of his left clavicle, in the root of the neck. Shortly afterward, his left lung collapses. Which explanation best fits the anatomy?","options":["The base of the lung rises above the clavicle, so the blade cut lung tissue there","The apex of the lung rises above the clavicle, so the blade let air into the pleural cavity","The hilum of the lung lies above the clavicle, so the blade cut the main bronchus there","The cardiac notch lies above the clavicle, so the blade entered the space around the heart"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The apex of each lung is its narrow superior tip, and it rises just above the clavicle into the root of the neck, covered by the pleura. A deep wound there can pass through the parietal pleura into the pleural cavity. That cavity is normally sealed and holds only a thin film of fluid, and the seal is what holds the lung out against the chest wall. Once air enters, the seal is lost and the lung collapses, which is a pneumothorax.","notes":["The base is the broad inferior surface of the lung, resting on the diaphragm, at the opposite end from the clavicle. A wound would reach the base from the lower chest, not the neck.","Correct. The apex reaches above the clavicle, so a wound there can break into the pleural cavity, letting air in and the lung collapse.","The hilum is the indentation on the medial surface of the lung where the bronchus and vessels enter, deep in the chest beside the mediastinum. It does not rise into the neck.","The cardiac notch is an indentation along the anterior border of the left lung, beside the heart in the middle of the chest. It is nowhere near the clavicle."],"fix":"Review the apex, base, hilum, and cardiac notch of the lung, and why air in the pleural cavity collapses the lung."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lower-respiratory-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lower-respiratory","comp":"resp-disorders","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Picture the path from the trachea down through the airways of a lung and then out past its surface. Rank these disorders by where the structure each one mainly affects sits along that path, starting nearest the trachea and ending outside the lung.","options":["Asthma, chronic bronchitis, pneumonia, pneumothorax","Chronic bronchitis, asthma, pneumothorax, pneumonia","Chronic bronchitis, asthma, pneumonia, pneumothorax","Chronic bronchitis, pneumonia, asthma, pneumothorax"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each disorder is tied to one structure. Chronic bronchitis is long-lasting inflammation of the bronchi, the larger airways that branch first from the trachea. Asthma narrows the bronchioles, the small airways farther down the tree. Pneumonia fills the alveoli, the air sacs at the very end of the airways. Pneumothorax is air in the pleural cavity, the space between the visceral pleura on the lung surface and the parietal pleura on the chest wall, so it lies outside the lung entirely.","notes":["Asthma affects the bronchioles, which come after the bronchi in the branching tree. Chronic bronchitis affects the bronchi, so it must come first.","This places the pleural cavity inside the lung, before the alveoli. The pleural cavity surrounds the lung, outside the visceral pleura, so pneumothorax belongs at the far end of the path.","Correct. Bronchi, then bronchioles, then alveoli, then the pleural cavity outside the lung.","Pneumonia affects the alveoli, which lie at the ends of the airways beyond the bronchioles. Students who think of pneumonia as a general lung infection may place it too early in the path."],"fix":"Review which structure each respiratory disorder affects and the order of the bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli, and pleural cavity."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lower-respiratory-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lower-respiratory","comp":"resp-tree","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A small tumor is growing in one bronchopulmonary segment of the middle lobe. A physician passes a thin scope down the trachea to reach it. In which order does the scope pass these structures?","options":["Right main bronchus, carina, lobar bronchus, segmental bronchus","Carina, right main bronchus, lobar bronchus, segmental bronchus","Carina, right main bronchus, segmental bronchus, lobar bronchus","Carina, lobar bronchus, right main bronchus, segmental bronchus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The trachea ends at the carina, the ridge where it forks into the two main bronchi. Only the right lung has a middle lobe, so the scope must turn into the right main bronchus, which is also the wider and more vertical of the two. Each main bronchus gives off lobar bronchi, one per lobe, so the scope next enters the lobar bronchus to the middle lobe. That lobar bronchus divides into segmental bronchi, one per bronchopulmonary segment, and the segmental bronchus for that segment leads to the tumor.","notes":["The carina sits at the bottom of the trachea, where the main bronchi begin. A scope coming down the trachea reaches the carina before it can enter either main bronchus.","Correct. The scope passes the carina, enters the right main bronchus, then the lobar bronchus to the middle lobe, and finally the segmental bronchus for that segment.","Segmental bronchi are branches of the lobar bronchi, so they come after them. This order would require the airway to narrow to a single segment before it reached the lobe.","Lobar bronchi branch from the main bronchi inside the lung. A scope cannot reach a lobar bronchus without first passing through the main bronchus it comes from."],"fix":"Review the branching order of the bronchial tree from the carina to the segmental bronchi and which lung has a middle lobe."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lower-respiratory-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lower-respiratory","comp":"bvn-vessel-tunics","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Oxygen crosses the respiratory membrane: the alveolar epithelium, the fused basement membranes, and the capillary endothelium. Which statement best explains why the pulmonary capillary adds so little thickness to this barrier?","options":["Its wall keeps a thin tunica media but has lost its tunica externa","Its tunica externa merges with the alveolar wall to form a basement membrane","Its wall has all three tunics, but each one is only a single cell thick","Its wall is only a tunica intima, endothelium on a basement membrane"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Arteries and veins have three tunics: the tunica intima (endothelium on a basement membrane), the tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic tissue, and the tunica externa of connective tissue. A capillary keeps only the tunica intima. That single layer of flat endothelial cells is the capillary's share of the respiratory membrane, and its basement membrane fuses with the basement membrane of the alveolus. With no muscle or connective tissue layers, the capillary adds almost nothing to the distance between air and blood.","notes":["Capillaries have no tunica media at all, so there is no smooth muscle in their walls. Students who pick this may be thinking of an arteriole, which still has a thin layer of smooth muscle in its wall.","The fused basement membranes come from the alveolar epithelium and the capillary endothelium, which each rest on one. Capillaries have no tunica externa to contribute, so this mixes up which layers are present.","Capillaries do not keep scaled-down versions of every tunic. Students often assume all vessels have the same three layers, but capillaries are the exception and keep only the inner lining.","Correct. A capillary wall is just the tunica intima, so it adds only a thin endothelium and its basement membrane to the barrier."],"fix":"Review the three tunics of a vessel wall, the tissue in each, and which tunic is the only one in a capillary."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lower-respiratory-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lower-respiratory","comp":"cv-blood-pathway","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient has a badly narrowed pulmonary valve, so less blood than normal can get through it. Blood begins to back up behind the narrowed valve. Which structure is the first to fill with this backed-up blood?","options":["The right atrium, which empties into the right ventricle","The pulmonary trunk, which carries blood toward the lungs","The right ventricle, which pumps blood through that valve","The pulmonary capillaries, which wrap around the alveoli"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood in the right atrium passes the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, and the right ventricle pumps it out through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. The pulmonary arteries then carry it to the capillaries that wrap each alveolus, and the pulmonary veins return it to the left atrium. Blood that cannot get through a narrowed valve stays in the chamber just upstream of that valve. For the pulmonary valve, that chamber is the right ventricle. The structures past the valve, the pulmonary trunk and the capillaries around the alveoli, receive less blood instead, and the right atrium sits one step farther back, behind the tricuspid valve.","notes":["The right atrium is upstream of the right ventricle, with the tricuspid valve between them, so it is not the first place blood collects. It would be the answer if the tricuspid valve, the valve at the exit of the right atrium, were the narrowed one.","The pulmonary trunk lies just past the pulmonary valve, so a narrowed valve sends it less blood, not more. It would be the answer only if the narrowing sat farther along, where the trunk divides into the pulmonary arteries.","Correct. The right ventricle pumps blood out through the pulmonary valve, so it is the chamber directly behind the narrowing and the first to hold blood that cannot get out.","The pulmonary capillaries are downstream in the lungs, so they receive less blood when the valve is narrowed. Blood would collect around the alveoli only with a blockage past them, such as in the pulmonary veins or the left side of the heart."],"fix":"Review the pulmonary circuit from the right atrium through the right ventricle and pulmonary valve to the alveolar capillaries, and which structures lie before and after each valve."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lower-respiratory-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lower-respiratory","comp":"resp-histo","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A toxic drug damages the cuboidal cells scattered in the alveolar walls but leaves the thin, flat cells around them unharmed. Which change in the lungs is most likely?","options":["Alveoli collapse more easily, because less surfactant is being made","Gases cross the wall more slowly, because the main wall cells are lost","Debris builds up on the alveoli, because the dust cells are lost","Small alveoli merge into large sacs, because the walls break down"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The alveolar wall has two kinds of epithelial cells. Type I cells are thin and flat, form most of the wall, and are the cells gases cross. Type II cells are cuboidal and scattered among them, and their job is to secrete surfactant. Surfactant lowers surface tension in the film of water lining each alveolus, which keeps the tiny sacs from collapsing. Damage limited to the cuboidal cells cuts surfactant, so collapse becomes more likely while the flat cells keep the wall thin.","notes":["Correct. The cuboidal cells are type II alveolar cells, and losing them reduces the surfactant that keeps alveoli open.","The thin, flat cells that gases cross are the type I cells, and the stem says they were spared. This would be the answer if the flat cells had been damaged instead.","The dust cells are alveolar macrophages, which wander over the alveolar surface. They are not the cuboidal cells built into the wall, so their loss is not what the stem describes.","Merging of small alveoli into large sacs is emphysema, where the walls themselves are destroyed. Here the flat cells that make up most of the wall are intact."],"fix":"Review the type I and type II alveolar cells, how to tell them apart by shape, and what each one does."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-vessels-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-vessels","comp":"w4-lymph-pathway","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A mass in the upper abdomen presses on the thoracic duct just above the cisterna chyli, blocking flow at that spot. Lymph from which regions would back up behind the block?","options":["The left arm and left side of the head, but not the lower limbs","Everything except the right arm and right side of the head and thorax","Only the left lower limb and the left side of the pelvis","Both lower limbs, the pelvis, and the gut, but not the left arm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thoracic duct begins at the cisterna chyli, the sac in the abdomen that receives both lumbar trunks and the intestinal trunk. Those trunks drain the lower limbs, pelvis, abdominal wall, and digestive organs, so all of that lymph must pass the spot just above the sac. The left jugular, left subclavian, and left bronchomediastinal trunks join the duct higher up, in the thorax and neck, near where it ends at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. A block low in the duct therefore stops lymph from the lower body but not from the upper left side.","notes":["Lymph from the left arm and left side of the head enters the thoracic duct high in the chest and neck, above the block. Those regions would still drain, while the lower body would not.","This is the whole territory of the thoracic duct. It would be the answer if the block sat at the end of the duct in the neck, where every trunk has already joined.","Both lumbar trunks empty into the cisterna chyli, so the right lower limb drains through the same spot as the left. A block above the sac traps lymph from both sides.","Correct. The lumbar and intestinal trunks enter below the block, while the left upper trunks join above it."],"fix":"Review which trunks empty into the cisterna chyli, which join the thoracic duct higher up, and what region each trunk drains."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-vessels-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-vessels","comp":"w4-lymph-pathway","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"After a fatty meal, absorbed fat enters lymph in the wall of the small intestine. In which order does this fat-rich lymph pass these structures on its way back to the blood?","options":["Lacteal, intestinal trunk, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct","Lacteal, cisterna chyli, intestinal trunk, thoracic duct","Intestinal trunk, lacteal, cisterna chyli, thoracic duct","Lacteal, intestinal trunk, thoracic duct, cisterna chyli"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Dietary fat is too large to enter the blood capillaries of a villus, so it enters the lacteal, the lymphatic capillary in the center of the villus, and the lymph turns milky (chyle). Collecting vessels carry the chyle through nodes to the intestinal trunk, which drains the digestive organs. The intestinal trunk empties into the cisterna chyli, the sac in the abdomen where the thoracic duct begins. The thoracic duct then carries the chyle up through the thorax to the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins.","notes":["Correct. Lacteal, intestinal trunk, cisterna chyli, and then the thoracic duct, the same order as capillary, trunk, and duct.","The cisterna chyli receives the intestinal trunk, so the trunk must come first. Lymph moves from smaller vessels into larger ones, and the trunk is upstream of the sac.","The lacteal is where the fat first enters lymph, so it has to be the starting point. A trunk forms only after many collecting vessels merge.","The cisterna chyli is the beginning of the thoracic duct, not a sac at its end. The duct ends in the neck, where it joins the veins."],"fix":"Review the lacteals, the intestinal trunk, the cisterna chyli, and the thoracic duct in the order chyle travels."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-vessels-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-vessels","comp":"w4-lymph-vessels-node","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"Suppose a disease destroyed the anchoring filaments around the lymphatic capillaries of one leg, while the vessel walls and valves stayed normal. What would most likely happen when fluid builds up in the tissues of that leg?","options":["Lymph would flow backward in the collecting vessels toward the foot","Lymph would leak back out of the capillaries into the tissue spaces","The capillaries would stay collapsed, so less fluid would drain away","The capillary cells would join tightly, so proteins could not enter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A lymphatic capillary has no blood pressure inside to hold it open, so anchoring filaments tie its endothelial cells to the surrounding tissue. When fluid builds up and the tissue swells, the filaments are pulled and they tug the cells apart, widening the gaps so more fluid can enter. Without the filaments, swelling tissue would press the thin capillaries shut instead of opening them. Less fluid would drain, so the swelling would get worse.","notes":["Backflow in collecting vessels is prevented by their valves, which the stem says are normal. This would be the answer if the valves had failed.","Fluid is kept from leaking back out by the overlapping endothelial cells, which act as one-way flaps. The anchoring filaments hold the capillary open but do not form the flaps.","Correct. Anchoring filaments keep lymphatic capillaries open and widen their gaps as tissue swells, so losing them lets the capillaries collapse.","How tightly the cells join is a feature of the endothelium itself, not the filaments outside it. Losing the filaments affects whether the capillary stays open, not which particles fit through."],"fix":"Review how anchoring filaments, overlapping endothelial cells, and valves each help lymphatic vessels take up and move fluid."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-vessels-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-vessels","comp":"w4-lymph-vessels-node","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A student is drawing blood and lymphatic vessels to scale for a study sheet. Rank these vessels from the narrowest to the widest.","options":["Lymphatic capillary, blood capillary, collecting vessel, thoracic duct","Blood capillary, lymphatic capillary, collecting vessel, thoracic duct","Blood capillary, collecting vessel, lymphatic capillary, thoracic duct","Blood capillary, lymphatic capillary, thoracic duct, collecting vessel"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood capillaries are so narrow that red blood cells pass through them nearly single file. Lymphatic capillaries are slightly wider, and their loosely overlapping endothelial cells let them take up proteins, cells, and other large particles. Lymphatic capillaries drain into collecting vessels, which are larger because many capillaries feed each one. The thoracic duct, formed after the trunks merge, is the largest lymphatic vessel in the body.","notes":["Lymphatic capillaries are wider than blood capillaries, not narrower. Their loose, overlapping cell junctions are also what let them take in large particles that blood capillaries cannot.","Correct. Blood capillary, then the slightly wider lymphatic capillary, then the collecting vessel, then the thoracic duct.","Collecting vessels receive lymph from many lymphatic capillaries, so they are larger than the capillaries that feed them. Lymph moves into larger and larger vessels as it travels.","The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel in the body. Collecting vessels merge into trunks and then ducts, so a collecting vessel cannot be wider than the thoracic duct."],"fix":"Review how lymphatic capillaries compare with blood capillaries and how lymph moves into larger vessels on its way to the ducts."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-vessels-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-vessels","comp":"w4-lymphatic-disorders","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Each of these patients has a swollen body part. Which patient's swelling is best called lymphedema rather than ordinary edema?","options":["A traveler whose ankles swell on a long flight and go down after a walk","A runner whose sprained ankle swells for a few days, then returns to normal","A child with a sore throat whose neck nodes are swollen and tender","A woman whose arm stays swollen for months after axillary node removal"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Edema is swelling of the interstitial space whenever fluid leaks out of blood capillaries faster than the lymphatics can drain it, and it goes away once drainage catches up. Lymphedema is long-term swelling caused by blocked or missing lymphatic vessels. Removing many axillary nodes also removes the vessels that pass through them, so the arm loses part of its drainage route for good. That lasting loss of vessels is what makes this patient's swelling lymphedema.","notes":["Sitting still on a flight slows the skeletal muscle pump, so fluid pools in the ankles. Walking restarts the pump and the swelling resolves, which makes this ordinary edema.","An injury makes blood capillaries leak more fluid than the lymphatics can carry away for a while. The vessels themselves are intact, so this is edema that resolves.","Swollen, tender nodes during an infection are lymphadenitis, a disorder of the lymph nodes. The nodes are working hard, and no limb is swelling from lost drainage.","Correct. Removing axillary nodes takes the lymphatic vessels with them, leaving long-term swelling from lost drainage."],"fix":"Review the difference between edema and lymphedema and the structure each one affects."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-vessels-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-vessels","comp":"w4-lymphatic-organs","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A gardener has an infected cut on the palm of her right hand. A day later, a clinician feels for swollen lymph nodes. Which node cluster is most likely to be enlarged and tender?","options":["The inguinal nodes on the right side","The cervical nodes on the left side","The axillary nodes on the right side","The axillary nodes on the left side"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Lymph from the hand travels up the arm in collecting vessels, which pass through lymph nodes before reaching a trunk. The large cluster that receives lymph from the upper limb is in the axilla, the armpit, on the same side. Nodes swell and become tender when their lymphocytes multiply to fight an infection in the region they drain. So the right axillary nodes are the ones a clinician expects to find enlarged.","notes":["The inguinal nodes in the groin filter lymph from the lower limb and lower body. They would swell with an infected cut on the foot or leg, not the hand.","The cervical nodes in the neck filter lymph from the head and neck. They are the nodes that swell with a throat infection, and these are also on the wrong side.","Correct. Lymph from the right upper limb is filtered by the axillary nodes in the right armpit before it reaches the right subclavian trunk.","Lymph from one arm does not cross the midline to the opposite armpit. The left axillary nodes would swell with an infection in the left hand."],"fix":"Review where the cervical, axillary, and inguinal node clusters sit and which region each one filters."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-organs-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-organs","comp":"w4-lymphatic-organs","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A baby born without a thymus develops very few working T cells. An older adult whose thymus is removed during chest surgery keeps a normal supply of T cells. Which explanation best accounts for the difference?","options":["In adults, mature T cells made earlier still live in secondary organs","In adults, red bone marrow takes over the job of maturing T cells","In adults, the spleen begins making T cells to replace the thymus","In adults, T cells are no longer needed, so losing them does no harm"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"All lymphocytes form in red bone marrow, but T cell precursors must travel to the thymus to mature. The thymus is largest in infancy and childhood, when the body builds its supply of mature T cells, and it shrinks after puberty until it is mostly fat. Those mature T cells leave for the secondary organs, where they live and divide. An adult already has that supply, while a baby without a thymus never builds one.","notes":["Correct. The thymus did its work early in life, and the mature T cells it produced continue living and dividing in the secondary organs.","Red bone marrow is where B cells mature, but T cells must finish maturing in the thymus. Losing the thymus does not shift that job to the marrow.","The spleen is a secondary organ where mature lymphocytes meet blood-borne pathogens. It filters blood but does not make or mature T cells.","Adults still rely on T cells, which make up about 80 percent of circulating lymphocytes and attack infected cells directly. The adult is fine because the T cells are still there, not because they are unneeded."],"fix":"Review where T cells form and mature, how the thymus changes over a lifetime, and where mature T cells live."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-organs-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-organs","comp":"w4-lymphatic-disorders","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"After a car crash, a patient's ruptured spleen is removed. Which change is most expected after the splenectomy?","options":["T cells can no longer mature, so the body loses cell-mediated immunity","Lymph from the lower limbs is no longer filtered before reaching the blood","Fat absorbed in the small intestine can no longer reach the thoracic duct","The liver and marrow clear old red cells, and blood infections become riskier"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The spleen is the lymphatic organ that filters blood. Its red pulp removes worn-out red cells and platelets, and its white pulp responds to pathogens carried in the blood. After a splenectomy, the liver and red bone marrow take over removing old red cells, but no other organ fully replaces the spleen's screening of the blood for bacteria. That is why people without a spleen are at higher risk of serious bacterial infections and need extra vaccines.","notes":["T cells mature in the thymus, a primary organ in the mediastinum. The spleen is a secondary organ, so removing it does not stop T cell maturation.","Lymph from the lower limbs is filtered by lymph nodes, such as the inguinal nodes, on its way to the lumbar trunks. The spleen filters blood, not lymph.","Fat enters the lacteals and travels as chyle through the intestinal trunk to the cisterna chyli. The spleen is not on that route.","Correct. The liver and red bone marrow take over clearing old red cells, but the loss of blood filtering raises the risk of serious bacterial infection."],"fix":"Review what the red and white pulp of the spleen do, what the spleen filters, and what happens after a splenectomy."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-organs-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-organs","comp":"w4-lymphatic-disorders","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Four patients have enlarged lymphatic structures. Which finding points most strongly to cancer cells spreading through lymph, rather than to an infection?","options":["A firm, painless axillary node on the same side as a breast tumor","Tender, swollen cervical nodes in a child who has strep throat","An enlarged spleen in a teenager recovering from mononucleosis","Red, swollen palatine tonsils in a child with fever and sore throat"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lymphatic capillaries are permeable enough to take in cancer cells, and lymph carries those cells to the first node the region drains into. Lymph from the breast drains largely to the axillary nodes on the same side, so a tumor there sends cells to that armpit. A node filled with cancer cells becomes firm and is usually not tender. Nodes, tonsils, and a spleen that swell while fighting an infection are usually tender or come with signs of infection, such as fever or a sore throat.","notes":["Correct. A firm, painless node in the region that drains the tumor is the typical sign of metastasis to a node.","Tender, swollen nodes during a throat infection are lymphadenitis, nodes enlarged while fighting the infection. The tenderness and the strep throat point to infection.","An enlarged spleen during mononucleosis is splenomegaly caused by the Epstein-Barr virus. It is a known effect of that infection, not a sign of cancer spreading through lymph.","Red, swollen palatine tonsils with fever are tonsillitis, an infection of the tonsils. It is common in children and does not involve cancer cells traveling in lymph."],"fix":"Review the lymphatic disorders by the structure each one affects, how a node holding cancer differs from a node fighting infection, and where the breast drains."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-organs-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-organs","comp":"w4-lymph-vessels-node","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A bacterium carried in lymph enters a lymph node through an afferent vessel. If no macrophage catches it, in which order does it pass these parts of the node before leaving?","options":["Cortical sinuses, subcapsular sinus, medullary sinuses, efferent vessel at the hilum","Subcapsular sinus, medullary sinuses, cortical sinuses, efferent vessel at the hilum","Subcapsular sinus, cortical sinuses, medullary sinuses, efferent vessel at the hilum","Subcapsular sinus, cortical sinuses, efferent vessel at the hilum, medullary sinuses"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Afferent vessels pierce the capsule at many points around the node's rounded surface. Lymph first collects in the subcapsular sinus just under the capsule, then trickles inward through the cortical sinuses past the B cell follicles and the T cells of the deep cortex. It then passes through the medullary sinuses, which are lined with macrophages. Finally it leaves through the efferent vessels at the hilum, the indented side of the node.","notes":["The subcapsular sinus lies just under the capsule, where the afferent vessels enter, so it must come first. The cortical sinuses are deeper.","The cortex lies outside the medulla, so lymph passes the cortical sinuses before the medullary sinuses. This order jumps inward to the medulla and then back out.","Correct. Subcapsular sinus, cortical sinuses, medullary sinuses, and then out through an efferent vessel at the hilum.","The efferent vessels leave at the hilum after the medulla, so they are the last step. Lymph cannot exit the node and then return to the medullary sinuses."],"fix":"Review the parts of a lymph node and the order lymph flows through them from the afferent vessels to the hilum."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-organs-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-organs","comp":"w4-lymphatic-organs","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these lymphatic organs and tissues by their position in the body, from most superior to most inferior, in a person standing in anatomical position.","options":["Thymus, pharyngeal tonsil, spleen, Peyer patches","Pharyngeal tonsil, spleen, thymus, Peyer patches","Pharyngeal tonsil, thymus, Peyer patches, spleen","Pharyngeal tonsil, thymus, spleen, Peyer patches"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The pharyngeal tonsil sits on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, behind the nasal cavity in the head. The thymus lies in the mediastinum of the thorax, above the heart and behind the sternum. The spleen is in the upper left abdomen against the diaphragm, under the lower left ribs. Peyer patches are in the wall of the ileum, the last part of the small intestine, which lies lower in the abdomen where it joins the cecum.","notes":["The pharyngeal tonsil is in the nasopharynx, in the head, so it lies above the thymus in the chest. This order would fit only if the thymus sat above the throat.","The thymus is in the thorax and the spleen is in the abdomen, below the diaphragm. The thymus must therefore come before the spleen.","The spleen sits high in the abdomen, protected by the lower ribs, while the ileum lies lower down near the cecum. Peyer patches are therefore below the spleen.","Correct. Head, then thorax, then upper abdomen, then lower abdomen."],"fix":"Review where the tonsils, thymus, spleen, and Peyer patches are located in the body."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-lymph-organs-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-lymph-organs","comp":"w4-lymph-pathway","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A melanoma is removed from the skin on the front of a patient's left thigh. To check whether cancer cells have begun to travel in lymph, the surgeon samples the sentinel node. Which pair names the most likely sentinel node group and the trunk the cells would reach if they got past those nodes?","options":["The left axillary nodes, then the left subclavian trunk","The left inguinal nodes, then the left lumbar trunk","The left inguinal nodes, then the intestinal trunk","The right inguinal nodes, then the right lumbar trunk"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sentinel node is the first node a region drains into, and cancer cells traveling in lymph lodge there first. Lymph from the skin of the thigh travels in collecting vessels to the inguinal nodes in the groin on the same side. Past those nodes, lymph from the lower limb continues to the lumbar trunk on that side, which drains the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdominal wall. The lumbar trunk then empties into the cisterna chyli at the start of the thoracic duct.","notes":["The axillary nodes filter lymph from the upper limb and the breast. They would be the sentinel group for a tumor on the arm or chest, not the thigh.","Correct. Thigh skin drains to the inguinal nodes on the same side, and lymph from the lower limb continues into the lumbar trunk.","The inguinal nodes are right, but the intestinal trunk drains the digestive organs of the abdomen. Lymph from a lower limb travels in a lumbar trunk.","Lymph from one lower limb drains to the nodes and lumbar trunk on its own side. The right-side route would be the answer for a tumor on the right thigh."],"fix":"Review where the inguinal nodes sit, which trunk drains the lower limbs, and what a sentinel node is."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-endocrine-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-endocrine","comp":"w6-endocrine-locate","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A radiologist reviews a full-body scan from the top of the head downward. Rank these endocrine glands in the order they appear, from most superior to most inferior.","options":["Parathyroid glands, pituitary gland, thymus, pancreatic islets","Pituitary gland, thymus, parathyroid glands, pancreatic islets","Pituitary gland, parathyroid glands, thymus, pancreatic islets","Pituitary gland, parathyroid glands, pancreatic islets, thymus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The pituitary gland sits in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, in the skull, so it is highest. The parathyroid glands are embedded in the posterior surface of the thyroid in the neck. The thymus is lower, in the mediastinum behind the sternum. The pancreatic islets are scattered through the pancreas, which lies behind the stomach in the abdomen, so they are the most inferior of the four.","notes":["The pituitary gland is in the skull base, hanging below the hypothalamus. It lies above the neck, so it must come before the parathyroid glands.","The thymus is in the mediastinum of the chest, below the neck. The parathyroid glands sit on the back of the thyroid in the neck, so they come before the thymus.","Correct. The pituitary is in the skull, the parathyroids are in the neck, the thymus is in the thorax, and the islets are in the abdomen.","The pancreas lies behind the stomach in the abdomen, below the diaphragm. The thymus is in the chest, so it comes before the pancreatic islets."],"fix":"Review where each major endocrine gland sits, from the skull down to the pelvis."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-endocrine-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-endocrine","comp":"w6-pituitary","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A severe head injury tears the infundibulum. Which statement best describes the links between the hypothalamus and the pituitary that are cut?","options":["Only the nerve tract, since portal vessels reach the anterior lobe another way","Both, since the nerve tract and the portal vessels run along the stalk","Neither, since the infundibulum holds only connective tissue and no links","Only the portal vessels, since the posterior lobe attaches straight to the brain"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The infundibulum is the slender stalk that connects the pituitary gland to the hypothalamus. The posterior lobe is nervous tissue, so the hypothalamus reaches it through a tract of nerve fibers running down through the stalk. The anterior lobe is glandular epithelium, so the hypothalamus reaches it through blood, using the hypophyseal portal system, whose vessels run along the same stalk. Tearing the infundibulum therefore cuts both links at once.","notes":["The nerve tract is cut, but the portal vessels to the anterior lobe also run along the infundibulum. They do not take a separate route to the anterior lobe.","Correct. Both the nerve tract to the posterior lobe and the portal vessels to the anterior lobe travel along the infundibulum.","The infundibulum carries both the nerve fiber tract and the portal vessels, so it is the physical link between the brain and the pituitary. It is more than a simple supporting stalk.","The posterior lobe is connected to the hypothalamus by axons that pass down through the infundibulum, not by a direct attachment. Its nerve tract is cut along with the portal vessels."],"fix":"Review the tissue of each pituitary lobe and how each lobe connects to the hypothalamus through the infundibulum."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-endocrine-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-endocrine","comp":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A biopsy from the anterior neck shows many hollow spheres, each ringed by a single layer of cells and filled with a pink, protein-rich fluid. A few pale cells sit in the spaces between the spheres. Which gland was sampled, and what are the pale cells between the spheres?","options":["The thyroid gland; the pale cells are parafollicular cells","The thyroid gland; the pale cells are chief cells","The parathyroid gland; the pale cells are its follicular cells","The parathyroid gland; the pale cells are chief cells"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Hollow spheres ringed by one layer of cells and filled with protein-rich fluid are thyroid follicles, and the fluid is colloid. The cells forming each ring are follicular cells. Parafollicular cells, also called C cells, sit in the spaces between the follicles. The parathyroid glands are built differently, with chief cells as their main cells, and they are not built of the colloid-filled follicles that mark the thyroid.","notes":["Correct. Colloid-filled follicles mark the thyroid, and the cells between the follicles are the parafollicular cells, also called C cells.","Chief cells are the main cells of the parathyroid glands, not the thyroid. Students mix them up because the parathyroids sit on the back of the thyroid, but the cell name tells the two glands apart.","Follicular cells belong to the thyroid, where they form the walls of the follicles, not the spaces between them. The parathyroid glands are made mainly of chief cells.","Chief cells are the right cells for the parathyroid glands, but those glands are not built of colloid-filled follicles. The follicles in this sample point to the thyroid."],"fix":"Review the thyroid follicle, colloid, follicular and parafollicular cells, and the chief cells of the parathyroid glands."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-endocrine-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-endocrine","comp":"w6-adrenal","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A pathologist's biopsy needle enters an adrenal gland through its capsule and passes straight to the center. In which order does it pass these tissues?","options":["Cells in straight columns, cells in rounded clusters, a branching network, chromaffin cells","Chromaffin cells, cells in rounded clusters, cells in straight columns, a branching network","Cells in rounded clusters, a branching network, cells in straight columns, chromaffin cells","Cells in rounded clusters, cells in straight columns, a branching network, chromaffin cells"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The adrenal cortex has three zones, and each zone's name describes how its cells are arranged. Just under the capsule is the zona glomerulosa, a thin zone of rounded clusters. Next is the zona fasciculata, the thick middle zone with its cells in straight columns. The zona reticularis, a branching network, lies deepest in the cortex against the medulla. The medulla at the center is made of chromaffin cells, which are modified sympathetic nervous tissue.","notes":["The straight columns belong to the zona fasciculata, the middle zone. The rounded clusters of the zona glomerulosa sit just under the capsule, so the needle meets them first.","Chromaffin cells make up the adrenal medulla, the inner core. A needle entering from the capsule reaches the medulla last, after crossing all three zones of the cortex.","The branching network is the zona reticularis, the deepest zone of the cortex. It lies against the medulla, so it comes after the straight columns of the zona fasciculata, not before.","Correct. Glomerulosa, fasciculata, reticularis, and then the chromaffin cells of the medulla."],"fix":"Review the three zones of the adrenal cortex, how the cells in each are arranged, and what lies at the center of the gland."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-endocrine-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-endocrine","comp":"w6-adrenal","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"Imaging ordered to check a patient's right kidney shows a small mass sitting on its superior pole. A biopsy of the mass finds chromaffin cells. Where is the mass, and what kind of tissue is that region made of?","options":["The adrenal cortex, which is modified sympathetic nervous tissue","The adrenal medulla, which is modified sympathetic nervous tissue","The zona reticularis, which is glandular tissue against the medulla","The zona glomerulosa, which is glandular tissue under the capsule"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The gland that caps the superior pole of each kidney is the adrenal, or suprarenal, gland, which is why kidney imaging often captures it. Each adrenal gland is two glands in one. The outer cortex is glandular tissue in three zones, while the inner medulla is modified nervous tissue belonging to the sympathetic nervous system. Chromaffin cells are the cells of the medulla, so a mass made of them arises from the adrenal medulla.","notes":["The adrenal cortex is glandular tissue, not nervous tissue, and it has no chromaffin cells. This pairs the right tissue with the wrong region of the gland.","Correct. Chromaffin cells are found in the adrenal medulla, the inner core of modified sympathetic nervous tissue.","The zona reticularis does border the medulla, but it is part of the glandular cortex. Chromaffin cells lie just past it, in the medulla itself.","The zona glomerulosa is the thin outer zone of the cortex, just under the capsule. It is glandular tissue with rounded clusters of cells, not chromaffin cells."],"fix":"Review where the adrenal glands sit, the tissue of the adrenal cortex and medulla, and which region holds the chromaffin cells."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-endocrine-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-endocrine","comp":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On a pancreas slide, inflammation has destroyed most of the darkly staining glandular clusters, but the pale, rounded islands scattered among them look normal. Which statement best describes what this pancreas has lost and what it keeps?","options":["It loses enzyme delivery through its ducts but keeps hormone release into the blood","It loses both jobs, because the pale islands send their hormones out through the ducts","It loses hormone release into the blood but keeps enzyme delivery through its ducts","It keeps both jobs, because the pale islands also make the digestive enzymes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Most of the pancreas is made of acini, darkly staining exocrine clusters that make digestive enzymes and send them through ducts to the small intestine. Scattered among them are the pancreatic islets, the pale islands of endocrine cells. The islets are ductless and release their hormones directly into the blood. Losing the acini takes away the exocrine enzyme job, while the intact islets keep the endocrine job going.","notes":["Correct. The destroyed acini were the exocrine enzyme makers, and the surviving islets still release hormones into the blood.","The islets are endocrine tissue, so they do not use ducts at all. They release hormones straight into the blood, which is why they keep working here.","This reverses the two tissues. The dark clusters are the exocrine acini that make enzymes, and the pale islands are the endocrine islets, which are the ones that survived.","The islets are endocrine cells that make hormones, not digestive enzymes. Enzyme production belongs to the acini, which were destroyed."],"fix":"Review the acini and the pancreatic islets, how each looks on a slide, and how each one delivers its product."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-gi-wall-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-gi-wall","comp":"w5-canal-wall","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A student examines a cross section from somewhere along the alimentary canal. It has a mucosa, a submucosa, and a muscularis with only two muscle layers, and its outermost layer is fibrous connective tissue that blends into the surrounding tissue with no serous membrane. Which region is the section most likely from?","options":["The body of the stomach","The jejunum of the small intestine","The esophagus in the thorax","The transverse colon"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The outer layer is the deciding clue. A fibrous adventitia in place of a serosa all the way around the tube means the section comes from a part of the canal outside the peritoneal cavity, and the esophagus is that part, because it runs through the thorax where there is no visceral peritoneum to cover it. The two-layer muscularis fits the esophagus too and also rules out the stomach, which has an extra oblique layer. The jejunum and the transverse colon both hang inside the peritoneal cavity on folds (the mesentery and the mesocolon), so their outer layer is a serosa. Only the esophagus in the thorax fits every feature on the slide.","notes":["The stomach lies in the peritoneal cavity, so its outer layer is a serosa, and its muscularis has three layers, not two. This would be the answer if the slide showed an extra oblique muscle layer and a serous outer covering.","The jejunum hangs on the mesentery inside the peritoneal cavity, so it is covered by a serosa (the visceral peritoneum). A section of jejunum would show a smooth serous outer layer, not a fibrous adventitia.","Correct. The esophagus runs through the thorax outside the peritoneum, so a fibrous adventitia takes the place of the serosa, and its muscularis has the usual two layers.","The transverse colon is held by the mesocolon inside the peritoneal cavity, so its outer layer is a serosa. Students sometimes pick it because parts of the colon are retroperitoneal, but those are the ascending and descending colon, not the transverse colon."],"fix":"Review the four layers of the canal wall and the two places where the pattern changes: the adventitia on the esophagus and the extra muscle layer in the stomach."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-gi-wall-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-gi-wall","comp":"w5-enteric-plexuses","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A rare condition destroys the nerve network that lies between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers in one segment of the colon, while the nerve network in the submucosa of that segment is left intact. What problem is most likely to show up in that segment?","options":["Contents stall there because the muscle no longer contracts in coordinated waves","The glands of the lining there stop releasing their usual secretions","The lining there loses its epithelium and forms an open sore","The outer serosa there dries out and sticks to the nearby organs"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The network between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers is the myenteric plexus, and it sits inside the muscularis, right next to the muscle it controls. Its job is to control the movement of the gut, the coordinated waves that push contents along. If it is destroyed in one segment, the smooth muscle there no longer moves contents forward in an organized way, so material stalls in that segment. The submucosal plexus, which controls secretion, is still working, so the glands of the lining keep secreting. Matching each plexus to the layer it sits in tells you which job is lost.","notes":["Correct. The myenteric plexus lies within the muscularis and controls movement, so losing it leaves that segment unable to push its contents along.","Secretion is controlled by the submucosal plexus, which the case says is intact. This would be the answer if the damage were in the submucosa instead of between the muscle layers.","An open sore in the lining is an erosion of the mucosa, like a peptic ulcer, and it does not come from losing a nerve network. Nothing in this case damages the epithelium directly.","The serosa is the visceral peritoneum on the outside of the wall, and neither enteric plexus sits in it. Students may pick this because the myenteric plexus lies in the outer part of the wall, but it sits inside the muscularis, not in the serosa."],"fix":"Review where the submucosal and myenteric plexuses sit in the wall and which job, secretion or movement, each one controls."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-gi-wall-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-gi-wall","comp":"w5-canal-disorders","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A peptic ulcer starts at the epithelium on the inside of the stomach wall and keeps eroding outward. In what order does it reach these four structures?","options":["Submucosal plexus, muscularis mucosae, myenteric plexus, serosa","Muscularis mucosae, myenteric plexus, submucosal plexus, serosa","Myenteric plexus, submucosal plexus, muscularis mucosae, serosa","Muscularis mucosae, submucosal plexus, myenteric plexus, serosa"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"An ulcer erodes layer by layer from the lumen outward. It first works through the mucosa, and the muscularis mucosae is the thin sheet of smooth muscle at the outer edge of the mucosa, so it is the first of these structures the ulcer reaches. Next comes the submucosa, which holds the submucosal plexus. Beyond that is the muscularis, and the myenteric plexus sits within it, between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. Last is the serosa, the visceral peritoneum, and once the ulcer perforates it, stomach contents spill into the peritoneal cavity.","notes":["This puts the submucosal plexus ahead of the muscularis mucosae. The muscularis mucosae belongs to the mucosa itself, so the ulcer must pass it before it can reach the submucosa and its plexus.","This swaps the two plexuses. The myenteric plexus sits within the muscularis, which lies outside the submucosa, so the ulcer meets the submucosal plexus first. The name helps here, since the submucosal plexus is the one in the submucosa.","This lists the first three structures from the outside in and then ends at the serosa, which mixes up the direction. An ulcer starts at the lumen, so the structures closest to the epithelium are reached first.","Correct. The muscularis mucosae is at the outer edge of the mucosa, the submucosal plexus is in the submucosa, the myenteric plexus is within the muscularis, and the serosa is outermost."],"fix":"Review the three parts of the mucosa and the four layers of the wall from the lumen outward, including where each nerve plexus sits."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-gi-wall-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-gi-wall","comp":"w5-peritoneum-folds","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A blood clot blocks the small vessels running between the two layers of one fold of peritoneum. Within hours, the long coiled stretch of small intestine between the end of the duodenum and the ileocecal valve loses its blood supply, while the duodenum, stomach, liver, and colon look healthy. Which fold carried the blocked vessels?","options":["The mesocolon","The mesentery","The greater omentum","The lesser omentum"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Folds of peritoneum do two jobs: they anchor organs, and they carry the vessels and nerves that reach those organs between their two layers. The coiled stretch between the end of the duodenum and the ileocecal valve is the jejunum and the ileum, so the fold you want is the one that holds those two regions. That is the mesentery, the fan-shaped fold that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. The duodenum staying healthy fits too, because it is mostly retroperitoneal and is not held by the mesentery. The other folds hold or supply the colon, stomach, or liver, and those organs are healthy here.","notes":["The mesocolon binds the transverse and sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall. A clot in its vessels would harm parts of the colon, and the colon looks healthy in this case.","Correct. The mesentery holds the jejunum and ileum and carries their vessels, so only those coils lose their blood supply.","The greater omentum is the fatty apron that hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach over the intestines. It drapes over the coils of the small intestine, but it does not anchor them or carry their vessels.","The lesser omentum runs from the stomach and duodenum to the liver and carries vessels to the liver and along the stomach. A clot here would harm the stomach or liver, and both look healthy in this case."],"fix":"Review the five folds of peritoneum and which organs each one connects and carries vessels to."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-gi-wall-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-gi-wall","comp":"w5-retroperitoneal","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Four patients each have a hole worn completely through the wall of the digestive tract at a different spot. For which patient will the contents most likely spill straight into the peritoneal cavity?","options":["A hole through the wall of the esophagus in the thorax","A hole through the back wall of the descending colon, where it lies against the posterior abdominal wall","A hole through the back wall of the C-shaped duodenum, where it lies against the posterior abdominal wall","A hole through the front wall of the body of the stomach"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"To spill into the peritoneal cavity, the hole has to open through a surface covered by visceral peritoneum. The stomach hangs inside the peritoneal cavity, and its front wall is covered by serosa, so a hole there opens directly into the cavity, which is exactly how a perforated peptic ulcer becomes dangerous. The esophagus runs through the thorax with an adventitia instead of a serosa, so a hole there opens into the chest rather than the abdomen. The duodenum and the descending colon are retroperitoneal, so their back walls sit against the posterior abdominal wall behind the peritoneum, and a hole through the back wall opens behind the peritoneum instead of into the cavity.","notes":["The esophagus runs through the thorax outside the peritoneal cavity and is covered by an adventitia, not a serosa. A hole here leaks into the tissues of the chest, not the abdomen, even though the esophagus is part of the same tube.","The descending colon is retroperitoneal, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall with peritoneum covering only its front. A hole through its back wall opens behind the peritoneum. If the hole were in its front wall, which the peritoneum covers, it could open into the cavity.","Most of the duodenum is retroperitoneal, so its back wall lies behind the peritoneum against the posterior abdominal wall. A hole through that wall leaks into the space behind the peritoneum rather than into the peritoneal cavity.","Correct. The stomach hangs inside the peritoneal cavity, and its front wall is covered by serosa, so a hole there opens straight into the cavity."],"fix":"Review which organs are retroperitoneal, which part of the canal has an adventitia instead of a serosa, and what lies just outside the serosa."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-gi-wall-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-gi-wall","comp":"w5-stomach","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these structures of the stomach wall from closest to the lumen to farthest from it.","options":["Submucosal plexus, oblique layer, myenteric plexus, longitudinal layer","Submucosal plexus, myenteric plexus, oblique layer, longitudinal layer","Oblique layer, submucosal plexus, myenteric plexus, longitudinal layer","Submucosal plexus, oblique layer, longitudinal layer, myenteric plexus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Two ideas have to be put together here. First, the plexuses: the submucosal plexus sits in the submucosa, just outside the mucosa, and the myenteric plexus sits inside the muscularis, between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. Second, the stomach muscularis has a third layer that the rest of the canal lacks, the oblique layer, and it is the innermost of the three, lying just outside the submucosa. So moving outward you reach the submucosal plexus, then the oblique layer, then (past the circular layer) the myenteric plexus, and finally the outer longitudinal layer. The extra layer sits inside the usual two, so it does not change where the myenteric plexus lies.","notes":["Correct. The submucosal plexus is in the submucosa, the oblique layer is the innermost muscle layer, the myenteric plexus lies between the circular and longitudinal layers, and the longitudinal layer is outermost.","This puts the myenteric plexus inside the oblique layer, right next to the submucosa. The myenteric plexus lies within the muscularis between the circular and longitudinal layers, so the oblique layer, the innermost muscle layer, comes before it.","This puts the oblique layer inside the submucosa. The oblique layer is part of the muscularis, which lies outside the submucosa, so the submucosal plexus must come first.","This places the myenteric plexus outside all of the muscle, where the serosa would be. The myenteric plexus sits between the circular and longitudinal layers, so it comes before the outer longitudinal layer."],"fix":"Review the three muscle layers of the stomach muscularis in order and where the submucosal and myenteric plexuses sit in the wall."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-mouth-esophagus-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-mouth-esophagus","comp":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A person swallows a small piece of hard candy, and it travels all the way to the stomach. In what order does it pass these four structures?","options":["Laryngopharynx, fauces, upper esophageal sphincter, esophageal hiatus","Fauces, upper esophageal sphincter, laryngopharynx, esophageal hiatus","Fauces, laryngopharynx, upper esophageal sphincter, esophageal hiatus","Fauces, laryngopharynx, esophageal hiatus, upper esophageal sphincter"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The candy leaves the mouth through the fauces, the opening from the oral cavity into the oropharynx. It drops through the oropharynx into the laryngopharynx, the lowest region of the pharynx, which leads into the esophagus. The upper esophageal sphincter guards the top of the esophagus, where the laryngopharynx joins it. The esophagus then carries the candy down through the thorax and through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm to reach the stomach in the abdomen.","notes":["This puts the laryngopharynx before the fauces. The fauces is the exit from the mouth, so food passes it before it reaches any part of the pharynx below.","This places the upper esophageal sphincter before the laryngopharynx. The sphincter sits where the laryngopharynx joins the esophagus, so food must pass through the laryngopharynx first.","Correct. Food passes the fauces, drops through the laryngopharynx, enters the esophagus at the upper esophageal sphincter, and crosses the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus.","This sends the candy through the diaphragm before it enters the esophagus. The upper esophageal sphincter is at the top of the esophagus in the neck, and the hiatus is near its lower end, so the sphincter comes first."],"fix":"Review the path from the oral cavity through the pharynx to the esophagus, and where each esophageal sphincter and the hiatus sit."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-mouth-esophagus-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-mouth-esophagus","comp":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"After an illness, a patient's uvula no longer rises when the patient opens wide and says \"ah.\" When the patient drinks water, some of it comes out through the nose. Which explanation best fits these findings?","options":["The tongue can no longer push the water back through the fauces","The soft palate cannot rise to seal off the nasopharynx","The epiglottis cannot fold down to cover the opening of the larynx","The lower esophageal sphincter stays open and lets the water flow back"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The uvula is the finger-like projection that hangs from the back edge of the soft palate, so a uvula that will not rise means the soft palate itself is not lifting. During swallowing, the soft palate and uvula normally rise and close off the nasopharynx, the part of the pharynx behind the nasal cavity. That seal keeps food and liquid heading down toward the esophagus instead of up into the nose. When the soft palate cannot lift, the nasopharynx stays open, so some water is pushed up into it and out through the nose. Linking the uvula to the soft palate, and the soft palate to the nasopharynx, explains both findings.","notes":["If the tongue could not push the water back, the water would pool in the mouth or dribble from the lips, not come out of the nose. The tongue forms the floor of the mouth and is not what seals off the nasopharynx.","Correct. The uvula hangs from the soft palate, so a uvula that will not rise points to a soft palate that cannot seal off the nasopharynx, and liquid escapes into the nose.","The epiglottis covers the opening of the larynx during swallowing. If it failed, liquid would slip into the larynx and cause coughing or choking, not escape through the nose, and it would not explain the uvula that will not rise.","The lower esophageal sphincter is at the bottom of the esophagus, where it opens into the stomach. If it stays open, stomach contents flow back into the esophagus, as in GERD, but that does not send water out through the nose."],"fix":"Review the hard and soft palate and how the soft palate and uvula close off the nasopharynx during swallowing."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-mouth-esophagus-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-mouth-esophagus","comp":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A patient has burning pain behind the sternum after meals. Imaging shows that the upper part of the stomach has slid up through the opening in the diaphragm that the esophagus passes through. Which explanation best connects the imaging finding to the pain?","options":["The cardia and lower esophageal sphincter have moved up into the thorax, the barrier weakens, and acid reaches the esophagus","The herniated stomach presses on the trachea, which lies in front of the esophagus, and the pressure irritates the airway lining","The herniated fundus squeezes the upper esophageal sphincter shut, so food backs up in the laryngopharynx and burns the throat","The esophagus loses its adventitia where it passes through the hiatus, so acid leaks out through its wall into the chest"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The opening in the diaphragm is the esophageal hiatus, and part of the stomach pushing up through it is a hiatal hernia. The part of the stomach next to the hiatus is the cardia, the region around the opening from the esophagus, where the lower esophageal sphincter guards the entrance. When that area slides up into the thorax, the barrier at the sphincter is weakened, so acidic stomach contents flow back into the esophagus, as in GERD. The esophagus runs through the thorax behind the sternum, so its irritated lining is felt as burning pain right there. Linking the hiatus, the sphincter at the cardia, and the position of the esophagus explains the whole picture.","notes":["Correct. The hernia carries the cardia and the lower esophageal sphincter up through the hiatus, the barrier weakens, and acid irritates the esophagus, which lies behind the sternum.","The trachea does lie in front of the esophagus, which is why this sounds reasonable, but a hiatal hernia sits low in the thorax at the diaphragm, well below where the trachea divides. The problem it causes is acid reflux into the esophagus, not pressure on the airway.","The upper esophageal sphincter is at the top of the esophagus, where it meets the laryngopharynx in the neck, far above the diaphragm. A hernia at the hiatus cannot reach it, and the burning here is behind the sternum, not in the throat.","The adventitia is the outer fibrous layer of the esophagus, and it is not lost in a hiatal hernia. Reflux harms the esophagus from the inside, where acid irritates the lining, rather than by leaking out through the wall."],"fix":"Review where the esophageal hiatus and the lower esophageal sphincter sit, what happens in a hiatal hernia and in GERD, and where the esophagus runs in the thorax."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-mouth-esophagus-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-mouth-esophagus","comp":"w5-teeth-tongue","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient with advanced gum disease has lost much of the fibrous tissue that fills the narrow space between the cementum of one lower molar's root and the bony wall of its socket. The tooth does not hurt, and an X-ray shows no decay anywhere in it. What change is the patient most likely to notice in that tooth?","options":["Sharp pain with cold drinks, because the enamel over the crown has worn away","A constant ache, because decay has reached the nerves in the pulp","Chipping of the crown, because the dentin underneath has softened","Loosening in its socket, because the root is no longer anchored to bone"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tissue between the cementum of the root and the bony wall of the socket is the periodontal ligament, a layer of dense fibrous connective tissue. Its fibers tie the root to the bone and hold the tooth in place, forming the fibrous joint called a gomphosis. If much of the ligament is destroyed, the root loses its anchor to the bone, so the tooth becomes loose and can shift when the patient chews. The other choices all depend on damage to the enamel, dentin, or pulp, and a tooth with no decay and no pain gives no sign of that.","notes":["Sharp pain with cold drinks comes from worn enamel that exposes the dentin, and this tooth does not hurt. This would fit a patient whose enamel had eroded over the crown.","A constant ache comes from decay that reaches the pulp, where the nerves and blood vessels are. The X-ray shows no decay and the tooth does not hurt, so this does not fit.","Dentin forms the bulk of the tooth under the enamel, and it softens when decay reaches it. The X-ray shows no decay, and losing the tissue around the root does not weaken the crown.","Correct. The lost tissue is the periodontal ligament, which anchors the cementum of the root to the bony socket, so losing it leaves the tooth loose."],"fix":"Review the layers of a tooth from crown to root and how the periodontal ligament anchors the root in its socket to form a gomphosis."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-mouth-esophagus-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-mouth-esophagus","comp":"w5-teeth-tongue","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A dentist works on a tooth below the gum line, starting at the bony wall of the socket and moving toward the center of the root. Rank these structures in the order the instrument would meet them.","options":["Cementum, periodontal ligament, dentin, pulp","Periodontal ligament, cementum, dentin, pulp","Periodontal ligament, dentin, cementum, pulp","Cementum, dentin, periodontal ligament, pulp"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Below the gum line you are working on the root, which sits in the bony socket. The periodontal ligament fills the gap between the bony wall and the root, so it is met first. The root itself is covered by cementum, a calcified layer that also gives the ligament something to attach to. Beneath the cementum is dentin, which forms the bulk of the tooth, and at the center is the pulp with its blood vessels and nerves. Enamel is not on this path, because it covers only the crown above the gum.","notes":["This puts the cementum outside the ligament. The cementum is the covering of the root itself, and the ligament runs between it and the bone, so the ligament comes first.","Correct. The ligament lies between the bone and the root, the cementum covers the root, the dentin forms its bulk, and the pulp is at the center.","This places dentin outside the cementum. On the root, the dentin is covered by cementum, much as it is covered by enamel on the crown, so cementum comes before dentin.","This puts the periodontal ligament inside the tooth. The ligament is connective tissue outside the root that ties it to the socket, so it cannot sit between the dentin and the pulp."],"fix":"Review the layers of a tooth root and the periodontal ligament that holds it in the socket."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-mouth-esophagus-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-mouth-esophagus","comp":"w5-teeth-tongue","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Suppose an adult can still curl, narrow, and flatten the tongue normally while speaking, but cannot stick the tongue out or move it from side to side. Which explanation best fits this pattern?","options":["Only the intrinsic tongue muscles have been weakened","Both the intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles are weak","Only the extrinsic tongue muscles have been weakened","Only the muscles of the soft palate have been weakened"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tongue has two muscle groups with different jobs. The intrinsic muscles lie entirely inside the tongue and change its shape and size, which is what curling, narrowing, and flattening require. The extrinsic muscles begin on bones outside the tongue and insert into it, so they move the whole tongue in and out and from side to side. This patient's shape changes are normal, but the tongue cannot change its position, so the extrinsic muscles are the weakened group while the intrinsic muscles still work.","notes":["The intrinsic muscles change the shape of the tongue, and this patient can still curl, narrow, and flatten it. If only the intrinsic muscles were weak, the shape changes would suffer while sticking the tongue out would still work.","If both groups were weak, the patient would lose the shape changes as well as the movement. Because the shape changes are normal, the intrinsic muscles must still be working.","Correct. The extrinsic muscles anchor outside the tongue and move it in and out and side to side, which is exactly what this patient has lost.","The soft palate lifts to close off the nasopharynx during swallowing and does not move the tongue. Weakness there would let liquid escape into the nose, not keep the tongue from moving."],"fix":"Review how the intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles differ in where they attach and what they do."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-stomach-intestines-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-stomach-intestines","comp":"w5-food-pathway","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A capsule camera is swallowed to film the lining of the intestines. After it leaves the stomach, in what order does it pass these four structures?","options":["Jejunum, cecum, splenic flexure, hepatic flexure","Jejunum, cecum, hepatic flexure, splenic flexure","Cecum, jejunum, hepatic flexure, splenic flexure","Jejunum, hepatic flexure, cecum, splenic flexure"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"After the stomach, the capsule passes through the small intestine in order: duodenum, jejunum, then ileum. The ileum empties through the ileocecal valve into the cecum, the pouch at the start of the large intestine in the lower right abdomen. From there the capsule climbs the ascending colon on the right side to the hepatic flexure under the liver, crosses the abdomen in the transverse colon, and turns down at the splenic flexure near the spleen into the descending colon. The flexures are named for the organs they sit beside, so the bend by the liver on the right comes first.","notes":["This swaps the two flexures. The colon rises on the right side first, and the liver is on the right, so the hepatic flexure comes before the splenic flexure on the left.","Correct. The jejunum is in the small intestine, the cecum begins the large intestine, and the colon turns at the hepatic flexure before the splenic flexure.","This puts the cecum before the jejunum. The cecum is the first part of the large intestine, so the capsule reaches it only after it has passed through all of the small intestine.","This sends the capsule to the hepatic flexure before the cecum. The ileum empties into the cecum first, and only then does material rise in the ascending colon to the hepatic flexure."],"fix":"Review the order of the regions of the small and large intestine and where the hepatic and splenic flexures sit."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-stomach-intestines-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-stomach-intestines","comp":"w5-small-intestine","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these features of the intestine from the one that involves the most layers of the wall to the one that involves the least.","options":["Circular fold, haustrum, villus, microvillus","Haustrum, villus, circular fold, microvillus","Circular fold, villus, haustrum, microvillus","Haustrum, circular fold, villus, microvillus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A haustrum is a pouch of the entire colon wall, gathered up by the pull of the teniae coli, so it involves every layer. A circular fold is a permanent ridge of the mucosa and the submucosa together. A villus is a finger-like projection of the mucosa alone. A microvillus is a microscopic projection of the plasma membrane of a single absorptive cell, so it involves less than one layer. In the small intestine the last three stack on top of each other, which is how the lining multiplies its absorptive surface.","notes":["This puts the circular fold ahead of the haustrum. A circular fold involves only the mucosa and submucosa, while a haustrum is a pouch of the entire colon wall, so the haustrum involves more.","This places the villus ahead of the circular fold. A villus is a projection of the mucosa only, while a circular fold includes the submucosa too, and the villi sit on top of the folds.","This drops the haustrum below the villus, perhaps because haustra belong to the large intestine rather than the small intestine. Which organ a feature belongs to does not change the fact that a haustrum is a pouch of the whole wall.","Correct. A haustrum involves the whole colon wall, a circular fold the mucosa and submucosa, a villus the mucosa only, and a microvillus just the membrane of one cell."],"fix":"Review the three surface features of the small intestine, which layers each one involves, and how the teniae coli form the haustra."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-stomach-intestines-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-stomach-intestines","comp":"w5-large-intestine","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"Imagine that the three teniae coli along one segment of the colon were cut, so the bands in that segment could no longer pull on the wall. What change would you predict in the shape of that segment?","options":["The pouches flatten, and the segment looks smoother, more like small intestine","The pouches deepen, because the circular muscle now shapes the wall alone","The omental appendices drop off, leaving the outer surface bare","Circular folds form inside the segment to replace the missing pouches"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The teniae coli are three thickened bands of longitudinal muscle, and they are shorter than the colon wall they run along. Their steady pull gathers the wall into the row of pouches called haustra, which gives the colon its puckered look. If the bands in a segment can no longer pull, nothing gathers the wall there, so the pouches flatten out and the segment looks smoother. This link between the bands and the pouches is why the teniae coli and haustra are learned together as features of the large intestine.","notes":["Correct. The haustra exist because the shorter teniae coli gather the wall, so without that pull the pouches smooth out.","The pouches are formed by the pull of the shorter teniae coli. With that pull gone, nothing gathers the wall into pouches, so they would flatten rather than deepen.","The omental appendices are small fat-filled pouches of peritoneum that hang from the teniae coli, but they are not held on by the pull of the bands. Students link them to the teniae because they are attached there, but the change in shape happens in the haustra.","Circular folds are permanent ridges of the mucosa and submucosa found in the small intestine. They are built into that lining and would not appear in the colon just because the teniae coli stopped pulling."],"fix":"Review the teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices and how the teniae coli form the haustra."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-stomach-intestines-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-stomach-intestines","comp":"w5-stomach","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A disease has left scar tissue that stiffens the inner lining of a patient's stomach, so the folds of the lining can no longer flatten out. The muscularis is unharmed. What problem is this patient most likely to have at mealtimes?","options":["Food leaves the stomach unmixed, because the stomach can no longer churn","Acid rises into the esophagus, because the cardiac sphincter is weakened","Feeling full after small meals, because the stomach cannot stretch to hold more","Food backs up in the stomach, because the pyloric sphincter cannot open"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The folds of the inner stomach lining are the rugae. When the stomach is empty they lie in ridges, and as a meal arrives they flatten out so the stomach can stretch and hold more. If scar tissue keeps them from flattening, the stomach cannot expand much, so it fills after only a small amount of food and the patient feels full quickly. The muscularis is unharmed, so churning by the oblique, circular, and longitudinal layers still works, and nothing in the case points to either sphincter.","notes":["Churning comes from the three muscle layers of the muscularis, especially the inner oblique layer, and the case says the muscularis is unharmed. This would fit damage to the muscle coat rather than the lining.","The cardiac sphincter is another name for the lower esophageal sphincter at the top of the stomach. Weakness there causes reflux, as in GERD, but this case describes stiff folds in the lining, not a weak sphincter.","Correct. The rugae flatten to let the stomach expand with a meal, so when they cannot flatten, the stomach fills quickly.","The pyloric sphincter is the ring of smooth muscle between the pylorus and the duodenum. A blocked outlet would trap food, but the case describes stiff folds in the lining, and the muscle is unharmed."],"fix":"Review the rugae and the three-layered muscularis of the stomach and what each one lets the stomach do."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-stomach-intestines-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-stomach-intestines","comp":"w5-canal-sphincters","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A tumor narrows the canal at one of its sphincters. Imaging shows the stomach stretched with retained food and liquid, while the esophagus looks normal and the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum are nearly empty. At which sphincter is the narrowing most likely?","options":["The lower esophageal sphincter","The ileocecal sphincter","The upper esophageal sphincter","The pyloric sphincter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"A blockage backs material up in the region just before it and leaves the regions after it nearly empty. Here the stomach is overfilled, the esophagus above it is normal, and every part of the small intestine below it is nearly empty. The sphincter that sits between those two zones is the pyloric sphincter, the ring of smooth muscle between the pylorus of the stomach and the duodenum. Knowing which two regions each sphincter separates lets you locate the problem from where material piles up.","notes":["The lower esophageal sphincter separates the esophagus from the stomach. A narrowing there would leave food stuck in a stretched esophagus, and little would reach the stomach to fill it.","The ileocecal sphincter separates the ileum from the cecum. A narrowing there would back material up through the small intestine, so the ileum would be stretched and full, not empty.","The upper esophageal sphincter separates the laryngopharynx from the esophagus. A narrowing there would make swallowing hard, and little food would reach the esophagus or the stomach at all.","Correct. The pyloric sphincter separates the stomach from the duodenum, so a narrowing there fills the stomach and leaves the small intestine empty."],"fix":"Review the major sphincters of the canal and which two regions each one separates."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-stomach-intestines-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-stomach-intestines","comp":"w5-canal-disorders","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A patient has pain and tenderness in the lower right abdomen. Surgery is delayed, and a day later the pain and tenderness have spread across the whole abdomen. Which explanation best accounts for the full course of this illness?","options":["The inflamed appendix spread infection up the inside of the colon to every part of it","The inflamed appendix burst, and its contents spread through the peritoneal cavity","Inflammation of the cecum moved along the mesentery to the jejunum and the ileum","Small pouches formed throughout the colon wall and became inflamed along its length"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Pain in the lower right abdomen points to the vermiform appendix, which hangs from the cecum in that corner of the abdomen. An inflamed appendix can swell and burst, and because it sits within the abdomen covered by peritoneum, its bacteria-laden contents spill into the peritoneal cavity. The peritoneum lines the whole abdominal wall as the parietal layer and covers the organs as the visceral layer, so infection spreading through the cavity reaches the lining of the entire abdomen. That is why pain that starts in one corner can spread everywhere, and why appendicitis is treated before the appendix ruptures.","notes":["Infection traveling along the inside of the colon would stay in the lumen of the canal, and that is not how a ruptured appendix spreads. Students may pick this because the appendix opens into the large intestine, but the whole abdomen hurts because the peritoneum lines the whole abdomen.","Correct. The appendix sits in the lower right abdomen, and when it bursts, its contents spread through the peritoneal cavity and inflame the peritoneum that lines the whole abdomen.","The mesentery holds the jejunum and ileum, not the cecum, and the problem in the lower right abdomen is the appendix rather than the cecum itself. Even if the small intestine became involved, that would not explain tenderness across the whole abdominal wall.","Small pouches bulging through weak spots in the colon wall describe diverticulosis, a structural change in the wall. It does not explain a problem that starts at one spot in the lower right abdomen and then spreads across the whole abdomen within a day."],"fix":"Review where the appendix sits, what happens when it ruptures, and how the parietal and visceral peritoneum line the abdomen."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-accessory-organs-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-accessory-organs","comp":"w5-bile-path","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"During a meal, a drop of bile is made by a hepatocyte in the right lobe of the liver and flows straight to the small intestine without detouring into the gallbladder. In what order does it pass these four structures?","options":["Right hepatic duct, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, major duodenal papilla","Common hepatic duct, right hepatic duct, common bile duct, major duodenal papilla","Right hepatic duct, common bile duct, common hepatic duct, major duodenal papilla","Right hepatic duct, common hepatic duct, major duodenal papilla, common bile duct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Bile drains along a path of widening ducts. The canaliculi between hepatocytes collect it, and on the right side of the liver it gathers into the right hepatic duct. The right and left hepatic ducts merge just outside the liver into the common hepatic duct. Once the cystic duct from the gallbladder joins it, the duct is called the common bile duct, which meets the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. The ampulla opens into the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla, so the papilla is the last stop.","notes":["Correct. Bile goes from the right hepatic duct into the common hepatic duct, then the common bile duct, and enters the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla.","This puts the common hepatic duct before the right hepatic duct. The right and left hepatic ducts drain the two halves of the liver first and then merge to form the common hepatic duct.","This swaps the common hepatic and common bile ducts. The common bile duct begins only below the point where the cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct, so the common hepatic duct comes first.","This puts the papilla before the common bile duct. The papilla is the opening into the duodenum at the end of the path, so bile must travel the common bile duct and the ampulla before it reaches the papilla."],"fix":"Review the path of bile from the canaliculi through the hepatic, common hepatic, and common bile ducts to the major duodenal papilla."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-accessory-organs-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-accessory-organs","comp":"w5-accessory-disorders","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A patient has severe pain in the right upper quadrant, and imaging shows a swollen, inflamed gallbladder with a gallstone stuck in one duct. The skin and eyes are not yellow, and pancreatic juice is draining normally. Where is the stone most likely lodged?","options":["In the common bile duct","In the hepatopancreatic ampulla","In the cystic duct","In the common hepatic duct"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Start with what is still working. The skin and eyes are not yellow, so there is no jaundice, which means bile made by the liver is still reaching the duodenum. Pancreatic juice is draining normally, so the shared passage at the hepatopancreatic ampulla is open. The swollen, inflamed gallbladder is cholecystitis, which follows when bile is trapped in the gallbladder. The cystic duct is the gallbladder's only connection, and it sits on a side branch, so a stone there traps bile in the gallbladder while the main route from the liver to the duodenum stays open.","notes":["A stone in the common bile duct blocks bile from both the liver and the gallbladder on its way to the duodenum, so bile would back up and jaundice would be likely. The skin and eyes here are not yellow.","A stone at the hepatopancreatic ampulla blocks bile and pancreatic juice at once, which could cause jaundice and pancreatitis together. Here pancreatic juice drains normally and there is no jaundice.","Correct. A stone in the cystic duct traps bile in the gallbladder and inflames it, while the main route from the liver to the duodenum stays open, so there is no jaundice.","A stone in the common hepatic duct blocks bile leaving the liver above the point where the cystic duct joins, so liver bile could reach neither the gallbladder nor the duodenum, and jaundice would be expected."],"fix":"Review the bile ducts in order, where the cystic duct joins, and which disorders follow from a stone at each spot."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-accessory-organs-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-accessory-organs","comp":"w5-pancreas","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A gallstone completely blocks the hepatopancreatic ampulla. Which statement best explains how some pancreatic juice could still reach the duodenum?","options":["The accessory duct empties into the duodenum on its own, apart from the ampulla","The main pancreatic duct opens into the duodenum before it reaches the ampulla","The pancreatic islets release their products straight into the duodenal lining","Pancreatic juice can flow up the common bile duct and drain out the cystic duct"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The hepatopancreatic ampulla is where the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet before opening at the major duodenal papilla, so a stone there blocks both bile and the main flow of pancreatic juice. The pancreas also has a smaller duct, the accessory pancreatic duct (duct of Santorini), which empties into the duodenum separately rather than through the ampulla. That separate opening gives some pancreatic juice a way around the blockage. The pancreatic islets are endocrine and release hormones into the blood, and pancreatic juice comes from the acini, so the islets are not a route for it at all.","notes":["Correct. The accessory pancreatic duct opens into the duodenum by itself, so it bypasses the blocked ampulla.","The main pancreatic duct (duct of Wirsung) joins the common bile duct at the ampulla, so a stone there blocks it. Only the accessory duct empties into the duodenum on its own.","The islets are the endocrine part of the pancreas, and they release hormones into the blood rather than into a duct. Pancreatic juice comes from the acini, the exocrine clusters, and must travel through ducts.","The cystic duct connects only the gallbladder to the bile ducts, so it leads to a dead end, not to the duodenum. Juice pushed up the common bile duct would still be stuck behind the stone."],"fix":"Review the two pancreatic ducts, where each one empties, and how the acini and islets release their products."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-accessory-organs-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-accessory-organs","comp":"w5-liver-gross-lobule","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Blood from the intestines reaches the liver in the hepatic portal vein. In what order does that blood pass these four structures as it travels through the liver?","options":["Porta hepatis, hepatic sinusoid, portal triad, central vein","Portal triad, porta hepatis, hepatic sinusoid, central vein","Porta hepatis, central vein, hepatic sinusoid, portal triad","Porta hepatis, portal triad, hepatic sinusoid, central vein"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The hepatic portal vein enters the liver through the porta hepatis, the doorway on the underside of the liver. Inside, it branches again and again until small branches run in the portal triads at the corners of each liver lobule. From there, blood flows through the hepatic sinusoids, the wide, leaky capillaries between the plates of hepatocytes, so the cells can take up what the blood carries. The sinusoids drain toward the center of the lobule into the central vein. Putting the doorway of the whole liver together with the layout of one lobule gives the order.","notes":["This puts the sinusoids before the portal triad. The branches of the hepatic portal vein run in the portal triads at the corners of each lobule, and only from there does the blood spread into the sinusoids between the plates of hepatocytes.","This puts the portal triad before the porta hepatis. The porta hepatis is where the hepatic portal vein first enters the liver, so blood passes it before branching out to the portal triads at the corners of the lobules.","This runs the lobule backward. Blood enters a lobule at the portal triads on its corners and drains through the sinusoids toward the central vein, so the central vein is the last stop, not the first after the porta hepatis.","Correct. Blood enters at the porta hepatis, branches to the portal triads at the lobule corners, flows through the sinusoids past the hepatocytes, and drains into the central vein."],"fix":"Review the porta hepatis, the parts of a liver lobule, and the direction blood flows from the portal triads through the sinusoids to the central vein."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-accessory-organs-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-accessory-organs","comp":"w5-salivary-glands","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A small stone blocks the single large duct that opens on a bump beside the lingual frenulum. Each time the patient eats, the gland drained by that duct swells. Where would you feel the swelling?","options":["In front of and below the ear, over the masseter muscle","On the inside of the cheek, beside the upper second molar","Under the jaw, along the inner side of the mandible","Beneath the tongue, above the submandibular gland"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A single large duct that opens beside the lingual frenulum is the duct of the submandibular gland. Each submandibular gland lies in the floor of the mouth along the inner side of the mandible, and its duct runs forward under the tongue to open near the frenulum. When a stone blocks that duct, saliva made during a meal cannot get out, so the gland itself swells, and the swelling is felt under the jaw. Working backward from the duct opening to the gland is how you place the swelling.","notes":["That is where the parotid gland sits. Its duct opens inside the cheek beside the upper second molar, not beside the lingual frenulum, so a blocked parotid duct would cause swelling in front of the ear.","This is where the parotid duct opens, not where a gland sits. A gland swells where it lies, and the duct in this case opens under the tongue, not in the cheek.","Correct. The duct beside the lingual frenulum drains the submandibular gland, which lies along the inner side of the mandible.","That is where the sublingual gland sits. It drains through several small ducts onto the floor of the mouth rather than through one large duct beside the frenulum."],"fix":"Review where each of the three large salivary glands sits and where its duct opens into the mouth."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m4-accessory-organs-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m4-accessory-organs","comp":"w5-bile-path","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"During surgery to remove the gallbladder, the surgeon must tie off and cut one duct, and bile from the liver still needs to reach the duodenum afterward. Which duct is the right one to tie off?","options":["The common bile duct","The cystic duct","The common hepatic duct","The main pancreatic duct"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The gallbladder connects to the duct system through only one duct, the cystic duct, which joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct. Because the cystic duct is a side branch, tying it off separates the gallbladder without blocking the main route. Bile can still flow from the hepatic ducts into the common hepatic duct, then the common bile duct, the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and the duodenum. Tying off either of the main bile ducts would cut off bile from the liver and back it up, which would lead to jaundice.","notes":["The common bile duct carries all of the bile, from the liver and the gallbladder, down to the ampulla. Tying it off would stop bile from reaching the duodenum and back it up toward the liver.","Correct. The cystic duct is the gallbladder's only connection and sits on a side branch, so tying it removes the gallbladder while bile keeps flowing to the duodenum.","The common hepatic duct carries bile out of the liver before the cystic duct joins it. Tying it off would block all bile from the liver, which is the opposite of what the surgeon wants.","The main pancreatic duct carries pancreatic juice, not bile, and it is not attached to the gallbladder. Tying it off would trap pancreatic juice and could inflame the pancreas."],"fix":"Review where the cystic duct joins the bile ducts and why it is a side branch rather than part of the main route."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-kidney-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-kidney","comp":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A catheter releases dye into one arcuate artery. Follow the dye through the blood supply of a single cortical nephron and back out toward the renal vein. In what order does the dye pass these four vessels?","options":["Afferent arteriole, cortical radiate vein, efferent arteriole, interlobar vein","Efferent arteriole, afferent arteriole, cortical radiate vein, interlobar vein","Afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole, cortical radiate vein, interlobar vein","Afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole, interlobar vein, cortical radiate vein"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"From an arcuate artery, the dye rises in a cortical radiate artery and enters an afferent arteriole, which delivers it into the glomerulus, the first capillary bed. It leaves the glomerulus through the efferent arteriole and flows into the second capillary bed, the peritubular capillaries around the tubules of that cortical nephron. From there the veins retrace the arteries in reverse: cortical radiate veins in the cortex, then arcuate veins along the pyramid bases, then interlobar veins between the pyramids, and finally the renal vein. Knowing that blood meets two capillary beds before it reaches any vein, and that the veins run the arterial path backward, gives the order.","notes":["This sends the dye into a vein before it has left the glomerulus. The efferent arteriole carries blood out of the glomerulus to the peritubular capillaries, and only after that second capillary bed does blood drain into veins. Students pick this when they forget that the kidney has two capillary beds in a row.","The afferent arteriole brings blood into the glomerulus and the efferent arteriole carries it out, so the afferent always comes first. Afferent means carrying toward, and efferent means carrying away. This order would be right only if blood entered the glomerulus through the vessel that drains it.","Correct. The dye passes the afferent arteriole, the glomerulus and the efferent arteriole, and then the peritubular capillaries drain into a cortical radiate vein, which leads through an arcuate vein to an interlobar vein.","The veins retrace the arteries in reverse, so the small cortical radiate veins in the cortex come before the interlobar veins between the pyramids. This order would have blood reach the larger veins near the center of the kidney first and then climb back into the cortex. It usually comes from mixing up the look-alike names interlobar and interlobular."],"fix":"Review the two capillary beds in the kidney and how the veins retrace the arterial path back to the renal vein."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-kidney-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-kidney","comp":"w6-kidney-gross","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A doctor passes a biopsy needle into the kidney from the back to collect renal corpuscles. Rank these structures from the first one the needle meets to the last one it must reach: renal capsule, renal cortex, adipose capsule, renal fascia.","options":["Renal fascia, adipose capsule, renal capsule, renal cortex","Adipose capsule, renal fascia, renal capsule, renal cortex","Renal capsule, adipose capsule, renal fascia, renal cortex","Renal fascia, renal capsule, adipose capsule, renal cortex"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Three layers wrap each kidney. From outside in, they are the renal fascia, which anchors the kidney to the abdominal wall, the adipose capsule, which is the fat that cushions it, and the renal capsule, the smooth fibrous layer that sits right on the kidney surface. Only after the capsule does the needle reach kidney tissue. All the renal corpuscles sit in the renal cortex, the outer tissue region, so the needle can stop there and does not need to reach the medulla.","notes":["Correct. The fascia is outermost, the fat lies under it, the capsule sits on the kidney itself, and the corpuscles are in the cortex just beneath the capsule.","The adipose capsule lies inside the renal fascia, not outside it. The fascia is the outer anchoring layer that holds the kidney and its fat against the abdominal wall. A student who picks this may think of fat as the outer padding, but the fascia wraps around that fat.","This lists the three wrappings from the inside out. The renal capsule is the innermost layer, sitting right on the kidney surface, so a needle coming in from the back meets the renal fascia first and the renal capsule last. Students pick this when they remember the three layers but start the list from the kidney.","The renal capsule is the innermost of the three layers, so the needle cannot reach it before the adipose capsule. The fat lies between the fascia and the capsule. This order would be right only if the fat sat directly on the kidney tissue."],"fix":"Review the three layers around the kidney from outermost to innermost and which kidney region holds the renal corpuscles."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-kidney-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-kidney","comp":"w6-kidney-internal","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A small stone completely plugs one minor calyx, the cup that fits around the tip of a single renal pyramid. Urine from which part of the kidney can no longer drain?","options":["The collecting ducts of every pyramid in that kidney","The renal columns on either side of that calyx","The urine already sitting in the renal pelvis","The collecting ducts of the one pyramid that drains into it"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Collecting ducts run down through each renal pyramid and join into papillary ducts that open on the renal papilla, the tip of the pyramid. Each minor calyx is a cup fitted around one papilla, so it catches the urine from that one pyramid. If the calyx is plugged, urine from that pyramid has nowhere to go. The other pyramids drain into their own minor calyces, which still empty into the major calyces and the renal pelvis.","notes":["Each pyramid drains into its own minor calyx, so one plugged calyx does not block the whole kidney. A block that stops urine from the entire kidney would have to sit where everything comes together, at the renal pelvis or the start of the ureter.","The renal columns are bands of cortex between the pyramids that carry blood vessels. They are not a drainage route for urine. Urine leaves through the collecting ducts in the pyramids, not through the columns.","The renal pelvis is downstream of the calyx, so urine already in the pelvis can still flow on into the ureter. A plug in a minor calyx only blocks urine coming toward it from above.","Correct. Each minor calyx surrounds one renal papilla, so plugging it traps the urine coming out of that single pyramid's collecting ducts."],"fix":"Review how collecting ducts, papillae, minor calyces, major calyces and the renal pelvis connect inside the kidney."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-kidney-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-kidney","comp":"w6-urine-path","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Four patients each have one blockage in the right kidney. Which blockage would stop urine from leaving the largest share of that kidney?","options":["A stone wedged inside a single minor calyx","A stone where the pelvis meets the ureter","A stone wedged inside a single major calyx","A clot inside a single cortical radiate artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Urine collects in spaces that keep merging as it moves out. Each minor calyx drains one pyramid, several minor calyces join into a major calyx, and all the major calyces empty into the single renal pelvis, which narrows into the ureter. A stone where the renal pelvis meets the ureter therefore sits at the one point that urine from the whole kidney has to pass, so it blocks every pyramid at once. A clot in a cortical radiate artery affects blood flow to a patch of cortex, not the route urine takes out of the kidney. The farther downstream a blockage sits on the urine path, the more of the kidney sits behind it.","notes":["A minor calyx catches urine from only one renal papilla, so this blockage traps urine from a single pyramid. It is the smallest of the urine blockages listed. It would be the answer if the question asked which blockage affects the least kidney tissue.","Correct. All the calyces empty into the renal pelvis, so a stone where the pelvis meets the ureter blocks urine from the entire kidney.","A major calyx collects from several minor calyces, so this blocks more than a minor calyx does. However, the kidney has more than one major calyx, and the others still drain into the renal pelvis. Students often stop here because major sounds big, but it is still upstream of the pelvis.","A cortical radiate artery carries blood into a region of the cortex, so a clot there affects the nephrons it feeds, not the path urine takes out. It is a blood supply problem in one small area. This would matter for a question about which region loses its blood flow."],"fix":"Review how minor calyces, major calyces and the renal pelvis merge, and how the urine route differs from the blood route."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-kidney-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-kidney","comp":"w6-kidney-gross","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A mass grows on the medial border of the left kidney and presses into the notch there. Which set of structures is the mass most likely to squeeze?","options":["The arcuate arteries along the bases of the pyramids","The adrenal gland and the upper pole of the kidney","The renal corpuscles in the outer renal cortex","The renal artery, the renal vein and the ureter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The notch on the medial border of the kidney is the renal hilum, the kidney's only doorway. The renal artery enters there, and the renal vein and ureter leave there, along with nerves and lymphatic vessels. A mass pressing into the hilum can therefore squeeze the vessels and the ureter together, which could affect both blood flow and urine drainage. The arcuate arteries and renal corpuscles lie inside the kidney tissue, away from the hilum.","notes":["The arcuate arteries sit inside the kidney, arching along the border between the cortex and the medulla. They are branches far downstream of the hilum, not structures that pass through it. A mass would have to grow into the kidney tissue itself to reach them.","The adrenal gland sits on top of the kidney, at its superior pole, not in the notch on the medial border. A mass at the upper pole would be more likely to press on it. The question points to the medial notch, which is the hilum.","The renal corpuscles are in the cortex, the outer tissue region just under the renal capsule. A mass would have to push through the capsule and into the kidney tissue to reach them. A mass sitting in the medial notch meets the structures passing through the hilum first.","Correct. The medial notch is the renal hilum, where the renal artery enters and the renal vein and ureter leave, so these are the structures packed into it."],"fix":"Review where the renal hilum is and which structures enter and leave the kidney through it."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-kidney-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-kidney","comp":"w6-kidney-internal","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A kidney biopsy sample looks striped under the microscope. It is packed with nephron loops and collecting ducts running side by side, and it contains no renal corpuscles at all. Which part of the kidney did the needle sample?","options":["A renal pyramid in the renal medulla","A renal column between two pyramids","The outer renal cortex under the capsule","The wall of the renal pelvis near the hilum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Renal corpuscles are found only in the renal cortex, so a sample with none of them did not come from cortical tissue. The renal medulla is made of cone-shaped renal pyramids, and the pyramids look striped because their nephron loops and collecting ducts run in parallel. The renal columns are extensions of the cortex that dip between the pyramids, so they count as cortical tissue too. A sample with parallel loops and ducts and no corpuscles fits a renal pyramid.","notes":["Correct. Pyramids are striped by parallel nephron loops and collecting ducts, and they hold no renal corpuscles because all corpuscles sit in the cortex.","The renal columns are extensions of the cortex that reach down between the pyramids. Because they are cortical tissue, they look like the outer cortex, not like the striped, corpuscle-free tissue described. Students choose this when they forget that the columns are cortex, not medulla.","The outer renal cortex is where the renal corpuscles live, along with the convoluted tubules. A cortical sample would show corpuscles, so it does not match a sample with none. This would be right if the sample showed glomeruli.","The renal pelvis is a collecting space, a funnel that gathers urine from the calyces. Its wall does not contain nephron loops or collecting ducts. This would fit a sample of a tube wall built for carrying urine, not a striped tissue full of tubules."],"fix":"Review what the renal cortex, renal columns and renal pyramids each contain and how they look on a section."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nephron-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nephron","comp":"w6-nephron-parts","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A tracer dye is placed in the capsular space of one nephron and followed as the fluid moves on. In what order does the dye pass these four places?","options":["Macula densa, proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, collecting duct","Proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, macula densa, collecting duct","Proximal convoluted tubule, macula densa, nephron loop, collecting duct","Proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, collecting duct, macula densa"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The capsular space opens straight into the proximal convoluted tubule, the coil closest to the renal corpuscle. From there the fluid runs down and back up the U-shaped nephron loop and returns to the cortex as the distal convoluted tubule. The macula densa is a patch of crowded cells in the wall of the distal tubule, at the spot where it touches the arterioles of its own corpuscle, so the dye passes it after the loop. Only then does the distal tubule empty into a collecting duct shared with other nephrons. Placing the macula densa on the right segment is the step that decides this question.","notes":["The macula densa sits beside the renal corpuscle, which makes it tempting to put first, but it belongs to the distal tubule, not the capsule or the proximal tubule. The capsular space empties straight into the proximal convoluted tubule. This order would be right only if the macula densa were part of the glomerular capsule.","Correct. The dye goes through the proximal tubule and the nephron loop, passes the macula densa in the wall of the distal tubule, and then enters the shared collecting duct.","This places the macula densa on the proximal tubule. The macula densa forms where the distal convoluted tubule comes back to touch its own corpuscle, which happens only after the loop. Students pick this when they remember that the macula densa is near the corpuscle but not which segment it belongs to.","The collecting duct receives fluid from the distal tubules of many nephrons, so it comes after every part of a single nephron, including the macula densa. This order would have fluid leave the shared duct and return to one nephron's distal tubule, which does not happen."],"fix":"Review the order of the renal tubule segments and which segment forms the macula densa."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nephron-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nephron","comp":"w6-jga-filtration","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On a slide, a student finds a renal corpuscle with a patch of crowded, tightly packed tubule cells pressed against the arteriole that enters the glomerulus. Which structure has she found, and which tubule segment does it belong to?","options":["The juxtaglomerular cells, part of the proximal tubule","The podocytes, part of the glomerular capsule","The macula densa, part of the distal tubule","The macula densa, part of the proximal tubule"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"After the nephron loop climbs back toward the cortex, the distal convoluted tubule passes right beside the afferent and efferent arterioles of its own renal corpuscle. Where they touch, a patch of tightly packed tubule cells forms the macula densa, a name that means dense spot. Together with the juxtaglomerular cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole, it makes up the juxtaglomerular apparatus. Because the crowded cells are tubule cells and the segment touches its own corpuscle's arteriole, they must be the macula densa of the distal tubule.","notes":["Juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole, not tubule cells, and they are not part of the proximal tubule. This would be the answer if the crowded cells were inside the arteriole wall itself.","Podocytes are the cells of the visceral layer of the glomerular capsule, wrapped around the glomerular capillaries. They sit inside the corpuscle, not in a patch of tubule wall pressed against the arteriole. This would be the answer if the cells were clinging to the capillaries of the glomerulus.","Correct. The distal convoluted tubule returns to touch its own corpuscle's arterioles, and its crowded cells at that spot are the macula densa.","The name is right, but the segment is not. The macula densa forms where the distal convoluted tubule touches the arterioles, after the loop has climbed back to the cortex. The proximal tubule leads away from the capsule toward the loop and is not the segment that forms this contact."],"fix":"Review where the juxtaglomerular apparatus forms and which of its cells belong to the tubule and which to the arteriole."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nephron-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nephron","comp":"w6-nephron-types","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these structures from closest to the kidney surface to deepest in the kidney: the tip of a juxtamedullary nephron loop, the renal corpuscle of a juxtamedullary nephron, the renal capsule, and the renal corpuscle of a cortical nephron.","options":["Renal capsule, cortical corpuscle, juxtamedullary corpuscle, juxtamedullary loop tip","Renal capsule, juxtamedullary corpuscle, cortical corpuscle, juxtamedullary loop tip","Cortical corpuscle, renal capsule, juxtamedullary corpuscle, juxtamedullary loop tip","Renal capsule, cortical corpuscle, juxtamedullary loop tip, juxtamedullary corpuscle"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The renal capsule lies right on the kidney surface, so it is the most superficial. Cortical nephrons have their renal corpuscles in the outer cortex, while juxtamedullary nephrons have their corpuscles deep in the cortex, right next to the medulla (juxta means next to). The long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons then run deep into the medulla, so the tip of one of those loops is the deepest point listed. The names of the two nephron types tell you where their corpuscles sit.","notes":["Correct. The capsule is on the surface, cortical corpuscles are in the outer cortex, juxtamedullary corpuscles sit beside the medulla, and their long loops reach deep into it.","This swaps the two kinds of corpuscle. Juxtamedullary means next to the medulla, so those corpuscles are the deeper ones, while cortical nephrons have their corpuscles in the outer cortex. Students choose this when they forget what juxta means.","The renal capsule sits directly on the kidney surface, outside all of the kidney tissue, so nothing inside the kidney can be closer to the surface. Every renal corpuscle lies under the capsule, in the cortex.","The juxtamedullary loop runs from its nephron's tubule down into the medulla, so its tip is deeper than the corpuscle it belongs to. It is the longest kind of loop in the kidney. This order would put the bottom of the loop above the top of its own nephron."],"fix":"Review where the renal corpuscles and loops of cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons sit in the kidney."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nephron-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nephron","comp":"w6-urine-path","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A tiny crystal could lodge in any one of these four tubes. Which single blockage would stop fluid from the greatest number of nephrons?","options":["One proximal convoluted tubule","One nephron loop","One distal convoluted tubule","One collecting duct"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop and distal convoluted tubule all belong to a single nephron, so a blockage in any of them affects only that one nephron. The collecting duct is different because it receives fluid from the distal convoluted tubules of many nephrons. A blockage there backs up every nephron that drains into it. The more tubes that merge into a duct, the more nephrons a blockage in that duct affects.","notes":["The proximal convoluted tubule is the first coil of one nephron, so a crystal here blocks only that nephron. It would matter for a question about which blockage stops fluid closest to the renal corpuscle.","The nephron loop belongs to a single nephron, so a blockage here stops only that nephron's fluid. Even a long juxtamedullary loop still serves just one nephron.","The distal convoluted tubule is the last segment that belongs to only one nephron. A crystal here blocks that nephron, but the nephrons around it still drain freely into the collecting duct. Students pick this because it is the farthest along of the single-nephron segments.","Correct. The collecting duct is shared, receiving fluid from the distal tubules of many nephrons, so blocking it backs up all of them."],"fix":"Review which tubule segments belong to one nephron and which duct is shared by many nephrons."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nephron-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nephron","comp":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"Dye is injected into the efferent arteriole of one nephron. Moments later it shows up in capillaries running deep into the medulla, close to a renal papilla. What does this tell you?","options":["It is a cortical nephron, and the dye is in the vasa recta","It is a juxtamedullary nephron, and the dye is in the vasa recta","It is a juxtamedullary nephron, and the dye is in the interlobar arteries","It is a cortical nephron, and the dye is in the peritubular capillaries"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood leaving a glomerulus is still in an arteriole, the efferent arteriole, which leads to a second capillary bed around the tubules. In juxtamedullary nephrons, this second bed includes the vasa recta, long straight capillaries that run beside the long nephron loops deep into the medulla. Cortical nephrons have short loops that barely enter the medulla, and their tubules are wrapped by peritubular capillaries in the cortex. Dye from an efferent arteriole that reaches the deep medulla near a papilla must therefore be in the vasa recta of a juxtamedullary nephron.","notes":["The vessel is named correctly, but the nephron type is wrong. Cortical nephrons have short loops that barely enter the medulla, and their efferent arterioles feed peritubular capillaries in the cortex. Capillaries reaching deep into the medulla belong to a juxtamedullary nephron.","Correct. Only juxtamedullary nephrons send capillaries deep into the medulla, and those long straight capillaries are the vasa recta.","Interlobar arteries run between the pyramids and carry blood toward the glomeruli, upstream of the efferent arteriole. Dye placed in an efferent arteriole cannot flow backward into them. This would fit dye injected into a segmental artery, not an efferent arteriole.","This describes the usual capillary bed of a cortical nephron, but it does not match the finding. Peritubular capillaries wrap the tubules in the cortex, so dye in them would stay in the cortex instead of reaching the area near a papilla."],"fix":"Review where the efferent arteriole leads and how the capillary beds of cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons differ."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nephron-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nephron","comp":"w6-kidney-internal","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A needle biopsy samples only the deep medulla near a renal papilla, with no cortex at all. Which set of structures could the sample contain?","options":["Renal corpuscles and proximal convoluted tubules","Distal convoluted tubules and the macula densa","Juxtamedullary loops, collecting ducts and vasa recta","Short cortical loops and peritubular capillaries"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"All renal corpuscles and both convoluted tubules sit in the cortex, so a sample with no cortex cannot hold them. The macula densa is part of the distal tubule where it touches its own corpuscle, so it stays in the cortex too. The deep medulla holds the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons, the vasa recta that run beside those loops, and the collecting ducts heading down toward the papilla. Cortical nephrons have short loops that barely enter the medulla, so they do not reach the region near the papilla.","notes":["Renal corpuscles are found only in the cortex, and the proximal convoluted tubule begins right at the corpuscle. Neither one reaches the deep medulla. This set would fit a sample taken from the outer cortex.","The distal convoluted tubule is a cortical coil that returns to touch its own corpuscle, and the macula densa is part of that coil. Both are in the cortex. This set would fit a sample taken right beside a renal corpuscle.","Correct. Long juxtamedullary loops, the vasa recta beside them and the collecting ducts heading to the papilla are the structures that reach the deep medulla.","Cortical nephrons have short loops that barely enter the medulla, and peritubular capillaries wrap the tubules in the cortex. Neither reaches the deep medulla near the papilla. This set would fit a sample taken just across the border between cortex and medulla."],"fix":"Review which nephron parts and capillaries sit in the cortex and which reach deep into the medulla."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-urinary-tract-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-urinary-tract","comp":"w6-urine-path","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Bacteria enter at the urethral opening and climb the urinary tract until they infect the kidney. In what order do they pass these structures?","options":["Urethra, urinary bladder, ureter, renal pelvis","Urethra, ureter, urinary bladder, renal pelvis","Ureter, urinary bladder, urethra, renal pelvis","Urethra, urinary bladder, renal pelvis, ureter"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Urine normally flows from the renal pelvis into the ureter, down to the urinary bladder, and out through the urethra. Bacteria that cause a urinary tract infection usually travel this path in reverse, entering at the urethral opening. From the urethra they reach the bladder, then climb a ureter, and finally reach the renal pelvis inside the kidney. Reading the normal urine path backward gives the route of a climbing infection.","notes":["Correct. Climbing bacteria follow the urine path backward: urethra, bladder, ureter, then the renal pelvis.","This places the ureter between the urethra and the bladder. The urethra leaves the bladder and the ureters enter it, so the bladder sits between the two look-alike tubes. Students choose this when they mix up the names ureter and urethra.","The bacteria enter from outside the body, so the urethra must come first. This order starts at the ureter, which is deep inside the body and has no opening to the outside.","The renal pelvis narrows into the ureter at the hilum, so bacteria climbing from the bladder must pass through the ureter before they reach the pelvis. This order would only make sense if the ureter sat above the kidney."],"fix":"Review the path of urine from the renal pelvis to the outside, then trace it in reverse."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-urinary-tract-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-urinary-tract","comp":"w6-urinary-disorders","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"After losing a large amount of weight quickly, a patient develops right flank pain. Imaging shows the right kidney sitting lower than normal and its renal pelvis swollen with urine. Which chain of events best explains both findings?","options":["The renal capsule wore away, so the kidney swelled and squeezed its pelvis","The renal fascia thinned, so the bladder rose and pressed on the ureter","Fat in the renal columns shrank, so the pyramids collapsed on the calyces","The adipose capsule shrank, so the kidney dropped and kinked the ureter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The adipose capsule is the layer of fat around the kidney that cushions it and helps hold it in place. Rapid, severe weight loss can shrink this fat, and the kidney may then drop lower in the abdomen, a condition called nephroptosis or floating kidney. As the kidney sags, the ureter can kink. Urine can no longer flow freely out of the renal pelvis, so it backs up and swells the pelvis above the kink. This one chain links the fat loss, the low kidney and the swollen pelvis.","notes":["The renal capsule is the thin fibrous layer on the kidney surface that keeps its shape, and weight loss does not wear it away. This choice also does not explain why the kidney sits lower than normal. It would be a guess for a question about kidney shape, not kidney position.","The renal fascia does anchor the kidney, but this chain moves the bladder instead of the kidney and leaves the low kidney unexplained. The bladder lies in the pelvis well below the kidney, so it would not rise to press on the upper ureter.","The renal columns are extensions of the cortex between the pyramids, not a fat layer, so there is no fat in them to lose. This choice also leaves the low kidney unexplained. It mixes up the internal tissue regions with the protective layers outside the kidney.","Correct. Loss of the fat cushion lets the kidney drop (nephroptosis), and the kinked ureter traps urine in the renal pelvis."],"fix":"Review the three layers around the kidney, what the adipose capsule does, and how nephroptosis can kink the ureter."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-urinary-tract-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-urinary-tract","comp":"w6-kidney-internal","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Urine passes through these four spaces on its way out of the kidney. Rank them from the smallest to the largest: renal pelvis, papillary duct, major calyx, minor calyx.","options":["Minor calyx, papillary duct, major calyx, renal pelvis","Papillary duct, major calyx, minor calyx, renal pelvis","Papillary duct, minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis","Papillary duct, minor calyx, renal pelvis, major calyx"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Collecting ducts join into papillary ducts that open on the tip of a renal pyramid, and each papilla drains into a small cup, the minor calyx. Several minor calyces merge into a larger major calyx, and the major calyces all empty into the single renal pelvis. Because each space collects from several of the one before it, the spaces grow larger at every step. The size order and the order urine meets them are therefore the same.","notes":["A papillary duct is a microscopic duct opening on the tip of one pyramid, so it is much smaller than the minor calyx that cups that whole tip. The minor calyx catches urine from many papillary ducts at once.","This swaps the two calyces. Several minor calyces join together to form one major calyx, so the major calyx must be the larger of the two. Major means larger and minor means smaller, which is the quickest check.","Correct. Each space collects from several of the one before it, so they grow from papillary duct to minor calyx to major calyx to renal pelvis.","The renal pelvis receives all the major calyces, so it is larger than any one of them. This order would put the pelvis upstream of the major calyx, which reverses how the spaces merge."],"fix":"Review how papillary ducts, minor calyces, major calyces and the renal pelvis connect and merge."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-urinary-tract-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-urinary-tract","comp":"w6-urinary-disorders","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A 4-mm kidney stone lodges in the middle of the right ureter and blocks it. Urine keeps arriving from the kidney. Which structures will swell with trapped urine?","options":["The urinary bladder and the urethra below it","The right renal pelvis and its calyces","The lower right ureter and the urinary bladder","Both renal pelves and both ureters"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Urine flows from the calyces into the renal pelvis and then down the ureter to the bladder. A stone in the middle of the right ureter blocks that one path, so urine still being made by the right kidney cannot get past it. The urine backs up behind the stone, into the upper ureter, the renal pelvis and the calyces of the right kidney, which swell. Structures below the stone, and everything on the left side, keep draining normally.","notes":["The bladder and urethra are downstream of the stone, so they are not filling with trapped urine from the right kidney. The left ureter is still delivering urine to the bladder normally. Swelling builds up behind a blockage, not in front of it.","Correct. Urine backs up above the blockage, so the renal pelvis and calyces of the right kidney swell.","The lower part of the right ureter is below the stone, so urine from the kidney cannot reach it. The bladder still drains normally through the urethra. This choice puts the swelling on the wrong side of the stone.","Each kidney has its own renal pelvis and ureter, so a stone in the right ureter does not block the left side. The left kidney keeps draining into the bladder. Both sides would back up only if the blockage sat below the bladder, such as in the urethra."],"fix":"Review the path of urine from the calyces to the bladder and predict which side of a blockage fills with urine."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-urinary-tract-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-urinary-tract","comp":"w6-urine-path","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"During a cystoscopy, a scope passes up the urethra into the bladder. On the bladder floor the doctor finds the smooth triangle with an opening at each of its two upper corners. Urine spurts out of the right-side opening at regular intervals, but none ever comes from the left-side opening. Where is the blockage most likely to be?","options":["On the left side, between the left renal pelvis and the bladder","In the urethra, just below the internal urethral sphincter","On the right side, between the right renal pelvis and the bladder","In the detrusor muscle of the bladder wall beside the trigone"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The smooth triangle on the bladder floor is the trigone. Its two upper corners are the openings of the two ureters, and its lower corner is the urethral opening. Each ureter carries urine from one kidney's renal pelvis down to its own opening in the bladder. Urine arriving at the right opening shows the right side is open, while no urine at the left opening means something is blocking the left path, somewhere from the left renal pelvis down through the left ureter. A blockage in the urethra would sit below the bladder and could not stop urine from entering it.","notes":["Correct. The left upper corner of the trigone is the left ureteral opening, so no urine there means the left renal pelvis or left ureter is blocked.","The urethra carries urine out of the bladder, so a block there would trap urine in the bladder but would not stop urine from entering it through the ureters. This would be the answer if the bladder were full and the patient could not empty it.","Urine is squirting normally from the right opening, so the right renal pelvis and right ureter are open. This would be the answer if the right opening were silent and the left one were working.","The detrusor is the smooth muscle layer of the bladder wall that contracts to empty the bladder. It is not part of the path urine takes into the bladder, so a problem there would not silence one ureteral opening."],"fix":"Review the trigone, the three openings at its corners, and which kidney each ureteral opening drains."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-urinary-tract-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-urinary-tract","comp":"w6-kidney-gross","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Imaging of a healthy adult with no symptoms shows the right kidney sitting a little lower than the left. Which explanation is most likely?","options":["The adipose capsule around the right kidney has shrunk","The spleen above the left kidney pushes it higher up","The liver above the right kidney pushes it slightly down","The right ureter is shorter and pulls the kidney down"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both kidneys lie behind the peritoneum against the posterior abdominal wall, between about T12 and L3, but they are not perfectly level. The liver is the largest organ in the abdomen and fills the upper right side, so the right kidney sits slightly lower than the left. This is the normal position in a healthy person. Shrinking of the adipose capsule can also let a kidney drop, but that is a disorder seen after rapid, severe weight loss, so it is not the most likely cause in a healthy adult.","notes":["Loss of the adipose capsule can let a kidney drop, a disorder called nephroptosis, but it follows rapid, severe weight loss and can kink the ureter. In a healthy adult with no symptoms, a slightly lower right kidney is simply the normal position. This would be the answer if the kidney had dropped well below normal after weight loss.","The spleen does sit above the left kidney, but an organ above a kidney would press it down, not push it up. The usual explanation is the organ above the right kidney, and students choose this when they remember that a neighboring organ matters but attach it to the wrong side.","Correct. The liver fills the upper right abdomen, so the right kidney normally sits slightly lower than the left.","The ureters do not pull the kidneys down. They are tubes that carry urine from each renal pelvis to the bladder, and they hang from the kidney rather than anchoring it. The kidney's position is set by the organs around it and by its fat and fascia."],"fix":"Review where the kidneys sit, why the right kidney is lower, and what nephroptosis is."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-male-repro-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-male-repro","comp":"w6-sperm-path","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A sperm cell has just formed in a seminiferous tubule. In what order does it pass these four places on its way out of the body?","options":["Epididymis, rete testis, inguinal canal, prostate","Rete testis, inguinal canal, epididymis, prostate","Rete testis, epididymis, prostate, inguinal canal","Rete testis, epididymis, inguinal canal, prostate"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Sperm leave the seminiferous tubules and collect in the rete testis, a network of channels inside the testis. From there they pass through the efferent ductules into the epididymis on the back of the testis, where they are stored and gain the ability to move. The ductus deferens then carries them up inside the spermatic cord and through the inguinal canal into the pelvis. Behind the bladder it joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct, which runs through the prostate and opens into the prostatic urethra. Knowing which duct runs through each of these places gives the order.","notes":["The rete testis is inside the testis, so sperm reach it before they leave the testis for the epididymis. This order would have sperm enter the epididymis and then go back into the testis.","The ductus deferens begins at the epididymis, so sperm must pass the epididymis before they can travel up the spermatic cord through the inguinal canal. Students pick this when they forget that the epididymis sits on the testis itself, still inside the scrotum.","The prostate sits just below the bladder, inside the pelvis, so sperm can reach it only after the ductus deferens has carried them up through the inguinal canal. This order would have sperm pass through the prostate while they are still in the scrotum.","Correct. Sperm go from the rete testis to the epididymis, up the ductus deferens through the inguinal canal, and then through the prostate in the ejaculatory duct and prostatic urethra."],"fix":"Review the path of sperm from the seminiferous tubules to the urethra and where each duct runs."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-male-repro-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-male-repro","comp":"w6-male-glands-penis","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A man has had a vasectomy, in which the ductus deferens is cut and tied inside the scrotum. Which contributions are still present in his semen?","options":["Prostate and bulbourethral fluid only, since the seminal vesicle duct is cut too","Fluid from all three accessory glands, but no sperm","Only bulbourethral mucus, since all ducts above the urethra are cut","Sperm and bulbourethral mucus, but no seminal vesicle fluid"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A vasectomy cuts the ductus deferens low in the spermatic cord, inside the scrotum, so sperm coming from the epididymis cannot get past that point. All three accessory glands join the path farther downstream. The seminal vesicle duct joins the ductus deferens behind the bladder to form the ejaculatory duct, the prostate empties into the prostatic urethra, and the bulbourethral glands empty into the spongy urethra. Because none of those connections are cut, their fluids still form semen, but it contains no sperm.","notes":["The seminal vesicle duct joins the ductus deferens behind the bladder, far above the scrotum where the cut is made. That duct is untouched, so seminal vesicle fluid is still released. Students choose this when they picture the whole ductus deferens being removed rather than cut at one point.","Correct. The cut is in the scrotum, upstream of where all three glands join, so their fluids still form semen while sperm are blocked.","Only the ductus deferens is cut, and only inside the scrotum. The seminal vesicle duct, the ejaculatory ducts and the urethra are left intact, so the seminal vesicles and prostate still add their fluid.","Sperm come from the testis and must travel up the ductus deferens, so cutting it blocks them. Meanwhile, the seminal vesicle duct joins beyond the cut and is not affected. This choice reverses which contribution is lost."],"fix":"Review where the ductus deferens runs and where each accessory gland joins the path of sperm."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-male-repro-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-male-repro","comp":"w6-male-disorders","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A 70-year-old man has a weak urine stream that is hard to start, and his bladder never feels fully empty. Imaging shows that both kidneys and both ureters are normal in size. Which explanation best fits, given where each structure sits?","options":["An enlarged prostate narrowing the urethra just below the bladder","Enlarged seminal vesicles pressing on the ureters behind the bladder","Swollen bulbourethral glands narrowing the urethra in the penis","A varicocele pressing on the urethra as it crosses the perineum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prostate sits just below the urinary bladder and wraps around the first part of the urethra, the prostatic urethra. In benign prostatic hyperplasia, a non-cancerous enlargement common in older men, the growing gland squeezes this part of the urethra and narrows the outlet. That explains a weak stream that is hard to start and a bladder that does not empty well. The normal kidneys and ureters show that the problem is below the bladder, not above it, which rules out anything pressing on the ureters.","notes":["Correct. The prostate surrounds the urethra right below the bladder, and its non-cancerous enlargement in older men squeezes that outlet.","The seminal vesicles do sit behind the bladder, but pressure on the ureters would back urine up into the kidneys. Imaging shows normal ureters and kidneys, so the problem is below the bladder. This would fit a patient with swollen ureters and renal pelves.","The bulbourethral glands are small, pea-sized glands beside the membranous urethra, below the prostate, and they are not the gland that enlarges with age and blocks urine flow. This choice also places them in the penis, where only their ducts reach. It mixes up the bulbourethral glands with the prostate.","A varicocele is a swelling of the veins that drain the testis, the pampiniform plexus inside the spermatic cord. It sits in the scrotum, not around the urethra, so it would not narrow the urine stream."],"fix":"Review where the prostate sits, which region of the urethra runs through it, and what benign prostatic hyperplasia does."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-male-repro-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-male-repro","comp":"w6-scrotum-testes","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A man has a disorder that weakens skeletal muscle but leaves smooth muscle working normally. When he steps into a cold pool, how does his scrotum respond?","options":["The testes are still pulled up, but the scrotal skin stays smooth and loose","The scrotal skin still wrinkles, but the testes are lifted weakly or not at all","Neither change happens, since both scrotal muscles are skeletal muscle","Both changes happen normally, since both scrotal muscles are smooth muscle"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees below the body core of 37 C (98.6 F), and two muscles adjust how much heat the testes lose. The cremaster is skeletal muscle that runs in the spermatic cord and loops around each testis, and in the cold it pulls the testes up toward the warm body. The dartos is smooth muscle in the wall of the scrotum, and it wrinkles and tightens the skin to cut heat loss. A disorder of skeletal muscle weakens the cremaster but leaves the dartos alone, so the skin still wrinkles while the testes are not lifted well. Matching each muscle to its tissue type tells you which job is lost.","notes":["This reverses the two tissue types. The muscle that lifts the testes is the cremaster, which is skeletal muscle, so it is the one weakened here. This would be the answer if the disorder affected smooth muscle and spared skeletal muscle.","Correct. The skeletal cremaster is weakened, so the testes are not lifted well, while the smooth dartos still wrinkles the scrotal skin.","Only the cremaster is skeletal muscle. The dartos is smooth muscle in the scrotal wall, so it keeps working in this disorder. Students pick this when they forget that the two scrotal muscles are different tissues.","Only the dartos is smooth muscle. The cremaster is skeletal muscle, the striated type, so a skeletal muscle disorder does affect it. Students pick this when they remember that both muscles work without conscious effort and assume both must be smooth."],"fix":"Review the cremaster and dartos muscles: where each one sits, what each one does, and which muscle tissue each is made of."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-male-repro-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-male-repro","comp":"w6-sperm-path","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these openings along the male urethra, from the one closest to the bladder to the one farthest from it: external urethral orifice, openings of the bulbourethral gland ducts, internal urethral orifice, openings of the ejaculatory ducts.","options":["Internal urethral orifice, bulbourethral ducts, ejaculatory ducts, external urethral orifice","Ejaculatory ducts, internal urethral orifice, bulbourethral ducts, external urethral orifice","Internal urethral orifice, ejaculatory ducts, external urethral orifice, bulbourethral ducts","Internal urethral orifice, ejaculatory ducts, bulbourethral ducts, external urethral orifice"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The urethra begins at the internal urethral orifice, where it leaves the bladder. It first passes through the prostate as the prostatic urethra, and that is where the ejaculatory ducts open. It then crosses the perineum as the membranous urethra and enters the penis as the spongy urethra, where the bulbourethral glands release their mucus. The urethra ends at the external urethral orifice on the glans. Knowing which urethral region each duct empties into gives you the order.","notes":["This swaps the ejaculatory ducts and the bulbourethral ducts. The ejaculatory ducts empty into the prostatic urethra just below the bladder, while the bulbourethral glands empty into the spongy urethra farther along. Students choose this because the bulbourethral glands sit close to the prostate, but their ducts run forward into the spongy urethra.","The internal urethral orifice is where the urethra begins at the bladder, so nothing along the urethra can come before it. The ejaculatory ducts open just below it, in the prostatic urethra.","The external urethral orifice is the opening at the tip of the glans, the very end of the urethra, so it must be last. The bulbourethral ducts open into the spongy urethra before that point.","Correct. From the bladder outward: internal urethral orifice, ejaculatory ducts in the prostatic urethra, bulbourethral ducts in the spongy urethra, and the external orifice on the glans."],"fix":"Review the three regions of the male urethra and which ducts empty into each one."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-male-repro-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-male-repro","comp":"w6-sperm-cell","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A semen analysis shows sperm that barely swim. Under an electron microscope, every part of each sperm has a normal shape and length, but the organelles that make ATP are very sparse. Which part of the sperm is defective?","options":["The acrosome","The middle piece","The principal piece","The neck"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"ATP, the energy molecule cells run on, is made by mitochondria. In a sperm cell, the mitochondria are packed into the middle piece, the section of the tail just behind the neck. That energy drives the whipping motion of the rest of the tail. If the middle piece has too few mitochondria, the tail is built normally but has little energy to move, so the sperm barely swim.","notes":["The acrosome is the enzyme-filled cap over the head that helps the sperm get into the oocyte. It does not hold the mitochondria, and a faulty acrosome would block entry into the oocyte rather than slow swimming.","Correct. The middle piece is the part of the tail packed with mitochondria, which make the ATP that powers the tail.","The principal piece is the longest part of the tail and does much of the whipping, which makes it tempting. It does not hold the packed mitochondria, though. It would be the answer if the tail itself were too short or misshapen.","The neck is the short part just behind the head, and it holds the centrioles, not the mitochondria. It sits right next to the middle piece, which is why students mix the two up."],"fix":"Review the parts of a sperm cell in order from head to tail and what each part contains."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-female-repro-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-female-repro","comp":"w6-ovum-path","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"In pelvic inflammatory disease, bacteria climb from the vagina all the way into the pelvic cavity. After passing through the uterine cavity, in what order do they pass these four places?","options":["Ampulla, isthmus, infundibulum, opening into the pelvic cavity","Isthmus, infundibulum, ampulla, opening into the pelvic cavity","Isthmus, ampulla, infundibulum, opening into the pelvic cavity","Infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus, opening into the pelvic cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"An oocyte travels from the ovary to the uterus through the infundibulum, then the ampulla, then the isthmus, which joins the uterus. Bacteria climbing up from the uterus travel that same tube in the opposite direction. They enter at the isthmus, the narrow part attached to the uterus, move through the wide ampulla, and reach the funnel-shaped infundibulum. The infundibulum is open to the pelvic cavity rather than sealed onto the ovary, which is how the infection can spread beyond the tube to the ovaries and pelvic lining.","notes":["The ampulla is the wide middle part of the tube, not the part joined to the uterus. Bacteria leaving the uterus enter the tube at the isthmus first. This order skips the isthmus and then doubles back to it.","The infundibulum is the far, funnel-shaped end of the tube near the ovary, so bacteria reach it only after passing the ampulla. This order would put the funnel in the middle of the tube.","Correct. Climbing bacteria run the oocyte's path backward: isthmus, ampulla, infundibulum, and then out through the open end of the tube into the pelvic cavity.","This is the order an oocyte meets the tube regions on its way to the uterus. Bacteria climbing up from the uterus travel the same tube in reverse, so the isthmus comes first and the infundibulum last."],"fix":"Review the regions of the uterine tube from the ovary to the uterus, then trace them in reverse."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-female-repro-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-female-repro","comp":"w6-ovum-path","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A woman's uterine tubes were scarred by pelvic inflammatory disease. An oocyte is fertilized in the ampulla, but the scarring slows the early embryo as it moves toward the uterus. Where is the embryo most likely to implant, and what makes that site a problem?","options":["In the uterine tube, which is too narrow and thin to stretch","In the endometrium, the normal site, so no problem arises","In the cervical canal, which is too narrow to hold it","In the ovarian cortex, after drifting back out of the tube"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Pelvic inflammatory disease can scar the uterine tubes, and a scarred tube slows the embryo as cilia and fluid move it toward the uterus. The embryo keeps developing on the way, so if it is delayed it may implant before it reaches the uterus. The most likely site is the uterine tube itself, which is called an ectopic pregnancy. The tube is narrow and thin-walled compared with the thick, muscular uterus, so it cannot stretch to hold a growing embryo and may rupture and bleed into the pelvic cavity.","notes":["Correct. A delayed embryo most often implants in the tube itself, an ectopic pregnancy, and the narrow, thin tube cannot stretch to hold it.","The endometrium is the normal implantation site, but the embryo described is delayed in a scarred tube and may implant before it gets there. This would be the answer if the tubes were healthy and the embryo reached the uterine cavity on time.","The cervix is below the uterine cavity, so an embryo coming down the tube would have to pass the whole uterus to get there. A delay in the tube makes it more likely to implant before reaching the uterus, not after passing through it.","The embryo is moving away from the ovary toward the uterus, since fertilization happened in the ampulla. A slowed embryo is held back in the tube, not carried back out to the ovary. The most common ectopic site is the tube itself."],"fix":"Review the path of the oocyte through the uterine tube, where fertilization and implantation normally happen, and what an ectopic pregnancy is."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-female-repro-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-female-repro","comp":"w6-vagina-vulva","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A nurse is placing a urinary catheter in a female patient lying on her back. Rank these structures from the most anterior (toward the pubic bone) to the most posterior: vaginal orifice, anus, clitoris, external urethral orifice.","options":["Clitoris, vaginal orifice, urethral orifice, anus","Clitoris, urethral orifice, vaginal orifice, anus","Urethral orifice, clitoris, vaginal orifice, anus","Clitoris, urethral orifice, anus, vaginal orifice"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The clitoris sits at the front of the vulva, where the labia minora meet. Behind it, inside the vestibule, the external urethral orifice lies in front and the vaginal orifice lies behind it. The anus is farther back still, outside the vestibule, in the anal triangle of the perineum. Knowing that the urethral opening sits between the clitoris and the vaginal opening is what lets the nurse find it.","notes":["This swaps the urethral and vaginal openings. The urethral orifice lies in front of the vaginal orifice, between it and the clitoris. A catheter placed using this order would be aimed at the vagina instead of the urethra.","Correct. From front to back: clitoris, urethral orifice, vaginal orifice, then the anus outside the vestibule.","The clitoris is the most anterior of these, sitting where the labia minora meet in front. The urethral orifice lies just behind it, not in front of it.","The anus lies behind the vaginal orifice, in the anal triangle of the perineum, outside the vestibule. It is the most posterior of the four. Students choose this when they forget that both the urethra and the vagina open into the vestibule ahead of the anus."],"fix":"Review the structures of the vulva and the front to back order of the openings in and near the vestibule."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-female-repro-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-female-repro","comp":"w6-uterus","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A doctor passes a thin scope through the vagina to remove a uterine fibroid, a non-cancerous tumor of the smooth muscle in the uterine wall. In what order does the scope reach these four places?","options":["Internal os, external os, endometrium, myometrium","External os, internal os, myometrium, endometrium","External os, endometrium, internal os, myometrium","External os, internal os, endometrium, myometrium"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cervical canal runs through the cervix, opening to the vagina at the external os and to the uterine cavity at the internal os. A scope coming up from the vagina therefore meets the external os first and the internal os second. Once inside the uterine cavity, it faces the endometrium, the inner lining of the uterine wall. Uterine fibroids grow from the myometrium, the thick smooth muscle layer under the endometrium, so the muscle layer is the last place the scope reaches.","notes":["The internal os opens into the uterine cavity at the top of the cervical canal, so a scope coming from the vagina cannot reach it first. The external os faces the vagina and is the entrance. This order would fit a scope coming down from inside the uterus.","The cervical openings are in the right order, but the wall layers are swapped. The endometrium lines the uterine cavity, so a scope inside the cavity meets it before the myometrium beneath it. Muscle before lining is the order a surgeon cutting in from outside the uterus would meet.","The endometrium lines the uterine cavity, which lies above the cervix, so the scope cannot reach it before passing the internal os. The internal os is the upper end of the cervical canal. Students pick this when they forget that the cervical canal has two openings.","Correct. The scope enters the cervical canal at the external os, leaves it at the internal os, meets the endometrium lining the cavity, and then reaches the myometrium where the fibroid grows."],"fix":"Review the cervical canal and its two openings, and the three layers of the uterine wall from inside to outside."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-female-repro-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-female-repro","comp":"w6-ovary","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A pathologist examining an ovary finds a small white scar of connective tissue inside the ovarian cortex. What is it, and what does it tell her about that ovary's history?","options":["A corpus albicans, left after a corpus luteum broke down","A corpus luteum, left right after a follicle released its oocyte","A vesicular follicle, about to release its oocyte","The tunica albuginea, the capsule under the surface"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"After ovulation, the follicle that released its oocyte becomes the corpus luteum, whose name means yellow body. The corpus luteum is temporary, and if no pregnancy occurs it breaks down. What remains is a white scar of connective tissue called the corpus albicans, meaning white body. Finding one tells the pathologist that a follicle in this ovary once ovulated and that the corpus luteum it formed later broke down.","notes":["Correct. A white connective tissue scar is a corpus albicans, which forms when a corpus luteum breaks down after an earlier ovulation.","The corpus luteum is the yellow body that forms right after ovulation, and it is an active structure, not a white scar. It becomes the white scar only later, after it breaks down. This would be the answer if the structure were yellow and newly formed.","A vesicular follicle is a large, fluid-filled follicle holding an oocyte, not a solid scar. It is the stage just before ovulation, so it comes before the corpus luteum rather than after it.","The tunica albuginea is the whitish capsule of dense connective tissue that wraps the whole ovary just under the germinal epithelium. It is white, which makes it tempting, but it is a capsule around the organ, not a small scar inside the cortex."],"fix":"Review the follicle stages in order and what happens to the follicle after ovulation."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-female-repro-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-female-repro","comp":"w6-mammary","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A breastfeeding mother has one lactiferous duct blocked near the nipple. Milk from which part of the breast can no longer reach the nipple?","options":["Milk from the whole breast, since all lobes share one duct","Milk from a single lobule, since each lobule has its own duct","Milk from the alveoli of the one lobe that duct drains","Milk from the lobes on the opposite side of the nipple"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The mammary gland is nested. Milk is made in the alveoli, the alveoli sit in lobules, and the lobules are grouped into lobes. Each lobe drains through its own lactiferous duct, which carries milk toward the nipple and opens on its surface. A blocked lactiferous duct therefore traps milk from every alveolus in that one lobe, while the other lobes keep draining through their own ducts.","notes":["Each breast has 15 to 20 or more lobes, and each lobe has its own lactiferous duct opening at the nipple. The lobes do not share one duct, so a single blocked duct cannot stop milk from the whole breast.","Lactiferous ducts drain whole lobes, not single lobules. A block in one lactiferous duct affects all the lobules inside that lobe. Students choose this when they mix up lobe and lobule.","Correct. Each lobe drains through one lactiferous duct, so blocking that duct traps the milk from all the alveoli in that lobe.","Each lobe drains through its own lactiferous duct, so a blocked duct does not affect the lobes on the other side of the nipple. Those lobes still drain normally. This choice links a duct to one side of the breast instead of to a single lobe."],"fix":"Review how alveoli, lobules, lobes and lactiferous ducts are arranged in the mammary gland."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nervous-tissue-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nervous-tissue","comp":"w8-neuron-parts","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"One neuron passes a signal to a second neuron at an axodendritic synapse. In what order does the signal reach these four places?","options":["First neuron's axon hillock, synaptic cleft, second neuron's axon hillock, second neuron's dendrite","First neuron's axon hillock, synaptic cleft, second neuron's dendrite, second neuron's axon hillock","Synaptic cleft, first neuron's axon hillock, second neuron's dendrite, second neuron's axon hillock","First neuron's axon hillock, second neuron's dendrite, synaptic cleft, second neuron's axon hillock"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In the first neuron, the signal leaves the cell body at the axon hillock, where the axon begins, and travels down the axon to the synaptic end bulbs. There it crosses the synaptic cleft as neurotransmitter. An axodendritic synapse is one where an axon ends on a dendrite, so the signal arrives on a dendrite of the second neuron. Dendrites carry signals toward the cell body, and from there the signal reaches the second neuron's axon hillock. Every neuron follows the same direction, dendrites in and axon out, so the pattern simply repeats in the second cell.","notes":["This has the signal reach the second neuron's axon hillock before its dendrite. At an axodendritic synapse the signal lands on a dendrite, and dendrites carry it toward the cell body and the hillock. This order runs the second neuron backward.","Correct. The signal leaves the first neuron at the axon hillock, crosses the cleft, lands on a dendrite of the second neuron, and travels to that neuron's axon hillock.","The synaptic cleft lies at the far end of the first neuron's axon, so the signal must pass the axon hillock at the start of that axon before it reaches the cleft. This order starts the signal in the gap between the two cells.","The two neurons do not touch at a chemical synapse, so the signal must cross the synaptic cleft before it reaches the second neuron's dendrite. This order would only work if the axon terminal and the dendrite were joined directly."],"fix":"Review the parts of a neuron in the order a signal travels through them and how synapses are named by the site they contact."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nervous-tissue-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nervous-tissue","comp":"w8-myelination","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A woman's peripheral nerve in the forearm is cut cleanly by glass, and her brother has an injury that cuts a tract in his spinal cord. Which prediction about regrowth of the cut axons is best supported?","options":["Both regrow, since Schwann cells myelinate axons in both places","His regrow, since oligodendrocytes leave a neurolemma, but hers do not","Neither regrows, since the part still joined to the cell body dies","Hers can regrow along a neurolemma, but his regrow very poorly"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the peripheral nervous system, Schwann cells form the myelin, and each one leaves its outer layer, the neurolemma, wrapped around the axon. After a cut, the neurolemma forms a tube that guides the regrowing axon back toward its target, so the forearm nerve can regrow. In the spinal cord, which is part of the CNS, oligodendrocytes form the myelin and leave no neurolemma, so a cut tract has no guide and regrowth is very limited. In both cases, the part of the axon beyond the cut dies because it is separated from the cell body, while the part still attached survives.","notes":["Schwann cells are found only in the PNS. Inside the spinal cord, the myelin is made by oligodendrocytes, so the brother's tract has no Schwann cells to help it regrow.","This reverses the two glial cells. Schwann cells in the PNS leave a neurolemma, while oligodendrocytes in the CNS do not. That difference is why the peripheral nerve has the better chance of regrowing.","When an axon is cut, the part beyond the cut dies because it loses its supply of proteins from the cell body. The part still attached to the cell body survives, and in the PNS it can regrow. This choice reverses which piece of the axon dies.","Correct. Her forearm nerve is in the PNS, where the Schwann cell neurolemma guides regrowth, but his tract is in the CNS, where oligodendrocytes leave no neurolemma."],"fix":"Review which glial cell forms myelin in the CNS and in the PNS, what the neurolemma is, and how it affects regrowth."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nervous-tissue-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nervous-tissue","comp":"w8-neuroglia","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On a slide of a posterior root ganglion, small flat cells form a ring around each large, round neuron cell body. What are these glial cells, and which CNS glial cell plays a similar support role?","options":["Satellite cells; astrocytes play a similar role in the CNS","Schwann cells; oligodendrocytes play a similar role in the CNS","Satellite cells; ependymal cells play a similar role in the CNS","Microglia; satellite cells play a similar role in the CNS"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A posterior root ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the CNS, so the glia there must be one of the two PNS types. Schwann cells wrap axons, while satellite cells surround and support the cell bodies inside ganglia, so the ring of cells around each cell body is made of satellite cells. In the CNS, astrocytes are the glial cells that support neurons in a similar way, giving structural support and keeping the chemical environment around neurons stable.","notes":["Correct. Satellite cells ring the neuron cell bodies inside ganglia, and astrocytes support neurons in a similar way in the CNS.","Schwann cells wrap axons in myelin, not cell bodies, and their CNS partner, the oligodendrocyte, also makes myelin. This pairing would be right for a question about the cells that myelinate axons.","The ganglion cells are named correctly, but ependymal cells line the ventricles and central canal and help circulate cerebrospinal fluid. They do not surround and support neuron cell bodies.","Microglia are CNS phagocytes that clear debris, so they are not the cells ringing cell bodies in a PNS ganglion. Satellite cells belong to the PNS, not the CNS, so this pairing also reverses the two divisions."],"fix":"Review the two PNS glial cells and the four CNS glial cells and the support role of each one."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nervous-tissue-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nervous-tissue","comp":"w8-tissue-collections","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A touch signal starts in the skin of the leg and travels into the spinal cord. Rank these structures from farthest from the spinal cord to inside it: posterior root ganglion, posterior gray horn, sensory receptor in the skin, spinal nerve.","options":["Sensory receptor, posterior root ganglion, spinal nerve, posterior gray horn","Spinal nerve, sensory receptor, posterior root ganglion, posterior gray horn","Sensory receptor, spinal nerve, posterior root ganglion, posterior gray horn","Sensory receptor, spinal nerve, posterior gray horn, posterior root ganglion"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sensory receptor in the skin is the farthest point, where the signal begins. The sensory axon travels in a spinal nerve, a bundle of axons in the PNS, back toward the cord. Near the cord, the spinal nerve divides into roots, and the posterior root carries a swelling, the posterior root ganglion, which is a cluster of sensory neuron cell bodies just outside the CNS. The signal then enters the cord and reaches the posterior gray horn, which is gray matter inside the CNS. Sorting each structure by whether it lies outside or inside the CNS, and how close it sits to the cord, gives the order.","notes":["This swaps the spinal nerve and the ganglion. The posterior root ganglion sits on the posterior root right beside the cord, while the spinal nerve lies farther out, beyond where the roots join. Signals from the skin travel along the spinal nerve before reaching the ganglion.","The sensory receptor in the skin is where the signal begins, so it is the farthest from the cord. The spinal nerve carries the signal from the receptor toward the cord, so it lies between them.","Correct. Receptor in the skin, then the spinal nerve, then the posterior root ganglion beside the cord, then the posterior gray horn inside the cord.","The posterior root ganglion lies just outside the cord, while the posterior gray horn is gray matter inside it. A cluster of cell bodies inside the CNS would be called a nucleus, not a ganglion, so the ganglion cannot be the innermost structure."],"fix":"Review the difference between nerves, ganglia, tracts and nuclei and where each sits relative to the CNS."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nervous-tissue-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nervous-tissue","comp":"w8-synapse-parts","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A toxin stops the synaptic vesicles in a neuron's synaptic end bulbs from releasing their contents. At which point does the signal stop?","options":["At the axon hillock, before it reaches the axon","At the dendrites, before it reaches the cell body","At the postsynaptic membrane, after crossing the cleft","At the synaptic end bulb, before crossing the cleft"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"At a chemical synapse, the two cells do not touch. When a signal reaches the synaptic end bulb at the tip of an axon terminal, the synaptic vesicles release neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft, and it diffuses across to the postsynaptic membrane. If the vesicles cannot release neurotransmitter, nothing crosses the cleft. The signal still travels down the axon normally but stops in the end bulb, and the receiving cell never gets the message.","notes":["The axon hillock is where the axon begins, upstream of the end bulbs. The toxin acts only on the vesicles at the far end of the axon, so the signal still passes the hillock and travels along the axon.","Dendrites receive signals and pass them toward the cell body, which happens before the signal ever reaches the vesicles. A toxin acting on the end bulbs would not stop the signal this early.","Neurotransmitter must be released from the vesicles to cross the cleft. If the vesicles cannot release it, nothing reaches the postsynaptic membrane. This would be the answer if the toxin blocked the receiving cell instead of the sending one.","Correct. The vesicles in the end bulb hold the neurotransmitter, so blocking their release stops the signal before it can cross the synaptic cleft."],"fix":"Review the parts of a chemical synapse in order from the synaptic end bulb to the postsynaptic membrane."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-nervous-tissue-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-nervous-tissue","comp":"w8-gray-white-matter","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A student is handed an unlabeled cross-section of nervous tissue and must decide whether it came from the cerebrum or the spinal cord. Which observation would best show that it came from the spinal cord?","options":["Both gray matter and white matter are present in it","Its white matter is built from bundles of myelinated axons","Gray matter sits in the center, ringed by white matter","Its gray matter is packed with neuron cell bodies and dendrites"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Gray matter is made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites and unmyelinated axons, and white matter is made of myelinated axons, in both the brain and the spinal cord. What differs is the arrangement. In the cerebrum, gray matter forms the outer cortex with white matter beneath it, while in the spinal cord a butterfly-shaped gray core is surrounded by white columns. The presence or makeup of gray and white matter cannot tell the two apart, but a central gray core ringed by white matter points to the spinal cord.","notes":["Both the cerebrum and the spinal cord contain gray and white matter, so this is true of either sample. It does not help decide which one the slice came from.","White matter is made of myelinated axons wherever it is found, in the brain or the cord. This is a true statement, but it describes both organs equally.","Correct. Gray matter in the center with white matter around it is the spinal cord pattern, the reverse of the cerebrum.","Gray matter holds neuron cell bodies and dendrites in both the brain and the spinal cord. This is true, but it would not separate a cerebrum sample from a cord sample."],"fix":"Review what gray and white matter are made of and how their positions differ in the brain and the spinal cord."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cerebrum-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cerebrum","comp":"w7-cortex-functional-areas","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"After a stroke, a man follows spoken instructions without any trouble, but his own speech is slow, halting, and effortful. His right hand and arm are also weak. Which area of the brain did the stroke most likely damage?","options":["The right frontal lobe, near the precentral and inferior frontal gyri","The left temporal and parietal junction, at Wernicke's area","The left frontal lobe, near the precentral and inferior frontal gyri","The left occipital lobe, along the calcarine sulcus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Two clues point to the same place. Slow, effortful speech with normal understanding is the pattern of damage to Broca's area, which sits in the inferior frontal gyrus, usually on the left. Weakness of the right arm points to the left primary motor cortex on the precentral gyrus, because most motor fibers cross to the opposite side at the decussation of the pyramids. Both areas lie close together in the left frontal lobe, so one stroke there explains both problems.","notes":["Motor fibers cross at the decussation of the pyramids, so the right frontal lobe controls the left side of the body, and Broca's area is usually on the left. This would be the answer if the left arm were weak and speech were normal.","Wernicke's area handles comprehension, so damage there leaves speech fluent but takes away understanding. This patient understands instructions, which rules it out. Pick this when a patient speaks fluently but cannot make sense of language.","Correct. Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus and the motor cortex for the right arm on the left precentral gyrus sit close together in the left frontal lobe.","This is the primary visual cortex, so damage here causes vision loss, not slow speech or arm weakness. It would be the answer if the patient lost part of his vision with normal speech and strength."],"fix":"Review where Broca's area and the primary motor cortex sit, and why the left hemisphere controls the right side of the body."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cerebrum-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cerebrum","comp":"w7-basal-ganglia","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On a coronal section through the middle of the cerebrum, a student traces a straight line from the lateral surface of one hemisphere inward toward the thalamus. Which list ranks these structures from most lateral to most medial?","options":["Cerebral cortex, putamen, globus pallidus, internal capsule","Cerebral cortex, globus pallidus, putamen, internal capsule","Cerebral cortex, internal capsule, putamen, globus pallidus","Putamen, cerebral cortex, globus pallidus, internal capsule"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cortex is the outer layer of gray matter, so it always comes first from the surface. Deep within the white matter sits the lens-shaped lentiform nucleus, with the putamen on its lateral side and the globus pallidus on its medial side. The internal capsule, the band of projection fibers, passes medial to the lentiform nucleus, between it and the thalamus. Reading the section from the outside in therefore gives cortex, putamen, globus pallidus, and then internal capsule.","notes":["Correct. The cortex is on the surface, the putamen is the lateral part of the lentiform nucleus, the globus pallidus is its medial part, and the internal capsule runs medial to both.","This swaps the two halves of the lentiform nucleus. The putamen lies on the lateral side and the globus pallidus on the medial side, a mix-up that is easy to make if you remember the lentiform nucleus but not which half faces outward.","The internal capsule does not run along the outside of the basal nuclei. It passes medial to the lentiform nucleus, between it and the thalamus, so it is the last of these structures the line reaches. This order would fit only if the capsule wrapped the lateral side of the basal nuclei.","The cerebral cortex is the outer layer of gray matter, so nothing deep can lie lateral to it. The putamen is one of the basal nuclei buried within the white matter, beneath the cortex. This order would make sense only if the basal nuclei sat on the surface of the hemisphere."],"fix":"Review the parts of the lentiform nucleus and where the internal capsule sits relative to the basal nuclei."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cerebrum-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cerebrum","comp":"w7-white-matter-tracts","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Four patients each have a small stroke of about the same size in a different spot. Which patient is most likely to have weakness of the face, arm, and leg on one whole side of the body?","options":["The patient whose stroke hit a small patch of the precentral gyrus","The patient whose stroke hit the internal capsule","The patient whose stroke hit the corpus callosum","The patient whose stroke hit the arcuate fasciculus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The internal capsule is a narrow band of projection fibers beside the basal nuclei, and nearly all the motor fibers heading from the cortex down to the brainstem and spinal cord squeeze through it. Because so much wiring is packed into a small space, a small stroke there cuts the commands for the face, arm, and leg at once. The same size stroke in the wide strip of motor cortex would hit only part of that strip. Commissural and association fibers connect cortical areas to each other, not to the body, so they do not carry these commands.","notes":["The precentral gyrus does hold the primary motor cortex, but it is a long strip, and a small patch of it controls only part of the body, such as the hand. A stroke covering the whole strip would be needed to weaken the entire side.","Correct. Nearly all the motor fibers leaving the cortex squeeze through this narrow band of projection fibers, so a small lesion cuts commands for the whole side.","The corpus callosum is made of commissural fibers that connect the two hemispheres to each other. It does not carry commands down to the body, so damage there disrupts sharing between the hemispheres rather than causing one-sided weakness.","The arcuate fasciculus is a bundle of association fibers linking Broca's and Wernicke's areas within one hemisphere. Damage there affects how the language areas work together, not the strength of the face and limbs."],"fix":"Review the three classes of cerebral white matter fibers and why the internal capsule packs so much wiring into a small space."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cerebrum-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cerebrum","comp":"w7-limbic-system","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A rare disease damages the amygdala on both sides of the brain, but the hippocampus and the rest of the temporal lobe are spared. Which change would you predict?","options":["Unable to form new memories, while fear responses stay normal","Loss of old childhood memories, while new learning stays normal","Slow, stiff movements with a resting tremor","Weaker fear responses, while new memories still form"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The amygdala and the hippocampus are neighbors in the medial temporal lobe, but they do different jobs in the limbic system. The amygdala handles fear and aggression and links emotion to memory, so losing it on both sides blunts fear responses. The hippocampus is the structure needed to form new long-term memories, and here it is intact, so new learning still works. Separating the two structures by their jobs is what lets you predict a deficit from the exact site of damage.","notes":["This is the pattern of hippocampal damage, since the hippocampus is needed to form new long-term memories. Here the hippocampus is spared, so this would be the answer only if the damage were limited to the hippocampus instead.","Old memories are stored across the cerebral cortex, so damage to the amygdala does not erase them. This choice tempts students who think the limbic system stores every memory, but even hippocampal damage mainly blocks new learning while older memories can still be recalled.","These are the signs of Parkinson disease, which comes from loss of the substantia nigra working with the basal nuclei. They are movement problems, not limbic ones, so they point to the basal nuclei circuit instead of the amygdala.","Correct. The amygdala handles fear and aggression, and the spared hippocampus can still lay down new long-term memories."],"fix":"Review the amygdala, hippocampus, and cingulate gyrus and the job each one does in the limbic system."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cerebrum-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cerebrum","comp":"w7-cerebrum-surface","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A neurosurgeon moves a probe along the top of the left hemisphere, starting just behind the forehead and moving toward the back of the head. In what order does the probe pass these regions and landmarks?","options":["Prefrontal cortex, precentral gyrus, central sulcus, postcentral gyrus","Prefrontal cortex, postcentral gyrus, central sulcus, precentral gyrus","Precentral gyrus, prefrontal cortex, central sulcus, postcentral gyrus","Prefrontal cortex, central sulcus, precentral gyrus, postcentral gyrus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The prefrontal cortex forms the most anterior part of the frontal lobe, right behind the forehead. Moving back, the last gyrus of the frontal lobe is the precentral gyrus, which holds the primary motor cortex. The central sulcus comes next and marks the border with the parietal lobe, and the first gyrus behind it is the postcentral gyrus with the primary somatosensory cortex. The prefixes give the order away, since pre means in front of the central sulcus and post means behind it.","notes":["Correct. The prefrontal cortex is the front of the frontal lobe, the precentral gyrus is its last ridge, and the central sulcus separates it from the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe.","This swaps the two gyri. Pre means in front of, so the precentral gyrus lies in front of the central sulcus and the postcentral gyrus lies behind it.","The prefrontal cortex is the most anterior part of the frontal lobe, so the probe meets it first. The precentral gyrus is the most posterior gyrus of the frontal lobe, right against the central sulcus.","This puts both gyri behind the central sulcus. Only the postcentral gyrus lies behind it, while the precentral gyrus sits just in front of it as the back edge of the frontal lobe."],"fix":"Review the gyri on either side of the central sulcus and where the prefrontal cortex sits in the frontal lobe."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cerebrum-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cerebrum","comp":"w7-brain-organization","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A severe injury destroys most of the cerebral cortex on both sides, but the diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum are undamaged. Which function is most likely to continue on its own?","options":["Feeling exactly where a touch lands on the skin","Recognizing familiar faces by sight","The automatic rhythm of breathing","Planning and starting voluntary movement"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each of the four major brain regions has its own jobs. The cerebrum, through its cortex, handles detailed sensation, recognition, and voluntary movement, so those are lost when most of the cortex is destroyed. The basic rhythm of breathing is set by the respiratory center in the medulla oblongata, which is part of the brainstem and was spared. That is why breathing can continue even when the cortex is badly damaged.","notes":["Pinpointing where a touch lands is the job of the primary somatosensory cortex on the postcentral gyrus. With the cortex destroyed, touch signals can no longer be mapped to an exact spot on the body, so this function is lost.","Recognizing faces depends on the visual association area around the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe. That is cortex, so this function is lost along with it.","Correct. The respiratory center that sets the breathing rhythm is in the medulla oblongata, part of the spared brainstem.","Voluntary movement is planned by the premotor cortex and started by the primary motor cortex, both in the frontal lobe. The spared cerebellum smooths movements but does not start them, so this function is lost."],"fix":"Review the four major brain regions and which jobs belong to the cerebrum and which belong to the brainstem."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-diencephalon-brainstem-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem","comp":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A small brainstem stroke causes weakness of the right half of the tongue along with weakness of the left arm and leg. Eye movements, chewing, and facial expression are all normal. Where is the lesion most likely?","options":["The left medulla, where the pyramids have not yet crossed","The right medulla, where the pyramids have not yet crossed","The right pons, near the nuclei of cranial nerves V through VIII","The right midbrain, near the nuclei of cranial nerves III and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Start with the tongue. The hypoglossal nucleus (CN XII) sits in the medulla, and it serves the tongue muscles on its own side, so right tongue weakness places the lesion in the right medulla. The descending motor tracts to the limbs run through the pyramids on the front of the medulla and cross to the other side only at the decussation at its lower end. A lesion in the right medulla above that crossing catches fibers that will serve the left limbs, so the arm and leg weakness shows up on the left. Normal eye movement, chewing, and facial expression rule out the midbrain and the pons.","notes":["The hypoglossal nucleus serves the tongue on its own side, so a left-sided lesion would weaken the left half of the tongue and the right limbs. This would be the answer if every side in the stem were flipped.","Correct. The right hypoglossal nucleus serves the right half of the tongue, and the right pyramid carries fibers that will cross lower down to serve the left limbs.","The motor fibers in the right pons also have not crossed yet, so left limb weakness fits, but the hypoglossal nucleus is in the medulla, not the pons. A pons lesion would also tend to affect chewing or facial expression, which are normal here.","The midbrain holds the nuclei for nerves that move the eye, and eye movements are normal. The tongue nucleus is much lower, in the medulla, so a midbrain lesion cannot explain the tongue weakness."],"fix":"Review which brainstem region holds each cranial nerve nucleus and where the corticospinal fibers cross to the other side."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-diencephalon-brainstem-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem","comp":"w7-brainstem-regions","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A motor command for the leg leaves the primary motor cortex and travels down toward the spinal cord. In what order does it pass these structures?","options":["Cerebral peduncle, internal capsule, pons, pyramid of the medulla","Internal capsule, pons, cerebral peduncle, pyramid of the medulla","Internal capsule, cerebral peduncle, pyramid of the medulla, pons","Internal capsule, cerebral peduncle, pons, pyramid of the medulla"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Motor fibers from the cortex first gather in the internal capsule, the band of projection fibers deep in the cerebrum. They continue down into the brainstem, where they form the cerebral peduncles on the front of the midbrain, and then pass through the pons. Below the pons they form the pyramids on the front of the medulla, where most of them cross before entering the spinal cord. The order simply follows the brainstem from top to bottom: midbrain, pons, medulla.","notes":["The cerebral peduncles are on the front of the midbrain, while the internal capsule is up in the cerebrum beside the basal nuclei. The fibers pass the internal capsule first, before they reach the brainstem at all.","This puts the pons above the midbrain. The brainstem runs midbrain, pons, medulla from top to bottom, so the fibers reach the cerebral peduncles of the midbrain before the pons.","The medulla is the lowest region of the brainstem, continuous with the spinal cord. The pyramids are the last stop before the cord, so they come after the pons, not before it.","Correct. The fibers gather in the internal capsule, then run down the front of the midbrain, through the pons, and form the pyramids of the medulla."],"fix":"Review the order of the brainstem regions and the named structures the descending motor tracts form in each one."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-diencephalon-brainstem-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem","comp":"w7-reticular-formation","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"After a small bleed in the core of the upper brainstem, a patient cannot be awakened. Her heartbeat and breathing continue normally, and scans show the cerebral cortex is undamaged. Damage to which structure best explains the coma?","options":["The ascending reticular activating system","The cardiac and respiratory centers of the medulla","The substantia nigra of the midbrain","The superior colliculi of the tectum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Staying awake depends on the reticular activating system, the ascending part of the reticular formation that runs through the core of the brainstem. It sends a steady stream of signals up to the cortex that keeps it alert. Here the cortex is intact, so the problem is the signal that wakes it, not the cortex itself. Normal heartbeat and breathing show that the vital centers of the medulla are working, which points to the ascending reticular network as the damaged part.","notes":["Correct. This ascending network in the brainstem core keeps the cortex awake, so damaging it can cause coma even when the cortex itself is intact.","Damage to these vital centers would disturb the heartbeat and breathing, which are normal here. They would be the answer if the patient had stopped breathing or lost control of heart rate.","The substantia nigra works with the basal nuclei to start and smooth movement. Its loss causes the tremor and stiffness of Parkinson disease, not loss of consciousness.","The superior colliculi are reflex centers that turn the eyes and head toward something seen. Damage there affects visual reflexes, not the ability to stay awake."],"fix":"Review the reticular formation, its ascending and descending parts, and what happens when the ascending part is damaged."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-diencephalon-brainstem-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem","comp":"w7-diencephalon","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A small tumor grows in the floor of the third ventricle, pressing on the region just below the thalamus. Which group of problems would you most expect?","options":["Poor relay of touch and pain signals up to the cortex","Clumsy, poorly coordinated movement and poor balance","Poor control of body temperature, thirst, and hunger","Weakness of facial expression and chewing muscles"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The region just below the thalamus, forming the floor of the third ventricle, is the hypothalamus. It is the master control center for homeostasis and regulates body temperature, hunger, and thirst, along with sleep-wake rhythms and the pituitary gland. Pressure on it therefore throws those set points off. The thalamus above it relays sensation, so sensory relay problems would point higher, to the thalamus itself.","notes":["Relaying sensation to the cortex is the job of the thalamus, which lies above this tumor. This would be the answer if the tumor sat in the side walls of the third ventricle, where the thalami are.","Coordination and balance are jobs of the cerebellum, which sits behind the brainstem, far from the third ventricle. This pattern would point to the cerebellum or its peduncles.","Correct. The region below the thalamus is the hypothalamus, the control center that regulates body temperature, thirst, and hunger.","Facial expression and chewing are served by cranial nerves VII and V, whose nuclei are in the pons. A tumor in the diencephalon would not reach those nuclei."],"fix":"Review the three parts of the diencephalon, where each one sits around the third ventricle, and what each one controls."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-diencephalon-brainstem-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem","comp":"w7-midbrain","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A student holds a brainstem upright and lists these structures by height. Which list ranks them from most superior to most inferior?","options":["Inferior colliculi, superior colliculi, pontine nuclei, pyramids","Superior colliculi, pontine nuclei, inferior colliculi, pyramids","Superior colliculi, inferior colliculi, pyramids, pontine nuclei","Superior colliculi, inferior colliculi, pontine nuclei, pyramids"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The brainstem runs midbrain, pons, medulla from top to bottom, so first place each structure in its region. The superior and inferior colliculi form the tectum, the roof of the midbrain, with the superior pair above the inferior pair. The pontine nuclei sit in the pons, and the pyramids are ridges on the front of the medulla. Combining the region order with the order inside the midbrain gives superior colliculi, inferior colliculi, pontine nuclei, and then pyramids.","notes":["The names give the order. The superior colliculi are the upper pair and the inferior colliculi the lower pair, so the superior pair comes first.","Both pairs of colliculi belong to the tectum, the roof of the midbrain, so both sit above the pons. Splitting them around the pontine nuclei puts part of the midbrain below the pons.","The pyramids are ridges on the front of the medulla, the lowest brainstem region. The pontine nuclei are in the pons, above the medulla, so they come before the pyramids.","Correct. Both colliculi are on the roof of the midbrain with the superior pair on top, the pontine nuclei are in the pons, and the pyramids are on the medulla."],"fix":"Review the structures that belong to each brainstem region, especially the two pairs of colliculi on the midbrain roof."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-diencephalon-brainstem-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-diencephalon-brainstem","comp":"w7-pons","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A disease damages the middle cerebellar peduncles on both sides, cutting the connection between the pontine nuclei and the cerebellum. Which result is most likely?","options":["Paralysis of the arm and leg on one side of the body","Clumsy, poorly coordinated movement with normal strength","Loss of consciousness from reduced arousal of the cortex","Loss of the reflex that turns the head toward a sound"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pontine nuclei receive information from the cerebral cortex about planned movements and pass it to the cerebellum through the middle cerebellar peduncles. The cerebellum compares that plan with what the body is actually doing and smooths out the difference. If the peduncles are cut, the cerebellum no longer knows what the cortex intends, so movements become clumsy and poorly coordinated. The commands themselves still reach the muscles through the descending tracts, so strength is preserved.","notes":["Paralysis comes from cutting the descending motor tracts, such as at the internal capsule or the pyramids. The pontine relay to the cerebellum carries a copy of the plan for coordination, not the commands themselves.","Correct. The cerebellum loses the cortex's plan for each movement, so it can no longer smooth movements, but the motor commands still reach the muscles.","Arousal of the cortex depends on the reticular activating system in the core of the brainstem. Damage to the cerebellar peduncles does not cut that ascending network.","That auditory reflex belongs to the inferior colliculi on the roof of the midbrain. It would be the answer if the damage were on the tectum instead."],"fix":"Review what the pontine nuclei relay, which cerebellar peduncle carries it, and what the cerebellum does with that information."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-meninges-csf-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-meninges-csf","comp":"w7-ventricles","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A child's scan shows that both lateral ventricles are swollen with CSF, while the third and fourth ventricles are normal in size. Where is the flow of CSF most likely blocked?","options":["At the cerebral aqueduct","At the apertures of the fourth ventricle","At the arachnoid granulations","At the interventricular foramina"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"CSF backs up behind a blockage, so every chamber upstream enlarges and every chamber downstream stays normal. CSF made in the lateral ventricles leaves them through the interventricular foramina to reach the third ventricle. If only the lateral ventricles are swollen, the block must sit at that very first exit, the interventricular foramina. A block anywhere farther along the route would also swell the third ventricle.","notes":["A block at the aqueduct backs CSF up into the third ventricle as well as the lateral ventricles. This would be the answer if the third ventricle were also enlarged and the fourth were normal.","A block at the apertures traps CSF in all four ventricles, since every chamber lies upstream of them. The normal third and fourth ventricles rule this out.","The granulations are the last step, where CSF returns to the blood. A block there backs fluid up through the whole route, so all four ventricles would enlarge, not just the lateral ones.","Correct. These openings are the only exits from the lateral ventricles, so a block here swells the lateral ventricles and nothing downstream."],"fix":"Review the order of the ventricles and the named passage that connects each one to the next."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-meninges-csf-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-meninges-csf","comp":"w7-cord-termination-lp","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"During a lumbar puncture between the L3 and L4 spines, the needle passes several layers before CSF appears. Which list ranks these four layers from most superficial to deepest?","options":["Interspinous ligament, ligamentum flavum, epidural space, dura mater","Ligamentum flavum, interspinous ligament, epidural space, dura mater","Interspinous ligament, epidural space, ligamentum flavum, dura mater","Interspinous ligament, ligamentum flavum, dura mater, epidural space"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A lumbar puncture needle travels from the back surface inward. It crosses the ligaments of the spinous processes first, the supraspinous ligament over their tips and then the interspinous ligament between them, and then the ligamentum flavum that joins the laminae and forms the back wall of the vertebral canal. Inside the canal it enters the epidural space, which is filled with fat and veins and lies outside the dura, and then it pierces the dura mater. After passing the arachnoid, it reaches CSF in the lumbar cistern.","notes":["Correct. The needle crosses the ligament between the spinous processes, then the ligamentum flavum that forms the back wall of the canal, then the epidural space, and then the dura.","The interspinous ligament runs between the spinous processes, which stick out toward the skin, so the needle meets it first. The ligamentum flavum joins the laminae deeper in, as the last ligament before the canal.","The epidural space lies inside the vertebral canal, and the ligamentum flavum forms the back wall of that canal. The needle has to pass the ligamentum flavum before it can enter the epidural space.","Epi means upon, so the epidural space lies outside the dura, between the dura and the bone. The needle reaches the epidural space before it pierces the dura."],"fix":"Review the layers a lumbar puncture needle crosses, in order, from the skin to the lumbar cistern."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-meninges-csf-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-meninges-csf","comp":"w7-cranial-meninges","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A patient has a sudden, severe headache, and CSF drawn from the lumbar cistern contains blood. Which bleed best explains the blood in this sample from the lower back?","options":["An epidural hematoma","A subarachnoid hemorrhage","A subdural hematoma","A bleed beneath the scalp, outside the skull"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Blood shows up in the CSF only if it enters the space that holds CSF, the subarachnoid space between the arachnoid and the pia. That space is one continuous sleeve around the brain and spinal cord, and the lumbar cistern is simply its lowest part, so blood from a bleed near the brain is carried down to the lower back. Epidural and subdural bleeds collect outside the arachnoid, which keeps that blood out of the CSF. Naming a bleed by the layers around it tells you whether a lumbar puncture will find it.","notes":["An epidural hematoma collects between the dura and the skull, outside all three meninges. The dura and arachnoid keep that blood away from the CSF, so a lumbar puncture would not find it.","Correct. This bleed fills the CSF-filled space itself, and that space is continuous from the brain down to the lumbar cistern.","A subdural hematoma fills the potential space between the dura and the arachnoid. The arachnoid still separates it from the CSF beneath, so the blood stays out of the fluid.","The scalp lies outside the bone, with the skull and all three meninges between it and the CSF. A scalp bleed can look dramatic, but it cannot reach the fluid around the brain."],"fix":"Review the three meninges, the space between each pair of layers, and which space holds CSF."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-meninges-csf-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-meninges-csf","comp":"w7-csf-circulation","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"After a bout of meningitis, scar tissue clogs the arachnoid granulations so they can no longer return CSF to the blood. The choroid plexus keeps making CSF at its normal rate. Which change would you predict on a brain scan?","options":["Only the lateral ventricles enlarge, because CSF is made there first","The lateral and third ventricles enlarge, but the fourth stays normal","All four ventricles enlarge, because the block is at the end of the route","The ventricles stay normal, because CSF can drain down the central canal"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"CSF flows one way: from the choroid plexus through the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles, out the apertures into the subarachnoid space, and back into the blood at the arachnoid granulations. Meningitis inflames the meninges, and the granulations are projections of the arachnoid, so scarring there blocks the very last step. Because production continues, fluid builds up behind the block, and every chamber upstream of it, which means all four ventricles, fills and enlarges. This buildup is hydrocephalus, and because the adult skull cannot expand, pressure on the brain rises.","notes":["CSF is made in all four ventricles, and fluid from the lateral ventricles still flows on through the rest of the system. Only the lateral ventricles would swell if the block were at the interventricular foramina.","This is the pattern of a block at the cerebral aqueduct, where the fourth ventricle lies downstream. A block at the granulations is downstream of the fourth ventricle too, so it fills as well.","Correct. The granulations are the last step of the loop, so every ventricle lies upstream of the block and fills with trapped fluid.","The central canal is a dead-end channel continuous with the fourth ventricle, not an exit from the system. CSF returns to the blood only at the arachnoid granulations, so blocking them traps the fluid."],"fix":"Review the full route of CSF from the choroid plexus to the arachnoid granulations, and how a block at each point changes which ventricles enlarge."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-meninges-csf-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-meninges-csf","comp":"w7-circle-of-willis","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A clot suddenly blocks the left middle cerebral artery just after it branches from the internal carotid. Which deficit is most likely?","options":["Weakness and numbness on the right side of the body","Weakness and numbness on the left side of the body","Loss of vision with normal strength and feeling","Clumsy movement and poor balance with normal strength"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The middle cerebral artery is the largest branch of the internal carotid, and it supplies the lateral surface of the cerebrum, including most of the motor and sensory cortex for the body. Because motor and sensory pathways cross to the opposite side, the left motor and sensory cortex serve the right side of the body. With its blood supply cut off, that cortex is damaged and the right side of the body becomes weak and numb. Vision and coordination depend on the posterior cerebral and cerebellar arteries, which are fed by the posterior circulation.","notes":["Correct. The left middle cerebral artery feeds the left motor and sensory cortex, which serve the right side of the body.","The left cortex is damaged, but motor and sensory pathways cross to the opposite side. The left side of the body would be affected only if the right middle cerebral artery were blocked.","The occipital lobe and its visual cortex are fed by the posterior cerebral artery from the posterior circulation. Vision loss with normal strength would point to that artery instead.","Coordination and balance belong to the cerebellum, which is fed by cerebellar branches of the vertebral and basilar arteries. A block in those posterior vessels would cause this pattern."],"fix":"Review the territory of each cerebral artery and why damage on one side of the cortex affects the opposite side of the body."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-meninges-csf-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-meninges-csf","comp":"w7-bbb","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A molecule in the blood of a brain capillary must cross the blood-brain barrier to reach a neuron. In what order does it meet these structures?","options":["Astrocyte end-foot, capillary endothelial cell, basement membrane, neuron","Capillary endothelial cell, astrocyte end-foot, basement membrane, neuron","Capillary endothelial cell, basement membrane, astrocyte end-foot, neuron","Basement membrane, capillary endothelial cell, astrocyte end-foot, neuron"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The blood inside a capillary first touches the endothelial cells that line it, and in the brain these cells are sealed by tight junctions and lack pores, so substances must pass through the cells themselves. Wrapped around the endothelium is its basement membrane. Outside that, the end-feet of astrocytes envelop the capillary and help control what gets through. Only after crossing all three layers does a substance reach the neurons.","notes":["The astrocyte end-feet wrap around the outside of the capillary, so they are the last barrier layer, not the first. The blood inside the vessel touches the endothelial lining first.","The basement membrane sits directly under the endothelial cells as their supporting layer. The astrocyte end-feet lie outside it, so the molecule meets the basement membrane before the end-feet.","Correct. The blood touches the sealed endothelium first, then the basement membrane around it, then the astrocyte end-feet wrapped outside, before reaching neurons.","The basement membrane supports the endothelium from the outside, not from the blood side. Blood in the capillary is in direct contact with the endothelial cells, so they come first."],"fix":"Review the three components of the blood-brain barrier and the order in which they wrap a brain capillary."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-cord-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-cord","comp":"w8-cord-roots","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A patient's patellar reflex is missing on one side. When she kicks on command, the thigh muscle on that side has full strength, but the skin over the front of the knee is numb. Damage to which structure best explains all three findings?","options":["The anterior roots that serve the knee region","The posterior roots that serve the knee region","The anterior horn cells of the lumbar enlargement","The descending motor tracts above the lumbar cord"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Sort the findings by direction. The missing reflex means the arc is broken somewhere, and the numb skin means sensory signals from that area are not reaching the cord. Full strength on command means the motor neurons, anterior roots, and muscle all work, because the brain's command still gets out to the muscle. The only break that blocks sensation and the reflex while sparing voluntary movement is in the posterior, sensory roots, which carry signals from both the skin and the muscle's stretch receptors into the cord.","notes":["The anterior roots are motor, so damage there would weaken the kick on command while skin sensation stayed normal. This would be the answer if the muscle were weak and the skin could still feel.","Correct. The posterior roots carry sensory axons from both the skin and the stretch receptors in the thigh muscle, so cutting them blocks feeling and the reflex while motor output stays intact.","These are the motor neuron cell bodies for the leg muscles. Losing them would weaken the thigh muscle, and sensation would be spared, which is the opposite of this patient.","Cutting these tracts blocks commands from the brain, so the kick on command would be weak. The reflex arc itself lies in the lumbar cord below the injury, so tapping the tendon would still produce a kick."],"fix":"Review which root carries sensory axons and which carries motor axons, and how each link of the patellar reflex arc depends on them."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-cord-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-cord","comp":"w8-reflex-arc","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A finger touches a hot pan and the arm pulls away in a withdrawal reflex. In what order does the signal pass these structures?","options":["Anterior root, anterior horn, posterior horn, posterior root ganglion","Posterior root ganglion, anterior horn, posterior horn, anterior root","Posterior horn, posterior root ganglion, anterior horn, anterior root","Posterior root ganglion, posterior horn, anterior horn, anterior root"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sensory neuron carries the signal from the skin along the spinal nerve and into the posterior root, passing its cell body in the posterior root ganglion just outside the cord. Its axon enters the posterior horn, where it synapses with interneurons that form the integration center. The interneurons pass the signal across to motor neurons in the anterior horn, and their axons leave the cord through the anterior root to reach the arm muscles. Posterior is sensory in and anterior is motor out, so the signal crosses the gray matter from back to front.","notes":["This runs the arc backward. Sensory signals always come in on the posterior side, and motor commands go out on the anterior side, so the anterior root is the exit, not the entrance.","The incoming sensory axons enter the posterior horn first, where they meet the interneurons of the integration center. Only after that does the signal reach the motor neurons in the anterior horn.","The posterior root ganglion sits on the root outside the cord, so the signal passes it before it enters the cord. The posterior horn is inside the gray matter, after the ganglion.","Correct. The signal comes in past the sensory cell bodies, reaches interneurons in the posterior horn, crosses to motor neurons in the anterior horn, and leaves through the anterior root."],"fix":"Review the five parts of a reflex arc and match each one to a root, ganglion, or horn of the spinal cord."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-cord-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-cord","comp":"w8-gray-white-matter","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Cross-sections are cut from four levels of the same spinal cord. Which list ranks them from the most white matter to the least?","options":["Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral","Sacral, lumbar, thoracic, cervical","Lumbar, cervical, thoracic, sacral","Cervical, lumbar, thoracic, sacral"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"White matter is made of the tracts that carry signals between the brain and the body. At the cervical level the tracts carry signals for the whole body below, but at each lower level fewer regions remain to be served, so fewer axons are still traveling in the tracts. That is why white matter thins steadily from cervical to sacral. The enlargements widen the cord because of extra gray matter for the limbs, not extra white matter, so the lumbar section still has less white matter than the thoracic one.","notes":["Correct. White matter thins steadily from top to bottom, because fewer axons are still traveling in the tracts at each lower level.","This is the reverse order. The bottom of the cord carries the fewest tracts, so the sacral section has the least white matter, not the most.","This mistakes the lumbar enlargement for extra white matter. The enlargement is wide because of extra gray matter for the lower limbs, while its white matter is less than the levels above it.","The lumbar section is wider than the thoracic one, but that width comes from its larger gray horns. In white matter alone, the thoracic level still has more, since it carries tracts for the lumbar and sacral regions too."],"fix":"Review how the amounts of white and gray matter change from cervical to sacral cross-sections, and why."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-cord-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-cord","comp":"w8-cord-internal","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A virus destroys only the neuron cell bodies in the anterior gray horns of the lumbar enlargement. The posterior horns, the roots, and the white matter are untouched. Which problem would you predict?","options":["Numb skin of the legs, with normal lower limb strength","Weak upper limb muscles, with normal skin sensation in the arms","Weak lower limb muscles, with normal skin sensation in the legs","Loss of all sensation and movement below the waist"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The anterior horns hold the somatic motor nuclei, the cell bodies of the motor neurons that make skeletal muscle contract. In the lumbar enlargement these neurons serve the lower limbs. Without them, the leg muscles lose their nerve supply and become weak, and in time they waste away. Sensation enters through the posterior roots and posterior horns, which are spared, so the skin of the legs can still feel normally.","notes":["Numbness with normal strength is the pattern of damage to the posterior roots or posterior horns, the sensory side of the cord. Here the posterior side is untouched.","The upper limbs are served by the cervical enlargement, not the lumbar one. This pattern would fit if the same virus struck the anterior horns from C4 to T1.","Correct. The anterior horns of the lumbar enlargement hold the motor neurons for the leg muscles, while sensation enters through the spared posterior side.","Losing everything below a level comes from cutting the ascending and descending tracts in the white matter, as in a spinal cord injury. The white matter is spared here."],"fix":"Review what each gray horn holds and which limbs each enlargement serves."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-cord-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-cord","comp":"w8-cord-external","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"An adult falls and breaks the body of the L3 vertebra, and bone fragments push into the vertebral canal. Which structure in the canal at that level is most at risk?","options":["The conus medullaris","The lumbar enlargement of the spinal cord","The denticulate ligaments","The cauda equina"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In an adult the spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc, because the vertebral column kept growing after the cord stopped lengthening. Below that point the vertebral canal holds no cord tissue, only the cauda equina, the bundle of nerve roots heading down to their exits, floating in the lumbar cistern. A fracture at L3 therefore threatens these roots rather than the cord itself. This is also why a lumbar puncture at L3 to L4 is safe for the cord.","notes":["The conus medullaris is the tapered end of the cord, near the L1 to L2 disc in an adult. It sits above L3, so it is out of reach of this fracture.","The lumbar enlargement is part of the cord itself, and the whole cord ends near the L1 to L2 disc. No cord tissue lies at L3, so the enlargement is higher than this injury.","The denticulate ligaments are pia mater extensions that anchor the sides of the cord to the dura along its length. They end where the cord ends, so they are not present at L3.","Correct. Below the end of the cord near L1 to L2, the canal holds the nerve roots of the cauda equina, so these are what the fragments would strike."],"fix":"Review where the adult spinal cord ends and what fills the vertebral canal below that level."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-cord-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-cord","comp":"w8-neuronal-pools","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"Patient one has a small stroke in the right internal capsule. Patient two has an injury that cuts the descending motor tracts in the right half of the spinal cord at T10. Which pair of findings would you predict?","options":["Patient one: left arm and leg weak. Patient two: right leg weak.","Patient one: right arm and leg weak. Patient two: right leg weak.","Patient one: left arm and leg weak. Patient two: left leg weak.","Patient one: left arm and leg weak. Patient two: both arms and legs weak."],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The key is where the descending motor fibers cross. Most cross at the decussation of the pyramids in the lower medulla. The internal capsule lies above that crossing, so the fibers in the right internal capsule are headed for the left side of the body, and the left arm and leg become weak. In the spinal cord the fibers have already crossed, so the right half of the cord carries commands for the right side, and an injury at T10 affects only the regions below it, which on that side means the right leg. The arms are served by cervical segments above the injury, so they are spared.","notes":["Correct. The internal capsule lies above the crossing in the medulla, while the cord tracts have already crossed, and a T10 injury affects only the regions below it.","Patient two is right, but patient one is not. The fibers in the right internal capsule have not crossed yet, so they serve the left side of the body.","Patient one is right, but patient two is not. By the time motor fibers run down the cord they have already crossed in the medulla, so the right half of the cord serves the right side.","The arms are served by the cervical cord, well above T10, so an injury at T10 leaves them working. Only one half of the cord is cut, so only one leg is affected."],"fix":"Review decussation, where the motor pathways cross, and how the level of a cord injury decides which regions lose function."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cranial-nerves-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cranial-nerves","comp":"w8-cranial-nerves","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A slow-growing tumor sits in the left internal acoustic meatus and has caused hearing loss and poor balance on the left. If the tumor begins to press on the other cranial nerve that runs through that same opening, which new sign is most likely?","options":["Weakness of the left side of the tongue","Loss of the sense of smell","Drooping of the left side of the face","Weakness shrugging the left shoulder"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The internal acoustic meatus carries two cranial nerves side by side: the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII), which carries hearing and balance, and the facial nerve (CN VII). Hearing loss and poor balance show that CN VIII is already compressed. If the tumor also presses on CN VII, the muscles of facial expression on that side weaken, so the left side of the face droops. The other signs belong to nerves that leave the skull through different openings.","notes":["The tongue is moved by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), which leaves the skull through the hypoglossal canal. A tumor in the internal acoustic meatus does not reach that opening.","Smell is carried by the olfactory nerve (CN I) through the cribriform plate at the front of the skull, far from the ear. This would point to a problem in the roof of the nasal cavity.","Correct. The facial nerve (CN VII) is the other nerve in the internal acoustic meatus, and it controls the muscles of facial expression on its own side.","Shrugging depends on the accessory nerve (CN XI), which leaves through the jugular foramen with IX and X. This sign would point to a mass at the jugular foramen instead."],"fix":"Review which cranial nerves share each skull opening, especially the internal acoustic meatus and the jugular foramen."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cranial-nerves-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cranial-nerves","comp":"w8-cranial-nerves","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Light strikes the retina of the left eye, and the visual signal travels to the place where the person becomes aware of the image. In what order does it pass these structures?","options":["Optic chiasm, optic nerve, lateral geniculate nucleus, occipital cortex","Optic nerve, optic chiasm, lateral geniculate nucleus, occipital cortex","Optic nerve, lateral geniculate nucleus, optic chiasm, occipital cortex","Optic nerve, optic chiasm, occipital cortex, lateral geniculate nucleus"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The optic nerve (CN II) carries the signal from the retina out of the orbit through the optic canal. The two optic nerves meet at the optic chiasm, where fibers from the two eyes partly cross, and the pathway continues as the optic tract. The optic tract ends in the lateral geniculate nucleus, a relay nucleus of the thalamus, because sensory signals stop in the thalamus before reaching the cortex. From there the signal goes to the primary visual cortex in the occipital lobe.","notes":["The optic nerve leaves the eye first, and the chiasm is where the two optic nerves meet inside the skull. The chiasm therefore comes after the nerve, not before it.","Correct. The optic nerve leads to the chiasm, the pathway relays in the thalamus at the lateral geniculate nucleus, and awareness happens in the occipital cortex.","The two optic nerves meet at the chiasm before the pathway reaches the thalamus. The lateral geniculate nucleus is at the end of the optic tract, which begins at the chiasm.","Sensory signals stop in the thalamus before they reach the cortex. The lateral geniculate nucleus is that thalamic relay, so it comes before the occipital cortex."],"fix":"Review the visual pathway from the retina to the occipital lobe and the role of the thalamus as the sensory relay."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cranial-nerves-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cranial-nerves","comp":"w8-cranial-nerves","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"After a small stroke, a patient has a hoarse voice, trouble swallowing, and weakness of one side of the tongue. Eye movements, chewing, hearing, and facial expression are all normal. Which brain region was most likely damaged?","options":["The medulla oblongata","The pons","The midbrain","The diencephalon"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"First name the nerves from the signs. A hoarse voice and trouble swallowing point to the vagus (CN X) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerves, and one-sided tongue weakness points to the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII). The nuclei of IX, X, and XII all sit in the medulla oblongata. The normal findings rule out the other regions: eye movements depend on nuclei in the midbrain and pons (III, IV, and VI), and chewing, facial expression, and hearing depend on nuclei in the pons (V, VII, and VIII).","notes":["Correct. The nuclei of the glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and hypoglossal (XII) nerves all sit in the medulla.","The pons holds the nuclei for V, VI, VII, and VIII, which serve chewing, an eye movement, facial expression, and hearing. All of those are normal, so the pons is not the site.","The midbrain holds the nuclei for III and IV, the nerves that move the eye. Eye movements are normal, and the voice, swallowing, and tongue nerves start much lower.","Only the optic nerve (II) attaches to the diencephalon, and it carries vision. None of the nerves for the voice, swallowing, or tongue have nuclei here."],"fix":"Review the cranial nerve nuclei in each brainstem region and the main job of each nerve."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cranial-nerves-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cranial-nerves","comp":"w8-cranial-nerves","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these cranial nerves by how far down the brainstem and spinal cord their nuclei reach, from the one that stays highest to the one that reaches lowest.","options":["Abducens, oculomotor, glossopharyngeal, accessory","Oculomotor, glossopharyngeal, abducens, accessory","Oculomotor, abducens, accessory, glossopharyngeal","Oculomotor, abducens, glossopharyngeal, accessory"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Cranial nerve nuclei run roughly in number order down the brainstem. The oculomotor nucleus (III) is in the midbrain, the abducens nucleus (VI) is in the pons, and the glossopharyngeal nuclei (IX) are in the medulla. The accessory nucleus (XI) starts in the medulla and runs down into the upper spinal cord, C1 to C5, so it reaches the lowest. Following the numbers is a good guide, and for these four nerves it matches the anatomy.","notes":["The oculomotor nucleus is in the midbrain, which sits above the pons where the abducens nucleus lies. Following the numbers, III comes before VI.","The abducens nucleus is in the pons, and the glossopharyngeal nuclei are in the medulla below it. The pons is above the medulla, so VI comes before IX.","The accessory nucleus starts in the medulla and runs down into the upper spinal cord, C1 to C5, so it reaches lower than any other cranial nerve nucleus here. It belongs at the bottom of the list.","Correct. The oculomotor nucleus is in the midbrain, the abducens nucleus in the pons, the glossopharyngeal nuclei in the medulla, and the accessory nucleus runs down into the upper spinal cord."],"fix":"Review which brainstem region holds the nuclei of each cranial nerve, including the accessory nucleus that reaches into the spinal cord."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cranial-nerves-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cranial-nerves","comp":"w8-nerve-plexuses","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"After a neck wound, one half of a patient's diaphragm no longer contracts. A student suggests the vagus nerve was cut, since it serves the organs of the chest. Which structure is most likely damaged instead?","options":["The vagus nerve as it passes down the neck","The phrenic nerve from the cervical plexus","The brachial plexus in the lower neck","The intercostal nerves of the thoracic wall"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The diaphragm is skeletal muscle, and its only motor supply is the phrenic nerve, which leaves the cervical plexus with fibers from C3, C4, and C5 and runs down through the thorax. Damage to it on one side leaves that half of the diaphragm still. The vagus nerve does travel through the neck and chest, but it carries parasympathetic fibers to the organs, plus voice and swallowing, not motor commands to the diaphragm. The teaching phrase \"C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive\" sums this up.","notes":["The vagus carries parasympathetic fibers to organs such as the heart, lungs, and gut, along with voice and swallowing. It does not drive the skeletal muscle of the diaphragm, so a cut vagus would cause hoarseness or swallowing trouble instead.","Correct. The phrenic nerve, from C3 to C5 by way of the cervical plexus, is the only motor supply to the diaphragm.","The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and upper limb. C5 feeds both plexuses, which causes this mix-up, but the phrenic nerve is a branch of the cervical plexus, so a brachial plexus injury would weaken the arm instead.","The intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of T2 to T12, running between the ribs to the thoracic wall. They serve the muscles between the ribs, not the diaphragm."],"fix":"Review the phrenic nerve, the plexus it comes from, and how its job differs from that of the vagus nerve."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-cranial-nerves-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-cranial-nerves","comp":"w8-nerve-structure","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A deep cut in the cheek severs a branch of the facial nerve. A surgeon stitches the epineurium so that the fascicles on each side line up. What is the most likely result over the following months?","options":["Facial movement returns within a day, once the stitches hold the ends together","Facial movement stays lost, because cut nerve fibers cannot regrow","Facial movement slowly returns, at least in part, as axons regrow","Facial sensation returns, but facial movement stays lost"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A cut peripheral axon can regrow from the part still attached to its cell body, which for the facial nerve lies in the pons. The connective tissue wrappings guide that regrowth: the endoneurium forms a tube around each axon, and the perineurium outlines each fascicle. Lining up the fascicles and stitching the tough epineurium gives the regrowing axons a path back to the facial muscles. Because axons grow slowly, movement returns over weeks to months rather than at once.","notes":["Stitching lines up the wrappings, but it does not reconnect the cut axons. The axons beyond the cut must be replaced by regrowth, which takes weeks to months.","This describes the CNS more than the PNS. A cut peripheral axon can regrow from the part still attached to its cell body, which is why nerve repair is worth doing.","Correct. Peripheral axons can regrow from the end still attached to the cell body, and the lined-up wrappings guide them back toward the facial muscles.","The facial nerve's main job is the muscles of facial expression, and sensation from the face is carried by the trigeminal nerve. Nothing here would let motor axons fail while other axons recover."],"fix":"Review the three connective tissue wrappings of a nerve and how they help a cut peripheral nerve regrow."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-nerves-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-nerves","comp":"w8-nerve-plexuses","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"After a deep knife wound to the side of the neck, a patient has numb skin over the neck and weakness of the diaphragm on that side. His arm and hand move and feel completely normal. Which structure was most likely damaged?","options":["The brachial plexus on that side","The spinal cord at the level of C2","The posterior rami of the cervical nerves","The cervical plexus on that side"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cervical plexus, built from the anterior rami of C1 to C4, serves the skin and muscles of the neck and gives off the phrenic nerve, the only motor supply to the diaphragm. Damage to it on one side explains numb neck skin and a weak diaphragm on that side. The arm is served by a different network, the brachial plexus from C5 to T1, so a normal arm shows the injury stopped short of it. A cord injury high in the neck would cut the tracts to everything below, so the arms would be affected as well.","notes":["The brachial plexus serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb, so damage there would weaken the arm and hand. It would be the answer if the arm had lost movement and sensation.","A cord injury cuts the tracts to every region below it, so the arms would also lose movement and sensation. A plexus injury stays limited to the region that plexus serves.","The posterior rami supply the deep muscles and skin of the back of the neck and trunk. They do not form plexuses and do not reach the diaphragm.","Correct. The cervical plexus serves the skin of the neck and gives off the phrenic nerve to the diaphragm, while the arm is served by a separate plexus."],"fix":"Review the cervical and brachial plexuses, the spinal nerves that form each one, and the region each one serves."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-nerves-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-nerves","comp":"w8-nerve-plexuses","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Which injury is most likely to take away movement and sensation across the entire upper limb on one side?","options":["A stretch injury that tears the brachial plexus","A herniated disc pressing on the C6 root alone","A cut through the posterior ramus of C6","A cut through the phrenic nerve in the thorax"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The main nerves of the shoulder, arm, forearm, and hand all leave the brachial plexus, where the anterior rami of C5 to T1 are gathered and sorted. An injury at the plexus itself catches fibers from every contributing level at once, so it can take out movement and sensation across the whole limb. A single root, such as C6, is only one of five levels feeding the plexus, so the others still reach the arm and the limb is weakened rather than lost. The posterior rami and the phrenic nerve serve other regions entirely.","notes":["Correct. The main nerves of the upper limb leave the brachial plexus, so damage there catches fibers from all of its levels at once.","A single root is only one of the five levels that feed the brachial plexus. The other levels still reach the arm, so the limb is weakened rather than lost.","The posterior ramus supplies the deep muscles and skin of the back. The upper limb is reached by the anterior rami through the brachial plexus, so the arm would be unaffected.","The phrenic nerve comes from the cervical plexus and supplies only the diaphragm. Cutting it would weaken breathing on that side, not the arm."],"fix":"Review why a plexus gives each region overlap from several spinal nerves, and why an injury at the plexus has wider effects than a single root injury."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-nerves-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-nerves","comp":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A stab wound in the middle of the back cuts the posterior rami of several thoracic spinal nerves just after they branch. Which result is most likely?","options":["Numb skin of the chest wall and weak muscles between the ribs","Numb skin and weak deep muscles over that part of the back","Weakness and numbness of both lower limbs","Loss of sympathetic signals to the organs of the abdomen"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Just past the intervertebral foramen, each spinal nerve splits into rami. The posterior ramus turns backward to supply the deep muscles of the back and the skin over the back of the trunk, so cutting it produces numbness and weakness only there. The anterior rami, which run around the chest wall as intercostal nerves and form the plexuses for the limbs, branch off separately and are spared. The rami communicantes that link to the sympathetic trunk come off the anterior ramus, so they are spared too.","notes":["The chest wall is served by the anterior rami of the thoracic nerves, which run between the ribs as the intercostal nerves. This would be the answer if the anterior rami had been cut.","Correct. The posterior rami supply the deep back muscles and the skin of the back, so the loss stays in the back.","The lower limbs are reached by the anterior rami of lumbar and sacral nerves through their plexuses. Cutting thoracic posterior rami does not reach those pathways.","Sympathetic fibers pass between the spinal nerve and the sympathetic trunk through the rami communicantes, which come off the anterior ramus. They are spared when only the posterior rami are cut."],"fix":"Review the branches of a spinal nerve and the territory each branch supplies."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-nerves-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-nerves","comp":"w8-nerve-structure","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Four injuries happen in the same upper limb: a tear through the whole brachial plexus, a clean cut through the median nerve, a cut through just one fascicle inside the median nerve, and a tiny cut that severs a single axon. Which list ranks them from the most axons damaged to the fewest?","options":["Median nerve cut, plexus tear, one fascicle cut, one axon cut","Plexus tear, one fascicle cut, median nerve cut, one axon cut","Plexus tear, median nerve cut, one fascicle cut, one axon cut","Plexus tear, median nerve cut, one axon cut, one fascicle cut"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Peripheral nerves are built in nested levels. Each axon is wrapped in endoneurium, many axons are bundled by perineurium into a fascicle, and many fascicles are held together by epineurium to form a nerve. The median nerve is one of several nerves that leave the brachial plexus, so all of its axons pass through the plexus along with the axons of the other upper limb nerves. Working from the largest structure to the smallest, a plexus tear damages the most axons, then a cut through the whole median nerve, then a cut through one of its fascicles, and finally a single axon.","notes":["The median nerve is only one of the nerves that leave the brachial plexus, so its axons are just part of the axons in the plexus. A tear of the whole plexus damages the median nerve's axons and those of the other upper limb nerves too, so it ranks first.","A fascicle is one bundle inside a nerve, wrapped by perineurium, while the whole nerve holds many fascicles inside its epineurium. Cutting the whole median nerve therefore damages more axons than cutting one of its fascicles, so the nerve cut comes before the fascicle cut.","Correct. The plexus holds the median nerve along with other nerves, the nerve holds many fascicles, and each fascicle holds many axons, so the damage shrinks at each step.","A single axon, wrapped in its own endoneurium, is the smallest unit, and a fascicle is a bundle of many axons. Cutting one fascicle damages many axons at once, so it ranks above a cut that severs only one axon."],"fix":"Review the levels of a peripheral nerve, the wrapping at each level, and how the nerves of the upper limb leave the brachial plexus."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-nerves-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-nerves","comp":"w8-reflex-arc","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A clinician taps the patellar tendon and the leg kicks. In what order does the signal pass through these parts of the reflex?","options":["Muscle spindle, posterior root, anterior horn motor neuron, thigh muscle","Muscle spindle, anterior horn motor neuron, posterior root, thigh muscle","Posterior root, muscle spindle, anterior horn motor neuron, thigh muscle","Thigh muscle, muscle spindle, posterior root, anterior horn motor neuron"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The tap stretches the thigh muscle, and the muscle spindles inside it, the receptors for stretch, detect the change. The sensory neuron carries the signal along the spinal nerve and into the cord through the posterior root. Because the stretch reflex is monosynaptic, the sensory axon synapses directly on a motor neuron in the anterior horn, with no interneuron in between. That motor neuron sends the command back out to the thigh muscle, the effector, which contracts and kicks the leg.","notes":["Correct. The spindle detects the stretch, the signal enters through the posterior root, synapses directly on an anterior horn motor neuron, and the thigh muscle contracts.","The sensory signal must enter the cord through the posterior root before it can reach the motor neuron. The posterior root comes right after the receptor.","Every reflex arc starts with the receptor, because nothing happens until the stimulus is detected. The muscle spindle senses the stretch before any signal travels in the root.","The tap stretches the thigh muscle, but its contraction is the response, so it ends the arc as the effector. The arc begins when the spindle detects the stretch."],"fix":"Review the five components of a reflex arc and the receptor, root, and horn that serve each one in the patellar reflex."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-spinal-nerves-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-spinal-nerves","comp":"w8-sensory-receptors","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A burn destroys the epidermis and the dermal papillae of a fingertip, but the deep dermis and hypodermis are spared. Which change in sensation is most likely?","options":["Deep pressure and vibration are lost, while fine touch is still felt","The sense of joint position in that finger is lost","All feeling in the fingertip is lost, including deep pressure","Fine touch is lost, while deep pressure and vibration are still felt"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Fine, discriminative touch depends on tactile (Meissner) corpuscles, which sit in the dermal papillae close to the surface, and on Merkel discs at the base of the epidermis. A burn through the dermal papillae destroys both, so fine touch is lost. Lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscles, which detect deep pressure and vibration, sit in the deep dermis and hypodermis, below the damage, so those sensations remain. Matching each receptor to its depth predicts what survives a burn.","notes":["This reverses the depths. The lamellar corpuscles for deep pressure and vibration sit in the deep dermis and hypodermis, which are spared, while the fine touch receptors near the surface are gone.","Joint position is reported by proprioceptors in the joint capsules, tendons, and muscles, not in the skin. A skin burn that stops above the hypodermis does not reach them.","This would be true only if the burn reached the deep dermis and hypodermis. Those layers are spared, so their lamellar corpuscles still report deep pressure and vibration.","Correct. The receptors for fine touch sit near the surface and are destroyed, while the lamellar corpuscles for deep pressure and vibration lie below the burn."],"fix":"Review where each skin mechanoreceptor sits and which stimulus each one detects."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-ans-01","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-ans","comp":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A disease destroys only the neurons of the lateral gray horns from T1 to L2. The anterior horns, the brainstem, and the sacral cord are unharmed. Which pattern would you predict?","options":["Rest-and-digest control fails, while fight-or-flight responses remain","Fight-or-flight responses fail, while rest-and-digest control remains","Voluntary movement of the trunk muscles fails, while organ control remains","Both autonomic divisions fail, because both start in the spinal cord"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The lateral gray horns from T1 to L2 hold the cell bodies of the sympathetic preganglionic neurons, the starting point of the whole thoracolumbar outflow. Losing them removes sympathetic commands to the organs, including the fibers that drive the adrenal medulla, so fight-or-flight responses fail. The parasympathetic division starts elsewhere, in the brainstem nuclei of cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X and in the S2 to S4 segments, so rest-and-digest control remains. The somatic motor neurons for skeletal muscle sit in the anterior horns, which were spared.","notes":["This reverses the divisions. Rest and digest is parasympathetic, which starts in the brainstem and S2 to S4, both spared. It would be the answer if those regions had been destroyed instead.","Correct. The lateral horns from T1 to L2 hold the sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies, while parasympathetic neurons start in the brainstem and the sacral cord.","Voluntary movement depends on somatic motor neurons in the anterior horns, which are unharmed. The lateral horns hold autonomic neurons, so organ control is what suffers.","Only the sympathetic division starts in the T1 to L2 cord. Parasympathetic preganglionic cells sit in brainstem nuclei and in S2 to S4, both spared here, so that division keeps working. This would be the answer only if the brainstem and sacral cord were destroyed as well."],"fix":"Review where the preganglionic cell bodies of each autonomic division sit and how that differs from the somatic motor neurons."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-ans-02","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-ans","comp":"w8-ans-pathway","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A sympathetic preganglionic neuron in the T5 segment sends its axon to synapse in a ganglion of the sympathetic trunk. In what order does the axon pass these structures?","options":["Lateral horn, ramus communicans, ventral root, sympathetic trunk ganglion","Ventral root, lateral horn, ramus communicans, sympathetic trunk ganglion","Lateral horn, ventral root, ramus communicans, sympathetic trunk ganglion","Lateral horn, ventral root, sympathetic trunk ganglion, ramus communicans"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sympathetic preganglionic cell body sits in the lateral gray horn of a segment between T1 and L2. Its axon leaves the cord through the ventral root, the motor root, and travels briefly in the spinal nerve. It then turns off through a ramus communicans, one of the small branches that link the spinal nerve to the sympathetic trunk, and enters a trunk ganglion beside the vertebral column, where it synapses with a postganglionic neuron. The postganglionic axon then carries the command on to the effector.","notes":["The ventral root is how the axon leaves the cord, so it comes right after the lateral horn. The ramus communicans is the small link the axon takes after it is already outside the cord.","The preganglionic cell body sits in the lateral horn, so the axon starts there. The ventral root is the exit path that follows.","Correct. The axon starts at its cell body in the lateral horn, exits through the ventral root, crosses a ramus communicans, and ends in a trunk ganglion.","The ramus communicans is the bridge between the spinal nerve and the sympathetic trunk. The axon must cross that bridge to reach the trunk ganglion, so the ganglion comes last."],"fix":"Review the path of a sympathetic preganglionic axon from the lateral horn to the sympathetic trunk."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-ans-03","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-ans","comp":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Each of these autonomic commands begins with a preganglionic cell body somewhere in the CNS. Which list ranks the commands by where those cell bodies sit, from most superior to most inferior?","options":["Pupil constriction, heart slowing, heart speeding, bladder contraction","Heart slowing, pupil constriction, heart speeding, bladder contraction","Pupil constriction, heart speeding, heart slowing, bladder contraction","Pupil constriction, heart slowing, bladder contraction, heart speeding"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Find the starting point of each command first. Pupil constriction is parasympathetic and starts in the oculomotor (CN III) nucleus in the midbrain, and heart slowing is parasympathetic through the vagus nerve (CN X), whose nuclei sit in the medulla. Heart speeding is sympathetic, so it starts in the lateral gray horn of the thoracic cord, within T1 to L2. Bladder contraction is parasympathetic from the sacral cord, S2 to S4, through the pelvic splanchnic nerves, which makes it the lowest.","notes":["Correct. These start in the midbrain (CN III), the medulla (CN X), the thoracic lateral horn, and the sacral cord, in that order from top to bottom.","Pupil constriction starts in the oculomotor nucleus of the midbrain, which sits above the vagus nuclei in the medulla. The pupil command therefore comes first.","Heart slowing travels in the vagus nerve, whose nuclei are in the medulla, while heart speeding is sympathetic from the thoracic cord below it. Students often place the vagus lower because it reaches the chest.","Bladder contraction is parasympathetic from S2 to S4, the lowest point of the CNS that sends autonomic fibers. The sympathetic heart command starts higher, in the thoracic cord."],"fix":"Review where each autonomic division leaves the CNS and which cranial nerves and spinal segments carry parasympathetic fibers."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-ans-04","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-ans","comp":"w8-ns-organization","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A drug blocks the synapses inside autonomic ganglia but has no effect anywhere else. Which of these would still work normally?","options":["Slowing of the heart by the vagus nerve","Constriction of the pupil in bright light","Secretion of tears and saliva driven by the facial nerve","Voluntary movement of the arm"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The somatic and autonomic motor systems are built differently. A somatic motor neuron runs from the anterior horn straight to skeletal muscle with no ganglion along the way, so blocking ganglionic synapses leaves voluntary arm movement untouched. Every autonomic command uses two neurons that synapse in a ganglion, whether it slows the heart through the vagus, constricts the pupil through the ciliary ganglion, or drives tear and saliva secretion. Blocking that synapse cuts each of these commands off before it reaches its effector.","notes":["The vagus fibers to the heart are preganglionic and synapse in terminal ganglia near the heart. Blocking that synapse stops the command, so the vagus could no longer slow the heart.","Pupil constriction is parasympathetic, with the oculomotor fibers synapsing in the ciliary ganglion behind the eye. The drug blocks that synapse, so the pupil could not constrict.","The facial nerve's parasympathetic fibers synapse in terminal ganglia of the head before reaching the glands. Blocking those ganglia cuts off the command to secrete.","Correct. Somatic motor neurons run from the anterior horn straight to skeletal muscle with no ganglion, so the drug never touches this pathway."],"fix":"Review how the somatic and autonomic motor pathways differ in the number of neurons and the presence of a ganglion."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-ans-05","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-ans","comp":"w8-ans-pathway","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A neuron's cell body sits in the ciliary ganglion behind the eye, and its axon ends on the smooth muscle that constricts the pupil. Which description fits this neuron and the neuron that synapses on it?","options":["Parasympathetic postganglionic, fed by a preganglionic axon in CN III","Sympathetic postganglionic, fed by a preganglionic axon from T1","Parasympathetic preganglionic, fed by a neuron in the oculomotor nucleus","Somatic motor neuron, fed by a motor neuron in the anterior horn"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A cell body inside an autonomic ganglion belongs to the postganglionic neuron, because preganglionic cell bodies always sit in the CNS. The ciliary ganglion is one of the four parasympathetic terminal ganglia of the head, and it serves the muscle that constricts the pupil, a rest-and-digest effect. The preganglionic neuron that synapses on it has its cell body in the midbrain and sends its axon out in the oculomotor nerve (CN III). This is the standard two-neuron pathway: preganglionic axon, ganglion, postganglionic axon, effector.","notes":["Correct. A cell body in a ganglion is postganglionic, the ciliary ganglion is a parasympathetic terminal ganglion, and its input arrives in the oculomotor nerve.","Sympathetic fibers dilate the pupil, and their ganglia sit near the spinal cord in the sympathetic trunk. The ciliary ganglion is a parasympathetic terminal ganglion beside the eye.","Preganglionic cell bodies always sit in the CNS, never in a ganglion. A cell body inside the ciliary ganglion therefore belongs to the postganglionic neuron, and the oculomotor neuron is the preganglionic one that feeds it.","Somatic motor neurons drive skeletal muscle with a single neuron and no ganglion. The muscle that constricts the pupil is smooth muscle, an autonomic effector."],"fix":"Review the two-neuron autonomic pathway, where each cell body sits, and the four cranial nerves that carry parasympathetic fibers."},
  {"id":"app-lec-m5-ans-06","mode":"lecture","topic":"m5-ans","comp":"w8-ns-organization","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"During surgery, the vagal trunks are cut where they pass into the abdomen. Days later, the patient's intestines are still moving food along. What best explains this?","options":["The phrenic nerve takes over control of the intestines","The sympathetic division speeds up gut movement to replace the vagus","The enteric plexuses in the gut wall can run the gut on their own","The pelvic splanchnic nerves now supply the whole small intestine"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The enteric nervous system is a network of neurons built into the wall of the digestive tract, organized as the myenteric plexus between the muscle layers and the submucosal plexus closer to the lining. It is independent enough to be called the brain of the gut, so smooth muscle keeps contracting in rhythm and glands keep secreting even when the link to the CNS is cut. The vagus normally adjusts this activity, but the gut does not need it to keep moving. Sympathetic input would slow the gut rather than move food along.","notes":["The phrenic nerve is the motor supply to the diaphragm, a skeletal muscle. It carries no fibers to the smooth muscle of the intestines.","The sympathetic division slows digestion as part of fight or flight. It would hold the gut back rather than move food along.","Correct. The enteric nervous system in the gut wall keeps smooth muscle contracting in rhythm even when its link to the CNS is cut.","The pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic fibers from S2 to S4 to the pelvic organs and lower large intestine. They do not take over the territory of the vagus higher in the abdomen."],"fix":"Review the enteric nervous system, its two plexuses, and how it relates to the two autonomic divisions."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-bodyplan-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-bodyplan","comp":"w1-serous-membranes","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A knife wound enters the right side of the chest between two ribs and just nicks the surface of the right lung. In what order did the blade pass through these structures?","options":["Chest wall, visceral pleura, pleural cavity, parietal pleura","Chest wall, parietal pleura, pleural cavity, visceral pleura","Chest wall, pleural cavity, parietal pleura, visceral pleura","Parietal pleura, chest wall, pleural cavity, visceral pleura"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The pleura is a serous membrane folded into two layers around each lung. The parietal pleura lines the inside of the chest wall, and the visceral pleura is attached tightly to the surface of the lung itself. Between them is the pleural cavity, a thin space holding a film of pleural fluid. A blade coming from outside must cross the chest wall first, then the parietal layer that lines it, then the fluid-filled space, and only then the visceral layer on the lung. The same rule holds for every serous membrane: the parietal layer lines the wall, the visceral layer covers the organ, and the cavity lies between them.","notes":["This order swaps the two layers. The visceral pleura sits on the lung surface, so it is the last layer reached, not the first. You would pick this if you mixed up which layer lines the wall and which one covers the organ.","Correct. The parietal pleura lines the chest wall, the pleural cavity comes next, and the visceral pleura covers the lung, so the blade meets them in that order.","This puts the pleural cavity before the parietal pleura, but the cavity is the space between the two layers, so a layer has to be crossed before the space can be entered. The parietal pleura lines the inside of the chest wall, so it comes right after the wall.","The parietal pleura lines the inner surface of the chest wall, so it cannot be reached before the wall itself. This order would only make sense if the membrane lay on the outside of the ribs, which it does not."],"fix":"Review the parietal and visceral layers of the pleura and where the pleural cavity sits between them."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-bodyplan-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-bodyplan","comp":"w1-abdominopelvic-map","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"After a hard blow to the right upper quadrant in a car crash, imaging shows a pool of blood collected behind the parietal peritoneum, against the posterior abdominal wall, with no blood inside the peritoneal cavity. Which organ is the most likely source of the bleeding?","options":["The right kidney","The gallbladder","The spleen","The left kidney"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Two facts have to line up here: the quadrant and the organ's relationship to the peritoneum. Blood that collects behind the parietal peritoneum, with none in the peritoneal cavity, points to a retroperitoneal organ, one of the SADPUCKER structures. The blow landed over the right upper quadrant, which holds the right kidney along with the liver and gallbladder. The right kidney is the only choice that is both in the right upper quadrant and retroperitoneal. An intraperitoneal organ that bled would spill blood into the peritoneal cavity instead.","notes":["Correct. The right kidney sits in the right upper quadrant and is retroperitoneal, so its bleeding stays behind the parietal peritoneum.","The gallbladder is in the right upper quadrant, so the location fits. It is intraperitoneal, though, so blood from it would collect inside the peritoneal cavity. It would be the answer if the scan showed blood in the peritoneal cavity under the liver.","The spleen is intraperitoneal and lies in the left upper quadrant, so it misses on both counts. It would be the answer for a blow to the left upper quadrant with blood free in the peritoneal cavity.","The left kidney is retroperitoneal, so the type of bleed fits, but it lies in the left upper quadrant. It would be the answer if the same retroperitoneal bleed followed a blow to the left side."],"fix":"Review which organs sit in each of the four quadrants and which ones are retroperitoneal using SADPUCKER."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-bodyplan-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-bodyplan","comp":"w1-body-cavities","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A transverse section is cut through the trunk at the level of the umbilicus. Moving straight back along the midline of the section, rank these from most anterior to most posterior.","options":["Umbilical skin, peritoneal cavity, vertebral cavity, retroperitoneal space","Umbilical skin, retroperitoneal space, peritoneal cavity, vertebral cavity","Umbilical skin, peritoneal cavity, retroperitoneal space, vertebral cavity","Peritoneal cavity, umbilical skin, retroperitoneal space, vertebral cavity"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A transverse section divides the body into superior and inferior parts, so one slice shows everything from front to back. The umbilical region is on the anterior surface. Just deep to the abdominal wall are the parietal peritoneum and the peritoneal cavity with the intraperitoneal organs. Behind the parietal peritoneum, against the posterior abdominal wall, is the retroperitoneal space, where the aorta runs directly in front of the vertebral bodies. The vertebral cavity, part of the dorsal body cavity, is enclosed within the vertebrae, so it is the most posterior of the four.","notes":["The vertebral cavity runs through the vertebrae, and in the midline the retroperitoneal structures such as the aorta lie in front of the vertebral bodies. This order would place the aorta behind the spine, which is not where it is.","This swaps the two abdominal spaces. Retroperitoneal means behind the peritoneum, so that space has to be posterior to the peritoneal cavity. Students pick this when they forget what the prefix retro means.","Correct. Skin is anterior, the peritoneal cavity lies just inside the abdominal wall, the retroperitoneal space sits behind it against the posterior wall, and the vertebral cavity is within the spine.","The umbilical region is the navel on the anterior skin surface, so nothing in this list is more anterior than it. The peritoneal cavity lies deep to the skin and the abdominal wall."],"fix":"Review the dorsal and ventral body cavities and where the retroperitoneal space lies relative to the peritoneal cavity."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-bodyplan-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-bodyplan","comp":"w1-planes-sections","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A radiologist wants a single image that shows the right kidney, the left kidney and a lumbar vertebra together, and that also shows whether a fluid collection sits in front of or behind the parietal peritoneum. Which plane best meets both needs?","options":["A parasagittal plane through the right kidney","A frontal (coronal) plane through the kidneys","A midsagittal plane through the vertebral column","A transverse plane through the kidneys"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Each plane shows only the relationships that lie within it. A transverse plane cuts the body into superior and inferior parts, so one slice at kidney level holds both kidneys, the vertebra between them, and everything from the anterior abdominal wall to the back. That front-to-back view is what shows whether fluid lies in the peritoneal cavity or behind the parietal peritoneum in the retroperitoneal space. A frontal plane also shows both kidneys, which makes it tempting, but it divides the body into anterior and posterior parts, so the front-to-back relationship is lost. A plane can only show relationships that lie within it, so the plane has to run front to back through both kidneys.","notes":["A parasagittal plane runs parallel to the midline but off center, so it can catch one kidney but never both. It would suit a close look at a single kidney from front to back.","A frontal plane through the kidneys does show both kidneys and the vertebral column, so it meets the first need. It divides the body into anterior and posterior parts, though, so it cannot show whether fluid lies in front of or behind the peritoneum. It would be the best choice if the only goal were to compare the two kidneys side by side.","A midsagittal plane runs exactly down the midline, so it shows the vertebral column but neither kidney, since both lie off to the sides. It is the right choice for viewing the full length of the spine.","Correct. A transverse slice at kidney level shows both kidneys and the vertebra and keeps the full front-to-back depth, so the fluid can be placed relative to the peritoneum."],"fix":"Review the transverse, frontal, midsagittal and parasagittal planes and the two parts each one divides the body into."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-tissues-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-tissues","comp":"w1-epithelial-id","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Two stratified squamous epithelium slides are on the bench, one from the skin of the palm and one from the esophagus. Both show many cell layers with flattened cells at the free surface. Which observation best tells you which slide came from the skin?","options":["Cube-shaped cells resting on the basement membrane","Surface cells with no nuclei, packed into a compact pink band","Cilia lining the apical surface of the top layer","Goblet cells scattered among the surface cells"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Both tissues are stratified squamous epithelium, so the layering and the flat surface cells cannot separate them. The difference is keratin. The epidermis is keratinized, which means its surface cells are dead, filled with keratin, and have lost their nuclei, forming a compact pink band. The esophagus is nonkeratinized because its surface stays moist, so its flattened surface cells are still alive and keep their nuclei. Looking for nuclei in the top layers is the quickest way to tell the two apart under the microscope.","notes":["Both slides have cube-shaped cells in their deep layers on the basement membrane, since stratified squamous epithelium is named for its surface cells, not its deep ones. This feature is shared, so it cannot tell you which slide is the skin.","Correct. Dead, keratin-filled surface cells without nuclei mark keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which is the epidermis.","Cilia are found on pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium in the airway, not on stratified squamous epithelium. A slide with cilia would point you to the trachea, not to the skin or the esophagus.","Goblet cells belong to simple columnar and pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, not to either stratified squamous tissue. Seeing them would tell you the slide is something like the intestine or the trachea."],"fix":"Review how keratinized and nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium differ at the free surface and where each is found."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-tissues-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-tissues","comp":"w1-cell-junctions","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"In an autoimmune blistering disease, antibodies attack the junctions that hold the deepest cells of the epidermis down onto the basement membrane. The cells stay attached to each other, but the whole epidermis lifts away from the dermis. Which junction has failed, and what is its major protein?","options":["Tight junctions, built with claudins and occludins","Gap junctions, built with connexins","Desmosomes, built with cadherins","Hemidesmosomes, built with integrins"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The clue is where the split happens. The cells still hold onto each other, so the junctions between neighboring cells are working. What failed is the link between the basal surface of the deepest cells and the basement membrane underneath. That is the job of the hemidesmosome, which anchors cells to the basement membrane using integrins. The name helps: desmosomes connect cell to cell, and hemidesmosomes, with half the name, connect cell to basement membrane.","notes":["Tight junctions seal the space between neighboring cells near the apical surface to stop leaks, as in the stomach and bladder lining. They do not anchor cells to the basement membrane, so losing them would not lift the epidermis off the dermis.","Gap junctions are protein channels that let small ions and molecules pass between neighboring cells for communication. They give no anchoring strength, so they would be the answer only for a question about signaling between cells.","Desmosomes join cells to each other with cadherins, so if they failed, the epidermal cells would separate from one another and the blister would form inside the epidermis. Here the cells stay attached to each other, so the desmosomes are still working.","Correct. Hemidesmosomes anchor the basal surface of epithelial cells to the basement membrane, and their major protein is integrin."],"fix":"Review the five cell junctions, what each one links to, and the major protein in each."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-tissues-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-tissues","comp":"w1-connective-id","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A deep cut in the wall of the trachea passes through the pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, the areolar connective tissue beneath it, and into one of the hyaline cartilage rings. Which tissue will be the slowest to repair, and why?","options":["The hyaline cartilage, because its matrix is calcified like bone","The epithelium, because it has no blood vessels of its own","The hyaline cartilage, because it has no blood vessels","The areolar connective tissue, because its fibers are loosely arranged"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Repair depends on a supply of nutrients and on cells that can divide. Epithelium is avascular, but it sits on vascular connective tissue and is full of cells that divide often, so it regrows quickly with nutrients that diffuse up from the capillaries below. Areolar connective tissue is highly vascular and has fibroblasts that rebuild fibers and ground substance. Hyaline cartilage is one of the main exceptions to the rule that connective tissue is vascular, and its chondrocytes are trapped in lacunae within a firm matrix. With no blood vessels to deliver nutrients and repair cells, the cartilage ring will be the last to heal.","notes":["This names the right tissue for the wrong reason. Hyaline cartilage has a firm, glassy matrix, but it is not calcified like bone, and bone itself heals well because it is vascular. Cartilage heals slowly because it has no blood vessels.","It is true that epithelium has no blood vessels, which makes this tempting. Epithelial cells divide often and draw nutrients from the capillaries in the connective tissue just below, so they regrow quickly. That is why a shallow cut that stays in an epithelium heals fast.","Correct. Hyaline cartilage is avascular, so nutrients and repair cells reach it slowly and it heals the most slowly of the three.","Loosely arranged fibers do not slow healing. Areolar connective tissue is highly vascular and has fibroblasts that rebuild the matrix, so it repairs well. It would be the slowest only if it lacked a blood supply, which it does not."],"fix":"Review which connective tissues are vascular and which are not, and how blood supply affects healing."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-tissues-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-tissues","comp":"w1-epithelial-id","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A nutrient molecule is absorbed from the lumen of the small intestine and moves into the connective tissue below the lining. In what order does it pass these structures?","options":["Microvilli, basal surface, basement membrane, lamina propria","Microvilli, basement membrane, basal surface, lamina propria","Lamina propria, basement membrane, basal surface, microvilli","Basement membrane, microvilli, basal surface, lamina propria"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The small intestine is lined by simple columnar epithelium, and each cell has an apical surface facing the lumen and a basal surface facing the tissue below. Microvilli project from the apical surface into the lumen, so they are the first structure an absorbed molecule meets. The molecule crosses the cell and leaves through the basal surface. Directly beneath the basal surface is the basement membrane, which anchors the epithelium, and beneath that is the lamina propria, a layer of areolar connective tissue that carries the blood vessels. The apical surface always faces the free space, and the connective tissue is always on the basal side.","notes":["Correct. Microvilli sit on the apical surface facing the lumen, and the molecule then leaves through the basal surface, crosses the basement membrane, and enters the lamina propria.","This puts the basement membrane before the basal surface, but the basement membrane is outside the cell, underneath it. The molecule has to leave the cell through its basal surface before it meets the basement membrane.","This is the full path run backward, from the connective tissue up to the lumen. It describes something moving from the connective tissue out toward the lumen, which is the opposite of absorption.","The basement membrane lies under the basal surface of the epithelium, so it cannot be the first structure reached from the lumen. This order would fit only if the epithelium were upside down, with its basement membrane facing the lumen."],"fix":"Review the apical, lateral and basal surfaces of an epithelial cell, the basement membrane, and the lamina propria beneath it."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-skin-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-skin","comp":"w1-epidermal-strata","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A wooden splinter is driven straight into the sole of the foot. After it passes the stratum corneum, in what order does it pass these strata before reaching the dermis?","options":["Stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum, stratum spinosum, stratum basale","Stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum","Stratum lucidum, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum basale","Stratum lucidum, stratum granulosum, stratum spinosum, stratum basale"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sole of the foot is thick skin, so it has all five epidermal strata, including the stratum lucidum that thin skin lacks. Keratinocytes are born in the stratum basale and are pushed toward the surface through the spinosum, granulosum, lucidum and finally the corneum. A splinter travels the opposite way, from the surface inward. Just under the stratum corneum is the thin, clear stratum lucidum, then the granulosum, then the spinosum, and finally the single row of dividing cells in the stratum basale resting on the basement membrane.","notes":["This swaps the lucidum and the granulosum. The stratum lucidum is the clear band directly under the stratum corneum, so it is the first one reached. This mix-up often comes from learning the strata only from deep to superficial.","This is the right list in the wrong direction. It runs from deep to superficial, which is the path a keratinocyte follows as it matures, not the path of a splinter driven in from the surface.","This flips the granulosum and the spinosum. The stratum spinosum sits directly on the stratum basale, and the granulosum lies above the spinosum. Saying the strata from deep to superficial (basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum) helps keep them in place.","Correct. Moving inward from the stratum corneum in thick skin, the splinter meets the lucidum, granulosum, spinosum and then basale."],"fix":"Review the five epidermal strata in order and which one is present only in thick skin."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-skin-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-skin","comp":"w1-dermis-layers","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A friction burn on a fingertip removes the epidermis and the papillary dermis but leaves the reticular dermis intact. Which pair of structures in the burned area has been lost?","options":["Capillary loops and Meissner's corpuscles","Eccrine sweat gland coils and thick collagen bundles","Arrector pili muscles and sebaceous glands","Lamellated (Pacinian) corpuscles and adipocytes"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The papillary dermis is the thin, superficial layer of the dermis, made of areolar connective tissue, and it rises into the dermal papillae under the epidermal ridges. The dermal papillae hold three things: capillary loops, Meissner's corpuscles for light touch, and free nerve endings. If the burn took the papillary layer, those structures went with it. The reticular dermis below, made of dense irregular connective tissue, still holds its sweat gland coils and thick collagen bundles, and the hypodermis below that is untouched.","notes":["Correct. Capillary loops and Meissner's corpuscles sit in the dermal papillae of the papillary dermis, which is the layer the burn removed.","The coiled secretory parts of eccrine sweat glands and the thick collagen bundles both sit in the reticular dermis, which survived. These would be lost in a burn that destroyed the full thickness of the dermis.","Arrector pili muscles and sebaceous glands come with hair follicles, and the fingertip is thick skin with no hair follicles, so neither structure was there to lose. They would be a fair choice only for a burn on hairy thin skin, such as the forearm.","Lamellated corpuscles and adipocytes belong to the hypodermis, which lies below the entire dermis. The burn stopped above the reticular dermis, so the hypodermis was never reached. These would be lost only in a burn that went through the full dermis."],"fix":"Review the papillary and reticular layers of the dermis, the tissue in each, and the three structures found in the dermal papillae."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-skin-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-skin","comp":"w1-skin-layers","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A needle for a subcutaneous injection passes straight through the skin of the posterior arm and stops in the hypodermis. Rank these tissues in the order the needle meets them, from most superficial to deepest.","options":["Areolar connective tissue, keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, dense irregular connective tissue, adipose tissue","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, dense irregular connective tissue, areolar connective tissue, adipose tissue","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, areolar connective tissue, dense irregular connective tissue, adipose tissue","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, adipose tissue, areolar connective tissue, dense irregular connective tissue"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each skin layer is built from a different tissue, so you can rank the tissues by ranking the layers. The epidermis on the surface is keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Beneath it, the papillary dermis is areolar connective tissue, loose and full of small vessels. The reticular dermis below that is dense irregular connective tissue, with thick collagen bundles running in all directions for strength. Deep to the whole skin is the hypodermis, made mostly of adipose tissue, which is where a subcutaneous injection ends up.","notes":["In the skin, connective tissue lies beneath the epidermis, not on the surface, so the epithelium has to be the first tissue the needle meets. Areolar tissue is the second tissue reached, in the papillary dermis.","This swaps the two dermal layers. The loose areolar tissue forms the thin papillary layer right under the epidermis, and the dense irregular tissue forms the thicker reticular layer below it. Remember that the strong reticular layer is the deep one.","Correct. The epidermis, papillary dermis, reticular dermis and hypodermis are made of these four tissues in this order.","Adipose tissue is the main tissue of the hypodermis, which lies beneath the entire dermis, so it cannot come right after the epidermis. It would sit second only if the skin had no dermis at all."],"fix":"Review the epidermis, papillary dermis, reticular dermis and hypodermis and the tissue that makes up each one."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m1-skin-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m1-skin","comp":"w1-skin-accessory","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A skin slide shows five epidermal strata, including a thin clear band just under a very thick stratum corneum. Which pair of structures should you not expect to find anywhere on this slide?","options":["Eccrine sweat glands and their ducts","Hair follicles and arrector pili muscles","Dermal papillae and capillary loops","Meissner's corpuscles and free nerve endings"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Start with the strata. A clear stratum lucidum under a thick stratum corneum means the sample is thick skin, which is found on the palms and soles. Thick skin has no hair follicles. The arrector pili is a small smooth muscle that attaches to a hair follicle, so where there are no follicles there are no arrector pili muscles either. Thick skin is still rich in eccrine sweat glands, and its tall dermal papillae under the epidermal ridges hold capillary loops, Meissner's corpuscles and free nerve endings for fine touch.","notes":["Eccrine sweat glands are abundant in thick skin, which is why palms and soles sweat. Their ducts open directly onto the skin surface, so you would expect to see them on this slide.","Correct. Thick skin has no hair follicles, and the arrector pili muscles attach to follicles, so neither is present.","Thick skin has prominent dermal papillae that push up the epidermal ridges, and capillary loops sit inside them. Both are expected on this slide.","Meissner's corpuscles and free nerve endings sit in the dermal papillae and are plentiful in the fingertips and palms, where touch is fine and precise. They would be missing only if the papillary dermis had been destroyed."],"fix":"Review how thick skin differs from thin skin and which accessory structures depend on hair follicles."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-bonetissue-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-bonetissue","comp":"w2-cartilage-types","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"On a cartilage slide, you can see chondrocytes in lacunae within a firm matrix. Which added observation best tells you the slide is fibrocartilage rather than hyaline or elastic cartilage?","options":["Thick collagen bundles fill the matrix, and there is no perichondrium","A smooth, glassy matrix with no visible fibers is wrapped in perichondrium","Each chondrocyte sits in its own lacuna surrounded by extracellular matrix","Dark, branching fibers run through the matrix under a perichondrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"All three cartilage types share chondrocytes in lacunae surrounded by extracellular matrix, so that feature cannot separate them. The fibers are what differ. Hyaline cartilage has fine collagen fibers that do not show with ordinary staining, so its matrix looks glassy. Elastic cartilage has dark, branching elastic fibers. Fibrocartilage is dominated by thick collagen bundles and has no perichondrium around it, which suits it to resisting compression in places like the intervertebral discs and the pubic symphysis.","notes":["Correct. Thick collagen bundles and the absence of a perichondrium identify fibrocartilage.","A glassy matrix with no visible fibers is the look of hyaline cartilage, whose fine collagen fibers do not stain clearly. This would be the answer for a slide of articular cartilage or a tracheal ring.","Chondrocytes in lacunae surrounded by matrix are found in every type of cartilage, so this observation is true but does not tell them apart. It only tells you the tissue is cartilage.","Dark, branching fibers in the matrix are elastic fibers, the signature of elastic cartilage in the external ear and the epiglottis. This would be the answer for an elastic cartilage slide."],"fix":"Review the three cartilage types, the fibers in each matrix, and which one lacks a perichondrium."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-bonetissue-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-bonetissue","comp":"w2-compact-spongy","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Nutrients carried by a blood vessel from the periosteum must reach an osteocyte buried deep in an osteon of compact bone. In what order do they travel through these spaces?","options":["Perforating (Volkmann) canal, central (Haversian) canal, lacuna, canaliculi","Perforating (Volkmann) canal, canaliculi, central (Haversian) canal, lacuna","Perforating (Volkmann) canal, central (Haversian) canal, canaliculi, lacuna","Central (Haversian) canal, perforating (Volkmann) canal, canaliculi, lacuna"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Blood vessels enter compact bone from the periosteum through perforating canals, which run crosswise and connect the surface to the central canals. Each central canal runs lengthwise through the core of an osteon and carries a vessel. From there, nutrients move outward through the canaliculi, tiny channels that cross the concentric lamellae, until they reach the lacunae where the osteocytes live. Without this chain of channels, osteocytes sealed in hard matrix would have no way to receive nutrients or get rid of wastes.","notes":["The lacuna holding the osteocyte is the destination, so it comes last. The canaliculi lead into the lacuna, so they must come before it.","Canaliculi are tiny channels that link the lacunae to the central canal, not to the perforating canals. Nutrients reach the central canal first and then spread out through the canaliculi.","Correct. Perforating canals bring vessels in from the periosteum to the central canals, and canaliculi carry nutrients from there to the lacunae.","The central canal runs lengthwise inside an osteon, while the perforating canal connects it to the periosteum. A vessel from the periosteum has to enter the perforating canal first. Starting in the central canal skips the step where the vessel first enters the bone from the periosteum."],"fix":"Review the parts of an osteon and how the perforating canals, central canals and canaliculi connect."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-bonetissue-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-bonetissue","comp":"w2-long-bone-gross","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"During surgery on an adult's tibia, the surgeon finds that the periosteum is hard to peel away from the diaphysis. What anchors it there, and what does the bone surface lose where a wide strip is removed?","options":["Interstitial lamellae; the yellow marrow stored in the medullary cavity","Sharpey's fibers; the osteogenic layer that adds bone to the surface","The epiphyseal line; the cartilage that lets the bone grow longer","Articular cartilage; the smooth surface where the bone meets a joint"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The periosteum covers the outer surface of a long bone except where articular cartilage caps the ends. It has an outer fibrous layer and an inner osteogenic layer, and it is locked onto the bone by perforating (Sharpey's) fibers, collagen bundles that run from the periosteum into the bone matrix. The inner osteogenic layer holds the cells that add new bone to the outer surface and help repair it. Stripping a wide piece removes that cell source along with the vessels that enter the bone from the periosteum, so that part of the surface loses much of its ability to build and repair bone.","notes":["Interstitial lamellae are fragments of old osteons inside compact bone, not anchors for the periosteum. The medullary cavity and its yellow marrow lie deep inside the shaft, lined by endosteum, so peeling off the outer covering would not reach them.","Correct. Sharpey's fibers anchor the periosteum, and its inner osteogenic layer supplies the cells that build bone at the surface.","The epiphyseal line is the bony remnant of the growth plate in the metaphysis, not an anchor on the diaphysis. In an adult, growth in length is already finished, so there is no growth plate cartilage left to lose.","Articular cartilage caps the epiphyses at the joints, and the periosteum does not cover those surfaces, so it cannot anchor the periosteum on the shaft. It would be the answer for a question about the smooth joint surface at the ends of the bone."],"fix":"Review the layers of the periosteum, what anchors it to the bone, and where the periosteum, endosteum and articular cartilage are found."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-bonetissue-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-bonetissue","comp":"w2-cartilage-types","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Four injuries happen in the femur of an adult. Which one is expected to heal most slowly?","options":["A gouge through the articular cartilage of the epiphysis","A tear in the periosteum that covers the shaft","A crack through the compact bone of the diaphysis","A fracture through the spongy bone of the epiphysis"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Healing depends on blood supply, and these four tissues differ sharply in that. Compact bone is vascular, with vessels in its central and perforating canals, and spongy bone has marrow and vessels in the spaces between its trabeculae, so both bring repair cells to a break. The periosteum is a vascular connective tissue membrane with an osteogenic layer, so it heals well too. Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage, which has no blood vessels, and its chondrocytes are locked in lacunae. A gouge in it heals slowly and poorly, which is one reason worn joint cartilage is such a lasting problem in osteoarthritis.","notes":["Correct. Articular cartilage is avascular hyaline cartilage, so it has the poorest supply of nutrients and repair cells of the four tissues.","The periosteum is a vascular connective tissue membrane, and its inner osteogenic layer holds bone-building cells. It is one of the main sources of repair after a fracture, so it heals well.","Compact bone looks solid, but it is threaded with central and perforating canals that carry blood vessels. That supply lets a fracture heal through a callus. It would be slowest only if its blood supply had been cut off.","Spongy bone has marrow and blood vessels in the spaces between its trabeculae, so it is well supplied and heals readily. Students sometimes assume its open lattice makes it poor at healing, but those spaces actually bring blood close to every strut."],"fix":"Review which bone and cartilage tissues have a blood supply and how that affects repair."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-skull-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-skull","comp":"w2-skull-foramina","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On the interior of the skull base, rank these openings from most anterior to most posterior.","options":["Olfactory foramina, foramen ovale, optic canal, jugular foramen","Optic canal, olfactory foramina, foramen ovale, jugular foramen","Olfactory foramina, optic canal, foramen ovale, jugular foramen","Olfactory foramina, optic canal, jugular foramen, foramen ovale"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The cranial base steps down into three fossae from front to back, and each opening sits in a predictable one. The cribriform plate of the ethmoid forms part of the floor of the anterior cranial fossa, so its olfactory foramina are the most anterior. The optic canal passes through the sphenoid at the front of the middle cranial fossa, where it lines up with the orbit. The foramen ovale pierces the greater wing of the sphenoid farther back in the middle cranial fossa. The jugular foramen lies between the temporal and occipital bones in the posterior cranial fossa.","notes":["This swaps the optic canal and the foramen ovale. The optic canal is at the front of the sphenoid, leading straight into the orbit, while the foramen ovale is set farther back in the greater wing.","The cribriform plate sits in the floor of the anterior cranial fossa, in front of the optic canals. The optic canal opens toward the orbit, but the olfactory foramina lie even farther forward, under the frontal lobes.","Correct. The olfactory foramina are in the anterior cranial fossa, the optic canal and foramen ovale are in the middle fossa from front to back, and the jugular foramen is in the posterior fossa.","The jugular foramen lies between the temporal and occipital bones in the posterior cranial fossa, so it is the most posterior of the four. The foramen ovale stays in the middle fossa, in front of it."],"fix":"Review the three cranial fossae and which foramina pass through the floor of each."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-skull-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-skull","comp":"w2-skull-cavities","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A probe is passed upward in the midline from the roof of the mouth, along the bony nasal septum, up into the anterior cranial fossa. In what order does it meet these structures?","options":["Hard palate, vomer, crista galli, perpendicular plate of the ethmoid","Hard palate, vomer, perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, crista galli","Hard palate, perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, vomer, crista galli","Vomer, hard palate, perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, crista galli"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The hard palate, made of the maxillae in front and the palatine bones behind, is the roof of the mouth and the floor of the nasal cavity. The bony nasal septum stands on it. The vomer forms the inferior part of the septum, and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid forms the superior part. The perpendicular plate hangs down from the ethmoid, and directly above it, on the cranial side of the cribriform plate, the crista galli projects up into the anterior cranial fossa.","notes":["The crista galli projects upward from the ethmoid into the cranial cavity, above the cribriform plate, while the perpendicular plate hangs down into the nasal cavity. The perpendicular plate is reached first on the way up.","Correct. The vomer sits on the hard palate as the lower septum, the perpendicular plate forms the upper septum, and the crista galli rises above it into the cranial cavity.","This swaps the two parts of the septum. The vomer is the inferior part that rests on the palate, and the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid is the superior part.","The hard palate is the floor of the nasal cavity, so a probe starting in the mouth must cross it before reaching any part of the septum. The vomer stands on top of the palate, not below it."],"fix":"Review the bones of the hard palate and the nasal septum and the parts of the ethmoid bone."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-skull-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-skull","comp":"w2-sutures-fontanelles","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On an adult skull, a student follows the coronal suture down the side of the head to the thin H-shaped junction where four bones meet. In a newborn, which fontanelle occupies this spot, and which bones border it?","options":["The posterolateral fontanelle; the parietal, occipital and temporal bones","The posterior fontanelle; both parietal bones and the occipital bone","The anterior fontanelle; the frontal bone and both parietal bones","The anterolateral fontanelle; the frontal, parietal, temporal and sphenoid bones"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The H-shaped junction on the side of the skull is the pterion, where the frontal, parietal, temporal and sphenoid bones meet. It is the thinnest part of the skull. In a newborn these bones have not met yet, and the gap on the side of the skull at this spot is the anterolateral fontanelle, also called the sphenoidal fontanelle because the greater wing of the sphenoid borders it. The anterior fontanelle sits on top of the head where the coronal and sagittal sutures cross, and the posterolateral fontanelle sits behind the ear. Matching each fontanelle to the adult landmark it becomes helps you place it.","notes":["The posterolateral (mastoid) fontanelle is on the side of the skull too, but it lies behind the ear where the parietal, occipital and temporal bones meet. It would be the answer if the student had followed the lambdoid suture down instead of the coronal suture.","The posterior fontanelle sits at the back of the head where the two parietal bones meet the occipital bone, at the top of the lambdoid suture. It is not on the side of the skull.","The anterior fontanelle lies on top of the head where the frontal bone meets the two parietal bones, at the upper end of the coronal suture. It is the soft spot checked for dehydration, not the spot on the side of the skull.","Correct. The pterion forms where the anterolateral (sphenoidal) fontanelle closes, at the meeting point of the frontal, parietal, temporal and sphenoid bones."],"fix":"Review the four fontanelles, where each one lies, and the bones that meet at the pterion."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-skull-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-skull","comp":"w2-skull-markings","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A student is handed a single loose skull bone. It has an ear canal opening, a rounded bump behind that opening, a slender downward spike near the bump, and a small hole between the spike and the bump. Which bone is it, what passes through the small hole, and which joint surface is found on the same bone?","options":["Occipital bone; the hypoglossal nerve; the condyles that meet the atlas","Temporal bone; the facial nerve; the mandibular fossa of the jaw joint","Sphenoid bone; a branch of the trigeminal nerve; the mandibular fossa of the jaw joint","Temporal bone; the internal jugular vein; the mandibular fossa of the jaw joint"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Three markings identify this bone. The ear canal opening is the external acoustic meatus, the rounded bump behind it is the mastoid process, and the slender downward spike is the styloid process, all markings of the temporal bone. The small hole between the styloid and mastoid processes is the stylomastoid foramen, where the facial nerve leaves the skull after traveling through the temporal bone from the internal acoustic meatus. The same bone carries the mandibular fossa just in front of the ear canal, the socket where the condylar process of the mandible forms the temporomandibular joint. The markings, the opening and the joint all point to the temporal bone.","notes":["The occipital bone has the hypoglossal canal and the occipital condyles that meet the atlas, but it has no mastoid process, styloid process or ear canal. This would be the answer for a bone with the foramen magnum and condyles at its base.","Correct. The external acoustic meatus, mastoid process and styloid process mark the temporal bone, the stylomastoid foramen passes the facial nerve, and the mandibular fossa forms the jaw joint.","The sphenoid has the foramen ovale for a branch of the trigeminal nerve, but it has no mastoid process, styloid process or ear canal, and the mandibular fossa belongs to the temporal bone. Pick the sphenoid for a bone with greater and lesser wings and a sella turcica.","The bone is right, but the internal jugular vein leaves through the jugular foramen, which lies between the temporal and occipital bones at the skull base, not between the styloid and mastoid processes. The stylomastoid foramen carries the facial nerve."],"fix":"Review the markings of the temporal bone, the stylomastoid foramen, and where the mandibular fossa sits."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-spine-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-spine","comp":"w2-regional-vertebrae","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A student picks up a loose vertebra with a body, a vertebral arch and a spinous process. Which single feature, if present, would prove it is a thoracic vertebra?","options":["Costal facets on the body where the head of a rib articulates","A spinous process projecting backward from the vertebral arch","A transverse foramen in each of the transverse processes","A large, kidney-shaped body built for bearing weight"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Almost every vertebra has a body, a vertebral arch and a spinous process, so those parts only tell you the bone is a vertebra. To place it in a region you need a feature that belongs to one region alone. Costal facets are found only on thoracic vertebrae, because the thoracic region is the only part of the column where ribs articulate. A transverse foramen would make it cervical, and a thick, kidney-shaped body with no costal facets would make it lumbar.","notes":["Correct. Costal facets exist only where ribs meet the spine, which is the thoracic region.","A spinous process is part of nearly every vertebra, so it cannot single out a region. Its shape helps (long and angled downward in the thoracic region, bifid in the cervical, blunt in the lumbar), but its presence alone proves nothing.","A transverse foramen is the signature of a cervical vertebra, where it carries the vertebral artery. Finding one would rule out a thoracic vertebra.","A thick, kidney-shaped body belongs to the lumbar vertebrae, the largest in the column. Thoracic bodies are heart-shaped, so this feature points to the lumbar region."],"fix":"Review the identifying features of cervical, thoracic and lumbar vertebrae."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-spine-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-spine","comp":"w2-typical-vertebra","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon works straight forward in the midline from the back of a lumbar vertebra toward its body. In what order does the path meet these parts?","options":["Lamina, spinous process, vertebral foramen, body","Spinous process, vertebral foramen, lamina, body","Spinous process, lamina, vertebral foramen, body","Spinous process, lamina, body, vertebral foramen"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The body is the thick, weight-bearing part at the front of a vertebra. Behind it, the vertebral arch forms a ring: the pedicles connect the arch to the body, and the two laminae complete the arch behind, meeting in the midline where the spinous process projects backward. A path from the back in the midline therefore crosses the spinous process, then the laminae where they join, then enters the vertebral foramen before reaching the body. Stacked together, the vertebral foramina form the vertebral canal, which is why the laminae are the bony roof a surgeon must get through to reach the canal from behind.","notes":["The spinous process sticks out behind the laminae, so it is reached first from the back. This order would only work if the spinous process pointed forward into the canal.","The vertebral foramen is enclosed by the arch, and the laminae form its back wall. You cannot enter the foramen from behind without first crossing the laminae.","Correct. The spinous process projects from where the laminae join, the laminae form the back wall of the vertebral foramen, and the body lies in front of the foramen.","The body is the anterior part of the vertebra, and the vertebral foramen lies between the body and the arch. Coming from the back, the foramen is reached before the body."],"fix":"Review the parts of a typical vertebra and how the body, pedicles, laminae and spinous process surround the vertebral foramen."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-spine-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-spine","comp":"w2-thoracic-cage","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On an articulated skeleton, a student follows one rib from its head at the thoracic vertebrae around to the front, where its costal cartilage joins the costal cartilage of the rib above instead of reaching the sternum. Which rib could this be, and how is it classified?","options":["Rib 5, a true rib","Rib 9, a false rib","Rib 7, a false rib","Rib 12, a floating rib"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Ribs are classified by how they reach the sternum at the front. True ribs, pairs 1 to 7, attach directly to the sternum, each by its own costal cartilage. False ribs, pairs 8 to 12, do not reach the sternum directly. Pairs 8 to 10 join the costal cartilage of the rib above, and pairs 11 and 12, the floating ribs, have no anterior attachment at all. A rib whose cartilage joins the rib above must be one of pairs 8 to 10, so rib 9 fits and is a false rib.","notes":["Rib 5 is a true rib, which attaches directly to the sternum with its own costal cartilage. It would be the answer if the cartilage reached the sternum.","Correct. Ribs 8 to 10 join the costal cartilage of the rib above, which makes them false ribs.","Rib 7 is the last true rib, and it attaches directly to the sternum by its own costal cartilage, so calling it false is wrong. Students pick it because it sits right next to the false ribs.","Rib 12 is a floating rib with no anterior attachment at all, so its cartilage does not join the rib above. It would fit a rib whose front end is free."],"fix":"Review how the twelve rib pairs attach at the front and which pairs are true, false and floating."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-spine-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-spine","comp":"w2-spine-regions","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"An X-ray shows that one of a patient's spinal curves is much deeper than normal. The vertebrae in that curve have thick, kidney-shaped bodies, short blunt spinous processes, and no costal facets or transverse foramina. Which description fits this finding?","options":["Kyphosis of a primary curve that was already present at birth","Kyphosis of a secondary curve that first formed when the infant held up its head","Lordosis of a primary curve that was already present at birth","Lordosis of a secondary curve that first formed when the child stood and walked"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The vertebral features place the curve first. Thick, kidney-shaped bodies, blunt spinous processes, and no costal facets or transverse foramina mean lumbar vertebrae. The lumbar curvature is a secondary curve, one that develops after birth as a child begins to stand and walk. An exaggerated lumbar curve, which makes the lower back sway inward, is called lordosis. Kyphosis is the hunched exaggeration of the thoracic curve, which is a primary curve present from birth.","notes":["Kyphosis is an exaggerated thoracic curve that rounds the upper back, and the thoracic curve is primary. That would fit vertebrae with heart-shaped bodies and costal facets, not the lumbar features described here.","This mixes two ideas. The curve that forms when an infant holds up its head is the cervical curve, whose vertebrae have transverse foramina, and kyphosis is a thoracic problem. Neither matches the lumbar features on the X-ray.","Lordosis is the right name, but the lumbar curve is not present at birth. Only the thoracic and sacral curves are primary, and the lumbar curve forms later as a secondary curve.","Correct. The features identify lumbar vertebrae, the lumbar curve is secondary and forms with standing and walking, and its exaggeration is lordosis."],"fix":"Review the four spinal curvatures, which are primary or secondary, and the abnormal curves that exaggerate them."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-upper-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-upper","comp":"w2-pectoral-girdle","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A cyclist breaks the middle third of the clavicle. On exam, the shoulder on that side sags downward and forward. Which explanation best fits the bones involved?","options":["The upper limb has lost its only bony strut to the axial skeleton","The acromion has broken away from the spine of the scapula","The head of the humerus has slipped out of the glenoid cavity","The sternal end of the clavicle has pulled off the manubrium"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The clavicle is the only bony link between the upper limb and the axial skeleton. Its sternal end meets the manubrium at the sternoclavicular joint, and its acromial end meets the acromion of the scapula at the acromioclavicular joint. Otherwise, the scapula is held to the trunk only by muscles. When the middle of the clavicle breaks, the strut that held the shoulder out and up is lost, and the weight of the limb pulls the scapula and shoulder down and forward, even though the joints at both ends of the clavicle are still intact.","notes":["Correct. The clavicle is the only bony connection from the upper limb to the trunk, so breaking it removes the strut that held the shoulder in place.","The acromion is the lateral end of the spine of the scapula, and the injury described is in the middle of the clavicle. A broken acromion would not explain why the whole shoulder drops after a break in the middle of the clavicle.","A humeral head slipping out of the glenoid cavity is a shoulder dislocation at the glenohumeral joint, not a clavicle injury. It would be the answer if the X-ray showed an empty glenoid cavity with an intact clavicle.","This describes a dislocation at the sternoclavicular joint, at the medial end of the clavicle. The break here is in the middle third, so the sternal end is still attached to the manubrium."],"fix":"Review the markings at each end of the clavicle and how the clavicle links the upper limb to the axial skeleton."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-upper-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-upper","comp":"w2-arm-forearm","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"In anatomical position, a finger traces the lateral side of the upper limb from the shoulder down to the wrist. In what order does it pass these markings?","options":["Greater tubercle, deltoid tuberosity, styloid process of the radius, capitulum","Deltoid tuberosity, greater tubercle, capitulum, styloid process of the radius","Greater tubercle, capitulum, deltoid tuberosity, styloid process of the radius","Greater tubercle, deltoid tuberosity, capitulum, styloid process of the radius"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"All four markings sit on the lateral side, so the task is to place them from proximal to distal. The greater tubercle is the large lateral bump near the head of the humerus. The deltoid tuberosity is a roughened area partway down the lateral humeral shaft where the deltoid attaches. The capitulum is the rounded knob on the lateral side of the distal humerus that meets the head of the radius. The radius is the lateral, thumb-side bone of the forearm, so its styloid process marks the lateral side of the wrist.","notes":["The styloid process of the radius is at the wrist, the most distal point in the list. The capitulum is at the elbow, so it is reached before the forearm.","The deltoid tuberosity is on the shaft of the humerus, below the greater tubercle that sits beside the humeral head. The tubercle has to come first when moving down from the shoulder.","The capitulum is at the distal end of the humerus, at the elbow, so it lies below the deltoid tuberosity on the shaft. This order would put the elbow above the middle of the arm.","Correct. The greater tubercle is proximal, the deltoid tuberosity is on the shaft, the capitulum is at the elbow, and the radial styloid process is at the wrist."],"fix":"Review the markings of the humerus and radius and where each one sits along the limb."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-upper-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-upper","comp":"w2-hand-bones","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A fall on an outstretched hand dislocates the lunate, the middle bone of the proximal row of carpals, so that it moves toward the palm. Soon after, the patient has numbness in part of the hand. Which structure is most likely being compressed, and why can the lunate reach it?","options":["The ulnar nerve, because the lunate is the carpal on the little-finger side","The flexor retinaculum, because it forms the floor beneath the carpal bones","The median nerve, because the carpal tunnel lies on the palm side of the carpals","The median nerve, because the carpal tunnel runs along the back of the wrist"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Three ideas come together here. The lunate sits in the middle of the proximal row of carpals (So Long To Pinky: scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform). The carpal tunnel is the space on the palm side of the wrist, with the carpal bones beneath it and the flexor retinaculum forming its roof. The tunnel carries the long flexor tendons of the fingers and thumb and the median nerve. A lunate pushed toward the palm moves into that tunnel and presses on its contents, and numbness points to the nerve, which is the median nerve.","notes":["The lunate is in the middle of the proximal row, not on the little-finger side, where the triquetrum and pisiform sit. The ulnar nerve also does not run through the carpal tunnel. Students pick this when they mix up the lunate with the medial carpals.","The flexor retinaculum is the roof of the carpal tunnel, not its floor, and it is a band of dense connective tissue rather than a nerve, so pressure on it does not cause numbness. The numbness comes from pressure on the nerve inside the tunnel.","Correct. The carpal tunnel is on the palm side of the carpals and holds the median nerve, so a lunate displaced toward the palm can press on it.","The nerve is right, but the location is wrong. The carpal tunnel is on the palm side of the wrist under the flexor retinaculum, not on the back. A lunate moving toward the back of the wrist would move away from the tunnel."],"fix":"Review the two rows of carpal bones and the walls and contents of the carpal tunnel."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-upper-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-upper","comp":"w2-bone-markings-vocab","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"On the anterior surface of the distal humerus, a student finds a small, smooth, shallow depression just above the capitulum. Which description best fits this marking?","options":["A sulcus that guides a tendon down the shaft of the bone","A fossa that receives the olecranon when the elbow straightens","A tuberosity that anchors a large muscle to the bone","A fossa that receives the head of the radius when the elbow bends"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Bone markings fall into three groups: projections, depressions and openings. A smooth, shallow depression is a fossa, and a fossa near a joint usually receives part of another bone. This one sits on the anterior surface just above the capitulum, the lateral knob that meets the radius, so it is the radial fossa. When the elbow bends fully, the head of the radius moves up into it. The coronoid fossa does the same job for the ulna on the medial side, and the olecranon fossa on the posterior surface receives the olecranon when the elbow straightens.","notes":["A sulcus is a groove, and the groove that guides a tendon on the humerus is the intertubercular sulcus at the proximal end, between the tubercles. A shallow pit at the distal end is not a groove.","This correctly calls it a fossa, but the olecranon fossa is on the posterior surface of the humerus and receives the olecranon of the ulna when the elbow straightens. The marking described is on the anterior surface above the capitulum.","A tuberosity is a large, rough projection, such as the deltoid tuberosity on the humeral shaft. The marking here is a smooth depression, so it cannot be a projection.","Correct. The radial fossa is a shallow depression above the capitulum that receives the head of the radius when the elbow is fully bent."],"fix":"Review the depressions, projections and openings of the distal humerus and what each one receives or anchors."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-lower-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-lower","comp":"w2-pelvic-girdle","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On a model of the right hip bone, a student runs a finger down the posterior border from the iliac crest toward the ischial tuberosity. Rank these markings from most superior to most inferior.","options":["Posterior superior iliac spine, greater sciatic notch, ischial spine, lesser sciatic notch","Posterior superior iliac spine, greater sciatic notch, lesser sciatic notch, ischial spine","Posterior superior iliac spine, lesser sciatic notch, ischial spine, greater sciatic notch","Greater sciatic notch, posterior superior iliac spine, ischial spine, lesser sciatic notch"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The posterior border of the hip bone runs from the ilium down to the ischium. The posterior superior iliac spine marks the back end of the iliac crest, at the top. Below it, past the posterior inferior iliac spine, the border cuts in deeply to form the greater sciatic notch. The ischial spine is the pointed projection that separates the two notches, and below it lies the smaller lesser sciatic notch, just above the ischial tuberosity. The ischial spine is the dividing point, with the greater notch above it and the lesser notch below.","notes":["Correct. The posterior superior iliac spine is at the top, the greater sciatic notch lies below it, the ischial spine separates the notches, and the lesser sciatic notch lies below the spine.","The ischial spine sits between the two sciatic notches, not below both of them. It divides the greater notch above from the lesser notch below.","This swaps the two notches. The greater sciatic notch is the large notch above the ischial spine, and the lesser sciatic notch is the smaller one below it. The larger notch is the upper one.","The posterior superior iliac spine is the posterior end of the iliac crest, the highest of these markings. The greater sciatic notch lies below it on the posterior border of the ilium."],"fix":"Review the markings along the posterior border of the hip bone, from the posterior superior iliac spine to the ischial tuberosity."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-lower-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-lower","comp":"w2-leg-bones","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A student picks up a loose long bone of the leg and must decide whether it is the tibia or the fibula. Which observation best identifies it as the tibia?","options":["A rounded projection that forms the outer bump of the ankle","A malleolus projecting from the distal end of the bone","A slender shaft topped by a small, knob-like head","A broad proximal end with two condyles and an intercondylar eminence"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The tibia and fibula both reach the ankle and both end in a malleolus, so a malleolus alone cannot separate them. The proximal ends are where they differ most. The tibia carries the body's weight, so its proximal end is broad, with medial and lateral condyles that meet the femur and an intercondylar eminence rising between them. The fibula is slender, does not bear weight, and ends proximally in a small head that does not take part in the knee joint. At the ankle, the tibia forms the medial malleolus and the fibula forms the lateral malleolus.","notes":["The outer bump of the ankle is the lateral malleolus, which belongs to the fibula. It would identify the fibula, not the tibia.","Both bones end distally in a malleolus, the medial one on the tibia and the lateral one on the fibula. Seeing a malleolus is true of either bone, so it does not settle the question.","A slender shaft with a small head at the top describes the fibula. This would be the right answer if the question asked how to identify the fibula.","Correct. Only the tibia has the broad condyles and the intercondylar eminence that meet the femur at the knee."],"fix":"Review the markings of the tibia and fibula and which one bears weight."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-lower-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-lower","comp":"w2-thigh-knee","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Four older adults with thinned bone each break a bone in a fall. Which fracture most threatens both the survival of the bone beyond the break and the patient's ability to walk?","options":["A fracture of the lateral malleolus","A fracture of the neck of the femur","A fracture of the head of the fibula","A fracture of the greater trochanter"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Two ideas decide this: whether the bone carries weight and whether the break can cut off blood to the part beyond it. The femur carries body weight from the hip to the knee, so a femoral break stops walking. The neck of the femur is the narrow, angled region just below the head, a common fracture site in bone thinned by osteoporosis, and the blood supply to the head of the femur runs close to it. A neck fracture can cut that supply and threaten the head itself. The fibula does not bear weight, and the greater trochanter is a muscle attachment on the lateral femur, away from the vessels to the head.","notes":["The lateral malleolus is the common ankle fracture site, and it limits walking for a while. It is on the fibula, which does not carry body weight, and the break does not cut off the blood supply to a large piece of bone.","Correct. The femoral neck carries the body's weight and lies next to the blood supply of the femoral head, so a break there can threaten the head itself.","The head of the fibula is at the proximal end of a bone that does not bear weight, and it is not part of the knee joint. A break there is less of a threat to walking than a break in the femur.","The greater trochanter is a large projection for muscle attachment on the lateral femur. A break there is painful, but it lies away from the narrow neck and the vessels to the femoral head, so it does not threaten the head."],"fix":"Review the markings of the proximal femur and why a fracture of the femoral neck is so serious."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-lower-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-lower","comp":"w2-foot-bones","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"During walking, body weight passes from the tibia into the foot and forward along the medial longitudinal arch toward the great toe. In what order does it pass these bones?","options":["Talus, navicular, metatarsal I, medial cuneiform","Navicular, talus, medial cuneiform, metatarsal I","Talus, navicular, medial cuneiform, metatarsal I","Talus, medial cuneiform, navicular, metatarsal I"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The talus is the tarsal that articulates with the tibia and fibula, so it receives the weight from the leg first. On the medial side of the foot, the navicular lies directly in front of the talus. The three cuneiforms sit in front of the navicular, and the medial cuneiform articulates with metatarsal I, which leads to the great toe, the hallux. These bones, together with the calcaneus behind them, form the medial longitudinal arch. The memory device from the structure list, the three C's surround the N and T, helps you place the navicular and talus among the other tarsals.","notes":["The metatarsals are distal to the tarsals, so the medial cuneiform, which is a tarsal, comes before metatarsal I. Every metatarsal begins where the tarsals end.","The talus is the bone that meets the tibia at the ankle, so weight enters the foot through it first. The navicular lies in front of the talus, so it comes second, not first.","Correct. The talus takes the weight from the tibia, then the navicular, the medial cuneiform and metatarsal I carry it toward the great toe.","This swaps the navicular and the medial cuneiform. The navicular sits directly in front of the talus, and the cuneiforms lie in front of the navicular."],"fix":"Review the positions of the seven tarsals and the bones that form the arches of the foot."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-joints-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-joints","comp":"w2-shoulder-knee","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A hard fall onto a bent knee breaks the patella straight across into an upper and a lower piece, and the two pieces pull apart. Which structure pulls the upper piece toward the thigh, and where does it attach?","options":["The quadriceps tendon, attached to the base of the patella","The patellar ligament, attached to the base of the patella","The patellar ligament, attached to the apex of the patella","The quadriceps tendon, attached to the apex of the patella"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The patella is a sesamoid bone that sits within the quadriceps tendon at the front of the knee. Its broad superior border is the base, and its pointed inferior tip is the apex. The quadriceps tendon comes down from the thigh muscles and attaches to the base, and the patellar ligament continues from the apex down to the tibial tuberosity. When the patella breaks across, the quadriceps tendon pulls the upper piece up toward the thigh, while the patellar ligament holds the lower piece down toward the tibia.","notes":["Correct. The quadriceps tendon attaches to the base, the superior border, so it pulls the upper piece toward the thigh.","The patellar ligament runs from the apex of the patella to the tibial tuberosity, so it is not attached to the base. It also pulls downward, not toward the thigh.","The patellar ligament does attach at the apex, but it runs down to the tibial tuberosity, so it holds the lower piece toward the tibia. This would be the answer for the lower piece.","The quadriceps tendon is the right structure, but it attaches to the base of the patella, the broad superior border. The apex is the pointed inferior tip, where the patellar ligament begins."],"fix":"Review the base and apex of the patella and how the quadriceps tendon and patellar ligament attach to it."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-joints-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-joints","comp":"w2-shoulder-knee","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"In a car crash, a passenger's bent knee strikes the dashboard, driving the tibia backward under the femur. Which ligament is most likely torn, and how is it classified by its position?","options":["The anterior cruciate ligament, an intracapsular ligament","The lateral collateral ligament, an extracapsular ligament","The posterior cruciate ligament, an intracapsular ligament","The posterior cruciate ligament, an extracapsular ligament"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two cruciate ligaments cross inside the knee, and each one stops the tibia from sliding in one direction. The anterior cruciate ligament stops the tibia from sliding forward on the femur, and the posterior cruciate ligament stops it from sliding backward. A dashboard blow pushes the tibia backward, so the posterior cruciate ligament takes the load. Both cruciate ligaments lie inside the articular capsule, so they are intracapsular, while the lateral (fibular) collateral ligament runs outside the capsule on the lateral side of the knee.","notes":["The anterior cruciate ligament is also intracapsular, but it stops the tibia from sliding forward. It would be the answer if the tibia had been forced forward on the femur.","The lateral (fibular) collateral ligament is extracapsular, but it runs down the outside of the knee to the head of the fibula and resists forces that open the lateral side of the joint. A backward push on the tibia does not load it.","Correct. The posterior cruciate ligament stops the tibia from sliding backward, and it lies inside the capsule as an intracapsular ligament.","The ligament is right, but the classification is wrong. The cruciate ligaments cross within the joint, inside the articular capsule, so they are intracapsular."],"fix":"Review the cruciate and collateral ligaments of the knee, what each one resists, and whether each is intracapsular or extracapsular."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-joints-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-joints","comp":"w2-shoulder-knee","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"An MRI of a knee shows a tear in a C-shaped pad wedged between the femur and the tibia on the side of the joint closest to the other knee. Which structure is torn, and what best predicts how well it will heal?","options":["The medial meniscus, hyaline cartilage with a rich blood supply","The medial meniscus, fibrocartilage with a poor blood supply","The medial meniscus, fibrocartilage with a rich blood supply","The lateral meniscus, fibrocartilage with a poor blood supply"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Three pieces of information are needed. The side of the knee closest to the other knee is the medial side, because medial means toward the midline. The menisci are the pads between the femoral and tibial condyles, and the medial meniscus is the C-shaped one, while the lateral meniscus is more circular, almost O-shaped. The menisci are made of fibrocartilage, and like other cartilage they have little blood supply. That is why a torn meniscus barely repairs itself.","notes":["The structure is right, but the menisci are fibrocartilage, not hyaline cartilage, and cartilage does not have a rich blood supply. Hyaline cartilage covers the ends of the femur and tibia as articular cartilage.","Correct. The C-shaped pad on the medial side is the medial meniscus, and its fibrocartilage has a poor blood supply, so it heals poorly.","The structure and the tissue are right, but cartilage, including the fibrocartilage of the menisci, has little or no blood supply. A rich blood supply would mean good healing, which is the opposite of what happens after a meniscus tear.","The tissue and the healing are right, but the lateral meniscus is on the side away from the other knee and is more circular, almost O-shaped. It would be the answer for a tear on the outer side of the knee."],"fix":"Review the medial and lateral menisci, their shapes, the cartilage they are made of, and why cartilage heals slowly."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m2-joints-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m2-joints","comp":"w2-joint-classification","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"Rank these joints from the most freely movable to the least movable.","options":["Glenohumeral joint, knee joint, coronal suture, pubic symphysis","Knee joint, glenohumeral joint, pubic symphysis, coronal suture","Glenohumeral joint, pubic symphysis, knee joint, coronal suture","Glenohumeral joint, knee joint, pubic symphysis, coronal suture"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"How much a joint moves depends on its structure. The glenohumeral joint is a synovial ball-and-socket joint with a shallow glenoid cavity and a loose capsule, so it moves around three axes and is the most mobile joint in the body. The knee is also a synovial joint and a diarthrosis, but it is a modified hinge whose main movements are flexion and extension, so it moves less freely than the shoulder. The pubic symphysis is a cartilaginous joint joined by a fibrocartilage disc that allows only slight movement, an amphiarthrosis. The coronal suture is a fibrous joint that does not move in the adult skull, a synarthrosis.","notes":["This swaps the last two. An adult suture is immovable, a synarthrosis, while the fibrocartilage disc of the pubic symphysis allows slight movement, so the suture has to be last.","The knee is a large, strong joint, but as a modified hinge it moves mainly in flexion and extension. The shoulder's ball and shallow socket allow movement around three axes, so the glenohumeral joint is more freely movable.","The pubic symphysis is a cartilaginous joint that allows only slight movement. The knee is a synovial joint with a joint cavity and moves freely, so it ranks above the symphysis.","Correct. A ball and socket moving around three axes is most mobile, a modified hinge moves mainly in one plane, a fibrocartilage symphysis moves slightly, and an adult suture does not move."],"fix":"Review the structure of the glenohumeral and knee joints and how joint structure sets how freely a joint moves."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-heart-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-heart","comp":"cv-coronary","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On a heart model, a clot is painted into the artery that runs down the anterior interventricular sulcus beside the great cardiac vein. If this blockage happened in a patient, which part of the heart wall would lose its blood supply?","options":["The right atrial wall, including the sinoatrial node near the superior vena cava","The back walls of both ventricles, along the posterior interventricular sulcus","The front of the septum, the front wall of the left ventricle, and the apex","The right ventricle along the lower right border of the heart"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The artery in the anterior interventricular sulcus, running beside the great cardiac vein, is the anterior interventricular artery (LAD), a branch of the left coronary artery. It runs down the front of the heart in the groove between the two ventricles toward the apex. Along that path it feeds the front part of the interventricular septum, much of the front wall of the left ventricle, and the apex. Because the left ventricle pumps blood to the whole body, losing this territory removes a large share of the heart's pumping muscle. Naming an artery from its groove and its companion vein is the first step in predicting what a blockage will damage.","notes":["The sinoatrial node sits in the right atrial wall near the opening of the superior vena cava, and in most people it is fed by the right coronary artery. This would be the answer if the clot were in the right coronary artery in the coronary sulcus on the right side.","The back walls of the ventricles are fed by the posterior interventricular artery, which runs with the middle cardiac vein on the back of the heart. Students pick this when they mix up the two interventricular grooves; the great cardiac vein marks the anterior one.","Correct. The artery in the anterior interventricular sulcus is the anterior interventricular artery (LAD), and it feeds the front of the interventricular septum, much of the front wall of the left ventricle, and the apex.","The lower right border of the heart is supplied by the right marginal artery, a branch of the right coronary artery that runs with the small cardiac vein. It would be the answer for a clot along that right edge, not in the groove between the ventricles on the front."],"fix":"Review the coronary arteries, the sulcus each one runs in, the cardiac vein that travels with it, and the part of the heart wall each one feeds."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-heart-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-heart","comp":"cv-chambers","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Blood that has just drained the front wall of the left ventricle enters the great cardiac vein. Trace it onward on a heart model. In what order does it reach these four structures?","options":["Coronary sinus, right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle","Right atrium, coronary sinus, tricuspid valve, right ventricle","Coronary sinus, tricuspid valve, right atrium, right ventricle","Coronary sinus, right atrium, right ventricle, tricuspid valve"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The great cardiac vein runs up the anterior interventricular sulcus and curves around to the back of the heart, where it empties into the coronary sinus in the coronary sulcus. The coronary sinus opens into the right atrium, the same chamber that receives the superior and inferior venae cavae. From the right atrium, blood passes down through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle. So even though this blood drained the wall of the left ventricle, it returns through the right side of the heart, because the cardiac veins carry oxygen-poor blood back just as other systemic veins do.","notes":["Correct. The great cardiac vein empties into the coronary sinus, the sinus opens into the right atrium, and the blood then crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle.","The coronary sinus drains into the right atrium, not out of it. Blood moves from the cardiac vein into the sinus and only then into the atrium, so this order would need blood to flow backward out of the chamber.","The tricuspid valve sits between the right atrium and the right ventricle, so blood has to be in the right atrium before it can cross that valve. This order jumps from the sinus straight to the valve, skipping the chamber the sinus actually opens into.","A valve is crossed on the way from one chamber to the next, so it belongs between them in the list. Blood passes the tricuspid valve while moving from the right atrium into the right ventricle, not after it has already reached the ventricle."],"fix":"Review where the coronary sinus opens, and trace blood from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-heart-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-heart","comp":"cv-valves","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"You are handed a heart cut open to show the inside of one ventricle. The great vessels have been trimmed away, and nothing is labeled. Which observation would best tell you that this is the right ventricle and not the left?","options":["Trabeculae carneae ridge its inner wall","Chordae tendineae tie its valve to papillary muscles","A semilunar valve guards the exit from the chamber","Its atrioventricular valve has three cusps"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Both ventricles share most of their internal features, so a useful clue has to be something only one side has. Trabeculae carneae, papillary muscles with chordae tendineae, and a semilunar valve at the exit are found in both ventricles. The atrioventricular valves differ: the tricuspid valve on the right has three cusps, and the bicuspid (mitral) valve on the left has two. Counting the cusps of the atrioventricular valve therefore points to one side only. A noticeably thinner wall would also suggest the right ventricle, but that clue is not one of the choices here.","notes":["Trabeculae carneae are muscular ridges on the inner wall of both ventricles, so finding them only tells you that you are inside a ventricle. They would be the answer if you were deciding between a ventricle and an atrium, which has pectinate muscles instead.","Chordae tendineae and papillary muscles anchor both atrioventricular valves, the tricuspid and the bicuspid. Because both ventricles have them, they cannot tell the two sides apart.","Each ventricle exits through a semilunar valve: the pulmonary semilunar valve on the right and the aortic semilunar valve on the left. Seeing one confirms an outflow path, but with the great vessels trimmed away it does not tell you which side you are on.","Correct. The tricuspid valve, between the right atrium and the right ventricle, has three cusps, while the bicuspid (mitral) valve on the left has only two."],"fix":"Review the four heart valves, how many cusps each atrioventricular valve has, and which internal features both ventricles share."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-heart-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-heart","comp":"bvn-fetal-remnants","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"During a cadaver dissection, you find a short fibrous cord running from the underside of the aortic arch to the large vessel that leaves the right ventricle. What was this cord before birth, and what did it do?","options":["The ductus venosus, which let blood bypass the fetal liver","The ductus arteriosus, which let blood bypass the fetal lungs","The foramen ovale, which let blood bypass the fetal lungs","The umbilical vein, which carried blood from the placenta to the liver"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The large vessel leaving the right ventricle is the pulmonary trunk, so a cord joining it to the underside of the aortic arch is the ligamentum arteriosum. Before birth this cord was the ductus arteriosus, a short open vessel. Blood pumped into the pulmonary trunk could cross through it into the aorta instead of going to the fetal lungs, which were not yet used for breathing. After birth the ductus arteriosus closes and shrinks into the ligamentum arteriosum. Solving this takes three steps: name the vessels from their positions, name the remnant, and recall which fetal shunt it came from and what that shunt did.","notes":["The ductus venosus did let blood bypass the liver, but it ran through the liver toward the inferior vena cava and became the ligamentum venosum on the liver. It would be the answer if the cord you found lay on the inferior surface of the liver, not under the aortic arch.","Correct. The cord is the ligamentum arteriosum, the remnant of the ductus arteriosus, which carried blood from the pulmonary trunk into the aorta so that most of it skipped the lungs.","The foramen ovale also helped blood bypass the lungs, but it was an opening in the interatrial septum, not a vessel. It closes into the fossa ovalis, a shallow depression inside the right atrium, rather than a cord between two great vessels.","The umbilical vein carried oxygen-rich blood from the placenta toward the liver, and it becomes the ligamentum teres in the free edge of the falciform ligament. That cord runs from the umbilicus to the liver, far from the aortic arch."],"fix":"Review the fetal shunts, what each one connects, and the adult remnant each one leaves."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-vessels-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-vessels","comp":"bvn-vessel-types","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A slide shows an artery and a vein lying side by side next to a nerve. Both contain erythrocytes, and both show three tunics. Which single observation is the best evidence for which vessel is the artery?","options":["Its tunica externa is the thickest layer of its wall","Its round lumen is ringed by a thick tunica media","A thin flap of tunica intima projects into its lumen","Its lumen is wide and holds more erythrocytes"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Arteries and veins have the same three tunics in different proportions, so the proportions decide it. An artery carries blood under high pressure, so its tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic tissue is its thickest layer, and that thick wall keeps the vessel round even on a slide. A vein carries blood at low pressure, so its media is thin, its tunica externa is its thickest layer, and it often looks collapsed with a wide lumen. Valves, folds of the tunica intima, are also a feature of veins. The artery is the vessel with the thick media and the round, smaller lumen.","notes":["A tunica externa that is the thickest layer describes a vein, whose thin media leaves the collagen of the externa to do most of the supporting. This would be the right answer if the question asked which vessel is the vein.","Correct. A thick tunica media that keeps the lumen round is the signature of an artery, which is built to carry blood under high pressure.","A flap of tunica intima in the lumen is a valve, and valves belong to veins, especially in the limbs. Finding one identifies the vein, not the artery.","A wide lumen holding many erythrocytes fits a vein, which has a thin wall and stretches to hold a large volume of blood. An artery has a smaller lumen compared with its thick wall."],"fix":"Review how arteries and veins differ in the thickness of each tunic, the shape of the lumen, and the presence of valves."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-vessels-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-vessels","comp":"bvn-vessel-tunics","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On the large muscular artery slide, a pointer starts in the lumen and moves straight outward through the wall. Rank these four structures in the order the pointer reaches them.","options":["Tunica media, internal elastic lamina, external elastic lamina, vasa vasorum","Internal elastic lamina, tunica media, vasa vasorum, external elastic lamina","Vasa vasorum, internal elastic lamina, tunica media, external elastic lamina","Internal elastic lamina, tunica media, external elastic lamina, vasa vasorum"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Moving out from the lumen, the first layer is the tunica intima, its endothelium resting on a basement membrane. The internal elastic lamina is a wavy sheet of elastic fibers that marks the boundary between the intima and the tunica media. The media, made of rings of smooth muscle, comes next, and the external elastic lamina marks its outer edge. Beyond that lies the collagen of the tunica externa, which carries the vasa vasorum, the small vessels that supply the wall itself. The two elastic laminae work like brackets around the media.","notes":["The internal elastic lamina lies between the tunica intima and the tunica media, so the pointer crosses it before reaching the smooth muscle. Putting the media first would place the internal lamina inside the muscle layer.","The vasa vasorum run in the tunica externa, which lies outside the external elastic lamina. A pointer moving outward reaches the external lamina first and only then the vasa vasorum.","The vasa vasorum are the outermost of these four structures, not the innermost. They supply the wall from the outside, in the tunica externa, so they cannot come before the layers closest to the lumen.","Correct. The internal elastic lamina sits at the inner edge of the tunica media, the external elastic lamina at its outer edge, and the vasa vasorum lie beyond both in the tunica externa."],"fix":"Review the three tunics of a muscular artery from the lumen outward, and where the internal elastic lamina, external elastic lamina, and vasa vasorum sit."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-vessels-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-vessels","comp":"bvn-vessel-tunics","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On a vessel slide, a thin-walled vessel with a wide, partly collapsed lumen has a delicate flap of tissue projecting from its lining into the lumen. Your lab partner says the flap is a torn piece of the tunica media. What is the flap, and what is it made of?","options":["A venous valve, formed from a fold of the tunica intima","A venous valve, formed from smooth muscle of the tunica media","A loose internal elastic lamina peeled off an arterial wall","A vasa vasorum vessel, cut where it enters the lumen"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A thin wall and a wide, partly collapsed lumen identify this vessel as a vein. Many veins, especially in the limbs, have valves that project from the lining into the lumen. These valves are folds of the tunica intima, the endothelium on its basement membrane, not muscle. They let blood move toward the heart and fall closed when it starts to slide back, which matters because venous blood travels at low pressure. The partner's idea fails because the tunica media lies outside the intima and does not reach into the lumen.","notes":["Correct. The thin wall and wide lumen identify a vein, and its valves are thin folds of the tunica intima that keep low-pressure blood moving toward the heart.","The flap is a venous valve, but it is not made of the tunica media. Valves are thin folds of the endothelial lining, the tunica intima, with no layer of smooth muscle, and students pick this when they assume anything that closes a passage must be muscle.","The internal elastic lamina belongs to arteries, where it marks the inner edge of a thick media, and this thin-walled, collapsed vessel is a vein. A wavy elastic band would be the answer if the vessel were round with a thick tunica media.","The vasa vasorum are small vessels in the tunica externa that supply the wall of a large vessel from the outside. They do not open into the lumen as flaps, so they cannot explain a structure projecting from the lining."],"fix":"Review how to recognize a vein on a slide and which tunic forms venous valves."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-vessels-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-vessels","comp":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A slide shows a vessel whose very thick tunica media is packed with many wavy sheets of elastic tissue, and whose tunica externa holds small blood vessels of its own. Which choice best identifies the vessel and explains the small vessels?","options":["The brachial artery; the small vessels supply its wall","The superior vena cava; the small vessels are its valves","The aorta; the small vessels supply its thick wall","An arteriole; the small vessels are the capillaries it feeds"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Many wavy sheets of elastic tissue (elastic lamellae) in a thick tunica media mark an elastic artery, one of the largest arteries close to the heart, such as the aorta or the pulmonary trunk. A muscular artery such as the brachial artery has a media made mostly of smooth muscle, with a prominent internal elastic lamina instead of many elastic sheets. A wall as thick as the aorta's cannot be nourished only by blood in its lumen, so the vasa vasorum, small vessels in the tunica externa, supply its outer layers. Putting together the tissue of the media, the thickness of the wall, and the job of the vasa vasorum points to the aorta.","notes":["The brachial artery is a muscular artery, so its media is mostly smooth muscle with one prominent internal elastic lamina rather than many elastic sheets. It would be the answer if the slide showed a thick ring of smooth muscle bracketed by two elastic laminae.","The superior vena cava is a large vein with a thin media, not a thick media full of elastic sheets. Venous valves are folds of the tunica intima inside the lumen, not small vessels in the tunica externa.","Correct. Many elastic sheets in a thick media mark an elastic artery such as the aorta, and a wall that thick needs the vasa vasorum in its tunica externa for its own blood supply.","An arteriole has only one or two layers of smooth muscle, far too thin a wall to need vessels of its own. The capillaries it feeds lie downstream of it, not inside its wall."],"fix":"Review how elastic arteries, muscular arteries, and arterioles differ on a slide, and what the vasa vasorum supply."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-blood-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-blood","comp":"w4-formed-elements","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On a normal blood smear you count 100 white blood cells. Cell 1 has a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules. Cell 2 has a round nucleus that fills almost the whole cell. Cell 3 has a nucleus hidden under dark blue-purple granules. Cell 4 is large, with a kidney-shaped nucleus and plenty of pale cytoplasm. Rank these cell types from the most numerous to the fewest.","options":["Cell 4, cell 2, cell 1, cell 3","Cell 2, cell 4, cell 3, cell 1","Cell 2, cell 1, cell 4, cell 3","Cell 2, cell 4, cell 1, cell 3"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"First identify each cell by its nucleus and granules. The two-lobed nucleus with red-orange granules is an eosinophil, the round nucleus filling the cell is a lymphocyte, the nucleus hidden under dark granules is a basophil, and the large cell with a kidney-shaped nucleus is a monocyte. From most to least numerous, the leukocytes are neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils (Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas). Neutrophils are not among these four, so the order begins with the lymphocyte and runs through the monocyte and eosinophil to the basophil.","notes":["This puts the monocyte ahead of the lymphocyte. Lymphocytes are the second most numerous white cell, at about 20 to 40 percent, while monocytes are only about 2 to 8 percent, even though they are the largest.","This puts the basophil ahead of the eosinophil. Basophils are the rarest leukocyte, under 1 percent, so the cell with the hidden nucleus belongs last; the eosinophil, at a few percent, comes just before it.","This puts the eosinophil ahead of the monocyte. In the order Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas, monocytes come before eosinophils, so the large cell with the kidney-shaped nucleus should be second here.","Correct. Cell 2 is a lymphocyte, cell 4 a monocyte, cell 1 an eosinophil, and cell 3 a basophil, which matches the order lymphocyte, monocyte, eosinophil, basophil."],"fix":"Review how to identify each leukocyte by its nucleus and granules, and their order from most to least numerous."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-blood-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-blood","comp":"w4-formed-elements","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"Two agranulocytes on a smear are about the same size. Which feature is the most reliable way to decide which one is the lymphocyte and which one is the monocyte?","options":["The nucleus shape and the amount of cytoplasm around it","Whether any granules can be seen in the cytoplasm","Whether the nucleus is split into lobes joined by strands","Whether the granules stain red-orange or dark purple"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Lymphocytes and monocytes are the two agranulocytes, so granules cannot tell them apart, and the stem removes size as a clue. What remains is the nucleus and the cytoplasm around it. A lymphocyte has a large, round, dark nucleus that fills nearly the whole cell, leaving only a thin rim of pale blue cytoplasm. A monocyte has an indented, kidney-shaped or horseshoe-shaped nucleus surrounded by plenty of pale gray-blue cytoplasm. Reading the nucleus first and the cytoplasm second is how you identify any white cell on a smear.","notes":["Correct. A lymphocyte has a round nucleus with only a thin rim of cytoplasm, while a monocyte has a kidney-shaped nucleus with plenty of pale cytoplasm.","Both lymphocytes and monocytes are agranulocytes, so neither shows visible granules. Checking for granules sorts granulocytes from agranulocytes, but it cannot split these two apart.","A nucleus split into lobes joined by thin strands belongs to the granulocytes, most of all the neutrophil. Neither a lymphocyte nor a monocyte has a nucleus like that, so this check gives the same answer for both cells and cannot separate them.","Granule color separates eosinophils, with red-orange granules, from basophils, with dark purple granules. Both of those are granulocytes, so this clue does not apply to two cells without visible granules."],"fix":"Review what the nucleus and cytoplasm of a lymphocyte and a monocyte look like on a smear."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-blood-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-blood","comp":"w4-formed-elements","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A child with a parasitic worm infection has a blood smear taken. Which cell would you expect to find in higher numbers than usual?","options":["A cell with a three to five lobed nucleus and fine pale granules","A cell whose lobed nucleus is hidden by dark blue-purple granules","A cell with a two-lobed nucleus and coarse red-orange granules","A large cell with a kidney-shaped nucleus and pale cytoplasm"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each choice describes a white cell by its look on a smear, so the first step is to name each one. The two-lobed nucleus with coarse red-orange granules is an eosinophil, the multi-lobed nucleus with fine pale granules is a neutrophil, the hidden nucleus under dark granules is a basophil, and the large cell with a kidney-shaped nucleus is a monocyte. Next, match the job to the case: eosinophils attack parasitic worms and help control allergic reactions. A rise in eosinophils, called eosinophilia, is the expected finding in a worm infection.","notes":["This is a neutrophil, the first responder that engulfs bacteria. Its count rises most in an acute bacterial infection, so it would be the answer for a child with a bacterial infection rather than worms.","This is a basophil, which releases histamine and heparin. Its count can rise in allergic reactions, but it is not the cell that targets parasitic worms.","Correct. This is an eosinophil, the white cell that attacks parasitic worms, so its numbers rise in a worm infection.","This is a monocyte, which leaves the blood to become a macrophage. Its count rises in long-lasting infections and inflammation, not mainly in worm infections."],"fix":"Review how each leukocyte looks on a smear and the main job of each one."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-blood-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-blood","comp":"w4-formed-elements","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"On a smear, you find a white cell with a large round nucleus that fills nearly the whole cell, surrounded by a thin rim of pale blue cytoplasm. Which set of descriptions fits this cell?","options":["Agranulocyte, myeloid line, largest of the white cells","Granulocyte, myeloid line, most numerous of the white cells","Agranulocyte, lymphoid line, second most common white cell","Agranulocyte, lymphoid line, least numerous of the white cells"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"A large round nucleus filling nearly the whole cell with a thin rim of cytoplasm identifies a lymphocyte. Lymphocytes have no visible granules, so they are agranulocytes, along with monocytes. They are the only white cells that come from the lymphoid line; every other formed element comes from the myeloid line. They make up about 20 to 40 percent of white cells, which puts them second after neutrophils. Getting this right means linking the look of the cell to its group, its cell line, and its abundance.","notes":["An agranulocyte from the myeloid line that is the largest white cell describes the monocyte, which has a kidney-shaped nucleus and plenty of cytoplasm. That would be the answer if the nucleus were indented and the cytoplasm abundant.","A myeloid granulocyte that is the most numerous white cell describes the neutrophil, with its three to five lobed nucleus and fine pale granules. A round nucleus filling the cell does not fit it.","Correct. This is a lymphocyte: it has no visible granules, it comes from the lymphoid line, and it is second only to neutrophils in number.","The group and the cell line are right for a lymphocyte, but the count is wrong. Lymphocytes make up about 20 to 40 percent of white cells; the least numerous white cell is the basophil."],"fix":"Review each leukocyte's appearance, whether it is a granulocyte or an agranulocyte, which cell line it comes from, and how numerous it is."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-muscle-micro-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-muscle-micro","comp":"w4-sarcomere","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"On the sarcomere model, a pointer starts at a Z disc and moves toward the center of the sarcomere. The regions it crosses are described only by what they contain. In what order does the pointer reach them?","options":["Thick and thin overlapping; thin filaments only; thick filaments only; the line joining the thick filaments","Thin filaments only; thick and thin overlapping; thick filaments only; the line joining the thick filaments","Thin filaments only; thick filaments only; thick and thin overlapping; the line joining the thick filaments","Thin filaments only; thick and thin overlapping; the line joining the thick filaments; thick filaments only"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Thin filaments are anchored at the Z disc and reach toward the center, while thick filaments sit in the middle and are tied together at the M line. Starting at the Z disc, the pointer first crosses the I band, where only thin filaments lie. It then enters the A band at the zone of overlap, where thin filaments slide between thick ones. Past the tips of the thin filaments is the H zone, which holds thick filaments only, and the M line runs through its center. Thinking about where each filament is anchored lets you rebuild the order without memorizing it.","notes":["Thin filaments are anchored at the Z disc, so the region right next to it holds thin filaments only. The overlap with thick filaments starts farther in, at the edge of the A band.","Correct. From the Z disc the pointer crosses the I band (thin only), the zone of overlap, the H zone (thick only), and reaches the M line at the center.","The thin filaments reach in from the Z disc and overlap the ends of the thick filaments before stopping. The region of thick filaments only, the H zone, lies beyond where the thin filaments end, so the overlap must come first.","The M line runs through the middle of the H zone and marks the exact center of the sarcomere. Moving from the Z disc toward the center, the pointer enters the H zone before it reaches the M line, so the line comes last."],"fix":"Review the bands, zones, and lines of the sarcomere and which filaments lie in each one."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-muscle-micro-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-muscle-micro","comp":"w4-sarcomere","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A student compares two micrographs of the same myofibril, one relaxed and one fully contracted. Which part of each sarcomere has the same width in both pictures, and why?","options":["The A band, because its width is set by the thick filaments","The I band, because the thin filaments themselves do not shorten","The H zone, because the thick filaments are anchored at the M line","The whole sarcomere, because titin holds the Z discs in place"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In contraction, the thin filaments slide toward the M line over the thick filaments, and neither filament changes its own length. The A band is defined by the thick filaments, since its width equals their length, so it stays the same. The I band and the H zone are defined by the space between filament tips, so they narrow as the filaments slide farther together. The Z discs move closer, so the whole sarcomere shortens. Linking each region to the filament that sets its width is how you predict which ones change.","notes":["Correct. The A band is as wide as the thick filaments are long, and the filaments themselves do not shorten, so the A band keeps its width.","The thin filaments do keep their length, but the I band still narrows because the thin filaments slide farther over the thick ones and the Z discs move closer. The I band is one of the regions that shrinks in contraction.","The thick filaments are tied together at the M line, but the H zone is the part of the A band not reached by thin filaments. As the thin filaments slide inward, they cover more of it, so the H zone narrows.","Contraction pulls the Z discs closer together, so the whole sarcomere shortens. Titin gives the sarcomere elastic recoil, but it does not stop the Z discs from moving inward."],"fix":"Review which filaments set the width of each region of the sarcomere and which regions narrow during contraction."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-muscle-micro-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-muscle-micro","comp":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A researcher bathes a living muscle fiber in a dye that cannot cross cell membranes, so it can only fill spaces that are open to the outside of the cell. Which structure deep within the fiber fills with the dye?","options":["The terminal cisterns","The sarcoplasmic reticulum","The myofibrils","The transverse (T) tubules"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the fiber, and at regular intervals it dives inward to form the T tubules. Because each T tubule is a fold of the surface membrane, the space inside it opens to the fluid outside the cell, so a dye that cannot cross membranes can flow into it. The sarcoplasmic reticulum and its terminal cisterns are a separate internal membrane system that never opens to the surface. This continuity with the surface is also why T tubules can carry the signal from the sarcolemma deep into the fiber.","notes":["Terminal cisterns are the enlarged end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, an internal membrane system sealed off from the outside. They sit beside the T tubules in each triad, which is why students mix them up, but they do not open to the cell surface.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the fiber's smooth endoplasmic reticulum, a closed network that stores calcium inside the cell. It is not continuous with the sarcolemma, so dye from outside cannot enter it.","Myofibrils lie in the sarcoplasm, inside the sarcolemma. A dye that cannot cross the membrane never reaches the sarcoplasm, so the myofibrils stay unstained.","Correct. T tubules are inward extensions of the sarcolemma, so their interior is continuous with the fluid outside the fiber."],"fix":"Review which structures of the muscle fiber come from the sarcolemma and which belong to the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-muscle-micro-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-muscle-micro","comp":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"On the muscle fiber model, a student needs to find the triads and describe them. Which description is the most complete and accurate?","options":["At each Z disc: a T tubule between two terminal cisterns, one per sarcomere","At each A band and I band junction: a T tubule between two terminal cisterns, two per sarcomere","At each M line: a terminal cistern between two T tubules, one per sarcomere","At each A band and I band junction: a terminal cistern between two T tubules, two per sarcomere"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"A triad is three structures: a T tubule in the middle with a terminal cistern of the sarcoplasmic reticulum on each side. In skeletal muscle, the T tubules dive into the fiber where each dark A band meets a light I band, and the terminal cisterns line up beside them there. A sarcomere has an A band with an I band junction at each end, so it has two triads. A complete description has to get the parts, their arrangement, and their position along the sarcomere all right at once.","notes":["The parts are right, but the place and the count are wrong for skeletal muscle. Triads sit where the A band meets the I band, not at the Z disc, and since each sarcomere has two such junctions, it has two triads.","Correct. A triad is one T tubule flanked by two terminal cisterns, and skeletal muscle has one at each A band and I band junction, which gives two per sarcomere.","The M line is the center of the sarcomere, where the thick filaments are tied together, not the site of the triads. This choice also reverses the triad, which has one T tubule in the middle, not one terminal cistern.","The place and the count are right, but the parts are reversed. The middle tube of a triad is the T tubule, an inward fold of the sarcolemma, and the two side sacs are terminal cisterns of the sarcoplasmic reticulum."],"fix":"Review the parts of a triad and where triads sit along a sarcomere in skeletal muscle."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-back-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-back","comp":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"To drain fluid from the chest, a needle is passed straight in through the lateral chest wall, just over the top of the sixth rib, where slips of the serratus anterior cover the ribs. In what order does the needle pass through these muscles?","options":["Serratus anterior, external intercostals, internal intercostals, innermost intercostals","External intercostals, serratus anterior, internal intercostals, innermost intercostals","Serratus anterior, internal intercostals, external intercostals, innermost intercostals","Serratus anterior, innermost intercostals, internal intercostals, external intercostals"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"On the lateral chest wall, the slips of the serratus anterior lie on the outer surfaces of the upper ribs, so a needle meets this muscle first. Beneath it, each intercostal space holds three sheets of muscle whose names give their order: external, internal, and innermost intercostals. The intercostal vein, artery, and nerve run in the costal groove along the lower border of each rib, between the internal and innermost layers. Passing the needle just over the top of the rib below keeps it as far as possible from that bundle.","notes":["Correct. The serratus anterior lies on the outer surface of the ribs, and the intercostal space beneath it is filled by the external, internal, and innermost intercostals from outside in.","The serratus anterior lies on top of the ribs and the intercostal spaces, not between them. Its slips arise from the outer surfaces of the upper ribs, so the needle meets it before entering any intercostal layer.","The external intercostals are the outermost layer of the intercostal space, as their name says. The internal intercostals lie just deep to them, with fibers at right angles, so the external layer must be pierced first.","This lists the intercostal layers from deep to superficial. A needle entering from outside meets the external intercostals first and the innermost intercostals last, just before it reaches the inside of the chest wall."],"fix":"Review the layers of the intercostal space from superficial to deep and where the neurovascular bundle runs."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-back-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-back","comp":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon approaches the upper thoracic spine from behind, working in the space between the medial border of the scapula and the spinous processes. Rank these muscles from the most superficial, met first, to the deepest, lying against the vertebrae.","options":["Rhomboid major, trapezius, longissimus, multifidus","Trapezius, longissimus, rhomboid major, multifidus","Trapezius, rhomboid major, multifidus, longissimus","Trapezius, rhomboid major, longissimus, multifidus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The back is built in layers, which is why it is dissected from the surface inward. The trapezius covers the upper back and is the first muscle met. Once it is reflected, the rhomboid major appears, running from the spinous processes of T2 to T5 to the medial border of the scapula. Deeper along the spine are the erector spinae columns, including the longissimus, and deeper still the multifidus lies against the laminae and spinous processes. Knowing the layers tells you what each cut will reveal next.","notes":["The rhomboid major is hidden under the trapezius in the upper back; it only comes into view once the trapezius is reflected. Putting the rhomboid first reverses the two most superficial layers.","The longissimus is part of the erector spinae, which lies deep to the rhomboids along the spine. The rhomboid major runs from the spinous processes to the scapula over the top of this deeper layer.","The multifidus is packed into the groove beside the spinous processes, deep to the erector spinae. Placing it above the longissimus reverses the two deepest layers.","Correct. The trapezius is the most superficial back muscle, the rhomboid major lies just under it, the erector spinae columns such as the longissimus lie deeper, and the multifidus lies deeper still against the vertebrae."],"fix":"Review the layers of the back muscles from the trapezius down to the deep muscles beside the vertebrae."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-back-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-back","comp":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"After reflecting the pectoralis major on a cadaver, you find a thin triangular muscle underneath. Your lab partner thinks it is just a deep part of the pectoralis major. Which observation best shows that it is a separate muscle, the pectoralis minor?","options":["It lies deep to the pectoralis major once that is reflected","Its fibers converge toward one narrow attachment","Its fibers end on the coracoid process, not the humerus","It lies on the front of the chest wall over the ribs"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"To prove two muscles are separate, you need a feature one has and the other cannot share, and the insertion is the strongest clue. The pectoralis major inserts on the lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus, so it moves the arm. The pectoralis minor runs from ribs 3 to 5 up to the coracoid process of the scapula, so it moves the scapula, depressing and protracting it. Depth, shape, and position on the chest are all shared or could belong to a deep part of the larger muscle.","notes":["A deep part of the pectoralis major would also lie under its superficial fibers, so depth alone cannot separate them. Depth helps you find the pectoralis minor, but it does not prove what it is.","The pectoralis major is itself a convergent muscle whose fibers gather into one tendon. Converging fibers fit both muscles, so this observation does not settle the question.","Correct. The pectoralis minor inserts on the coracoid process of the scapula, while every part of the pectoralis major ends on the humerus, so a different insertion proves a different muscle.","Both pectoral muscles lie on the front of the chest over the ribs. This location is shared, so it cannot show that the deeper muscle is separate."],"fix":"Review the origins and insertions of the pectoralis major and pectoralis minor and what each one moves."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-back-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-back","comp":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"On the lateral chest wall of the cadaver, a student follows a zigzag line where the lower slips of the serratus anterior interlock with the slips of another muscle. She follows that muscle down onto the abdomen and cuts through it, finding a deeper flat muscle whose fibers run roughly at right angles to the first. Which two muscles has she found, and which flat muscle lies deeper still?","options":["Internal oblique, then external oblique, with transversus abdominis deepest","External oblique, then internal oblique, with transversus abdominis deepest","External oblique, then transversus abdominis, with internal oblique deepest","External intercostals, then internal intercostals, with innermost intercostals deepest"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The external oblique, the most superficial lateral abdominal muscle, arises by fleshy slips from the lower ribs, and its upper slips alternate with the lower slips of the serratus anterior in a zigzag line. Following it down onto the abdomen confirms it is an abdominal wall muscle. Beneath it lies the internal oblique, whose fibers run roughly at right angles to the external layer, and deepest of the three flat muscles is the transversus abdominis, whose fibers run horizontally. The crossing fiber directions, like the plies of plywood, make the wall strong. This question links a surface landmark, the layer order, and the fiber direction of each layer.","notes":["The internal oblique cannot be the muscle on the surface, because it lies deep to the external oblique. Only the outermost flat muscle reaches up to interlock with the serratus anterior on the chest wall.","Correct. The muscle whose slips interlock with the serratus anterior is the external oblique, the internal oblique lies under it with fibers at right angles, and the transversus abdominis is the deepest flat muscle.","The external oblique is identified correctly, but the next two layers are reversed. The internal oblique lies right under the external oblique, and the transversus abdominis, with horizontal fibers, is the deepest.","The intercostals fill the spaces between the ribs and stay on the thoracic wall; they do not continue down over the abdomen. This would be the answer if she had cut into an intercostal space instead of following the muscle onto the abdomen."],"fix":"Review how the external oblique meets the serratus anterior and the order and fiber direction of the three flat abdominal muscles."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-arm-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-arm","comp":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On the back of a cadaver scapula, you find one muscle filling the fossa below the spine and a smaller muscle just beneath it arising from the lateral border. Both tendons pass behind the shoulder joint to the greater tubercle. If both tendons were torn, which movement would be weakest?","options":["Turning the arm inward (medial rotation)","Starting to lift the arm out to the side","Shrugging the scapula up toward the ear","Turning the arm outward (lateral rotation)"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The muscle filling the fossa below the scapular spine is the infraspinatus, and the smaller muscle from the lateral border just below it is the teres minor. Both are rotator cuff muscles that pass behind the shoulder joint to the greater tubercle. Because they wrap around the back of the humeral head, their pull turns the arm outward, which is lateral rotation. Losing both tendons would leave lateral rotation the weakest movement, while the other cuff muscles and the deltoid could still do their own jobs.","notes":["Medial rotation is the job of the subscapularis, the one cuff muscle on the front of the scapula that reaches the lesser tubercle. It would be the answer if the torn tendon crossed in front of the shoulder joint.","Starting abduction is the job of the supraspinatus, which fills the fossa above the spine and passes under the acromion. That would be the answer if the torn muscle lay above the scapular spine, not below it.","Shrugging the scapula upward is elevation, done by the upper fibers of the trapezius and the levator scapulae, which attach to the scapula, not the humerus. Neither cuff muscle in this dissection moves the scapula, so elevation would be unaffected.","Correct. The infraspinatus and teres minor wrap behind the humeral head to the greater tubercle, and together they laterally rotate the arm."],"fix":"Review where each rotator cuff muscle arises and inserts and which movement each one produces."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-arm-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-arm","comp":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A weightlifter tears the biceps brachii tendon away from the radial tuberosity. The brachialis and coracobrachialis are untouched. Which movement will be the most weakened?","options":["Turning the palm up with the elbow bent","Bending the elbow with the palm facing down","Raising the arm forward and across the chest","Straightening the elbow against resistance"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The biceps brachii inserts on the radial tuberosity, so its pull both flexes the elbow and spins the radius to turn the palm up, which is supination. The brachialis lies deep to the biceps and inserts on the ulna, so it keeps flexing the elbow after the biceps tendon tears, and it flexes equally well with the palm up or down. The coracobrachialis still flexes and adducts the arm at the shoulder. What the patient loses most is strong supination with the elbow bent, the movement the biceps does best.","notes":["Correct. The biceps spins the radius through its pull on the radial tuberosity and is the strongest supinator, especially with the elbow bent.","Elbow flexion with the palm down is mostly the work of the brachialis, which inserts on the ulna and flexes equally well in any forearm position. The biceps adds little in this position, so this movement is largely spared.","Raising the arm forward and across the chest is flexion and adduction at the shoulder, done by the coracobrachialis and pectoralis major, which are intact. The biceps helps only slightly at the shoulder.","Straightening the elbow is extension, done by the triceps brachii in the posterior compartment. The biceps opposes this movement, so tearing it would not weaken it."],"fix":"Review the insertions of the biceps brachii and brachialis and how each attachment explains its actions."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-arm-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-arm","comp":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On a humerus model with muscle attachments painted on, rank where these four muscles insert, from the most proximal (closest to the shoulder) to the most distal.","options":["Supraspinatus, teres minor, teres major, deltoid","Teres minor, supraspinatus, teres major, deltoid","Supraspinatus, teres major, teres minor, deltoid","Supraspinatus, teres minor, deltoid, teres major"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The three posterior cuff tendons attach to the greater tubercle in order from top to bottom: supraspinatus, infraspinatus, then teres minor. The teres major is not part of the cuff; it inserts lower, on the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus on the humeral shaft. The deltoid converges on the deltoid tuberosity, partway down the lateral side of the shaft, so its insertion is the most distal of the four. Telling the teres minor (tubercle) from the teres major (shaft) is the key step.","notes":["Correct. The supraspinatus takes the highest spot on the greater tubercle, the teres minor the lowest spot on it, the teres major lands below the tubercles on the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus, and the deltoid inserts farthest down at the deltoid tuberosity.","The cuff tendons stack on the greater tubercle in the same order as their origins on the scapula. The supraspinatus, from above the spine, takes the top spot, and the teres minor, from the lateral border, attaches lowest of the three.","The teres major is not a cuff muscle; it inserts on the medial lip of the intertubercular sulcus on the shaft, below the tubercles. The teres minor reaches the greater tubercle itself, so it lies higher than the teres major.","The deltoid inserts on the deltoid tuberosity, a roughened area partway down the lateral shaft. That is farther from the shoulder than the teres major insertion, which lies just below the tubercles."],"fix":"Review the insertions of the rotator cuff muscles, the teres major, and the deltoid on the humerus."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-arm-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-arm","comp":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient has pain at the front of the shoulder that gets worse when she bends the elbow against resistance with the palm up. Imaging shows an inflamed tendon sliding in the groove between the greater and lesser tubercles. Which tendon is it, and where does it begin?","options":["Short head of the biceps brachii, from the coracoid process","Long head of the triceps brachii, from the infraglenoid tubercle","Long head of the biceps brachii, from the supraglenoid tubercle","Coracobrachialis, from the coracoid process of the scapula"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The groove between the greater and lesser tubercles is the intertubercular sulcus. The tendon that slides in it belongs to the long head of the biceps brachii, which arises from the supraglenoid tubercle, crosses over the head of the humerus, and drops into the groove on its way down the arm. Bending the elbow with the palm up is exactly what the biceps does best, so resisted flexion pulls on the sore tendon. The short head and the coracobrachialis both start at the coracoid process instead and stay out of the groove.","notes":["The short head of the biceps does flex the elbow, but it arises from the coracoid process and never enters the intertubercular sulcus. It would be the answer if the inflamed tendon were at the tip of the coracoid instead of in the groove.","The long head of the triceps arises from the infraglenoid tubercle below the glenoid cavity and runs down the back of the arm. It extends the elbow, so bending the elbow against resistance would not load it.","Correct. The tendon of the long head of the biceps brachii arises from the supraglenoid tubercle, crosses over the humeral head, and runs down the intertubercular sulcus.","The coracobrachialis arises from the coracoid process and inserts on the medial humeral shaft, so it acts only at the shoulder. It does not cross the elbow and does not run in the intertubercular sulcus."],"fix":"Review the origins of the two heads of the biceps brachii and the path of the long head tendon through the intertubercular sulcus."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-forearm-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-forearm","comp":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A deep cut across the back of the forearm just below the elbow damages the superficial muscles that arise from the lateral epicondyle. Which pair of movements would be weakest?","options":["Flexing the wrist and curling the fingers into a fist","Extending the wrist and straightening the fingers","Turning the palm down and bending the wrist forward","Bending the elbow and turning the palm up"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The posterior compartment of the forearm holds the extensors and the supinator. Most of its superficial muscles, including extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, and extensor carpi ulnaris, share the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle. Their tendons cross the back of the wrist and the fingers, so they extend the wrist and straighten the fingers. Knowing the compartment and the common origin lets you predict the lost movements before you name every muscle.","notes":["Flexing the wrist and curling the fingers belong to the anterior compartment, whose superficial muscles arise from the medial epicondyle. This would be the answer for a cut on the front of the forearm.","Correct. The muscles of the common extensor origin, such as extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, and extensor carpi ulnaris, extend the wrist and fingers.","Turning the palm down is pronation, done by the pronator teres and pronator quadratus in the anterior compartment, and bending the wrist forward is flexion, also an anterior job. Both movements belong to the side opposite this cut.","Elbow flexion and supination come mainly from the biceps brachii in the arm, with help from the brachialis. The supinator is a deep posterior muscle, but the cut damaged the superficial layer, and the biceps would still supinate strongly."],"fix":"Review the muscles of the posterior forearm compartment, the common extensor origin, and the actions they share."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-forearm-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-forearm","comp":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A knife enters the front of the forearm in the midline, just above the wrist, and goes straight back toward the bones. Rank these structures in the order the blade would reach them.","options":["Flexor digitorum superficialis, palmaris longus tendon, flexor digitorum profundus, pronator quadratus","Palmaris longus tendon, flexor digitorum profundus, flexor digitorum superficialis, pronator quadratus","Palmaris longus tendon, flexor digitorum superficialis, pronator quadratus, flexor digitorum profundus","Palmaris longus tendon, flexor digitorum superficialis, flexor digitorum profundus, pronator quadratus"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The anterior forearm is stacked in layers. In the midline just above the wrist, the thin palmaris longus tendon lies just under the skin, on its way to the palmar aponeurosis. Beneath it are the tendons of flexor digitorum superficialis, and deeper still the tendons of flexor digitorum profundus, the deep finger flexor. The pronator quadratus lies flat against the distal radius and ulna, under all the flexor tendons. In someone who lacks a palmaris longus, the blade would simply meet the superficialis first.","notes":["The palmaris longus tendon runs down the center of the front of the wrist just under the skin, superficial to the finger flexors. It is the tendon that stands out in the midline when you cup the palm and flex the wrist.","The names give the order: superficialis means nearer the surface and profundus means deep. The superficialis lies on top, and the profundus tendons pass beneath it.","The pronator quadratus lies flat on the distal radius and ulna, under the flexor tendons. The profundus lies on top of it, so the blade reaches the profundus before the pronator quadratus.","Correct. The slender palmaris longus tendon runs down the middle just under the skin, the superficialis lies beneath it, the profundus lies deeper, and the pronator quadratus sits on the bones under both."],"fix":"Review the superficial, intermediate, and deep layers of the anterior forearm near the wrist."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-forearm-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-forearm","comp":"w2-hand-bones","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"The flexor retinaculum stretches from the pisiform and the hook of the hamate to the scaphoid and the trapezium, forming the roof of the carpal tunnel. A worker's tendons swell inside the tunnel. Besides the tingling from the squeezed median nerve, which movement uses a tendon that runs through the swollen tunnel?","options":["Tightening the palmar aponeurosis while cupping the palm","Tilting the hand toward the little finger side","Bending the tip of the thumb at its last joint","Straightening the tip of the thumb at its last joint"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The carpal bones form a curved trough on the palm side of the wrist, and the flexor retinaculum bridges across it, making a tunnel with rigid walls. Through it run the median nerve and nine tendons: four from flexor digitorum superficialis, four from flexor digitorum profundus, and one from flexor pollicis longus. Flexor pollicis longus inserts on the distal phalanx of the thumb, so it is the muscle that bends the thumb tip. To answer, you list what passes under the retinaculum, then match each tendon to the movement it makes.","notes":["Tightening the palmar aponeurosis is the job of palmaris longus, whose tendon passes in front of the flexor retinaculum, outside the tunnel. It is the tendon you see in the middle of the wrist, but swelling inside the tunnel does not reach it.","Tilting the hand toward the little finger is adduction, done by flexor carpi ulnaris and extensor carpi ulnaris. Flexor carpi ulnaris inserts on the pisiform, one of the bones the retinaculum attaches to, so its tendon ends at the edge of the tunnel instead of passing through it.","Correct. Bending the thumb tip is the work of flexor pollicis longus, whose tendon runs through the carpal tunnel with the median nerve and the eight long finger flexor tendons.","Straightening the thumb tip is the work of extensor pollicis longus, which crosses the back of the wrist and borders the anatomical snuffbox. The carpal tunnel is on the palm side, so no extensor tendon passes through it."],"fix":"Review the bones that form the carpal tunnel, the tendons and nerve that pass through it, and the action of each of those tendons."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-forearm-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-forearm","comp":"w2-hand-bones","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"After a fall on an outstretched hand, a patient has tenderness in the hollow on the back of the wrist that is bordered by the extensor pollicis longus tendon on one side and the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis tendons on the other. Which bone lies in the floor of this hollow, and which artery crosses it?","options":["The scaphoid, with the radial artery crossing over it","The scaphoid, with the ulnar artery crossing over it","The pisiform, with the radial artery crossing over it","The lunate, with the ulnar artery crossing over it"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A hollow bordered by the extensor pollicis longus on one side and the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis on the other is the anatomical snuffbox. It sits on the thumb side of the back of the wrist, and the scaphoid lies in its floor, which is why tenderness here after a fall suggests a scaphoid fracture. The radial artery crosses the floor of the snuffbox over the scaphoid on its way to the back of the hand, and you can feel its pulse there. Naming the hollow from its tendon borders leads to both the bone and the artery.","notes":["Correct. The hollow is the anatomical snuffbox; the scaphoid lies in its floor, and the radial artery crosses it on the way to the back of the hand.","The bone is right, but the ulnar artery runs on the little finger side of the wrist and stays on the palm side. The snuffbox is on the thumb side, so the artery crossing it is the radial artery.","The pisiform sits on the little finger side of the palm, where flexor carpi ulnaris inserts. The snuffbox is on the thumb side of the back of the wrist, far from the pisiform.","The lunate lies in the middle of the proximal row, not under the thumb tendons. The ulnar artery is also on the wrong side, since it runs on the little finger side of the wrist."],"fix":"Review the borders and floor of the anatomical snuffbox, the carpal bones in the proximal row, and the path of the radial artery at the wrist."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-ul-review-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-ul-review","comp":"w7-lab-neck","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A baby is born with a shortened, tight right sternocleidomastoid, a condition called torticollis. How would the baby's head be held?","options":["Tilted toward the right shoulder, with the chin turned to the right","Tilted toward the right shoulder, with the chin turned to the left","Tilted toward the left shoulder, with the chin turned to the left","Bent straight forward, with the chin tucked toward the chest"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The sternocleidomastoid runs from the manubrium and medial clavicle up to the mastoid process behind the ear. When one side pulls alone, it draws its mastoid process down toward its own shoulder, tilting the head to that side, and because the mastoid is behind the ear, the same pull swings the face toward the opposite side. A shortened right muscle holds the head in exactly that position: tilted right, chin turned left. When both sides contract together, they flex the neck straight forward instead.","notes":["The tilt is right, but the turn is not. The sternocleidomastoid runs from the sternum and clavicle up and back to the mastoid process, so pulling the right mastoid down and forward turns the face to the left.","Correct. One sternocleidomastoid tilts the head toward its own side and turns the face to the opposite side, so a short right muscle holds the head tilted right with the chin turned left.","This is the head position caused by a short left sternocleidomastoid. The tilt always goes toward the shortened muscle, so a right-sided problem tilts the head to the right.","Bending the neck straight forward happens when both sternocleidomastoids contract together. A single shortened muscle pulls the head unevenly, so it causes a tilt and a turn rather than straight flexion."],"fix":"Review the attachments of the sternocleidomastoid and its actions when one side or both sides contract."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-ul-review-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-ul-review","comp":"w7-lab-facial-expression","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A scalp wound cuts straight across the galea aponeurotica, running from above one ear toward the other. The edges of the wound pull widely apart. Which explanation fits best?","options":["The galea aponeurotica is elastic and springs back like a band when it is cut","The platysma pulls the lower edge of the scalp down toward the neck","The orbicularis oculi pulls the front edge of the wound down toward the eyes","The frontal and occipital bellies pull the two cut edges apart"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The epicranius (occipitofrontalis) has two muscular bellies joined by the galea aponeurotica, a flat sheet of tendon over the top of the skull. The occipital belly runs from the back of the skull to the galea and pulls the scalp backward, and the frontal belly runs from the galea to the skin of the forehead and eyebrows. A cut running from ear to ear severs the galea between the two bellies. Each belly then pulls its own edge, one forward and one back, so the wound gapes.","notes":["The galea aponeurotica is a flat sheet of dense connective tissue, an aponeurosis, not an elastic band. It does not recoil on its own; the gap comes from the muscles attached to its front and back edges.","The platysma runs from the fascia of the upper chest to the mandible and the corner of the mouth. It does not reach the scalp, so it cannot pull on the galea.","The orbicularis oculi encircles the eye and closes the eyelids, attaching around the orbit rather than to the galea. Its pull stays around the eye and does not open a wound on top of the head.","Correct. The galea aponeurotica links the frontal belly in front with the occipital belly behind, so once it is cut across, each belly pulls its edge away from the other."],"fix":"Review the frontal belly, occipital belly, and galea aponeurotica of the epicranius and where each attaches."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-ul-review-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-ul-review","comp":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Follow one tendon of the flexor digitorum profundus from the wrist to the tip of the index finger. In what order does it pass these four landmarks?","options":["Lumbrical origin, under the flexor retinaculum, through the split superficialis tendon, distal phalanx","Under the flexor retinaculum, lumbrical origin, through the split superficialis tendon, distal phalanx","Under the flexor retinaculum, through the split superficialis tendon, lumbrical origin, distal phalanx","Under the flexor retinaculum, lumbrical origin, distal phalanx, through the split superficialis tendon"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Flexor digitorum profundus tendons cross the wrist through the carpal tunnel, under the flexor retinaculum, together with the superficialis tendons and the median nerve. In the palm, each profundus tendon gives origin to a lumbrical, which runs to the extensor expansion of its finger. Near the base of the finger, the superficialis tendon splits into two slips that attach to the middle phalanx, and the profundus tendon passes through that split. It then continues to the distal phalanx, which is why it is the only muscle that bends the fingertip.","notes":["The lumbricals arise from the profundus tendons in the palm, which the tendon reaches only after leaving the carpal tunnel. The wrist, where the flexor retinaculum is, comes before the palm.","Correct. The tendon passes through the carpal tunnel, gives origin to a lumbrical in the palm, slips through the split in the superficialis tendon at the finger, and ends on the distal phalanx.","The lumbrical arises from the profundus tendon in the palm, before the tendon enters the finger. The superficialis tendon splits near the base of the finger, which is farther along the path.","The profundus tendon slips through the split superficialis tendon before it reaches its insertion, because the superficialis ends on the middle phalanx. The distal phalanx is the end of the path, so it must come last."],"fix":"Review the path of the flexor digitorum profundus tendons from the carpal tunnel to the distal phalanx, including the lumbricals and the split superficialis tendon."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-ul-review-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-ul-review","comp":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A fracture breaks the whole coracoid process off the scapula, and every muscle attached to it loses its anchor. Which set of movements would be weakened?","options":["Elevating the scapula, abducting the arm, and extending the elbow","Retracting the scapula, medially rotating the arm, and flexing the elbow","Depressing the scapula, flexing and adducting the arm, flexing the elbow","Depressing the scapula, laterally rotating the arm, and extending the elbow"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Three muscles attach to the coracoid process. The pectoralis minor runs up from ribs 3 to 5 and inserts there, so it depresses and protracts the scapula. The coracobrachialis and the short head of the biceps brachii both arise from the coracoid. The coracobrachialis flexes and adducts the arm, and the biceps flexes the elbow and supinates the forearm. Losing the coracoid weakens all three, although the long head of the biceps and the brachialis would still bend the elbow. Solving this means naming every muscle at one landmark and then recalling the action of each.","notes":["Elevating the scapula is done by the upper trapezius and levator scapulae, abduction by the supraspinatus and deltoid, and elbow extension by the triceps. None of these attach to the coracoid process.","Retraction of the scapula is the job of the rhomboids and middle trapezius, which attach to the spine and the medial border of the scapula. Medial rotation of the arm comes from muscles such as the subscapularis, not from the coracoid muscles.","Correct. The pectoralis minor inserts on the coracoid and depresses the scapula, the coracobrachialis arises there and flexes and adducts the arm, and the short head of the biceps arises there and flexes the elbow.","Depressing the scapula fits the pectoralis minor, but lateral rotation and elbow extension do not fit any coracoid muscle. Lateral rotation comes from the infraspinatus and teres minor, and elbow extension from the triceps."],"fix":"Review the three muscles attached to the coracoid process and the action of each one."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-uevn-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-uevn","comp":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A medication is injected into the basilic vein of the left arm. As it travels back to the heart, in what order does it pass through these four veins?","options":["Axillary vein, subclavian vein, left brachiocephalic vein, superior vena cava","Subclavian vein, axillary vein, left brachiocephalic vein, superior vena cava","Axillary vein, left brachiocephalic vein, subclavian vein, superior vena cava","Axillary vein, subclavian vein, superior vena cava, left brachiocephalic vein"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The basilic vein runs up the medial arm and joins the paired brachial veins to form the axillary vein. At the lateral border of the first rib, the axillary vein becomes the subclavian vein, the same landmark where the subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery. Behind the sternoclavicular joint, the subclavian vein joins the internal jugular vein to form the brachiocephalic vein; on the left, this vein is longer because it must cross the midline. The two brachiocephalic veins then join to form the superior vena cava, which empties into the right atrium.","notes":["Correct. The basilic vein joins the brachial veins to form the axillary vein, which becomes the subclavian vein at the first rib; the subclavian joins the internal jugular to form the left brachiocephalic vein, which drains into the superior vena cava.","The axillary vein comes before the subclavian vein on the way back to the heart. The name changes from axillary to subclavian at the lateral border of the first rib, and blood in a vein is heading toward the trunk.","The subclavian vein has to come before the brachiocephalic vein, because the brachiocephalic vein is formed when the subclavian vein meets the internal jugular vein. The brachiocephalic vein cannot come first and then feed the subclavian.","The superior vena cava is formed by the joining of the right and left brachiocephalic veins, so the brachiocephalic vein must come before it. The superior vena cava then empties into the right atrium."],"fix":"Review the deep veins of the upper limb and thorax and the landmarks where each vein changes its name."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-uevn-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-uevn","comp":"w2-bone-markings-vocab","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A student bumps the inner side of the elbow and feels a tingling jolt shoot into the little finger. Which nerve was struck, and what kind of bone marking does that nerve pass behind?","options":["The ulnar nerve, behind the olecranon fossa, a depression","The median nerve, behind the medial epicondyle, a projection","The radial nerve, in the radial groove, a depression","The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle, a projection"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The funny bone is not a bone at all; it is the ulnar nerve where it passes behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus, lying right against the bone under the skin. The ulnar nerve carries sensation from the little finger side of the hand, which is why the jolt travels there. The medial epicondyle is a projection, a bony bump that rises above the surrounding surface and serves as a muscle attachment. Depressions, such as a fossa or a groove, sink below the surface, and the radial groove is the depression that shelters the radial nerve.","notes":["The nerve is right, but the marking is not. The olecranon fossa is a depression on the back of the humerus that holds the olecranon when the elbow straightens, and the ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle instead.","The marking is right, but the median nerve runs down the front of the arm with the brachial artery and through the cubital fossa. It does not pass behind the medial epicondyle and does not carry sensation from the little finger.","The radial nerve runs in the radial groove, a depression on the back of the humeral shaft, far above the elbow. An injury there causes wrist drop, not tingling in the little finger after a bump on the inner elbow.","Correct. The ulnar nerve passes behind the medial epicondyle, a bony projection on the inner side of the distal humerus, where little padding protects it."],"fix":"Review the bone markings at the distal humerus, whether each is a projection or a depression, and which nerve runs beside each one."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-uevn-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-uevn","comp":"w8-nerve-plexuses","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A tumor at the very top of the lung presses upward on the lowest part of the brachial plexus, where it lies close to the first rib. Where is the patient most likely to notice weakness and numbness?","options":["The shoulder and the upper part of the arm","The hand and the medial side of the forearm","A patch of skin over the deltoid on the outer shoulder","The diaphragm, with trouble taking a deep breath"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The brachial plexus is built from C5 through T1. The lowest roots, C8 and T1, join to form the lower trunk, which lies lowest in the neck close to the first rib, right where a tumor at the top of the lung can press on it. The C8 and T1 fibers carry most of the supply to the hand and the medial side of the forearm, so that is where weakness and numbness show up. Working from the location to the trunk, then to the roots, then to the region they serve is how you predict the deficit from any plexus injury.","notes":["The shoulder and upper arm are served mainly by the upper trunk, formed by C5 and C6, which lies highest in the neck. This would be the answer for an injury that stretches the top of the plexus, such as the head being pulled away from the shoulder.","Correct. The lowest part of the plexus is the lower trunk, formed by C8 and T1, and these roots serve mainly the hand and the medial side of the forearm.","Numbness in a patch over the deltoid points to the axillary nerve, which wraps the surgical neck of the humerus. That is a single terminal branch injured near the shoulder, not the lowest part of the plexus near the first rib.","The diaphragm is supplied by the phrenic nerve from C3, C4, and C5, which comes from the cervical plexus and runs down through the thorax. Pressure on the lowest part of the brachial plexus would not affect it."],"fix":"Review the roots and trunks of the brachial plexus, where each trunk lies, and the region of the limb each one serves."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m3-uevn-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m3-uevn","comp":"bvn-great-vessels","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A patient has a narrowing at the origin of the first branch that leaves the aortic arch. The radial pulse is weaker on one side. Which side is it, and which arteries does blood take from the arch to reach the axillary artery on that side?","options":["Left; left subclavian artery directly from the arch","Right; right common carotid artery, then right subclavian artery","Right; brachiocephalic trunk, then right subclavian artery","Left; brachiocephalic trunk, then left subclavian artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"As the aortic arch curves from right to left, it gives off the brachiocephalic trunk first, then the left common carotid artery, then the left subclavian artery. The brachiocephalic trunk is a short stem that serves only the right side, dividing into the right common carotid artery and the right subclavian artery. A narrowing at its origin reduces flow into the right subclavian artery, which becomes the axillary artery at the first rib, then the brachial artery, which divides into the radial and ulnar arteries, so the right radial pulse is weaker. The left upper limb is spared because its subclavian artery comes straight off the arch.","notes":["The left subclavian artery does leave the arch directly, but it is the third branch, not the first. A narrowing at the first branch spares it, so the left pulse would be normal.","The side is right, but the route is not. The right common carotid and right subclavian arteries are sister branches of the brachiocephalic trunk, so blood does not pass through the carotid to reach the subclavian.","Correct. The first branch of the arch is the brachiocephalic trunk, which serves only the right side and divides into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries, so the right radial pulse is weaker.","There is no left brachiocephalic trunk; the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries leave the arch separately. The brachiocephalic trunk is a right-sided vessel only."],"fix":"Review the three branches of the aortic arch in order, why the right and left sides differ, and the route from the arch to the radial artery on each side."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-resp-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-resp","comp":"resp-larynx","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"During intubation, a breathing tube is guided from the laryngopharynx down into the trachea. On the way, its tip passes the flap that guards the laryngeal inlet, both pairs of folds inside the larynx, and the ring at the base of the larynx. In what order does the tip pass these structures?","options":["Epiglottis, vocal folds (true vocal cords), vestibular folds (false vocal folds), cricoid cartilage","Vestibular folds (false vocal folds), epiglottis, vocal folds (true vocal cords), cricoid cartilage","Epiglottis, vestibular folds (false vocal folds), vocal folds (true vocal cords), cricoid cartilage","Epiglottis, cricoid cartilage, vestibular folds (false vocal folds), vocal folds (true vocal cords)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The epiglottis sits at the top of the larynx over the laryngeal inlet, so the tube meets it first. Inside the larynx, the vestibular folds (the false vocal cords) lie above the true vocal folds, so the tube passes the false folds before it slips through the glottis between the true vocal folds. The cricoid cartilage is the complete ring at the bottom of the larynx, just below the thyroid cartilage, and the trachea begins right under it. If you remember that the false folds sit above the true folds and that the cricoid ring is the floor of the larynx, you can place every structure along the tube's path.","notes":["This order swaps the two pairs of folds. The vestibular folds (false vocal folds) are the upper pair and the true vocal folds lie below them, so the tube cannot reach the true folds first. Students pick this when they mix up which pair of folds sits on top.","The epiglottis is at the very top of the larynx, guarding the laryngeal inlet, so it is the first structure the tube meets, not the second. The vestibular folds lie inside the larynx, below the epiglottis.","Correct. The tube passes the epiglottis at the inlet, then the false folds, then the true vocal folds at the glottis, and finally the cricoid ring at the base of the larynx.","The cricoid cartilage is the ring at the bottom of the larynx, below both pairs of folds. Placing it second would put the floor of the larynx above the folds that sit inside it."],"fix":"Review the larynx from top to bottom on the midsagittal model: epiglottis, vestibular folds, vocal folds, then the cricoid cartilage."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-resp-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-resp","comp":"resp-tree","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A bronchoscope is passed through the glottis and down the trachea. At the carina the doctor steers it into the primary bronchus that continues more directly downward, and a little farther along she sees three secondary (lobar) bronchi branching from it. Which conclusion is best supported by what she sees?","options":["She is in the right primary bronchus, and one of these lobar bronchi leads to the middle lobe","She is in the left primary bronchus, and one of these lobar bronchi leads to the lingula","She is in the right primary bronchus, and each lobar bronchus leads to a single segment","She is in the left primary bronchus, which is the wider and more vertical of the two"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The right primary bronchus is wider and more vertical than the left, so a scope that continues more directly downward from the carina has entered the right side. Each secondary (lobar) bronchus supplies one lobe, and the right lung has three lobes (superior, middle and inferior) while the left lung has only two. Seeing three lobar bronchi therefore confirms the right side, and the lobe found only on the right is the middle lobe. The tertiary (segmental) bronchi are the next branches down, and they are the ones that each supply a single bronchopulmonary segment.","notes":["Correct. A more vertical path and three lobar bronchi both point to the right side, and the middle lobe exists only in the right lung.","The left primary bronchus gives off only two lobar bronchi, one for the superior lobe and one for the inferior lobe. The lingula is part of the left superior lobe, not a separate lobe with its own lobar bronchus, so three lobar branches point to the right lung instead.","The side is right, but the level is wrong. Lobar (secondary) bronchi each supply a whole lobe, and it is the tertiary (segmental) bronchi, one step farther down, that each supply a single bronchopulmonary segment. Students pick this when they mix up the lobar and segmental levels of the tree.","The left primary bronchus is the narrower, more horizontal one, and the wider, more vertical bronchus is the right. The left side would also show only two lobar bronchi, not three."],"fix":"Review how the right and left primary bronchi differ, and how many lobar bronchi each lung receives."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-resp-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-resp","comp":"resp-lungs-pleura","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A needle is pushed between two ribs on the side of the chest until its tip just touches the surface of the lung. Rank these structures from the most superficial to the deepest, in the order the needle reaches them.","options":["Internal intercostals, external intercostals, parietal pleura, visceral pleura","External intercostals, internal intercostals, visceral pleura, parietal pleura","External intercostals, parietal pleura, internal intercostals, visceral pleura","External intercostals, internal intercostals, parietal pleura, visceral pleura"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The intercostal muscles fill the spaces between the ribs, and the external intercostals form the outer layer while the internal intercostals lie deep to them. The parietal pleura lines the inner surface of the thoracic wall, so it is the first membrane the needle meets after the muscles. Between it and the lung is the pleural cavity, which holds only a thin film of fluid. The visceral pleura is stuck to the outer surface of the lung itself, which makes it the deepest layer the needle touches.","notes":["The two muscle layers are reversed. The external intercostals are the outer layer of the intercostal space and the internal intercostals lie deep to them, which the names themselves tell you.","The two pleural layers are reversed. The parietal pleura lines the thoracic wall, so the needle reaches it first, and the visceral pleura is stuck to the lung surface, so it is the deepest layer. Students pick this when they mix up which pleural layer lines the wall and which covers the lung.","The parietal pleura cannot lie between the two intercostal layers. It lines the inner surface of the thoracic wall, deep to all of the intercostal muscles, so both muscle layers come before it.","Correct. Both intercostal layers come first, outer before inner, then the parietal pleura lining the wall, and last the visceral pleura on the lung surface."],"fix":"Review the layers of the thoracic wall and the difference between the parietal pleura, the pleural cavity and the visceral pleura."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-resp-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-resp","comp":"resp-upper-tract","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A 6-year-old snores and breathes through his mouth because he cannot breathe through his nose, even though his nasal cavity looks clear. He swallows food without any trouble. The doctor finds a swollen tonsil blocking air just behind the posterior openings of the nasal cavity. Which tonsil is swollen, and why is swallowing unaffected?","options":["The palatine tonsil, because it sits in the oropharynx, which carries only air","The pharyngeal tonsil, because it sits in the nasopharynx, which carries only air","The lingual tonsil, because it sits at the base of the tongue, behind the food path","The pharyngeal tonsil, because the oropharynx it sits in closes during each swallow"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Air leaving the back of the nasal cavity enters the nasopharynx, the region of the pharynx behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. The pharyngeal tonsil sits in the wall of the nasopharynx, so when it swells it blocks nasal breathing and the child switches to breathing through his mouth. The nasopharynx carries only air, and during a swallow the soft palate and uvula lift to close it off, so food never passes the swollen tonsil. Food travels through the oropharynx and laryngopharynx below, which the pharyngeal tonsil does not block. The palatine and lingual tonsils, by contrast, sit in the oropharynx, where food and air both travel.","notes":["The palatine tonsils sit between the palatal arches in the oropharynx, and the oropharynx carries both food and air, not air only. Swollen palatine tonsils are the ones you can see when a patient opens wide, and they are more likely to make swallowing painful.","Correct. The pharyngeal tonsil lies in the nasopharynx, above the soft palate, so it can block nasal breathing while the food path below it stays clear.","The lingual tonsil sits at the base of the tongue in the oropharynx, which is part of the food path. It lies below the soft palate, not behind the nasal cavity, so it could not block air coming out of the nose.","The tonsil is right, but its region is wrong. The pharyngeal tonsil is in the nasopharynx, above the soft palate, and it is the nasopharynx, not the oropharynx, that the soft palate seals off during a swallow."],"fix":"Review the three regions of the pharynx, which tonsil sits in each, and which regions carry air only or both air and food."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-thigh-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-thigh","comp":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A nurse feels a strong femoral pulse just below the inguinal ligament and needs to place a needle in the femoral vein. Using the order of structures in the femoral triangle, where should the needle go, and which structure lies on the other side of the artery?","options":["Just medial to the pulse; the femoral nerve lies lateral to the artery","Just lateral to the pulse; the femoral nerve lies medial to the artery","Just medial to the pulse; the sciatic nerve lies lateral to the artery","Directly deep to the pulse; the femoral nerve lies on top of the artery"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In the femoral triangle, just below the inguinal ligament, the femoral nerve, femoral artery and femoral vein line up from lateral to medial. The pulse marks the artery, so the vein lies just medial to it. The femoral nerve lies on the other side of the artery, lateral to it, which is why a needle aimed too far laterally risks the nerve. The memory aid NAVL (nerve, artery, vein, lymphatics) reads in this lateral to medial order.","notes":["Correct. The femoral vein is just medial to the femoral artery, and the femoral nerve is lateral to the artery.","This reverses the order. The vein is medial to the artery and the nerve is lateral, so moving laterally from the pulse heads toward the femoral nerve, not the vein.","The direction for the vein is right, but the sciatic nerve is not in the femoral triangle. It runs down the back of the thigh, deep to the hamstrings, and the nerve lateral to the femoral artery is the femoral nerve.","The three structures lie side by side across the femoral triangle rather than stacked on top of one another. A needle pushed straight down through the pulse would enter the femoral artery, not the vein."],"fix":"Review the contents of the femoral triangle in order from lateral to medial (nerve, artery, vein)."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-thigh-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-thigh","comp":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A deep stab wound enters the middle of the back of the thigh, between the biceps femoris on the lateral side and the semitendinosus on the medial side, and goes deep to both muscles. Which structure is most at risk?","options":["The femoral artery","The femoral nerve","The vastus intermedius","The sciatic nerve"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the posterior thigh, the sciatic nerve runs straight down the middle, deep to the hamstring muscles and resting on the adductor magnus. A wound between the biceps femoris and the semitendinosus that passes deep to them is aimed right at that path. The femoral nerve and femoral artery enter the thigh through the femoral triangle on the front of the thigh, far from a posterior wound. At mid-thigh the sciatic nerve has not yet divided, since it usually splits into the tibial and common fibular nerves just above the knee.","notes":["The femoral artery runs down the front and medial side of the thigh from the femoral triangle, and it only reaches the back of the knee in the lower thigh, where it becomes the popliteal artery. A wound on the front of the thigh, just below the inguinal ligament, is where it would be most at risk.","The femoral nerve enters the front of the thigh through the femoral triangle and fans out into the anterior thigh within a short distance. It does not run in the posterior thigh, so a wound behind the femur would not reach it.","The vastus intermedius lies on the front of the femur, deep to the rectus femoris. A posterior wound would have to pass through the femur to reach it, so it would be the answer only for a deep wound on the front of the thigh.","Correct. The sciatic nerve runs down the middle of the posterior thigh, deep to the hamstrings, exactly where this wound is heading."],"fix":"Review where the sciatic nerve runs in the thigh compared with the femoral nerve and femoral artery."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-thigh-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-thigh","comp":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On the cadaver, a probe is pushed straight in through the upper buttock at a spot where all three gluteal muscles overlap, until it stops against bone. Rank these structures from the most superficial to the deepest, in the order the probe meets them.","options":["Gluteus medius, gluteus maximus, gluteus minimus, ilium","Gluteus maximus, gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, ilium","Gluteus maximus, gluteus minimus, gluteus medius, ilium","Gluteus minimus, gluteus medius, gluteus maximus, ilium"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The three gluteal muscles are stacked from superficial to deep, and their names follow their size as well as their depth. The gluteus maximus is the largest and most superficial, forming most of the bulk of the buttock. The gluteus medius lies beneath it, and the gluteus minimus is the smallest and deepest, lying directly on the outer surface of the ilium. A probe pushed straight in therefore passes the maximus, the medius and the minimus before it reaches bone.","notes":["The gluteus medius is partly covered by the gluteus maximus, so where the two overlap the maximus is met first. A probe placed higher and farther forward, past the edge of the maximus, would meet the medius first, but then it would not pass through the maximus at all.","Correct. The maximus is the surface layer, the medius lies under it, and the minimus lies deepest, right on the ilium.","The medius and minimus are reversed. The gluteus minimus is the deepest gluteal muscle, lying directly on the ilium, which its name (the smallest) also hints at.","This lists the three muscles from deep to superficial but still puts the bone last, so it mixes two directions. The gluteus minimus lies right on the ilium, so it is the last muscle the probe meets before bone, not the first."],"fix":"Review the three gluteal muscles in order from superficial to deep and which one lies directly on the ilium."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-thigh-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-thigh","comp":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"During knee surgery, a surgeon finds three tendons inserting side by side on the medial surface of the upper tibia. Each tendon comes from a muscle in a different compartment of the thigh. Which set names the three muscles with the correct compartment for each?","options":["Sartorius (anterior), adductor longus (medial), biceps femoris (posterior)","Sartorius (medial), gracilis (anterior), semitendinosus (posterior)","Sartorius (anterior), gracilis (medial), semitendinosus (posterior)","Rectus femoris (anterior), gracilis (medial), semitendinosus (posterior)"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Only a few thigh muscles reach the medial side of the upper tibia, and three of them insert there side by side. The sartorius crosses the front of the thigh diagonally from the anterior superior iliac spine and belongs to the anterior compartment. The gracilis is the long strap of the medial compartment and the only adductor that crosses the knee. The semitendinosus is the superficial medial hamstring of the posterior compartment, and its long, rope-like tendon ends beside the other two. Together these three tendons are called the pes anserinus.","notes":["The adductor longus inserts on the linea aspera of the femur and never reaches the tibia, and the biceps femoris is the lateral hamstring, ending on the head of the fibula. Neither one could be a tendon on the medial tibia.","These are the right muscles with two compartments swapped. The sartorius belongs to the anterior compartment even though its tendon ends on the medial tibia, and the gracilis is a medial compartment muscle. Students pick this when they label the sartorius by where its tendon ends rather than where its belly lies.","Correct. The sartorius (anterior), gracilis (medial) and semitendinosus (posterior) each come from a different compartment and insert together on the medial upper tibia.","The gracilis and semitendinosus are right, but the rectus femoris ends with the rest of the quadriceps on the tibial tuberosity, on the front of the tibia. The anterior compartment muscle that reaches the medial tibia is the sartorius."],"fix":"Review which thigh compartment holds the sartorius, the gracilis and each hamstring, and where each one inserts."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-leg-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-leg","comp":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A soccer player fractures the neck of the fibula, and the nerve that wraps around it is crushed. In the leg dissection, which two nerves lie downstream of this injury, and which vessel runs beside one of them?","options":["The tibial and deep fibular nerves; the anterior tibial artery runs beside the tibial nerve","The superficial and deep fibular nerves; the posterior tibial artery runs with the superficial one","The tibial and superficial fibular nerves; the posterior tibial artery runs beside the tibial nerve","The superficial and deep fibular nerves; the anterior tibial artery runs with the deep one"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The nerve that curves around the neck of the fibula, just under the skin, is the common fibular nerve, so a fracture there crushes it. Below this point the common fibular nerve divides into the superficial fibular nerve, which runs in the lateral compartment, and the deep fibular nerve, which passes into the anterior compartment. In the anterior compartment the deep fibular nerve travels alongside the anterior tibial artery, which is why the two appear together in the lab dissection. The tibial nerve is the other branch of the sciatic nerve and runs down the back of the leg, so it is spared.","notes":["The tibial nerve is a branch of the sciatic nerve, not of the common fibular nerve, and it runs in the back of the leg with the posterior tibial artery. The anterior tibial artery pairs with the deep fibular nerve in the front of the leg.","The two nerves are right, but the vessel pairing is wrong. The posterior tibial artery runs down the back of the leg with the tibial nerve, while the superficial fibular nerve runs in the lateral compartment among the fibularis muscles.","The second half is true, since the posterior tibial artery does run with the tibial nerve, but the tibial nerve is not downstream of a fibular neck injury. It continues from the sciatic nerve down the back of the leg, away from the fibula.","Correct. The crushed common fibular nerve feeds the superficial and deep fibular nerves, and the deep fibular nerve runs with the anterior tibial artery in the anterior compartment."],"fix":"Review the course of the common fibular nerve around the fibular neck, its two branches, and the vessel that travels with each nerve in the leg."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-leg-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-leg","comp":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A small clot breaks loose in the external iliac artery and travels with the blood until it lodges in the artery whose pulse is felt just behind the medial malleolus. In order, which landmarks and vessels did it pass?","options":["Femoral artery, under the inguinal ligament, adductor hiatus, popliteal artery, posterior tibial artery","Under the inguinal ligament, femoral artery, adductor hiatus, popliteal artery, posterior tibial artery","Under the inguinal ligament, femoral artery, popliteal artery, adductor hiatus, posterior tibial artery","Under the inguinal ligament, femoral artery, adductor hiatus, posterior tibial artery, popliteal artery"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The external iliac artery becomes the femoral artery where it passes under the inguinal ligament into the thigh. The femoral artery runs down the thigh and passes through the adductor hiatus, a gap in the tendon of the adductor magnus, to reach the back of the knee, where it is renamed the popliteal artery. Below the knee the popliteal artery divides into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries. The pulse behind the medial malleolus belongs to the posterior tibial artery, so that is where the clot ends up.","notes":["The femoral artery begins at the inguinal ligament, and above the ligament the same vessel is called the external iliac artery. A clot coming from the external iliac artery must pass under the ligament before it is in the femoral artery.","Correct. The clot passes under the inguinal ligament into the femoral artery, through the adductor hiatus into the popliteal artery, and then into the posterior tibial artery.","The adductor hiatus comes before the popliteal artery, not after. Passing through the hiatus to the back of the knee is exactly what turns the femoral artery into the popliteal artery.","The popliteal artery gives rise to the posterior tibial artery, so it must come first. A clot moving with the blood cannot reach the posterior tibial artery and then travel back up into the popliteal artery."],"fix":"Review the main artery of the lower limb from the external iliac artery to the posterior tibial artery, and the landmark where each name changes."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-leg-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-leg","comp":"w5-lab-leg-muscles","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"To screen a patient's leg muscles, an examiner asks her to walk on her heels with the front of each foot lifted off the floor. On the left side, the front of her foot keeps slapping down. In the leg dissection, which pair of muscles is most likely weak?","options":["Gastrocnemius and soleus","Fibularis longus and fibularis brevis","Tibialis anterior and extensor digitorum longus","Tibialis anterior and fibularis brevis"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Walking on the heels requires holding each foot in dorsiflexion so the front of the foot stays off the floor. The dorsiflexors sit in the anterior compartment of the leg, in front of the ankle, and the two seen in the lab dissection are the tibialis anterior and the extensor digitorum longus. When they are weak, the front of the foot drops and slaps the floor. The calf muscles do the opposite movement, plantar flexion, and the fibularis muscles of the lateral compartment evert the foot.","notes":["The gastrocnemius and soleus plantar flex the foot through the calcaneal tendon. They would be tested by walking on the toes, which is the opposite of this test.","The fibularis longus and brevis are the lateral compartment muscles, and their main job is eversion, turning the sole outward. Weakness here would show up as trouble turning the sole outward, not as trouble holding the front of the foot up.","Correct. Both are anterior compartment muscles that dorsiflex the foot, which is exactly what heel walking demands.","The tibialis anterior fits, but the fibularis brevis belongs to the lateral compartment and everts the foot. Its partner for dorsiflexion in the anterior compartment is the extensor digitorum longus."],"fix":"Review the three compartments of the leg, which muscles sit in each, and the main action each compartment shares."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-leg-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-leg","comp":"w5-lab-leg-muscles","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A deep puncture wound enters the upper part of the back of the calf and travels straight forward toward the interosseous membrane. Rank these structures from the most superficial to the deepest, in the order the wound would reach them.","options":["Gastrocnemius, soleus, posterior tibial artery, tibialis posterior","Gastrocnemius, soleus, tibialis posterior, posterior tibial artery","Soleus, gastrocnemius, posterior tibial artery, tibialis posterior","Gastrocnemius, posterior tibial artery, soleus, tibialis posterior"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The gastrocnemius is the most superficial muscle on the back of the leg and forms the bulge of the upper calf. The soleus is the broad, flat muscle directly deep to it, and the two join in the calcaneal tendon. Deep to the soleus, the posterior tibial artery runs down the leg with the tibial nerve, lying on the deep layer of muscles. The tibialis posterior is the deepest muscle on the back of the leg, resting on the interosseous membrane between the tibia and fibula.","notes":["Correct. The gastrocnemius and soleus form the superficial layer, the posterior tibial artery runs deep to the soleus, and the tibialis posterior lies deepest.","The posterior tibial artery runs between the superficial and deep muscle layers, so the wound reaches it before the tibialis posterior, not after. The tibialis posterior is the deepest muscle, lying on the interosseous membrane.","The two calf muscles are reversed. The gastrocnemius is the superficial calf muscle and the soleus lies directly deep to it, which is why the soleus shows mainly at the sides of the lower calf.","The posterior tibial artery does not run between the gastrocnemius and the soleus. It lies deep to the soleus, along with the tibial nerve, on top of the deep muscles."],"fix":"Review the superficial and deep layers of the posterior leg and where the posterior tibial artery and tibial nerve run between them."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-digest-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-digest","comp":"w5-small-intestine","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"An endoscope passes through the pyloric sphincter and follows the C-shaped curve of the duodenum until it reaches the jejunum. Along the way it passes the opening where bile and pancreatic juice enter. In order, which structures does the tip pass?","options":["Major duodenal papilla, superior part of the duodenum, inferior (horizontal) part, ascending part","Superior part of the duodenum, major duodenal papilla, inferior (horizontal) part, ascending part","Superior part of the duodenum, inferior (horizontal) part, major duodenal papilla, ascending part","Superior part of the duodenum, ascending part, major duodenal papilla, inferior (horizontal) part"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The duodenum curves around the head of the pancreas in four parts: the superior part leaving the pylorus, the descending part running down, the inferior (horizontal) part crossing to the left, and the ascending part rising to meet the jejunum. The common bile duct and main pancreatic duct join at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens at the major duodenal papilla on the wall of the descending part. So the scope meets the papilla after the superior part and before the horizontal part. That makes the descending part the place where bile and pancreatic juice first reach the small intestine.","notes":["The papilla is not at the very start of the duodenum. The first stretch past the pyloric sphincter is the superior part, and the ducts open farther along, in the descending part.","Correct. The papilla sits in the descending part, so it comes after the superior part and before the horizontal and ascending parts.","The inferior (horizontal) part comes after the descending part, so placing the papilla after it would put the opening in the wrong part. The papilla is on the descending part, which runs down beside the head of the pancreas.","This puts the ascending part before the horizontal part and places the papilla near the end. The horizontal part crosses to the left first and the ascending part then rises to the jejunum, and the papilla sits back in the descending part, right after the superior part."],"fix":"Review the four parts of the duodenum in order and which part holds the major duodenal papilla."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-digest-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-digest","comp":"w5-bile-path","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A gallstone lodges in the common bile duct just above the point where it joins the main pancreatic duct. Using the biliary tree and pancreatic duct models from lab, which outcome fits?","options":["Bile from the liver still reaches the duodenum, but the gallbladder can no longer fill or empty","Bile and pancreatic juice are both trapped, because the stone sits in their shared passage","No bile reaches the duodenum, but pancreatic juice still enters through the ampulla","Only bile from the right lobe is blocked, while the left hepatic duct still drains normally"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Bile from the right and left hepatic ducts joins in the common hepatic duct, and the cystic duct from the gallbladder joins it to form the common bile duct. Every route bile can take to the duodenum, whether straight from the liver or out of the gallbladder, runs through the common bile duct, so a stone there stops all of it. The main pancreatic duct does not join the common bile duct until the hepatopancreatic ampulla, below the stone, so pancreatic juice still reaches the duodenum. A stone lodged lower, in the ampulla itself, would block the main pancreatic duct as well as the bile.","notes":["This is what happens when a stone blocks the cystic duct. Bile from the liver can still run down the common hepatic duct and common bile duct, but the gallbladder is cut off. A stone in the common bile duct sits below the point where the cystic duct joins, so it blocks liver bile as well.","This describes a stone at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, where the common bile duct and main pancreatic duct share a final passage. The stone in this case sits above that junction, so the pancreatic duct is still open.","Correct. The common bile duct carries all bile, but the main pancreatic duct joins below the stone, so only bile is blocked.","A stone in the right hepatic duct would block bile from part of the liver while the left side drained normally. The common bile duct carries bile from both hepatic ducts, so a stone there blocks bile from the whole liver."],"fix":"Review the order of the hepatic, cystic and common bile ducts and where the main pancreatic duct joins them."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-digest-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-digest","comp":"w5-large-intestine","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A colonoscope is inserted through the anus and pushed all the way to the beginning of the large intestine. In what order does its tip pass these landmarks?","options":["Rectum, sigmoid colon, splenic flexure, hepatic flexure, cecum","Rectum, sigmoid colon, hepatic flexure, splenic flexure, cecum","Rectum, splenic flexure, sigmoid colon, hepatic flexure, cecum","Cecum, hepatic flexure, splenic flexure, sigmoid colon, rectum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"A colonoscope travels against the normal flow of feces, so it meets the parts of the large intestine in reverse order. From the anus it enters the rectum, then the S-shaped sigmoid colon, and climbs the descending colon on the left side to the splenic flexure (left colic flexure) near the spleen. It crosses the transverse colon to the hepatic flexure (right colic flexure) near the liver, then runs down the ascending colon to the cecum, the pouch at the start of the large intestine. The spleen sits on the left and the liver on the right, which is how the two flexures got their names.","notes":["Correct. Working backward from the anus, the scope passes the rectum, the sigmoid colon, the splenic flexure on the left, the hepatic flexure on the right, and ends at the cecum.","The two flexures are swapped. Coming up from the sigmoid colon on the left side, the scope reaches the splenic (left colic) flexure first, then crosses the transverse colon to the hepatic (right colic) flexure.","The sigmoid colon lies just above the rectum in the pelvis, so the scope must pass it before climbing to any flexure. The splenic flexure sits high on the left, at the top of the descending colon.","This is the path feces take, from the cecum toward the rectum. The scope enters from the anus, so it meets the same landmarks in the opposite order."],"fix":"Review the parts of the large intestine in order and which flexure sits on the left and which on the right."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-digest-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-digest","comp":"w5-liver-gross-lobule","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On the inferior view of the liver model, a student pins a small lesion on the lobe that lies between the gallbladder and the ligamentum teres (round ligament), in front of the spot where the vessels and ducts enter the liver. Using the four liver lobes on the lab structure list, which lobe holds the lesion?","options":["Caudate lobe","Left lobe of liver","Right lobe of liver","Quadrate lobe"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"On the inferior surface of the liver, two small lobes sit between the large right and left lobes. The quadrate lobe is the squarish lobe in front of the porta hepatis (the gateway where vessels and ducts enter), bordered by the gallbladder on one side and the ligamentum teres on the other. The caudate lobe lies behind the porta hepatis, toward the inferior vena cava. The ligamentum teres runs in the edge of the falciform ligament, which marks the boundary of the left lobe, so a lesion between the round ligament and the gallbladder sits in the quadrate lobe.","notes":["The caudate lobe is the other small lobe on the inferior surface, but it lies behind the porta hepatis, near the inferior vena cava. It is not the lobe in front of the porta hepatis between the gallbladder and the round ligament.","The left lobe lies on the far side of the ligamentum teres and the falciform ligament from the gallbladder. A lesion on the gallbladder side of the round ligament is not in the left lobe.","The right lobe lies on the far side of the gallbladder. Some books count the quadrate lobe as part of the right lobe because it lies to the right of the falciform ligament, but on the lab structure list it is its own lobe, so the strip between the gallbladder and the round ligament is named the quadrate lobe.","Correct. The quadrate lobe fills the space between the gallbladder and the ligamentum teres, in front of the porta hepatis."],"fix":"Review the four liver lobes on the inferior view and the landmarks (gallbladder, ligamentum teres, porta hepatis) that border each small lobe."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-abwall-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-abwall","comp":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon places a laparoscopic port through the lateral abdominal wall, well away from the rectus abdominis, and pushes until the tip enters the peritoneal cavity. Rank these structures from the most superficial to the deepest.","options":["External abdominal oblique, internal abdominal oblique, transversus abdominis, parietal peritoneum","External abdominal oblique, transversus abdominis, internal abdominal oblique, parietal peritoneum","Transversus abdominis, internal abdominal oblique, external abdominal oblique, parietal peritoneum","Internal abdominal oblique, external abdominal oblique, transversus abdominis, parietal peritoneum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The lateral abdominal wall is built from three flat muscles stacked on top of one another. The external abdominal oblique is the outermost layer, the internal abdominal oblique lies beneath it with fibers running at roughly right angles to it, and the transversus abdominis, with horizontal fibers, is deepest. The parietal peritoneum lines the inner surface of the wall, so it is the last layer before the peritoneal cavity. Because each muscle layer runs in a different direction, the three thin sheets together make a strong wall, much like plywood.","notes":["Correct. The three flat muscles go external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, and the parietal peritoneum lines the wall beneath them.","The transversus abdominis is the deepest of the three flat muscles, not the middle one. The internal abdominal oblique lies between it and the external abdominal oblique.","This lists the three muscles from deep to superficial but still puts the peritoneum last, so it mixes two directions. The port goes in from the skin, so it meets the external abdominal oblique first and the transversus abdominis last before the parietal peritoneum.","The two oblique muscles are reversed. The external abdominal oblique is the outermost flat muscle, as its name says, and the internal abdominal oblique lies under it."],"fix":"Review the three flat muscles of the abdominal wall in order from superficial to deep and the membrane that lines the wall."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-abwall-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-abwall","comp":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A surgeon opens the abdomen with a vertical cut exactly in the midline between the left and right rectus abdominis muscles, from below the xiphoid process toward the umbilicus. Which statement best explains why this cut divides no muscle fibers?","options":["The cut enters the medial edge of one rectus abdominis, whose vertical fibers part without being cut","The cut passes through the rectus sheath of one side, which is fascia that holds no muscle inside it","The cut passes through the linea alba, where the flat muscle aponeuroses of both sides interweave","The cut passes through the transversus abdominis, whose fibers run parallel to the incision"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The linea alba is the white fibrous line running down the midline from the xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis. It forms where the aponeuroses (flat, sheet-like tendons) of the external oblique, internal oblique and transversus abdominis of each side wrap the rectus abdominis and then interweave with their partners from the other side. Because it is dense connective tissue rather than muscle, a cut exactly along it separates the two rectus abdominis muscles without dividing any muscle fibers. On a lean athlete the same line shows as the groove down the center of the six-pack.","notes":["A cut exactly in the midline does not enter either rectus abdominis, because the left and right muscles are separated by the linea alba. Splitting the edge of one rectus along its fibers would still pass through muscle tissue, so this describes a cut placed slightly off the midline.","The rectus sheath is the aponeurotic covering that wraps each rectus abdominis, so cutting through one side's sheath would expose that rectus muscle. The sheaths of the two sides meet and blend in the midline at the linea alba, which is the structure a true midline cut follows.","Correct. The linea alba is made of interwoven aponeuroses, not muscle, so a cut along it passes between the two rectus muscles.","The transversus abdominis fibers run horizontally across the lateral wall, not vertically, and the muscle belly stops well lateral to the midline. Only its aponeurosis reaches the midline, where it helps form the linea alba."],"fix":"Review what forms the linea alba and the rectus sheath and how they relate to the rectus abdominis."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-abwall-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-abwall","comp":"w5-peritoneum-folds","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"After the anterior abdominal wall and parietal peritoneum are opened on the cadaver, students try to lift several organs forward to see what holds them in place. Which organ will be hardest to lift, and why?","options":["The jejunum, because the mesentery holds it flat against the posterior wall","The transverse colon, because it has no mesocolon of its own to hang from","The sigmoid colon, because it lies fixed in the pelvis behind the peritoneum","The pancreas, because it sits behind the peritoneum with no fold to hang from"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Organs that hang from a fold of peritoneum can be lifted and moved, because the fold acts like a hinge that also carries their vessels. The jejunum hangs on the mesentery, and the transverse and sigmoid colon each hang on a mesocolon, so all three can be lifted forward. The pancreas is retroperitoneal, lying behind the parietal peritoneum against the posterior abdominal wall with no fold of its own, so it stays fixed in place. To answer, you first sort each organ as retroperitoneal or held by a fold, then predict which one cannot move. Other retroperitoneal organs include the kidneys, most of the duodenum, and the ascending and descending colon.","notes":["The mesentery is a long, fan-shaped fold, so it lets the jejunum swing freely rather than holding it flat. The jejunum is one of the most mobile organs in the abdomen and lifts easily on the cadaver.","The transverse colon does hang on its own mesocolon, so it can be lifted forward and upward. That is exactly how students reach the small intestine and mesentery underneath it on the cadaver.","The sigmoid colon is held by its own mesocolon, so it lies within the peritoneal cavity and is fairly mobile. The parts of the colon fixed behind the peritoneum are the ascending and descending colon, so this reasoning would fit them instead.","Correct. The pancreas is retroperitoneal, fixed behind the parietal peritoneum with no fold to lift it by."],"fix":"Review which abdominal organs are retroperitoneal and which fold of peritoneum holds each of the others."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-abwall-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-abwall","comp":"w5-peritoneum-folds","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A knife wound to the lower left abdomen passes through the three flat muscles of the abdominal wall and then through the parietal peritoneum. The next thing the blade touches is a fatty apron draped over the intestines. Which fold is it, what does it hang from, and what space is the blade now in?","options":["Lesser omentum, hanging from the lesser curvature of the stomach; the peritoneal cavity","Greater omentum, hanging from the greater curvature of the stomach; the peritoneal cavity","Greater omentum, hanging from the greater curvature of the stomach; the retroperitoneum","Mesentery, hanging from the posterior abdominal wall; the retroperitoneum behind the gut"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Once a blade passes through the parietal peritoneum, which lines the abdominal wall, it enters the peritoneal cavity, the slim fluid-filled space between the parietal and visceral layers. The fatty apron lying just inside the front wall, draped over the intestines, is the greater omentum, the largest fold of peritoneum. It hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach, the long, convex lower border. The lesser omentum is much smaller and runs from the lesser curvature up to the liver, so it lies high under the liver, not over the intestines in the lower abdomen.","notes":["The lesser omentum is a small fold connecting the lesser curvature of the stomach and the duodenum to the liver. It sits high in the upper abdomen under the liver and does not drape over the intestines, so a wound in the lower left abdomen would not meet it first.","Correct. The greater omentum hangs from the greater curvature and drapes over the intestines, and a blade through the parietal peritoneum is in the peritoneal cavity.","The fold and its attachment are right, but the space is wrong. The retroperitoneum lies behind the peritoneum against the posterior wall, where organs such as the pancreas and kidneys sit, while a blade that has just passed through the parietal peritoneum of the front wall is in the peritoneal cavity.","The mesentery is the fan-shaped fold that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall, so it lies deep, behind the coils of small intestine. It is not a fatty apron over the intestines, and the space just inside the front wall is the peritoneal cavity, not the retroperitoneum."],"fix":"Review the parietal and visceral peritoneum, the peritoneal cavity, and what the greater and lesser omentum each connect."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-abdves-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-abdves","comp":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On the cadaver, a student follows the abdominal aorta down from the diaphragm toward its bifurcation and tags each branch as she reaches it. Rank these branches by where they leave the aorta, from highest to lowest.","options":["Celiac trunk, left renal artery, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery, left renal artery","Superior mesenteric artery, celiac trunk, left renal artery, inferior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, left renal artery, inferior mesenteric artery"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The celiac trunk is the first unpaired branch, leaving the front of the aorta just below the diaphragm at about T12. The superior mesenteric artery follows just below it at about L1, and the renal arteries leave the sides of the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery, at about L1 to L2. The inferior mesenteric artery arises much lower, at about L3, closer to the bifurcation. The three unpaired gut branches follow the order of the gut they supply: stomach and liver first, then the small intestine, then the last part of the colon.","notes":["The renal arteries leave the aorta just below the superior mesenteric artery, not above it. On the cadaver, find the superior mesenteric artery first, then look just below it on each side for the renal arteries.","The inferior mesenteric artery is the lowest of these branches, at about L3. The renal arteries arise higher, at about L1 to L2, level with the kidneys they supply.","The celiac trunk is the highest of the gut branches, leaving the aorta almost as soon as the aorta passes through the diaphragm. The superior mesenteric artery arises just below the celiac trunk.","Correct. The celiac trunk is highest, the superior mesenteric artery comes next, the renal arteries leave just below it, and the inferior mesenteric artery is lowest."],"fix":"Review the branches of the abdominal aorta from top to bottom, especially where the renal arteries sit relative to the two mesenteric arteries."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-abdves-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-abdves","comp":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A mass presses on the left renal vein where it crosses in front of the abdominal aorta, just below the origin of the superior mesenteric artery. Besides the left kidney, which veins seen in the lab will have their drainage slowed, and why?","options":["The left and right gonadal veins, because both gonadal veins empty into the left renal vein","The left gonadal vein and the left suprarenal vein, because both empty into the left renal vein","The left and right suprarenal veins, because both suprarenal veins empty into the left renal vein","The left gonadal vein and the inferior mesenteric vein, because both empty into the left renal vein"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The inferior vena cava runs up the right side of the abdomen, so the left renal vein has to cross in front of the aorta to reach it, just below where the superior mesenteric artery leaves the aorta. Two smaller veins from the left side empty into the left renal vein before it crosses: the left gonadal vein and the left suprarenal vein. Pressure on the left renal vein at the crossing slows the drainage of both. On the right side, the gonadal and suprarenal veins empty straight into the inferior vena cava, so they are not affected. This same pattern is one reason swollen veins around the testis (a varicocele) are more common on the left.","notes":["Only the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein. The right gonadal vein empties directly into the inferior vena cava, which lies close to it on the right side, so pressure on the left renal vein does not touch it.","Correct. The left gonadal and left suprarenal veins both empty into the left renal vein, so both back up when it is compressed.","The left suprarenal vein does drain into the left renal vein, but the right suprarenal vein empties directly into the inferior vena cava. Only the small veins on the left side depend on the left renal vein.","The left gonadal vein fits, but the inferior mesenteric vein belongs to the hepatic portal system. It usually empties into the splenic vein, which joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the hepatic portal vein, so its blood goes to the liver rather than the left renal vein."],"fix":"Review the tributaries of the inferior vena cava and why the left gonadal and left suprarenal veins drain differently from those on the right."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-abdves-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-abdves","comp":"w5-liver-gross-lobule","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A clot blocks the common hepatic artery shortly after it leaves the celiac trunk, yet blood keeps flowing through the liver sinusoids. Which explanation is best?","options":["The branch of the hepatic portal vein in each portal triad still brings in blood from the gut","The central vein of each lobule reverses its flow and brings blood in from the inferior vena cava","The bile duct in each portal triad carries blood into the lobule while the artery is blocked","The splenic artery, another celiac branch, enters the liver at the porta hepatis to replace it"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The liver has a double blood supply, and each portal triad shows both sources: a branch of the hepatic artery proper bringing oxygen-rich blood, and a branch of the hepatic portal vein bringing nutrient-rich blood from the stomach, intestines, pancreas and spleen. The hepatic portal vein forms from the splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein, which carry blood that has already passed through the gut, so a clot in the common hepatic artery does not stop that flow. With the artery blocked, portal blood still flows from the portal triads through the sinusoids toward the central vein. The bile duct, the third member of the triad, carries bile out of the liver rather than blood in.","notes":["Correct. The hepatic portal vein is a second, separate supply, so its branches keep the sinusoids filled even when the arterial supply is blocked.","The central vein collects blood that has already passed through the sinusoids and sends it out toward the hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava. Blood does not reverse and flow into the lobule through it.","The bile duct is part of the portal triad, but it carries bile away from the lobule, not blood into it. Of the three structures in the triad, only the arterial branch and the portal vein branch bring blood in.","The splenic artery is a branch of the celiac trunk, but it runs to the left along the pancreas to the spleen, not into the liver. The source that keeps the sinusoids filled is the hepatic portal vein, which the splenic vein (not the artery) helps form."],"fix":"Review the three parts of the portal triad and the two vessels that bring blood into the liver."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m4-abdves-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m4-abdves","comp":"w5-bile-path","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A hepatocyte in a lobule of the right lobe of the liver releases bile. Trace that bile, without a detour into the gallbladder, to the point where it is about to enter the duodenum.","options":["Bile canaliculi, common hepatic duct, right hepatic duct, common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla","Bile canaliculi, right hepatic duct, common bile duct, common hepatic duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla","Bile canaliculi, right hepatic duct, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla","Right hepatic duct, bile canaliculi, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, hepatopancreatic ampulla"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Bile is first collected by the bile canaliculi, tiny channels between the hepatocytes, and drains toward the bile duct at the corner of the lobule in the portal triad. Bile from the right lobe gathers into the right hepatic duct, which merges with the left hepatic duct to form the common hepatic duct. Without a detour into the gallbladder, the bile continues straight past the cystic duct into the common bile duct, which meets the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. From there it enters the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla. Notice that bile flows outward toward the portal triad, the opposite direction from blood, which flows inward to the central vein.","notes":["The common hepatic duct forms where the right and left hepatic ducts merge, so it must come after the right hepatic duct. Bile cannot reach the common hepatic duct before it has gathered into one of the hepatic ducts.","The common hepatic duct comes before the common bile duct, not after. The common bile duct only forms where the common hepatic duct is joined by the cystic duct.","Correct. Bile moves from the canaliculi into the right hepatic duct, then the common hepatic duct, the common bile duct, and the hepatopancreatic ampulla.","The bile canaliculi are the very first channels, lying between the hepatocytes where bile is made. The right hepatic duct is a much larger duct that collects bile from many lobules, so it comes after the canaliculi."],"fix":"Review the path of bile from the canaliculi through the hepatic, common hepatic and common bile ducts to the hepatopancreatic ampulla."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-urinary-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-urinary","comp":"w6-kidney-internal","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On a coronal section of a kidney, a biopsy needle is pushed straight in from the lateral border, through the middle of one renal pyramid, toward the renal sinus. Which list ranks these four structures from the first one reached to the last?","options":["Renal cortex, renal pyramid, minor calyx, renal papilla","Renal pyramid, renal cortex, renal papilla, minor calyx","Renal cortex, renal pyramid, renal papilla, minor calyx","Renal cortex, renal papilla, renal pyramid, minor calyx"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The renal cortex is the outer region just under the renal capsule, so a needle coming in from the lateral border meets it first. Deep to the cortex lies the medulla, which is built of cone-shaped renal pyramids with their wide bases facing the cortex. Each pyramid narrows toward the renal sinus and ends in its tip, the renal papilla. The papilla pokes into a cup-shaped minor calyx, so the minor calyx is the last space the needle enters. Reading the pyramid as a cone, base out and tip in, is what puts the papilla after the pyramid and before the calyx.","notes":["This order puts the minor calyx before the renal papilla. The calyx is the cup that wraps around the papilla, so the needle reaches the papilla first and then drops into the calyx.","This order starts in the renal pyramid, but the pyramids lie deep to the cortex. A needle from the outside always crosses the cortex first, so this order would only fit a needle that started inside the medulla.","Correct. The needle passes the outer cortex, then the cone of the pyramid from base to tip, then the papilla at that tip, and finally the minor calyx that cups it.","This order puts the renal papilla before the pyramid, but the papilla is the inner tip of the pyramid. Students pick this when they picture the papilla as a separate layer instead of the narrow end of the cone."],"fix":"Review how the renal pyramids sit in the medulla, which way their bases and tips face, and where each renal papilla drains."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-urinary-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-urinary","comp":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On the kidney model, a small clot lodges in one arcuate artery where it arches along the base of a renal pyramid. Which arteries lose their blood supply, and which kidney region do they feed?","options":["The cortical radiate arteries, so the renal cortex above that arch loses blood","The interlobar artery, so the renal column beside the pyramid loses blood","The segmental artery, so a whole region of the kidney near the hilum loses blood","The afferent arterioles of the medulla, so the renal pyramid itself loses blood"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Blood in the kidney follows one route: renal artery, segmental artery, interlobar artery, arcuate artery, cortical radiate artery, and then the afferent arteriole. The arcuate arteries arch along the bases of the pyramids at the border between the cortex and the medulla, and the cortical radiate arteries branch from them straight up into the cortex. A clot in one arcuate artery therefore starves the cortical radiate arteries beyond it and the patch of cortex they feed. Vessels upstream of the clot, like the interlobar and segmental arteries, still fill normally because blood reaches them before the blockage.","notes":["Correct. The cortical radiate arteries are the next branches after the arcuate artery, and they carry blood up into the cortex, where the renal corpuscles sit.","The interlobar artery is upstream of the arcuate artery, running between the pyramids in a renal column to reach the arch. It still fills because blood reaches it before the clot. It would lose blood only if the clot sat further upstream, in a segmental artery.","The segmental artery comes off the renal artery even further upstream, so a clot in one arcuate artery cannot starve it. A blocked segmental artery would cut off a much larger region, which is why students who mix up the order choose this.","Afferent arterioles feed glomeruli, and glomeruli sit in the renal cortex, not in the pyramids. The pyramids of the medulla hold the nephron loops and collecting ducts, so this choice places the arterioles in the wrong region."],"fix":"Review the order of the arteries from the renal artery to the afferent arteriole and where the arcuate and cortical radiate arteries run."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-urinary-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-urinary","comp":"w6-kidney-gross","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"In the cadaver lab, your group finds that the right kidney sits much lower than the left, and the fat around it is very thin. The right ureter is kinked just below the hilum, and the right renal pelvis and calyces are swollen, while the urinary bladder and the left kidney look normal. Which explanation ties all of these findings together?","options":["A stone at the internal urethral orifice blocked the outlet of the urinary bladder","A tear in the renal capsule let urine leak out into the fat around the kidney","A blocked right renal vein made the right kidney swell from the inside","Loss of the perirenal fat capsule let the kidney drop and kink its own ureter"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The perirenal fat capsule cushions the kidney and helps hold it in place, while the renal fascia anchors it to the surrounding structures. When that fat is lost, the kidney can drop out of position (nephroptosis), and the ureter leaving the hilum can bend sharply. Urine then backs up into the structures upstream of the kink, which are the renal pelvis and the calyces on that side. The urinary bladder is downstream and the left kidney drains through its own ureter, so both look normal. Only an explanation that starts on one side, above the bladder, can account for that pattern.","notes":["A blocked internal urethral orifice would trap urine in the urinary bladder and back it up into both ureters and both kidneys. Here the bladder is normal and only one side is swollen, so the block must sit above the bladder on the right.","A tear in the renal capsule would not move the kidney or kink the ureter. Swelling of the renal pelvis and calyces means urine is trapped inside the collecting system, not leaking out of it.","The renal vein carries blood, not urine, so blocking it would not fill the renal pelvis and calyces with urine. It also does not explain why the kidney sits low or why the ureter is kinked.","Correct. Losing its fatty support lets the kidney drop, the ureter kinks, and urine backs up into the renal pelvis and calyces on that side only."],"fix":"Review the three layers around the kidney, the job of the perirenal fat capsule, and how urine backs up above a blockage."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-urinary-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-urinary","comp":"w6-jga-filtration","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On a slide of the renal cortex you see a renal corpuscle. Pressed against the arterioles at its vascular pole is a tubule whose wall has a patch of tall, crowded cells. Which structure is that patch, and why is this tubule touching the corpuscle?","options":["The proximal convoluted tubule wall, because it leaves the capsule right beside the arterioles","The macula densa of the distal convoluted tubule, which returns to touch its own afferent arteriole","The juxtaglomerular cells of a collecting duct, which gathers fluid from many corpuscles","The parietal layer of the glomerular capsule, which folds back over the arterioles"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus forms where the distal convoluted tubule of a nephron comes back up from the nephron loop and touches the afferent arteriole of its own renal corpuscle. The tubule cells at that contact point are tall and packed close together, and this patch is the macula densa, which senses the sodium and chloride in the filtrate. The juxtaglomerular (granular) cells sit in the wall of the afferent arteriole on the other side of that contact. Knowing that the nephron loops away into the medulla and then returns to its own corpuscle is what lets you name the tubule.","notes":["The proximal convoluted tubule leaves the glomerular capsule on the side opposite the arterioles, and its cells carry a brush border of microvilli rather than forming the macula densa. Students choose it because it is the tubule they see attached to the capsule on the nephron model.","Correct. The macula densa is the crowded patch of distal tubule cells that touches the afferent arteriole of the same nephron.","Juxtaglomerular cells sit in the wall of the afferent arteriole, not in a tubule, and the collecting duct is not part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus. This would fit only a question about the cells in the arteriole wall.","The parietal layer of the glomerular capsule is thin simple squamous epithelium, not a patch of tall cells. It is the outer wall of the capsule itself rather than a separate tubule pressed against it."],"fix":"Review where the distal convoluted tubule meets its own afferent arteriole and which cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus sit in the tubule and which sit in the arteriole."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-female-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-female","comp":"w6-ovum-path","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"An oocyte is released at ovulation, is fertilized in the usual place, and the embryo implants normally. Which list gives the order in which the oocyte, and then the embryo, reaches these four structures?","options":["Fimbriae, ampulla, isthmus, endometrium","Fimbriae, isthmus, ampulla, endometrium","Ampulla, fimbriae, isthmus, endometrium","Fimbriae, ampulla, endometrium, isthmus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The fimbriae at the ovary end of the uterine tube sweep the released oocyte into the funnel-shaped infundibulum. From there it moves into the ampulla, the wide middle part of the tube where fertilization usually happens. The young embryo is then carried through the isthmus, the narrow segment that joins the uterus, and finally implants in the endometrium, the inner lining of the uterine wall. Placing fertilization in the ampulla tells you the isthmus comes after it, not before.","notes":["Correct. The fimbriae catch the oocyte, fertilization happens in the ampulla, the embryo passes the isthmus, and it implants in the endometrium.","This swaps the isthmus and the ampulla. The isthmus is the narrow end next to the uterus, so it comes last in the tube, after the wide ampulla where fertilization takes place.","The ampulla cannot come before the fimbriae, because the fimbriae are the finger-like edge at the ovary end that first catches the oocyte. This order would only fit an oocyte that entered the tube from the middle.","The embryo must pass through the isthmus to reach the uterine cavity, so it cannot implant in the endometrium first. An embryo that stops in the tube and implants there is an ectopic pregnancy, not a normal one."],"fix":"Review the regions of the uterine tube from the ovary end to the uterus and where fertilization and implantation happen."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-female-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-female","comp":"w6-ovary","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"The ovarian artery branches from the abdominal aorta near the kidneys and runs down into the pelvis along the lateral pelvic wall. During surgery to remove one ovary, which attachment of the ovary must the surgeon clamp to control this artery and the ovarian vein before cutting the ovary free?","options":["The ovarian ligament","The fimbriae of the uterine tube","The suspensory ligament of the ovary","The round ligament of the uterus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The ovary is held by two cords. The ovarian ligament ties it medially to the uterus, and the suspensory ligament of the ovary ties it laterally to the pelvic wall. Because the ovarian artery comes down from the aorta along the side of the pelvis, it has to reach the ovary from the lateral side, and it does so running inside the suspensory ligament together with the ovarian vein. That is why the surgeon clamps the suspensory ligament. Telling the two ovarian attachments apart by the direction they run, toward the uterus or toward the pelvic wall, and matching that to the direction the vessels come from, is the key lab skill here.","notes":["The ovarian ligament anchors the ovary to the uterus on its medial side and does not carry the ovarian artery and vein. It would be the answer to a question about what ties the ovary to the uterus.","The fimbriae are the finger-like edge of the infundibulum that sweep the oocyte into the uterine tube. They drape near the ovary but do not carry its main vessels.","Correct. The suspensory ligament of the ovary runs from the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall and carries the ovarian artery and ovarian vein.","The round ligament of the uterus runs from the uterus toward the labia majora and does not attach to the ovary at all. Students pick it when they mix up the cord-like ligaments that leave the side of the uterus."],"fix":"Review the ovarian ligament, the suspensory ligament of the ovary, and the round ligament, including where each one attaches and which one carries the ovarian vessels."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-female-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-female","comp":"w6-uterus","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"On a female pelvis model, a student points to a cord leaving the side of the uterus just below the uterine tube and calls it the ovarian ligament. Which observation would best show that it is actually the round ligament of the uterus?","options":["It lies within the broad ligament, the sheet of peritoneum draped over the uterus","It carries the ovarian artery and ovarian vein out to the lateral pelvic wall","It runs forward and down toward the labia majora instead of ending on the ovary","It attaches low on the cervix and runs back toward the sacrum behind the uterus"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Both cords leave the side of the uterus near where the uterine tube joins it, which is why they are easy to confuse on a model. The way to tell them apart is to follow each one to its other end. The ovarian ligament runs back and out to end on the ovary, while the round ligament of the uterus runs forward and down toward the labia majora. A feature that both cords share, such as lying within the broad ligament, cannot settle the question.","notes":["Both the ovarian ligament and the round ligament run within the broad ligament, so this observation is true of either cord. It cannot tell you which one you are pointing at.","Carrying the ovarian vessels to the pelvic wall describes the suspensory ligament of the ovary, which attaches to the ovary rather than the uterus. This observation would identify a third ligament, not the round ligament.","Correct. The round ligament heads forward and down toward the labia majora, while the ovarian ligament ends on the ovary.","Running from the cervix back toward the sacrum describes the uterosacral ligaments, which support the uterus from behind. The cord in question leaves the side of the uterus near the uterine tube, not the cervix."],"fix":"Review the ligaments of the ovary and uterus by tracing where each one starts and ends on the model."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-female-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-female","comp":"w6-urine-path","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A nursing student places a urinary catheter in a female patient but slides it into the vaginal orifice instead of the external urethral orifice. Where does the catheter tip end up, and which landmark should she use to find the correct opening on the next try?","options":["In the urinary bladder anyway; the two openings share one canal just inside the vestibule","In the uterine cavity past the internal os; the urethral orifice is just anterior to the vaginal orifice","In the vagina near the cervix; the urethral orifice is just posterior to the vaginal orifice","In the vagina near the cervix; the urethral orifice is just anterior to the vaginal orifice"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"In the vestibule, the external urethral orifice sits just anterior to the vaginal orifice, between it and the clitoris. The two openings lead to separate tubes: the urethra runs up to the urinary bladder, while the vagina is a fibromuscular canal that ends at the cervix, with the fornix forming a recess around it. A catheter in the vaginal orifice therefore stops in the vagina near the cervix, because the external os is a narrow opening that a catheter does not normally pass. No urine drains, which is the clue that the tube is wrong. To find the urethra on the next try, she should look just anterior to the vaginal orifice, below the clitoris.","notes":["The urethra and the vagina open separately into the vestibule and never share a canal, so a catheter in the vaginal orifice cannot reach the bladder. A tube in the vaginal orifice drains no urine, which is the sign it is in the wrong opening.","The landmark in this choice is right, since the urethral orifice is anterior to the vaginal orifice. The location is wrong, because the catheter stops in the vagina at the cervix and does not pass the narrow external os into the uterine cavity.","The catheter location is right, but the landmark is reversed. The urethral orifice lies anterior to the vaginal orifice, closer to the clitoris, so searching posterior to it would lead away from the urethra.","Correct. The vagina ends at the cervix, and the external urethral orifice sits just anterior to the vaginal orifice, below the clitoris."],"fix":"Review the order of the clitoris, external urethral orifice, and vaginal orifice in the vestibule and where the urethra and vagina each lead."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-male-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-male","comp":"w6-sperm-path","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Sperm leave the epididymis in the ductus (vas) deferens, which climbs out of the scrotum inside the spermatic cord. In which order do they pass these four landmarks on the way out of the body?","options":["Ejaculatory duct, inguinal canal, prostatic urethra, spongy (penile) urethra","Inguinal canal, ejaculatory duct, prostatic urethra, spongy (penile) urethra","Inguinal canal, prostatic urethra, ejaculatory duct, spongy (penile) urethra","Inguinal canal, ejaculatory duct, spongy (penile) urethra, prostatic urethra"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The ductus (vas) deferens travels in the spermatic cord through the inguinal canal into the pelvis. Behind the urinary bladder it joins the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct. The ejaculatory duct runs through the prostate gland and empties into the prostatic urethra. From there sperm pass the membranous urethra and finally the spongy (penile) urethra inside the corpus spongiosum before leaving the body.","notes":["The ejaculatory duct sits deep in the pelvis behind the bladder, so sperm cannot reach it before climbing through the inguinal canal. This order would only work if the ejaculatory duct were in the scrotum.","Correct. The ductus deferens passes the inguinal canal, joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct, which empties into the prostatic urethra, and the urethra ends as the spongy urethra.","This puts the prostatic urethra before the ejaculatory duct, but the ejaculatory duct is what delivers sperm into the prostatic urethra. Students choose this when they forget that the duct runs through the prostate to reach the urethra.","The prostatic urethra is the first region of the urethra below the bladder, and the spongy urethra is the last, inside the penis. Reversing them would send sperm back toward the bladder."],"fix":"Review the path of sperm from the epididymis to the outside, including where the ejaculatory duct forms and where it empties."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-male-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-male","comp":"w6-male-glands-penis","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A slow-growing tumor in the prostate gland squeezes every tube that passes through the gland. Which two tubes would it compress?","options":["The membranous urethra and the ductus (vas) deferens","The spongy (penile) urethra and the ejaculatory ducts","The prostatic urethra and the seminal vesicles","The prostatic urethra and the ejaculatory ducts"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The prostate gland sits just below the urinary bladder and wraps around the first part of the urethra, which is why that segment is named the prostatic urethra. The ejaculatory ducts also tunnel through the prostate on their way to empty into the prostatic urethra. A mass inside the gland can therefore press on both the urine path and the sperm path. The seminal vesicles and the end of the ductus deferens lie behind the bladder, above the prostate, so they are outside the gland.","notes":["The membranous urethra is the short segment below the prostate in the perineum, and the ductus deferens ends behind the bladder before reaching the prostate. Neither one runs through the gland.","The ejaculatory ducts are right, but the spongy urethra runs through the penis inside the corpus spongiosum, well below the prostate. The spongy urethra would be the one squeezed by a problem in the penis, not the prostate.","The prostatic urethra is right, but the seminal vesicles are separate glands pressed against the back of the bladder. Only their ducts, after joining the ductus deferens, pass into the prostate.","Correct. The prostatic urethra and the ejaculatory ducts both run through the prostate gland."],"fix":"Review where the prostate gland sits and which ducts pass through it."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-male-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-male","comp":"w6-scrotum-testes","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"On a cold day, a man's scrotal skin wrinkles and tightens normally on both sides, but his left testis does not rise toward the body while the right one does. Which structure on the left side is most likely not working?","options":["The cremaster muscle, thin bands of skeletal muscle wrapped around the spermatic cord","The pampiniform plexus, a tangle of testicular veins around the testicular artery","The ductus (vas) deferens, the firm muscular tube running up the spermatic cord","The dartos muscle, the layer of smooth muscle in the wall of the scrotum"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"Two muscles respond to cold around the testis, and the findings separate them. The dartos is smooth muscle in the scrotal wall that wrinkles and tightens the skin, and the skin is doing that normally on both sides, so the dartos is working. The cremaster muscle is skeletal muscle that wraps the spermatic cord and testis in thin loops, and when it contracts it lifts the testis toward the body to keep it warm. Only the left testis fails to rise, so the problem sits in the left spermatic cord, and the cremaster is the only structure in the cord whose job is to move the testis. The pampiniform plexus cools the blood in the testicular artery and the ductus deferens carries sperm, so neither one lifts the testis.","notes":["Correct. The cremaster muscle in the spermatic cord raises the testis in the cold, so its loss on the left leaves that testis hanging while the scrotal skin still wrinkles.","The pampiniform plexus does help with temperature, but by cooling the blood in the testicular artery, not by moving the testis. It would be the answer if the question asked how incoming blood is cooled.","The ductus deferens is the firm tube you can feel in the cord, and it carries sperm from the epididymis toward the urethra. It does not lift the testis, so losing it would not change how the testis sits in the cold.","The dartos wrinkles and tightens the scrotal skin to cut heat loss, and the skin is still doing that on both sides, so the dartos is working. It would be the answer if the scrotal skin stayed smooth and loose in the cold."],"fix":"Review the contents of the spermatic cord and the different jobs of the cremaster muscle, the dartos, and the pampiniform plexus."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-male-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-male","comp":"w6-urine-path","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"Which single lesion would block both urine and sperm from leaving the body?","options":["A scar that closes both ejaculatory ducts where they enter the prostate","A stricture that closes the membranous urethra below the prostate","A stone wedged in the internal urethral orifice at the bladder outlet","A vasectomy that cuts the ductus (vas) deferens on both sides"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Urine and sperm travel separate routes until they meet in the urethra. Urine leaves the urinary bladder through the internal urethral orifice into the prostatic urethra, and the ejaculatory ducts empty sperm into that same prostatic urethra. Everything below that meeting point, the membranous urethra and then the spongy (penile) urethra, carries both fluids. So only a block downstream of the ejaculatory duct openings can stop both, and the membranous urethra is the only choice that sits there.","notes":["Closing the ejaculatory ducts stops sperm and seminal vesicle fluid, but urine still flows from the bladder through the whole urethra. This lesion sits on the sperm route only.","Correct. The membranous urethra lies downstream of where the ejaculatory ducts join the urethra, so it carries both urine and semen.","A stone at the internal urethral orifice blocks urine leaving the bladder, but sperm enter the urethra below it, through the ejaculatory ducts in the prostatic urethra. Sperm could still get out.","A vasectomy keeps sperm from getting past the spermatic cord, but urine is untouched. It would be the answer if the question asked about blocking sperm alone."],"fix":"Review where the urine path and the sperm path join in the male urethra and the order of the prostatic, membranous, and spongy urethra."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-brain-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-brain","comp":"w7-ventricles","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A tumor of the pineal gland grows down and pinches shut the cerebral aqueduct just below it. Cerebrospinal fluid is still made by the choroid plexus in every ventricle. On a brain scan, which spaces will be enlarged?","options":["Only the fourth ventricle and the central canal, which lie below the block","Both lateral ventricles and the third ventricle, but not the fourth ventricle","All four ventricles, because the fluid backs up through the whole system","Only the third ventricle, because the interventricular foramen stays open"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Cerebrospinal fluid flows one way: from each lateral ventricle through an interventricular foramen into the third ventricle, then through the cerebral aqueduct of the midbrain into the fourth ventricle, and out through the apertures into the subarachnoid space. The pineal gland sits at the back of the third ventricle, just above the corpora quadrigemina of the midbrain, so a mass there can squeeze the cerebral aqueduct that runs under them. Fluid made upstream of the block, in the lateral ventricles and the third ventricle, has no way out, so those chambers swell. The fourth ventricle sits downstream, so it stays a normal size. An open interventricular foramen does not help, because it only lets the lateral ventricles drain into the third ventricle, which is itself trapped.","notes":["The fourth ventricle and the central canal lie downstream of the cerebral aqueduct, so fluid trapped above the block never reaches them. They would enlarge only if the block sat lower, at the apertures of the fourth ventricle.","Correct. Everything upstream of the cerebral aqueduct swells, which is both lateral ventricles and the third ventricle, while the fourth ventricle below the block stays normal.","All four ventricles enlarge when the block is at the very end of the circuit, such as at the apertures or the arachnoid granulations. A block at the aqueduct spares the fourth ventricle.","The open interventricular foramen is exactly why the lateral ventricles also swell. Fluid from them pours into the third ventricle, which cannot empty, so the lateral ventricles fill up as well."],"fix":"Review the path of cerebrospinal fluid through the ventricles and which chambers sit upstream and downstream of the cerebral aqueduct."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-brain-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-brain","comp":"w7-circle-of-willis","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"A patient's left internal carotid artery slowly closes in the neck, yet the left middle cerebral artery still receives blood. Which list traces one route that blood can take from the posterior circulation to reach it?","options":["Basilar artery, vertebral artery, posterior cerebral artery, posterior communicating artery","Vertebral artery, basilar artery, posterior communicating artery, posterior cerebral artery","Vertebral artery, basilar artery, posterior cerebral artery, posterior communicating artery","Vertebral artery, posterior inferior cerebellar artery, basilar artery, posterior communicating artery"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The two vertebral arteries enter the skull through the foramen magnum and unite to form the basilar artery on the front of the pons. At the top of the pons the basilar artery splits into the right and left posterior cerebral arteries. Each posterior communicating artery joins the posterior cerebral artery to the internal carotid artery on the same side, linking the posterior and anterior circulations. Blood can flow forward through that bridge into the end of the internal carotid artery and on into the middle cerebral artery, which is why a slow closure in the neck may cause no stroke.","notes":["This starts at the basilar artery and then goes back to the vertebral artery, but the vertebral arteries come first. They unite to form the basilar artery, so blood cannot flow from the basilar artery back into them on this route.","This swaps the last two vessels. The posterior communicating artery joins the posterior cerebral artery to the internal carotid artery, so blood must reach the posterior cerebral artery before it can cross the posterior communicating artery.","Correct. Blood runs from the vertebral artery to the basilar artery, out the posterior cerebral artery, and forward across the posterior communicating artery to the internal carotid and the middle cerebral artery.","The posterior inferior cerebellar artery is a branch that leaves the vertebral artery to feed the inferior cerebellum and the medulla. It is a dead end for this route, not a link in the ring."],"fix":"Review the vessels of the Circle of Willis and how the posterior communicating artery links the posterior cerebral artery to the internal carotid artery."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-brain-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-brain","comp":"w7-cranial-meninges","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Cerebrospinal fluid over the top of the brain is returned to the blood through the arachnoid granulations. In which order does that blood pass these dural venous sinuses on its way to the internal jugular vein?","options":["Superior sagittal sinus, confluence of sinuses, transverse sinus, sigmoid sinus","Superior sagittal sinus, transverse sinus, confluence of sinuses, sigmoid sinus","Confluence of sinuses, superior sagittal sinus, sigmoid sinus, transverse sinus","Superior sagittal sinus, sigmoid sinus, confluence of sinuses, transverse sinus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The arachnoid granulations push up into the superior sagittal sinus, which runs along the top edge of the falx cerebri in the longitudinal fissure. That sinus flows back to the confluence of sinuses at the internal occipital protuberance, where the major sinuses meet. From the confluence, the paired transverse sinuses run out to each side, and each one turns down as an S-shaped sigmoid sinus toward the jugular foramen. There the blood leaves the skull in the internal jugular vein.","notes":["Correct. Blood runs back along the superior sagittal sinus to the confluence of sinuses, then out the transverse sinus and down the sigmoid sinus to the internal jugular vein.","This puts the transverse sinus before the confluence of sinuses. The transverse sinuses leave from the confluence, so blood from the superior sagittal sinus has to reach the meeting point first.","The confluence of sinuses sits at the back of the skull, at the end of the superior sagittal sinus, so it cannot come first. This order also has the sigmoid sinus draining into the transverse sinus, which is backward.","The sigmoid sinus is the last sinus before the jugular foramen, not the second. Students choose this when they remember that it curves but forget that it is the final turn down toward the neck."],"fix":"Review the dural venous sinuses in order from the superior sagittal sinus to the internal jugular vein."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-brain-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-brain","comp":"w7-cerebrum-surface","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A small stroke damages the cortex of the left hemisphere on the gyrus that lies between the precentral sulcus and the central sulcus. Which problem is the patient most likely to have?","options":["Loss of touch and other body sensation on the right side","Weakness of voluntary movement on the left side of the body","Loss of vision even though both eyes and optic nerves are healthy","Weakness of voluntary movement on the right side of the body"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The gyrus bounded in front by the precentral sulcus and behind by the central sulcus is the precentral gyrus, the last gyrus of the frontal lobe. It holds the primary motor cortex, which starts voluntary movement. Most motor fibers cross to the other side at the decussation of the pyramids in the medulla, so the left motor cortex controls the right side of the body. Damage there causes weakness on the right.","notes":["Touch is handled by the postcentral gyrus, which lies just behind the central sulcus in the parietal lobe. This would be the answer if the stroke hit the gyrus behind the central sulcus instead of in front of it.","The location is right for movement, but the side is wrong. Motor fibers cross in the medulla, so a left-hemisphere stroke weakens the right side, not the left.","Vision is processed in the occipital lobe at the back of the cerebrum, far from the central sulcus. This would fit a stroke in the occipital lobe.","Correct. The precentral gyrus holds the primary motor cortex, and the left side of the brain controls the right side of the body."],"fix":"Review how to find the precentral and postcentral gyri using the central sulcus and what each gyrus controls."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-cn-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-cn","comp":"w8-cranial-nerves","type":"Sequence","dok":3,"q":"Light hits the retina of the right eye. In which order does that visual signal travel past these structures on its way to the thalamus?","options":["Optic canal, optic tract, optic chiasm, lateral geniculate nucleus","Optic chiasm, optic canal, optic tract, lateral geniculate nucleus","Optic canal, optic chiasm, optic tract, lateral geniculate nucleus","Optic canal, optic chiasm, lateral geniculate nucleus, optic tract"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The optic nerve (CN II) leaves the back of the eyeball and passes out of the orbit through the optic canal of the sphenoid bone. Inside the cranial cavity, the two optic nerves meet at the optic chiasm, just in front of the pituitary stalk. At the chiasm the fibers from the nasal half of each retina cross to the other side, so the right eye's signal continues in both optic tracts. Behind the chiasm the fibers continue as the optic tract, which wraps around the cerebral peduncle of the midbrain to reach the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus. On the inferior view of the brain you can follow this as nerve, then chiasm, then tract.","notes":["This puts the optic tract before the optic chiasm. The name tract is used only behind the chiasm, so the signal must cross the chiasm first.","The optic chiasm lies inside the cranial cavity, so the optic nerve must pass the optic canal in the sphenoid bone to get there. This order would have the nerve meeting its partner while still in the orbit.","Correct. The optic nerve passes the optic canal, meets the other nerve at the optic chiasm, continues as the optic tract, and ends in the lateral geniculate nucleus.","The optic tract is the link that carries the signal to the lateral geniculate nucleus, so it cannot come after it. This order skips straight from the chiasm to the thalamus."],"fix":"Review the optic nerve, optic chiasm, and optic tract on the inferior view of the brain and where the optic canal sits."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-cn-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-cn","comp":"w8-cranial-nerves","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A slow-growing tumor inside the right internal acoustic meatus causes hearing loss in the right ear. If it also presses on the other cranial nerve that passes through this same opening, which problem would that cause?","options":["A drooping right face and trouble closing the right eye","Numbness of the right cheek and weakness when chewing on that side","Trouble swallowing and a hoarse voice from a weak right vocal fold","Weakness when shrugging the right shoulder against resistance"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The facial nerve (CN VII) and the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) leave the side of the brainstem at the pontomedullary junction and enter the internal acoustic meatus together. Hearing loss comes from pressure on CN VIII. The only other nerve in that opening is the facial nerve, and the facial nerve controls the muscles of facial expression. That causes drooping of the face and trouble closing the eye on the same side.","notes":["Correct. The facial nerve shares the internal acoustic meatus with the vestibulocochlear nerve, and pressure on it weakens the muscles of facial expression.","Facial numbness and weak chewing come from the trigeminal nerve (CN V), whose branches leave the skull through the superior orbital fissure, the foramen rotundum, and the foramen ovale. It does not pass through the internal acoustic meatus.","Trouble swallowing and a hoarse voice point to the glossopharyngeal (CN IX) and vagus (CN X) nerves. They leave through the jugular foramen, so this would fit a tumor there instead.","Weak shoulder shrug points to the accessory nerve (CN XI), which leaves through the jugular foramen. It does not pass through the internal acoustic meatus."],"fix":"Review which cranial nerves pass through the internal acoustic meatus and the jugular foramen and what each nerve does."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-cn-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-cn","comp":"w8-cranial-nerves","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A hard blow to the nose drives the nasal bones back and cracks the thin, sieve-like plate of bone that forms the roof of the nasal cavity. Which loss is the most likely result?","options":["Loss of vision in one eye","Loss of taste on the front of the tongue","Loss of feeling across the forehead","Loss of the sense of smell"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The thin, sieve-like plate in the roof of the nasal cavity is the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone. The olfactory nerve (CN I) is made of many small bundles that pass up through the olfactory foramina in this plate to reach the olfactory bulb on the floor of the anterior cranial cavity. A fracture of the plate can tear these delicate fibers, so smell is the sense most at risk. The optic nerve leaves through the optic canal of the sphenoid bone, and the other senses in the choices travel in nerves that do not pass through the cribriform plate.","notes":["Vision travels in the optic nerve (CN II), which passes through the optic canal of the sphenoid bone, not the cribriform plate. It would be at risk with a fracture through the optic canal.","Taste from the front of the tongue travels in the facial nerve (CN VII), which runs through the internal acoustic meatus in the temporal bone. A fracture in the roof of the nasal cavity would not reach it.","Feeling across the forehead is carried by the ophthalmic branch (V1) of the trigeminal nerve, which passes through the superior orbital fissure. The cribriform plate in the roof of the nasal cavity does not carry it.","Correct. The olfactory nerve fibers pass through the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb, so a crack in this plate of the ethmoid bone can tear them."],"fix":"Review which cranial nerve passes through the cribriform plate and where the olfactory bulb and tract sit."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-cn-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-cn","comp":"w8-cranial-nerves","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A small tumor presses on the roots of the cranial nerves that leave the side and front of the medulla oblongata on the left, below the pontomedullary junction. Which set of findings fits this lesion best?","options":["Double vision, a drooping left eyelid, and a left pupil that will not constrict","Hearing loss in the left ear and a drooping left side of the face","Numbness of the left face and weakness when chewing on the left","Hoarse voice, trouble swallowing, and a tongue that is weak on the left"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The cranial nerves attach to the brainstem roughly in number order from top to bottom. The oculomotor nerve leaves the midbrain, the trigeminal nerve leaves the pons, and the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves leave at the pontomedullary junction. Below that junction, the glossopharyngeal, vagus, and accessory nerves leave the side of the medulla oblongata, and the hypoglossal nerve leaves its front. Damage there affects swallowing and voice (CN IX and CN X) and moving the tongue (CN XII), which matches the hoarse voice, trouble swallowing, and weak tongue.","notes":["These signs point to the oculomotor nerve (CN III), which leaves the midbrain at the top of the brainstem. They would fit a lesion much higher up.","Hearing loss and facial drooping point to CN VIII and CN VII, which leave right at the pontomedullary junction rather than below it. This choice is one level too high.","Facial numbness and weak chewing point to the trigeminal nerve (CN V), which attaches to the side of the pons. That is above the medulla, so this choice is also too high.","Correct. The glossopharyngeal, vagus, and hypoglossal nerves leave the medulla oblongata, so damage there causes a hoarse voice, trouble swallowing, and a weak tongue."],"fix":"Review where each cranial nerve attaches to the brainstem and what each nerve does."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-spinal-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-spinal","comp":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"During a lumbar puncture, the needle passes the ligamentum flavum and keeps going until cerebrospinal fluid drips out. Which list ranks these four layers and spaces from the first one the needle reaches to the last?","options":["Dura mater, epidural space, arachnoid mater, subarachnoid space","Epidural space, dura mater, arachnoid mater, subarachnoid space","Epidural space, dura mater, subarachnoid space, arachnoid mater","Epidural space, arachnoid mater, dura mater, subarachnoid space"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"In the vertebral canal, the epidural space lies between the vertebral bone and the spinal dura mater and is filled with fat and veins, so the needle enters it first. Next comes the tough dura mater, then the thin subdural space and the web-like arachnoid mater pressed against it. Only after passing the arachnoid mater does the needle reach the subarachnoid space, which holds the cerebrospinal fluid. The fluid dripping out is the sign that the needle tip has just entered that last space, which lies between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater.","notes":["The dura mater cannot come before the epidural space, because the epidural space is defined as the gap outside the dura, between it and the bone. This order would put the space inside the dura.","Correct. The needle crosses the epidural space, the dura mater, and the arachnoid mater, and then reaches the cerebrospinal fluid in the subarachnoid space.","The subarachnoid space lies deep to the arachnoid mater, as its name says, so the needle must pass the arachnoid mater first. Reaching the fluid before the arachnoid is not possible.","This places the arachnoid mater outside the dura mater, but the meninges always run dura, arachnoid, pia from outside in. Students choose this when they mix up the order of the outer two membranes."],"fix":"Review the spinal meninges and the epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid spaces in order from the bone inward."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-spinal-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-spinal","comp":"w8-cord-external","type":"Priority","dok":4,"q":"A lumbar puncture is mistakenly done between L1 and L2 instead of between L3 and L4. Which structure does this put most at risk, and why is the lower site normally safe?","options":["The conus medullaris; below it the canal holds loose nerve roots and the filum terminale","The lumbar enlargement; below it the cord narrows into a thin thread of pia mater","The dorsal root ganglia; below L2 they move out of the vertebral canal entirely","The cauda equina; below L2 most of its nerve roots have already left through their foramina"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"In an adult the spinal cord ends as the tapered conus medullaris at about the disc between L1 and L2, because the vertebral column grows longer than the spinal cord during development. A needle at that level can strike the end of the cord itself. Below the conus, the vertebral canal holds a large subarachnoid space filled with cerebrospinal fluid, the floating nerve roots of the cauda equina, and the filum terminale. The roots tend to slide away from a needle, which is why L3 to L4 is the usual site. Putting together the vertebral level where the cord ends, what lies below it, and which space holds the fluid is what makes the lower site safe.","notes":["Correct. The cord ends at the conus medullaris near L1 to L2, and below that the needle meets only floating nerve roots and the filum terminale.","The lumbar enlargement lies just above the conus medullaris, so the part of the cord a needle at L1 to L2 can hit is the tapered end, not the enlargement. The thin thread of pia mater described here is the filum terminale, which lies below the conus.","The dorsal root ganglia sit near the intervertebral foramina, off to the side of the midline needle path. They do not explain why one level is safer than another for a midline puncture.","The cauda equina is still in the vertebral canal at L3 to L4, which is exactly where a lumbar puncture is done. Its roots do not leave all at once, and they are less at risk than the solid end of the cord."],"fix":"Review where the adult spinal cord ends, what fills the vertebral canal below the conus medullaris, and why lumbar puncture is done at L3 to L4."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-spinal-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-spinal","comp":"w8-cord-roots","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"To treat severe pain, a surgeon cuts only the dorsal roots of several neighboring spinal segments, between each dorsal root ganglion and the spinal cord. What happens in the band of skin and the muscles served by those segments?","options":["The muscles cannot contract on command, but skin sensation stays normal","Skin sensation is lost and the muscles cannot contract on command","Skin sensation is lost, but the muscles can still contract on command","Neither is lost, because each spinal nerve is still intact past the roots"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"Each spinal nerve joins the cord by two roots. The dorsal root carries sensory axons into the cord, and their cell bodies sit in the dorsal root ganglion, the swelling on that root. The ventral root carries motor axons out to the muscles. Cutting only the dorsal roots between the ganglion and the cord stops sensory signals from entering the cord, so the band of skin those segments serve goes numb. Several neighboring segments are cut because the skin areas of next-door segments overlap. The ventral roots are untouched, so the muscles still receive their motor signals and can contract when the brain calls for it.","notes":["Losing muscle contraction would mean the ventral roots were cut, since they carry the motor axons out to the muscles. This is the pattern you would see after a ventral root injury.","Losing both would require cutting the whole spinal nerve beyond the point where the two roots join, or cutting both roots. Here only the dorsal roots were cut.","Correct. The dorsal roots carry sensory axons into the cord, so cutting them numbs the skin while the motor axons in the ventral roots still reach the muscles.","The spinal nerves outside the vertebral canal are still intact, but their sensory axons can no longer reach the cord. A signal that never enters the cord is not felt."],"fix":"Review which spinal root carries sensory axons and which carries motor axons, and where the dorsal root ganglion sits."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-spinal-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-spinal","comp":"w8-cord-external","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On the posterior view of the cadaver spinal cord, you see two places where the cord is visibly wider than the cord around it. What explains the extra width at each spot?","options":["Each one holds the extra neurons for the nerves of a limb","Each one holds the thicker dura mater needed where the column bends most","Each one holds the extra cerebrospinal fluid that pools in the central canal","Each one holds the cell bodies of the dorsal root ganglia for that region"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The cervical enlargement and the lumbar enlargement are the two wide spots of the spinal cord. The cervical enlargement gives rise to the nerves of the upper limbs, which reach them through the brachial plexus. The lumbar enlargement gives rise to the nerves of the lower limbs through the lumbar and sacral plexuses. The limbs have many muscles and a large area of skin, so the cord at those levels needs extra neurons, and that extra gray matter is what makes it wider.","notes":["Correct. The cervical and lumbar enlargements hold the extra neurons that serve the upper and lower limbs.","The width belongs to the cord itself, the nervous tissue inside the meninges, not to the dura mater wrapped around it. The enlargements are seen on the cord after the dura is opened.","The central canal is a tiny channel in the middle of the cord, and it does not swell at these levels. Extra fluid in it would point to a problem, not to normal anatomy.","The dorsal root ganglia sit on the dorsal roots outside the cord, near the intervertebral foramina. They are separate swellings and do not widen the cord itself."],"fix":"Review the cervical and lumbar enlargements and which limbs and plexuses each one serves."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-eye-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-eye","comp":"w7-lab-extraocular","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"You are sorting the six extraocular muscles on a cadaver orbit. Which feature sets the inferior oblique apart from all five of the others?","options":["It inserts on the inferior surface of the eyeball and pulls the eye straight down","It arises from the floor of the orbit near the front instead of from the back of the orbit","It arises from the common tendinous ring at the back of the orbit","Its tendon runs through the trochlea, a pulley on the upper medial wall of the orbit"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"Five of the six extraocular muscles start at the back of the orbit. The four rectus muscles arise from the common tendinous ring, and the superior oblique arises from the sphenoid bone near it. The inferior oblique is the only one that arises from the floor of the orbit near the front, on the maxilla. It then runs back under the eyeball to insert on its posterior, inferolateral side. That front origin is the fastest way to pick it out on a cadaver or a model.","notes":["Inserting on the inferior surface of the eyeball and pulling it straight down describes the inferior rectus. The inferior oblique inserts farther back and to the side and moves the eye up and out.","Correct. The inferior oblique is the only extraocular muscle that starts at the front of the orbit, on its floor.","The common tendinous ring is the origin of the four rectus muscles. It is shared by four muscles, so it cannot set any one of them apart.","Running through the trochlea is the feature of the superior oblique, not the inferior oblique. It would be the answer if the question asked how to pick out the superior oblique."],"fix":"Review the origin and insertion of each extraocular muscle, especially the two oblique muscles."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-eye-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-eye","comp":"w7-lab-extraocular","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A patient's right eye cannot turn out toward the ear. When she looks straight ahead, the right eye drifts toward the nose. Which muscle is not working, and why does the eye drift?","options":["The medial rectus is weak, so the lateral rectus pulls the eye toward the nose","The inferior rectus is weak, so the superior rectus pulls the eye toward the nose","The superior oblique is weak, so the inferior oblique pulls the eye toward the nose","The lateral rectus is weak, so the medial rectus pulls the eye toward the nose"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"The lateral rectus inserts on the lateral surface of the eyeball and abducts it, turning it out toward the ear. Its partner on the other side of the eye is the medial rectus, which inserts on the medial surface and adducts the eye toward the nose. Normally the two balance each other. When the lateral rectus stops working, the medial rectus pulls without opposition, so the eye cannot turn out and drifts inward even at rest.","notes":["The medial rectus pulls the eye toward the nose, so a weak medial rectus would let the eye drift out toward the ear. This choice also has the lateral rectus pulling toward the nose, but it pulls the eye outward.","The inferior and superior rectus muscles move the eye down and up. A weak inferior rectus would let the eye drift upward, not toward the nose.","The superior oblique moves the eye down and out, and the inferior oblique moves it up and out. Neither one turns the eye in toward the nose, so this would not explain an inward drift.","Correct. The lateral rectus turns the eye out, and without it the medial rectus pulls the eye toward the nose."],"fix":"Review the action of each rectus muscle and which muscle opposes each one."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-eye-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-eye","comp":"w7-lab-extraocular","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"On the eye model, a student pulls on the tendon that runs forward from the back of the orbit, passes through the trochlea on the upper medial wall, and then turns back to attach to the upper outer part of the back of the eyeball. Which way does the front of the eye turn?","options":["Down and toward the nose","Up and toward the ear","Down and toward the ear","Up and toward the nose"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The tendon described is the superior oblique, which arises from the sphenoid bone at the back of the orbit and passes through the trochlea, a pulley at the front of the upper medial wall. Because the tendon turns back at the pulley, it pulls on the eyeball from the front and medial side rather than from the back. It attaches on the posterior, superolateral part of the eyeball, so pulling it draws the back of the eyeball up and toward the pulley. That tips the front of the eye down and out toward the ear, and it also rotates the eye inward.","notes":["Down and toward the nose is the wrong side. The superior oblique turns the front of the eye out toward the ear, and the medial rectus is the muscle that turns it toward the nose.","Up and toward the ear is the action of the inferior oblique, the other oblique muscle, which arises from the floor of the orbit. Students choose this when they think a superior muscle must turn the eye up.","Correct. The superior oblique, working through the trochlea, turns the front of the eye down and out.","Up and toward the nose does not match either oblique muscle. Both oblique muscles turn the eye out toward the ear, one downward and one upward."],"fix":"Review the path of the superior oblique tendon through the trochlea and the actions of the two oblique muscles."},
  {"id":"app-lab-m5-eye-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-m5-eye","comp":"w7-lab-extraocular","type":"Ranking","dok":3,"q":"On a coronal section through the orbit just behind the eyeball, a probe is pushed straight down through the middle of the orbit, from the roof to the floor. Which list ranks these structures from the first one the probe meets to the last?","options":["Levator palpebrae superioris, superior rectus, optic nerve (CN II), inferior rectus","Levator palpebrae superioris, superior rectus, inferior rectus, optic nerve (CN II)","Superior rectus, levator palpebrae superioris, optic nerve (CN II), inferior rectus","Levator palpebrae superioris, optic nerve (CN II), superior rectus, inferior rectus"],"correctIndex":0,"why":"The levator palpebrae superioris runs forward just under the roof of the orbit to reach the upper eyelid, so it is the first muscle below the roof. The superior rectus lies directly beneath it, above the optic nerve. The four rectus muscles form a cone around the optic nerve (CN II), which runs through the middle of the orbit from the back of the eyeball to the optic canal. Below the optic nerve lies the inferior rectus, just above the orbital floor. Stacking the levator on top of the superior rectus is the step students most often miss on the model.","notes":["Correct. From the roof down, the probe meets the levator palpebrae superioris, the superior rectus, the optic nerve in the middle of the cone, and then the inferior rectus.","The optic nerve runs through the center of the cone of rectus muscles, so it lies above the inferior rectus, not below it. This order would place the nerve on the floor of the orbit.","This puts the superior rectus above the levator palpebrae superioris, but the levator lies on top, closest to the roof, because it has to reach the upper eyelid. Students reverse them because the two muscles lie pressed together.","The optic nerve sits inside the cone formed by the rectus muscles, so the probe meets the superior rectus before it reaches the nerve. This order puts the nerve outside the cone."],"fix":"Review how the levator palpebrae superioris, the four rectus muscles, and the optic nerve are stacked inside the orbit."},
  {"id":"app-lab-mx-face-01","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-mx-face","comp":"w7-lab-facial-expression","type":"Trigger","dok":4,"q":"A patient wakes up unable to raise her right eyebrow, close her right eye, or pull the right corner of her mouth up and out to smile. When she clenches her teeth, the muscles over both temples and the backs of both cheeks harden normally. Which conclusion fits all of these findings?","options":["The orbicularis oculi, zygomaticus minor, and risorius are weak, so the oculomotor nerve (CN III) is affected and the facial nerve (CN VII) is spared","The frontalis, orbicularis oculi, and masseter are weak, so the trigeminal nerve (CN V) is affected and the facial nerve (CN VII) is spared","The frontalis, orbicularis oris, and temporalis are weak, so both the facial nerve (CN VII) and the trigeminal nerve (CN V) are affected","The frontalis, orbicularis oculi, and zygomaticus major are weak, so the facial nerve (CN VII) is affected and the trigeminal nerve (CN V) is spared"],"correctIndex":3,"why":"Raising the eyebrow is the job of the frontalis, closing the eye is the job of the orbicularis oculi, and pulling the corner of the mouth up and out is the job of the zygomaticus major. All three are muscles of facial expression, which are controlled by the facial nerve (CN VII). The muscles you feel harden over the temple and the back of the cheek when clenching are the temporalis and the masseter, which are muscles of chewing controlled by the trigeminal nerve (CN V). Since chewing is normal, CN V is working, and the problem is limited to CN VII on the right, the pattern seen in Bell's palsy.","notes":["The oculomotor nerve moves the eyeball and raises the upper eyelid, but it does not control any muscle of facial expression. Closing the eye is the work of the orbicularis oculi, which belongs to the facial nerve.","The masseter is one of the muscles that hardens normally when she clenches, so it is not weak. Chewing is the trigeminal nerve's motor job, so normal clenching shows CN V is working.","The temporalis is also working, since she can feel it harden over her temples. The orbicularis oris closes the lips, which was not tested, so this choice names muscles the findings do not support.","Correct. All three lost movements belong to muscles of facial expression, while the chewing muscles are normal, so CN VII is affected and CN V is spared."],"fix":"Review what the frontalis, orbicularis oculi, zygomaticus major, masseter, and temporalis each do and which cranial nerve controls facial expression and which controls chewing."},
  {"id":"app-lab-mx-face-02","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-mx-face","comp":"w7-lab-mastication","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A car crash breaks a patient's zygomatic arch and also chips off the coronoid process of the mandible on the same side. Which jaw-closing muscles have lost a bony attachment?","options":["Only the masseter, because both of these bony marks are its attachments","The masseter at its origin and the temporalis at its insertion","The masseter at its insertion and the temporalis at its origin","Only the temporalis, because both of these bony marks are its attachments"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The masseter arises from the zygomatic arch and inserts on the angle and ramus of the mandible. The temporalis arises from the temporal fossa on the side of the skull, and its tendon passes deep to the zygomatic arch to insert on the coronoid process of the mandible. A broken zygomatic arch therefore disturbs the origin of the masseter, and a chipped coronoid process disturbs the insertion of the temporalis. Both muscles elevate the mandible, so closing the jaw on that side will be weak and painful.","notes":["The masseter does arise from the zygomatic arch, but it inserts on the angle and ramus of the mandible, not the coronoid process. The coronoid process belongs to the temporalis.","Correct. The zygomatic arch is the origin of the masseter, and the coronoid process is the insertion of the temporalis.","This reverses the attachments. The masseter starts on the zygomatic arch and ends on the mandible, while the temporalis starts in the temporal fossa and ends on the coronoid process.","The temporalis inserts on the coronoid process, but it arises from the temporal fossa, not the zygomatic arch. Its tendon only passes deep to the arch on the way down."],"fix":"Review the origin and insertion of the masseter and the temporalis."},
  {"id":"app-lab-mx-face-03","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-mx-face","comp":"w7-lab-neck","type":"Trigger","dok":3,"q":"A baby is born with one shortened, tight muscle on the side of the neck. The baby's head stays tilted toward the right shoulder, and the chin is turned up and to the left. Which muscle is shortened?","options":["The left sternocleidomastoid","The platysma on the right side","The right sternocleidomastoid","The platysma on the left side"],"correctIndex":2,"why":"The sternocleidomastoid runs from the sternum and clavicle to the mastoid process behind the ear. When one side contracts, it pulls that mastoid process down toward the same shoulder, which tilts the head to that side and turns the face to the opposite side. A tight right sternocleidomastoid therefore tilts the head toward the right shoulder and turns the chin to the left. This condition is called torticollis, or wry neck.","notes":["A tight left sternocleidomastoid would tilt the head toward the left shoulder and turn the chin to the right, the mirror image of what this baby shows.","The platysma is a thin sheet in the skin of the neck that runs from the chest up to the mandible and the corner of the mouth. It does not attach to the mastoid process, so it tenses the neck skin and pulls the mouth down but cannot tilt and turn the head.","Correct. The right sternocleidomastoid pulls the right mastoid process down, tilting the head right and turning the chin to the left.","The platysma on either side runs from the chest to the mandible, not to the mastoid process behind the ear. Students pick it because it covers the front of the neck, but it cannot hold the head tilted and turned."],"fix":"Review the attachments of the sternocleidomastoid and which way the face turns when one side contracts."},
  {"id":"app-lab-mx-face-04","mode":"lab","topic":"lab-mx-face","comp":"w7-lab-extraocular","type":"Priority","dok":3,"q":"A patient's right upper eyelid droops, and she cannot lift it. She can still squeeze that eye shut tightly and wrinkle her forehead. Which muscle is most likely not working?","options":["The orbicularis oculi, the ring of muscle that circles the eye","The levator palpebrae superioris, which runs to the upper eyelid","The frontalis, which raises the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead","The superior rectus, which lies just above the eyeball in the orbit"],"correctIndex":1,"why":"The levator palpebrae superioris is the extra muscle in the orbit, besides the six that move the eyeball, and it runs forward above the superior rectus to the upper eyelid. Its job is to raise the upper eyelid, so when it fails the lid droops and cannot be lifted. Squeezing the eye shut is done by the orbicularis oculi, and wrinkling the forehead is done by the frontalis, and both still work. The superior rectus lies just below the levator but moves the eyeball upward, not the lid.","notes":["The orbicularis oculi closes the eye, and the patient can still squeeze it shut, so this muscle is working. A weak orbicularis oculi would leave the eye unable to close, not unable to open.","Correct. The levator palpebrae superioris raises the upper eyelid, so its loss leaves a drooping lid while closing the eye and wrinkling the forehead still work.","The frontalis raises the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead, and the patient can still do that. It can lift the brow a little but is not the muscle that opens the eyelid.","The superior rectus lies right under the levator palpebrae superioris, which is why students mix them up. It elevates the eyeball, so its loss would affect looking up, not lifting the eyelid."],"fix":"Review the levator palpebrae superioris and how its job differs from the orbicularis oculi, the frontalis, and the superior rectus."}
] };
